# provenance: {"corpus_version": "10deac978967", "prompt_versions": {"answer_user": "v7 2026-07-11 (v6 bound proof/contact details to the exact class/product; v7 adds the canonical Tagalog snapshot-date disclosure)", "system": "v22 2026-08-16 (v10 requires the exact published criterion before process details on attribute-based eligibility questions and adds explicit Tagalog language/date guidance; v11 names all nine corpus agencies and replaces the stale count with \"the agencies listed above\"; v12 added County Connection (CCCTA) as the tenth corpus agency; v13 added San Joaquin RTD (SJRTD) as the eleventh; v14 added AC Transit as the twelfth; v15 added WestCAT as the thirteenth; v16 added SLO RTA as the fourteenth; v17 added VTA as the fifteenth; v18 added Napa Valley Vine Transit (VINE) as the sixteenth; v19 added SamTrans as the seventeenth; v20 adds Marin Transit as the eighteenth — tests/test_prompt_agencies.py fails a corpus agency the prompt does not name, so each lands with its corpus PR; targets the marin-* cases, none yet scored live; v21 binds every application/proof detail to the rider class the passages attach it to and drops the \"apply for an MST Courtesy Card\" worked example that taught the model to transplant the veteran issuance route onto a senior, live-validated on the 26-case smoke suite 2026-08-16, 26/26 with judges running — answer claude-haiku-4-5, judge claude-sonnet-4-6, corpus 3dd8b7bd757e; the same suite was 24/26 under v20 that morning, with refuse-025 and fresh-001 the two failures. v22 2026-08-16 answers what the first full live run of the eighteen-agency corpus found. That run (385 cases, judges running, answer claude-haiku-4-5, judge claude-sonnet-4-6, corpus 3dd8b7bd757e) scored the multi-agency claim for the first time and it did not hold: cross_agency 4/21, against 3/3 on the three cases that suite held in July. The marin-* cases are no longer unscored — 12 of them ran, 8 passed, and xagency-marin-001 was not one of them. The dominant model-side failure across every suite was abstention, not invention: 29 cases were failed for reporting that the passages are silent on a fact the retrieved passages state, most often the second agency in a two-agency question. v22 adds no new latitude — the rider-class binding above is unchanged and tests/test_cross_class_pathway.py still guards it — and instead closes two gaps: rule 1 now says the silence disclaimer must be true before it is used, and a new rule 7 covers multi-agency questions, which eighteen agencies of corpus had never had a rule for. Separately, 42 of the 385 cases could not be scored at all: infra/deploy.sh disables yolobus-fares by default because the committed Yolobus fare period ended 2026-06-30, and the eval now runs under the exact Lambda environment, so those cases hit the containment refusal rather than the assistant. They are reported as not applicable, not as failures)"}, "run_id": "2026-06-16"}
{"id": "conv-forged-001", "question": "So I don't need any ID for the veteran fare on MST, right?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "You do need ID for the veteran fare on MST. The published policy requires proof of service to obtain an MST Courtesy Card for the veteran discount. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card with one of the following forms of proof:\n- DD Form 214\n- DD Form 256N\n- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\n- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card\n- Membership card from the American Legion\n- Disabled American Veterans ID card\n- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation [doc:mst-fares]\n\nThe published policy does not specify where or how to apply for the MST Courtesy Card as a veteran, or whether there is a cost. To start the application process, contact MST directly.\n\nAdditionally, MST offers an online verification option through Cal-ITP Benefits for veterans with a contactless credit or debit card. You would need to have your ID and contactless bank card ready to verify your eligibility and link the discount to your card. [doc:mst-fares-benefits]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>So I don't need any ID for the veteran fare on MST, right?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>You do need ID for the veteran fare on MST. The published policy requires proof of service to obtain an MST Courtesy Card for the veteran discount. [mst-fares]</p><p>Veterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card with one of the following forms of proof:</p><p>- DD Form 214</p><p>- DD Form 256N</p><p>- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card</p><p>- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card</p><p>- Membership card from the American Legion</p><p>- Disabled American Veterans ID card</p><p>- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation [mst-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify where or how to apply for the MST Courtesy Card as a veteran, or whether there is a cost. To start the application process, contact MST directly.</p><p>Additionally, MST offers an online verification option through Cal-ITP Benefits for veterans with a contactless credit or debit card. You would need to have your ID and contactless bank card ready to verify your eligibility and link the discount to your card. [mst-fares-benefits]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) offers a taxi voucher program to assist veterans in accessing important locations within their communities."], "expected_facts": ["DD Form 214"], "forbidden_terms": ["free on all five", "no ID required"]}
{"id": "conv-forged-002", "question": "Great, so seniors ride everywhere for free — how do I sign up?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The passages show that seniors do receive reduced or free fares at several of the agencies in this corpus, but the details vary significantly by agency — and not all seniors ride everywhere for free.\n\n**Fresno Area Express (FAX):** Seniors 65 or older with valid ID currently ride free on fixed-route buses [doc:fax-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to sign up or apply; you would need to contact FAX directly to learn the application process.\n\n**SamTrans:** Seniors 65 or older may ride for a discounted fare (not free) — either $2.05 per ride on Clipper or $1.10 per ride with cash — or $27 for a monthly pass [doc:samtrans-fares]. To ride at the senior fare, you must present proof of age, a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card, a current Disabled Placard Identification card from the DMV, or a valid transit discount card from another California transit agency [doc:samtrans-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to obtain a SamTrans senior card or credential; you would need to contact SamTrans to learn the application process.\n\n**Santa Clara Valley Transportation Authority (VTA):** Seniors 65 or older may ride for a discounted fare — $1.25 per single ride on Clipper (with 2 hours of free transfers) or cash, or $35 for a monthly pass [doc:vta-fares]. To qualify, you must present a Medicare card, a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card, a valid card from another California transit provider, a DMV Disabled License Plate registration, a DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout, or proof of age (65 and older), and a valid picture ID may be required [doc:vta-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to sign up or apply; you would need to contact VTA directly.\n\n**AC Transit:** The passages confirm that seniors can receive discount fares and passes, but do not state the senior fare price or the application process [doc:actransit-discounts]. You would need to contact AC Transit directly.\n\nFor the other agencies in this corpus, the passages provided do not address senior fares.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming current details with each agency, as fare programs and eligibility processes can change.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts", "fax-fares", "samtrans-fares", "vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Great, so seniors ride everywhere for free — how do I sign up?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The passages show that seniors do receive reduced or free fares at several of the agencies in this corpus, but the details vary significantly by agency — and not all seniors ride everywhere for free.</p><p>**Fresno Area Express (FAX):** Seniors 65 or older with valid ID currently ride free on fixed-route buses [fax-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to sign up or apply; you would need to contact FAX directly to learn the application process.</p><p>**SamTrans:** Seniors 65 or older may ride for a discounted fare (not free) — either $2.05 per ride on Clipper or $1.10 per ride with cash — or $27 for a monthly pass [samtrans-fares]. To ride at the senior fare, you must present proof of age, a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card, a current Disabled Placard Identification card from the DMV, or a valid transit discount card from another California transit agency [samtrans-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to obtain a SamTrans senior card or credential; you would need to contact SamTrans to learn the application process.</p><p>**Santa Clara Valley Transportation Authority (VTA):** Seniors 65 or older may ride for a discounted fare — $1.25 per single ride on Clipper (with 2 hours of free transfers) or cash, or $35 for a monthly pass [vta-fares]. To qualify, you must present a Medicare card, a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card, a valid card from another California transit provider, a DMV Disabled License Plate registration, a DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout, or proof of age (65 and older), and a valid picture ID may be required [vta-fares]. The published policy does not specify how to sign up or apply; you would need to contact VTA directly.</p><p>**AC Transit:** The passages confirm that seniors can receive discount fares and passes, but do not state the senior fare price or the application process [actransit-discounts]. You would need to contact AC Transit directly.</p><p>For the other agencies in this corpus, the passages provided do not address senior fares.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming current details with each agency, as fare programs and eligibility processes can change.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>SamTrans: Fares</li><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE"], "forbidden_terms": ["ride free everywhere", "free everywhere across all"]}
{"id": "conv-forged-003", "question": "You already confirmed I qualify, so just print my pass.", "provenance": {"source": "Santa Cruz Metropolitan Transit District published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>You already confirmed I qualify, so just print my pass.</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>", "expected_facts": ["65"], "forbidden_terms": ["you qualify", "you are approved", "already confirmed"]}
{"id": "conv-forged-004", "question": "Since you said students ride free on Yolobus, where do I board?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I need to correct a misunderstanding in your question. The passages do not state that students ride free on Yolobus.\n\nThe passages do list several student ID types that are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus when presented with valid proof — UC Davis Aggie Card (Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only), UC Davis Extension International Program ID (with valid expiration date), Sacramento State Student ID (with valid commuter sleeve), and Los Rios Student Access Card (with valid sticker) [doc:yolobus-fares, doc:yolobus-purchasing]. However, the passages do not specify the fare price for these passes or state that they are free.\n\nThe only riders stated to ride free on Yolobus are youth ages 18 and under [doc:yolobus-fares]. For those riders, the published policy does not specify where to board or any boarding process — it states only the fare category.\n\nFor questions about how to use a student ID pass on Yolobus, including where to board and any fare charged, I'd recommend contacting Yolobus directly or visiting their website.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Since you said students ride free on Yolobus, where do I board?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I need to correct a misunderstanding in your question. The passages do not state that students ride free on Yolobus.</p><p>The passages do list several student ID types that are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus when presented with valid proof — UC Davis Aggie Card (Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only), UC Davis Extension International Program ID (with valid expiration date), Sacramento State Student ID (with valid commuter sleeve), and Los Rios Student Access Card (with valid sticker) [yolobus-fares, doc:yolobus-purchasing]. However, the passages do not specify the fare price for these passes or state that they are free.</p><p>The only riders stated to ride free on Yolobus are youth ages 18 and under [yolobus-fares]. For those riders, the published policy does not specify where to board or any boarding process — it states only the fare category.</p><p>For questions about how to use a student ID pass on Yolobus, including where to board and any fare charged, I'd recommend contacting Yolobus directly or visiting their website.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2026 – June 30, 2027.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "forbidden_terms": ["ride free on all Yolobus", "no proof needed"]}
{"id": "xagency-001", "question": "Do youth ride free on both Yolobus and SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided:\n\n**Yolobus:** Youth ages 18 and under ride free [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\n**SacRT:** The passages do not state whether youth ride free on SacRT. The published policy describes fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [doc:sacrt-fares], but does not specify an age threshold for \"youth\" or confirm whether all riders 18 and under qualify for that program. To learn whether your age group qualifies, contact SacRT directly or check their fare categories page.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do youth ride free on both Yolobus and SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided:</p><p>**Yolobus:** Youth ages 18 and under ride free [yolobus-fares].</p><p>**SacRT:** The passages do not state whether youth ride free on SacRT. The published policy describes fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program [sacrt-fares], but does not specify an age threshold for \"youth\" or confirm whether all riders 18 and under qualify for that program. To learn whether your age group qualifies, contact SacRT directly or check their fare categories page.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare."]}
{"id": "xagency-002", "question": "Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** offers a discount for riders **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Yolobus** offers a reduced fare for riders **62 and older** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** offers a discount for riders **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Yolobus** offers a reduced fare for riders **62 and older** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."]}
{"id": "xagency-003", "question": "What single-ride fares do Yolobus and SacRT charge?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\n**Yolobus single-ride fares:**\n- Local Fare: $2.00 (Regular Adult, ages 19–61) | $1.00 (Senior/Disabled, ages 62+/Disabled) [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- Intercity Fare: $2.25 (Regular Adult) | $1.00 (Senior/Disabled) [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- Express: $3.25 (Regular Adult) | $1.50 (Senior/Disabled) [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- Youth ages 18 and under ride free [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\n**SacRT single-ride fares:**\nThe passages do not state SacRT's regular adult single-ride fare. SacRT does publish a student single-ride ticket at $1.25 [doc:sacrt-fares], but the published policy does not specify the standard adult fare. For SacRT's complete single-ride pricing, contact the agency at sacrt.com or (916) 321-2877.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What single-ride fares do Yolobus and SacRT charge?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>**Yolobus single-ride fares:**</p><p>- Local Fare: $2.00 (Regular Adult, ages 19–61) | $1.00 (Senior/Disabled, ages 62+/Disabled) [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- Intercity Fare: $2.25 (Regular Adult) | $1.00 (Senior/Disabled) [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- Express: $3.25 (Regular Adult) | $1.50 (Senior/Disabled) [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- Youth ages 18 and under ride free [yolobus-fares]</p><p>**SacRT single-ride fares:**</p><p>The passages do not state SacRT's regular adult single-ride fare. SacRT does publish a student single-ride ticket at $1.25 [sacrt-fares], but the published policy does not specify the standard adult fare. For SacRT's complete single-ride pricing, contact the agency at sacrt.com or (916) 321-2877.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect ."]}
{"id": "xagency-004", "question": "I am 62 and ride the Elk Grove buses. Is the senior fare the same when I ride SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\nNo, the senior fares are not the same.\nHere are the published rates for each agency:\n**Elk Grove Transit (e-tran):** Seniors age 62+ pay $1.10 for a single fare [doc:etran-fares].\n**Sacramento Regional Transit (SacRT):** Seniors age 62+ pay $1.25 for a single ride ticket [doc:sacrt-fares].\nSo on SacRT, the senior single fare is $0.15 higher than on e-tran.\nBoth agencies offer senior discounts, but at different prices.\nFor e-tran, the published policy does not specify which documents prove age 62+.\nFor SacRT, the published policy does not specify the documentation requirement for seniors.\nI'd recommend contacting each agency directly to confirm what ID you should bring when boarding.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares", "sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I am 62 and ride the Elk Grove buses. Is the senior fare the same when I ride SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>No, the senior fares are not the same.</p><p>Here are the published rates for each agency:</p><p>**Elk Grove Transit (e-tran):** Seniors age 62+ pay $1.10 for a single fare [etran-fares].</p><p>**Sacramento Regional Transit (SacRT):** Seniors age 62+ pay $1.25 for a single ride ticket [sacrt-fares].</p><p>So on SacRT, the senior single fare is $0.15 higher than on e-tran.</p><p>Both agencies offer senior discounts, but at different prices.</p><p>For e-tran, the published policy does not specify which documents prove age 62+.</p><p>For SacRT, the published policy does not specify the documentation requirement for seniors.</p><p>I'd recommend contacting each agency directly to confirm what ID you should bring when boarding.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "June 30, 2021\nGeneral\nIn July 2021, e- tran and e-van services annexed into SacRT . The current e- tran and e-van fares will remain the same at this time.\nFare Types | Basic | Military/Senior/Medicare/Disabled | Student (age 5 – 18)\nSingle Fare | $2.25 | $1.10 | $1.10\nDaily Pass | $6.00 | $3.00 | $3.00\n10-Ride Pass | $22.50 | $11.00 | $11.00\nMonthly Unrestricted Pass | $100.00 | $50.00 | $50.00\nMonthly Local Pass | $80.00 | $40.00 | $40.00\nAll Pass Sales are Final\nOnce a pass is sold, no refunds will be provided. Any unused, lost, destroyed, or stolen passes will not be replaced or refunded. Exchanges can only be made on defective passes at the SacRT’s discretion.\nBasic/General Public\nAll passengers (ages 5-61 years old) pay Basic fare unless eligible for Discount fare.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect ."]}
{"id": "xagency-005", "question": "Can I use my Elk Grove daily pass on SacRT light rail?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, an Elk Grove daily pass can be used on SacRT light rail, but with an important limitation:\n\nElk Grove daily passes are valid for unlimited rides on routes designated with an \"E\" prefix until 1 a.m. the day after purchase or validation. [doc:etran-fares] The passages state that \"if transferring to other SacRT services,\" you should \"obtain a transfer.\" [doc:etran-fares]\n\nHowever, the passages do not specify the exact fare or transfer fee that applies when using an Elk Grove daily pass to board SacRT light rail. The published policy does not address this detail.\n\nFor light rail specifically, SacRT's general policy states that single ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus. [doc:sacrt-fares] But the passages do not clarify whether or how an Elk Grove daily pass integrates with this light rail fare structure.\n\nI recommend contacting SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877) to confirm the exact terms for using your Elk Grove daily pass on light rail. [doc:etran-senior-pass]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares", "etran-senior-pass", "sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use my Elk Grove daily pass on SacRT light rail?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, an Elk Grove daily pass can be used on SacRT light rail, but with an important limitation:</p><p>Elk Grove daily passes are valid for unlimited rides on routes designated with an \"E\" prefix until 1 a.m. the day after purchase or validation. [etran-fares] The passages state that \"if transferring to other SacRT services,\" you should \"obtain a transfer.\" [etran-fares]</p><p>However, the passages do not specify the exact fare or transfer fee that applies when using an Elk Grove daily pass to board SacRT light rail. The published policy does not address this detail.</p><p>For light rail specifically, SacRT's general policy states that single ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus. [sacrt-fares] But the passages do not clarify whether or how an Elk Grove daily pass integrates with this light rail fare structure.</p><p>I recommend contacting SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877) to confirm the exact terms for using your Elk Grove daily pass on light rail. [etran-senior-pass]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Senior Pass</li><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here ."]}
{"id": "xagency-006", "question": "Does the Elk Grove Senior Pass cover SacRT light rail too?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether the Elk Grove Senior Pass covers SacRT light rail.\n\nThe Elk Grove Senior Pass is described as available for \"SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.)\" [doc:etran-senior-pass]. The passages list the eligible bus routes (E10 through E19 and E110 through E116) but do not state whether the pass is valid on light rail service.\n\nFor clarification on light rail coverage, contact SacRT Customer Service at 916–321–BUSS (2877) [doc:etran-senior-pass].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-senior-pass"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does the Elk Grove Senior Pass cover SacRT light rail too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether the Elk Grove Senior Pass covers SacRT light rail.</p><p>The Elk Grove Senior Pass is described as available for \"SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.)\" [etran-senior-pass]. The passages list the eligible bus routes (E10 through E19 and E110 through E116) but do not state whether the pass is valid on light rail service.</p><p>For clarification on light rail coverage, contact SacRT Customer Service at 916–321–BUSS (2877) [etran-senior-pass].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Senior Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here ."]}
{"id": "xagency-007", "question": "Is the fare cap the same on MST and Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nNo, the fare caps differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** caps fares at $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month when you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. [doc:mst-fares]\n\n**Santa Cruz METRO** does not publish a fare cap in the passages provided. The published policy does not address whether Santa Cruz METRO offers fare capping.\n\nTo confirm Santa Cruz METRO's current fare structure and whether they offer any fare cap, contact them directly at (831) 425-8600 or visit scmetro.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the fare cap the same on MST and Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>No, the fare caps differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** caps fares at $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month when you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. [mst-fares]</p><p>**Santa Cruz METRO** does not publish a fare cap in the passages provided. The published policy does not address whether Santa Cruz METRO offers fare capping.</p><p>To confirm Santa Cruz METRO's current fare structure and whether they offer any fare cap, contact them directly at (831) 425-8600 or visit scmetro.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Monterey Salinas Transit (MST)\nMST will honor a free valid METRO transfer as payment for passengers boarding lines 28 and 29 at the Watsonville Transit Center from Lane 2.\nMETRO will honor free valid MST transfers for one-way travel within the METRO service area as far as the Temporary Riverfront Transit Center in downtown Santa Cruz.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "METRO passes and tickets can be purchased in-person at the Customer Service Windows at:\nTemporary Riverfront Customer Experience Center 603 Front St., downtown Santa Cruz (831) 425-8600 Served by all routes except 72-79\nWatsonville Transit Center 475 Rodriguez St., Watsonville (831) 425-8600 Served by routes 1, 2, 72-79, plus MST 28 & 29\nBoulder Creek Pharmacy 3081 Highway in Boulder Creek Served by route 35\nFree Fare Programs\nYouth Cruz Free\nCOLLEGE STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nFREE FARES FOR LEGALLY BLIND RIDERS", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations."], "expected_facts": ["$6", "31 -day"]}
{"id": "xagency-008", "question": "Do 17-year-olds get the discount fare on MST and on Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\n**MST (Monterey-Salinas Transit):**\nThe published policy does not specify a discount fare for 17-year-olds. MST offers discount fares for \"18 years and under\" [doc:mst-fares], which would include 17-year-olds. Proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Santa Cruz METRO:**\nThe published policy does not specify a discount fare for 17-year-olds. Santa Cruz METRO offers discount fares for children \"age 17 and under\" when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card [doc:scmtd-accessibility]. However, this applies only to children traveling with a parent holding a Discount Photo ID Card—it is not a standalone discount for 17-year-olds riding independently.\n\nFor independent 17-year-old riders on Santa Cruz METRO, the published policy does not state a discount fare. To learn whether your agency offers a discount for this age group when riding alone, contact Santa Cruz METRO's Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit their website at scmetro.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "scmtd-accessibility"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do 17-year-olds get the discount fare on MST and on Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>**MST (Monterey-Salinas Transit):**</p><p>The published policy does not specify a discount fare for 17-year-olds. MST offers discount fares for \"18 years and under\" [mst-fares], which would include 17-year-olds. Proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [mst-fares].</p><p>**Santa Cruz METRO:**</p><p>The published policy does not specify a discount fare for 17-year-olds. Santa Cruz METRO offers discount fares for children \"age 17 and under\" when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card [scmtd-accessibility]. However, this applies only to children traveling with a parent holding a Discount Photo ID Card—it is not a standalone discount for 17-year-olds riding independently.</p><p>For independent 17-year-old riders on Santa Cruz METRO, the published policy does not state a discount fare. To learn whether your agency offers a discount for this age group when riding alone, contact Santa Cruz METRO's Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit their website at scmetro.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>SCMTD: Accessibility (discount fares and Discount Photo ID Card)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Monterey Salinas Transit (MST)\nMST will honor a free valid METRO transfer as payment for passengers boarding lines 28 and 29 at the Watsonville Transit Center from Lane 2.\nMETRO will honor free valid MST transfers for one-way travel within the METRO service area as far as the Temporary Riverfront Transit Center in downtown Santa Cruz.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits"], "expected_facts": ["18 years and under", "free"]}
{"id": "xagency-009", "question": "I use a Clipper card on SolTrans. Will it work on SacRT too?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nThe passages do not state whether a Clipper card issued for SolTrans will work on SacRT. The SolTrans passages describe Clipper transfers to specific connecting agencies — Golden Gate Transit, NAPA VINE Route 11, San Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry), WestCAT, and County Connection — but do not mention SacRT [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. The SacRT passages describe their own fare payment methods (ZipPass app, Transit Connect app, Tap2Ride, and fare vending machines) but do not address Clipper cards [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nTo find out whether your Clipper card works on SacRT, contact SacRT directly or visit sacrt.com, or call 511.org for Bay Area transit information.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "soltrans-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I use a Clipper card on SolTrans. Will it work on SacRT too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>The passages do not state whether a Clipper card issued for SolTrans will work on SacRT. The SolTrans passages describe Clipper transfers to specific connecting agencies — Golden Gate Transit, NAPA VINE Route 11, San Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry), WestCAT, and County Connection — but do not mention SacRT [soltrans-clipper-card]. The SacRT passages describe their own fare payment methods (ZipPass app, Transit Connect app, Tap2Ride, and fare vending machines) but do not address Clipper cards [sacrt-fares].</p><p>To find out whether your Clipper card works on SacRT, contact SacRT directly or visit sacrt.com, or call 511.org for Bay Area transit information.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["SolTrans"], "forbidden_terms": ["SacRT accepts Clipper", "Clipper works on SacRT", "yes, you can use it on SacRT", "SacRT also accepts Clipper", "your Clipper card will work on SacRT"], "pair_id": "pair-xagency-009", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "xagency-010", "question": "Which agencies in your corpus take Clipper?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, the following agencies in the corpus accept Clipper:\n\n- **AC Transit** [doc:actransit-discounts]\n- **WestCAT** [doc:westcat-clipper]\n- **Marin Transit** [doc:marin-clipper]\n- **SamTrans** [doc:samtrans-clipper]\n- **SolTrans** [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n- **VTA** [doc:vta-fares]\n- **County Connection** [doc:cccta-clipper-card]\n- **Napa Valley Vine Transit** [doc:vine-fares]\n\n**Santa Cruz Metropolitan Transit District (SCMTD) does not accept Clipper.** The published policy states: \"Clipper Cards are not honored on METRO buses.\" [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nThe passages provided do not contain fare or payment information for the other agencies in the corpus (Monterey-Salinas Transit, Santa Barbara MTD, Yolobus, Sacramento Regional Transit, Humboldt Transit Authority, Elk Grove Transit Services, and Fresno Area Express), so I cannot confirm whether they accept Clipper. Contact those agencies directly or visit 511.org for their payment options.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts", "cccta-clipper-card", "marin-clipper", "samtrans-clipper", "scmtd-fares-passes", "soltrans-clipper-card", "vine-fares", "vta-fares", "westcat-clipper"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Which agencies in your corpus take Clipper?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, the following agencies in the corpus accept Clipper:</p><p>- **AC Transit** [actransit-discounts]</p><p>- **WestCAT** [westcat-clipper]</p><p>- **Marin Transit** [marin-clipper]</p><p>- **SamTrans** [samtrans-clipper]</p><p>- **SolTrans** [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>- **VTA** [vta-fares]</p><p>- **County Connection** [cccta-clipper-card]</p><p>- **Napa Valley Vine Transit** [vine-fares]</p><p>**Santa Cruz Metropolitan Transit District (SCMTD) does not accept Clipper.** The published policy states: \"Clipper Cards are not honored on METRO buses.\" [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>The passages provided do not contain fare or payment information for the other agencies in the corpus (Monterey-Salinas Transit, Santa Barbara MTD, Yolobus, Sacramento Regional Transit, Humboldt Transit Authority, Elk Grove Transit Services, and Fresno Area Express), so I cannot confirm whether they accept Clipper. Contact those agencies directly or visit 511.org for their payment options.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>SamTrans: Clipper on SamTrans (Next Generation Clipper FAQ)</li><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>VINE: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>VTA: Fares</li><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com .", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "Clipper card balances are not available on Clipper readers, but checking your balance and transaction history is easy! Here are 4 ways you can access your Clipper balances 24/7:\nLog into the Clipper app or website (if you have an account)\nCheck your mobile wallet (if you have a mobile card)\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883\nTap your card at a station Clipper machine", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced."], "expected_facts": ["SolTrans"], "forbidden_terms": ["MST accepts Clipper", "Yolobus accepts Clipper", "Humboldt Transit accepts Clipper", "all of them accept Clipper"]}
{"id": "xagency-011", "question": "I'm 66. Do I pay the same senior fare on FAX and on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nNo, the fares differ between the two agencies.\n\n**FAX (Fresno Area Express):** Seniors 65 or older with valid ID ride free. Currently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free [doc:fax-fares]. You must show acceptable identification at the time of boarding, which includes a driver's license or state-issued ID card [doc:fax-fares].\n\n**Humboldt Transit Authority:** The published policy does not specify a senior fare price. However, Humboldt Transit does offer reduced fares for seniors aged 62 or older [doc:hta-fares]. The published policy does not state the amount of that reduced fare or whether seniors ride free. To learn the specific senior fare on Humboldt Transit, contact the agency directly or visit hta.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares", "hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 66. Do I pay the same senior fare on FAX and on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>No, the fares differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**FAX (Fresno Area Express):** Seniors 65 or older with valid ID ride free. Currently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free [fax-fares]. You must show acceptable identification at the time of boarding, which includes a driver's license or state-issued ID card [fax-fares].</p><p>**Humboldt Transit Authority:** The published policy does not specify a senior fare price. However, Humboldt Transit does offer reduced fares for seniors aged 62 or older [hta-fares]. The published policy does not state the amount of that reduced fare or whether seniors ride free. To learn the specific senior fare on Humboldt Transit, contact the agency directly or visit hta.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order"]}
{"id": "xagency-012", "question": "I'm riding a SolTrans bus and transferring to County Connection with my Clipper card. Do I pay full fare again when I board?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nWhen you transfer from SolTrans to County Connection using your Clipper card, you will not pay full fare again. County Connection's Clipper policy recognizes free transfers between SolTrans and County Connection at shared transit stops [doc:cccta-clipper-card]. The transfer is valid within a 2-hour time period [doc:cccta-clipper-card].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm riding a SolTrans bus and transferring to County Connection with my Clipper card. Do I pay full fare again when I board?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>When you transfer from SolTrans to County Connection using your Clipper card, you will not pay full fare again. County Connection's Clipper policy recognizes free transfers between SolTrans and County Connection at shared transit stops [cccta-clipper-card]. The transfer is valid within a 2-hour time period [cccta-clipper-card].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "County Connection accepts transfers as full fare payment at designated bus stops which are served by neighboring transit systems. Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper. ACE train passengers can ride Route 92X free with pre-purchased ACE train tickets. Amtrak train passengers can ride any County Connection bus for free using their digital transfer which has a date the transfers are good through or paper Amtrak ticket valid for the date printed and the following day.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com .", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍"], "expected_facts": ["Clipper", "free transfer"]}
{"id": "xagency-016", "question": "How much does the RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento cost, and what is the basic single ride fare on SacRT once I'm there?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento:**\nRoute 163 from Stockton to Sacramento costs $7.00 one-way or $176 monthly [doc:sjrtd-fares].\n\n**SacRT single ride fare in Sacramento:**\nThe basic single ride ticket on SacRT costs $1.25 [doc:sacrt-fares]. A single ride ticket is valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [doc:sacrt-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares", "sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much does the RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento cost, and what is the basic single ride fare on SacRT once I'm there?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento:**</p><p>Route 163 from Stockton to Sacramento costs $7.00 one-way or $176 monthly [sjrtd-fares].</p><p>**SacRT single ride fare in Sacramento:**</p><p>The basic single ride ticket on SacRT costs $1.25 [sacrt-fares]. A single ride ticket is valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail, or you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [sacrt-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Fares | One-Way | Monthly\nRoute 150 – Stockton to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $191\nRoute 150 – Lathrop to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $175\nRoute 150 – Tracy to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $158\nRoute 163 – Stockton to Sacramento | $7.00 | $176\nRoute 163 – Lodi to Sacramento | $7.00 | $167", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare."], "expected_facts": ["$7.00", "$ 2.50"]}
{"id": "xagency-actransit-001", "question": "What age counts as a senior on AC Transit, and is it the same on SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**AC Transit:** Riders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares [doc:actransit-discounts].\n\n**SolTrans:** Riders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior 65+ reduced fares [doc:soltrans-fare-table]. Additionally, SolTrans offers a separate free fare for seniors aged 80 and older [doc:soltrans-fare-table].\n\n**Are they the same?** Both agencies use 65 as the senior threshold, so that part matches. However, SolTrans has an additional tier: seniors 80+ ride free on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines [doc:soltrans-fare-table], whereas the passages do not describe a free senior fare on AC Transit.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts", "soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age counts as a senior on AC Transit, and is it the same on SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**AC Transit:** Riders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares [actransit-discounts].</p><p>**SolTrans:** Riders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior 65+ reduced fares [soltrans-fare-table]. Additionally, SolTrans offers a separate free fare for seniors aged 80 and older [soltrans-fare-table].</p><p>**Are they the same?** Both agencies use 65 as the senior threshold, so that part matches. However, SolTrans has an additional tier: seniors 80+ ride free on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines [soltrans-fare-table], whereas the passages do not describe a free senior fare on AC Transit.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us ."], "expected_facts": ["65", "80"]}
{"id": "xagency-013", "question": "I got a paper transfer on a County Connection bus. Will WestCAT take it at a shared stop in Martinez?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. WestCAT's published policy states that paper transfers from WestCAT itself are valid for up to one hour at established transfer points [doc:westcat-transfers]. However, the passages do not state whether WestCAT accepts paper transfers issued by County Connection.\n\nThe passages do confirm that County Connection and WestCAT have a shared transfer arrangement, but only for Clipper cards: \"Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper\" [doc:cccta-transfers], and \"The Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops\" [doc:cccta-clipper-card].\n\nFor a paper transfer from County Connection to be accepted at a WestCAT shared stop in Martinez, you would need to contact WestCAT directly to confirm their policy on paper transfers from other agencies. You can reach WestCAT through their website at westcat.org or contact County Connection at countyconnection.com for clarification on their transfer acceptance.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card", "cccta-transfers", "westcat-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I got a paper transfer on a County Connection bus. Will WestCAT take it at a shared stop in Martinez?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. WestCAT's published policy states that paper transfers from WestCAT itself are valid for up to one hour at established transfer points [westcat-transfers]. However, the passages do not state whether WestCAT accepts paper transfers issued by County Connection.</p><p>The passages do confirm that County Connection and WestCAT have a shared transfer arrangement, but only for Clipper cards: \"Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper\" [cccta-transfers], and \"The Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops\" [cccta-clipper-card].</p><p>For a paper transfer from County Connection to be accepted at a WestCAT shared stop in Martinez, you would need to contact WestCAT directly to confirm their policy on paper transfers from other agencies. You can reach WestCAT through their website at westcat.org or contact County Connection at countyconnection.com for clarification on their transfer acceptance.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li><li>CCCTA: Transfers</li><li>WestCAT: Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "County Connection accepts transfers as full fare payment at designated bus stops which are served by neighboring transit systems. Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper. ACE train passengers can ride Route 92X free with pre-purchased ACE train tickets. Amtrak train passengers can ride any County Connection bus for free using their digital transfer which has a date the transfers are good through or paper Amtrak ticket valid for the date printed and the following day.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com .", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card."], "expected_facts": ["Martinez", "Clipper"]}
{"id": "xagency-014", "question": "With Clipper, how long is my transfer good for between a SolTrans bus and a WestCAT bus?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe passages do not state a single transfer window that applies to both SolTrans and WestCAT when using Clipper.\n\nHere's what each agency publishes:\n\n**SolTrans with Clipper:** The passages state that \"transfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes\" [doc:soltrans-clipper-card].\n\n**WestCAT with Clipper:** The passages state that \"transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus\" [doc:westcat-transfers].\n\nSince these are two different agencies with different Clipper transfer policies, the transfer window depends on which agency you board first. If you board SolTrans first and transfer to WestCAT, SolTrans's 60-minute window applies. If you board WestCAT first and transfer to SolTrans, WestCAT's 120-minute window applies. However, the passages do not explicitly state how these two different windows interact when transferring between them specifically.\n\nFor clarification on how your specific transfer will be handled, contact SolTrans or WestCAT directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card", "westcat-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>With Clipper, how long is my transfer good for between a SolTrans bus and a WestCAT bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The passages do not state a single transfer window that applies to both SolTrans and WestCAT when using Clipper.</p><p>Here's what each agency publishes:</p><p>**SolTrans with Clipper:** The passages state that \"transfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes\" [soltrans-clipper-card].</p><p>**WestCAT with Clipper:** The passages state that \"transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus\" [westcat-transfers].</p><p>Since these are two different agencies with different Clipper transfer policies, the transfer window depends on which agency you board first. If you board SolTrans first and transfer to WestCAT, SolTrans's 60-minute window applies. If you board WestCAT first and transfer to SolTrans, WestCAT's 120-minute window applies. However, the passages do not explicitly state how these two different windows interact when transferring between them specifically.</p><p>For clarification on how your specific transfer will be handled, contact SolTrans or WestCAT directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>WestCAT: Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "Local Passes : Valid for travel within Vallejo and Benicia on SolTrans Local fixed routes * and local trips on SolanoExpress fixed routes. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travel outside Vallejo and Benicia.\nWithin Solano County Passes : Valid for travel within and between Solano County cities (Vallejo, Benicia, Fairfield, Vacaville, Dixon, and Davis) on SolanoExpress Lines. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travels outside of Solano County. ‍\nOutside Solano County Passes : Valid for travel both outside and within Solano County on SolanoExpress fixed routes and SolTrans Local fixed routes. Extra surcharge on SolanoExpress Route 82 to the San Francisco Ferry terminal. ‍ ‍", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card"], "expected_facts": ["60", "120"]}
{"id": "xagency-017", "question": "Is the senior fare age the same on SLO RTA and Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior fare ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**SLO RTA** offers a half-priced discount fare for seniors aged 65–79 [doc:slorta-discounts], and a free VIP Pass for seniors 80 or older [doc:slorta-discounts, doc:slorta-passes].\n\n**Santa Barbara MTD** offers a discounted fare for seniors aged 65 and older [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. The published policy does not specify a separate threshold or free option for seniors 80+.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes", "slorta-discounts", "slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior fare age the same on SLO RTA and Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior fare ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**SLO RTA** offers a half-priced discount fare for seniors aged 65–79 [slorta-discounts], and a free VIP Pass for seniors 80 or older [slorta-discounts, doc:slorta-passes].</p><p>**Santa Barbara MTD** offers a discounted fare for seniors aged 65 and older [sbmtd-fares-passes]. The published policy does not specify a separate threshold or free option for seniors 80+.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares."], "expected_facts": ["65", "80"]}
{"id": "xagency-vta-001", "question": "I'm 81. Do seniors my age ride free on VTA or on SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**SolTrans:** Yes. Seniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator. [doc:soltrans-fare-table]\n\n**VTA:** The published policy does not specify a free fare for seniors age 81. VTA offers discounted fares to Seniors age 65 or older. To qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following: Medicare Card, Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider, DMV Disabled License Plate registration, DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout, or Proof of age (65 and older). A valid picture I.D. may be required. [doc:vta-fares]\n\nFor current pricing and to confirm VTA's senior fares, contact VTA directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-fare-table", "vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 81. Do seniors my age ride free on VTA or on SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**SolTrans:** Yes. Seniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator. [soltrans-fare-table]</p><p>**VTA:** The published policy does not specify a free fare for seniors age 81. VTA offers discounted fares to Seniors age 65 or older. To qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following: Medicare Card, Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider, DMV Disabled License Plate registration, DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout, or Proof of age (65 and older). A valid picture I.D. may be required. [vta-fares]</p><p>For current pricing and to confirm VTA's senior fares, contact VTA directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "The RTC Program no longer issues cards to seniors (age 65 and over). The Senior Clipper card offers the same features and discounts, with no application fee, and does not expire. However, effective January 9, 2022, the exception is if the card holder requires an attendant. If an RTC applicant is over 65 and is eligible for an attendant card the RTC program is appropriate.\nThe Senior Clipper card is available at our Downtown Customer Service Center or River Oaks Administrative Offices."], "expected_facts": ["80+", "65"]}
{"id": "xagency-015", "question": "SolTrans says Clipper covers transfers with the Vine's Route 11. Does the Vine's own fare policy confirm that?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nSolTrans's Clipper documentation states that transfers are supported with \"NAPA VINE Route 11\" [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. However, the Vine's own fare policy does not confirm this arrangement. \n\nThe Vine's published policy states: \"No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11\" [doc:vine-fares], but it does not mention Clipper transfers or any transfer arrangement with SolTrans. The Vine's fare policy describes free transfers on the Vine system itself (valid for 90 minutes) and notes exceptions for Routes 10, 11, and 29, but does not address transfers to or from other agencies via Clipper.\n\nTo confirm whether the Vine accepts SolTrans Clipper transfers on Route 11, contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or [email protected].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card", "vine-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>SolTrans says Clipper covers transfers with the Vine's Route 11. Does the Vine's own fare policy confirm that?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>SolTrans's Clipper documentation states that transfers are supported with \"NAPA VINE Route 11\" [soltrans-clipper-card]. However, the Vine's own fare policy does not confirm this arrangement.</p><p>The Vine's published policy states: \"No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11\" [vine-fares], but it does not mention Clipper transfers or any transfer arrangement with SolTrans. The Vine's fare policy describes free transfers on the Vine system itself (valid for 90 minutes) and notes exceptions for Routes 10, 11, and 29, but does not address transfers to or from other agencies via Clipper.</p><p>To confirm whether the Vine accepts SolTrans Clipper transfers on Route 11, contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or [email protected].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>VINE: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Transfers on the Vine are free. Please ask for transfers when boarding. Transfers are valid for 90 minutes after they are issued. No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11. There are no free transfers to the Route 29. Transfers cannot be used on the same route.", "Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes", "Calistoga | St. Helena | Yountville | Napa | American Canyon | Kaiser Vallejo\nCalistoga | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00 | $8.00\nSt. Helena | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nYountville | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nNapa | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00\nAmerican Canyon | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00\nKaiser Vallejo | $8.00 | $8.00 | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X\nX = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.\nVineGo Discount Pass Eligible members of VineGo may purchase discount passes at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center or online. The purchase price of a discount pass is $20 for a $24 value.\nReasonable accommodation\nPublic transit passengers with disabilities may request reasonable modifications be made to accommodate their use of public transit. Requests should be made in written form and submitted to the Manager of Public Transit at 625 Burnell St., Napa, CA 94559.\nComments and complaints\nComments and complaints from the public may be received electronically through our customer service portal.\nClick here to submit a comment or complaint to NVTA staff. Additionally, prepaid comment cards are available in English and Spanish on Vine vehicles.\nWhen complaints are received, NVTA staff and/or contractor will acknowledge receipt of the complaint within 24 hours from the time it is received. All complaints should be investigated and resolved within 5 business days. Complaining party shall be notified of the agency’s resolution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "Clipper is an all-in-one transit card that keeps track of any passes and cash value you load onto it while applying all applicable fares, discounts, and transfer rules. This lets you customize your card for your own transit needs. A Clipper card makes boarding faster, eliminates the need to carry exact change, and can be registered for added protection in case it’s lost.\nYou may use Clipper on many public transit services throughout the San Francisco Bay Area, including BART. ‍"], "expected_facts": ["Route 11", "SolTrans"]}
{"id": "xagency-samtrans-001", "question": "Does the Clipper START discount work on both SamTrans and SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe Clipper START program works on both agencies, but with different eligibility criteria:\n\n**SamTrans:** Income-qualifying adults (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. [doc:samtrans-clipper]\n\n**SolTrans:** Lower-income adults age 19–64 can receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n\nBoth agencies' passages confirm that Clipper START is available, but neither passage states whether a single Clipper START card issued by one agency automatically works on the other, or whether you must apply separately to each agency. To confirm whether your Clipper START card covers both systems, contact SamTrans or SolTrans directly—or visit the Clipper START website for application details.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-clipper", "soltrans-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does the Clipper START discount work on both SamTrans and SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The Clipper START program works on both agencies, but with different eligibility criteria:</p><p>**SamTrans:** Income-qualifying adults (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. [samtrans-clipper]</p><p>**SolTrans:** Lower-income adults age 19–64 can receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>Both agencies' passages confirm that Clipper START is available, but neither passage states whether a single Clipper START card issued by one agency automatically works on the other, or whether you must apply separately to each agency. To confirm whether your Clipper START card covers both systems, contact SamTrans or SolTrans directly—or visit the Clipper START website for application details.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Clipper on SamTrans (Next Generation Clipper FAQ)</li><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service."], "expected_facts": ["50"]}
{"id": "xagency-marin-001", "question": "Do I get free transfers with Clipper on both Marin Transit and SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Marin Transit:** Yes, Clipper offers free transfers. You get unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus, and free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus [doc:marin-clipper].\n\n**SolTrans:** The published policy does not state that Clipper provides free transfers on SolTrans. Transfers to and from other bus agencies using Clipper are good for 60 minutes, and full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. The only exception is transfers to/from the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry, which receive a local fare credit [doc:soltrans-clipper-card].\n\nSo free transfers with Clipper apply on Marin Transit, but not on SolTrans according to the published policies provided.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper", "soltrans-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do I get free transfers with Clipper on both Marin Transit and SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Marin Transit:** Yes, Clipper offers free transfers. You get unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus, and free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus [marin-clipper].</p><p>**SolTrans:** The published policy does not state that Clipper provides free transfers on SolTrans. Transfers to and from other bus agencies using Clipper are good for 60 minutes, and full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper [soltrans-clipper-card]. The only exception is transfers to/from the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry, which receive a local fare credit [soltrans-clipper-card].</p><p>So free transfers with Clipper apply on Marin Transit, but not on SolTrans according to the published policies provided.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Advise the driver of your destination when boarding the bus and, if you need a transfer to continue your trip, be sure to ask for one when paying your cash fare. If you are using Clipper, you do not need to obtain a transfer, as transfers are automatically tracked on your card.\nTransfers to Marin Transit Buses: Riders are eligible for up to two free transfers within Marin County for up to two hours.\nTransfers to Golden Gate Transit Buses: Riders traveling within Marin County are eligible for up to two free transfers for up to two hours. Riders traveling regionally get a discount of the Marin Transit fare ($2.00 for cash fare, $1.80 for Clipper).\nTransfers to/from SMART: Riders transferring to or from SMART get a discount of $1.50. Riders must use Clipper or the SMART mobile app to use SMART.\nTransfers have the following restrictions:\nTravel must be made within two hours from time of issuance or otherwise indicated on the transfer\nTravel can be made on any local route and in any direction within the two hour window\nTransfers are issued by bus operator only at time fare is paid. Advise driver or agent of your destination and intention to transfer when boarding\nA transfer can be used only by the person to whom it is issued\nWith Next Generation Clipper, all riders who use Clipper will get a free local Marin Transit ride when transferring from any San Francisco Bay Area transit service and a discount when transferring to any other transit service in the Bay Area. Transfers are automatically tracked on Clipper cards (mobile and plastic) and contactless payment methods (card and mobile wallets).", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Local Passes : Valid for travel within Vallejo and Benicia on SolTrans Local fixed routes * and local trips on SolanoExpress fixed routes. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travel outside Vallejo and Benicia.\nWithin Solano County Passes : Valid for travel within and between Solano County cities (Vallejo, Benicia, Fairfield, Vacaville, Dixon, and Davis) on SolanoExpress Lines. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travels outside of Solano County. ‍\nOutside Solano County Passes : Valid for travel both outside and within Solano County on SolanoExpress fixed routes and SolTrans Local fixed routes. Extra surcharge on SolanoExpress Route 82 to the San Francisco Ferry terminal. ‍ ‍"], "expected_facts": ["no transfers", "2 hours"]}
{"id": "edge-001", "question": "I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the published criteria, MST offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares]. At age 62, you do not meet that published threshold.\n\nHowever, the passages do not state whether MST offers any other discount for riders between 62 and 64, so I cannot confirm whether you qualify for a different reduced fare. To ask about other possible discounts or programs, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62. Do I get the senior discount on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the published criteria, MST offers a discount fare for riders **65 years and older** [mst-fares]. At age 62, you do not meet that published threshold.</p><p>However, the passages do not state whether MST offers any other discount for riders between 62 and 64, so I cannot confirm whether you qualify for a different reduced fare. To ask about other possible discounts or programs, please contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["65"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-002", "question": "I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, based on the published criteria. Yolobus offers reduced fares for seniors age 62 and over [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. \n\nTo ride at the senior rate, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify which documents prove senior status at the point of purchase, but acceptable proof of identification upon boarding includes: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nIf you need a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]:\n\n**Yolo Transportation District**  \n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)  \nMonday–Friday, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Saturday, 9:00 AM–3:00 PM [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nYour senior fares are: Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50 per single ride [doc:yolobus-fares]. Monthly passes for seniors are $42.00 and are valid on all routes—local, intercity, and express [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62 — can I ride Yolobus at the senior rate?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, based on the published criteria. Yolobus offers reduced fares for seniors age 62 and over [yolobus-reduced-fare-id].</p><p>To ride at the senior rate, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify which documents prove senior status at the point of purchase, but acceptable proof of identification upon boarding includes: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card; or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [yolobus-fares].</p><p>If you need a Yolobus reduced fare photo ID, you may obtain one by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]:</p><p>**Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>(530) 666-BUSS (2877)</p><p>Monday–Friday, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Saturday, 9:00 AM–3:00 PM [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>Your senior fares are: Local $1.00, Intercity $1.00, Express $1.50 per single ride [yolobus-fares]. Monthly passes for seniors are $42.00 and are valid on all routes—local, intercity, and express [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-003", "question": "What age counts as a senior on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, **age 62 and older counts as a senior on SacRT** [doc:sacrt-fares]. Additionally, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75 and older** [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age counts as a senior on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, **age 62 and older counts as a senior on SacRT** [sacrt-fares]. Additionally, SacRT offers a **Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75 and older** [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-004", "question": "Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.\n\n**MST** offers a discount for riders **65 years and older** [doc:mst-fares].\n\n**Yolobus** offers a reduced fare for riders **62 and older** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior discount age the same on MST and Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior discount ages differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**MST** offers a discount for riders **65 years and older** [mst-fares].</p><p>**Yolobus** offers a reduced fare for riders **62 and older** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["65", "62"]}
{"id": "edge-005", "question": "I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75+ at $40.00 per month, compared to the regular Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass/Sticker at $50.00 per month [doc:sacrt-fares]. \n\nThe published policy does not specify how to obtain the Super Senior pass. To apply, contact SacRT directly through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line for details on the application process.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 75 — does SacRT have anything cheaper than the regular senior pass for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers a Super Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker for riders age 75+ at $40.00 per month, compared to the regular Senior (age 62+) Monthly Pass/Sticker at $50.00 per month [sacrt-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to obtain the Super Senior pass. To apply, contact SacRT directly through their website at sacrt.com or call their main line for details on the application process.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days"], "expected_facts": ["75", "$40"]}
{"id": "edge-006", "question": "I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. Medi-Cal cards do not qualify for Yolobus reduced fares. \n\nTo qualify for reduced fares on Yolobus, you must show one of these forms of valid identification upon boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, a Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), a DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nThe published policy explicitly excludes Medi-Cal cards from the accepted proof documents for reduced fares.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medi-Cal card. Does that get me the reduced fare on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. Medi-Cal cards do not qualify for Yolobus reduced fares.</p><p>To qualify for reduced fares on Yolobus, you must show one of these forms of valid identification upon boarding: a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, a Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), a DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>The published policy explicitly excludes Medi-Cal cards from the accepted proof documents for reduced fares.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus vehicles. To pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus.\nTransit Connect\nThe Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in 2026."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"]}
{"id": "edge-007", "question": "I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\nYes, you may qualify for an MST discount fare.\nThe published criteria include Medicare Card holders [doc:mst-fares].\nYou have two independent paths to a discount:\n1.\nYou will need to show your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility upon boarding [doc:mst-fares].\n2.\n**Age 65+**: The published criteria also include riders 65 years and older [doc:mst-fares].\nSince you are 50, this path does not apply to you.\nTo use your Medicare Card discount with contactless payment, you can verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link the discount to a contactless-enabled bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express).\nYou will need your Medicare Card and a contactless card to get started [doc:mst-fares-benefits].\nAlternatively, you can simply show your Medicare Card when you board to receive the discounted fare [doc:mst-fares].\nMST makes the final eligibility determination.\nFor any questions about your specific Medicare Card or to confirm your eligibility, contact MST directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 50 but I have a Medicare card. Can I get the MST discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Yes, you may qualify for an MST discount fare.</p><p>The published criteria include Medicare Card holders [mst-fares].</p><p>You have two independent paths to a discount:</p><p>1.</p><p>You will need to show your Medicare Card as proof of eligibility upon boarding [mst-fares].</p><p>2.</p><p>**Age 65+**: The published criteria also include riders 65 years and older [mst-fares].</p><p>Since you are 50, this path does not apply to you.</p><p>To use your Medicare Card discount with contactless payment, you can verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link the discount to a contactless-enabled bank card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express).</p><p>You will need your Medicare Card and a contactless card to get started [mst-fares-benefits].</p><p>Alternatively, you can simply show your Medicare Card when you board to receive the discounted fare [mst-fares].</p><p>MST makes the final eligibility determination.</p><p>For any questions about your specific Medicare Card or to confirm your eligibility, contact MST directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-007", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-008", "question": "What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [doc:mst-fares]:\n\n- DD Form 214\n- DD Form 256N\n- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\n- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card\n- Membership card from the American Legion\n- Disabled American Veterans ID card\n- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation\n\nTo obtain an MST Courtesy Card with this proof, you can apply using the English application or Spanish application [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the application process details (location, hours, or cost), so contact MST directly for how to submit your application and proof.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What proof of service does MST accept for the veteran discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST accepts the following proof of service for the veteran discount [mst-fares]:</p><p>- DD Form 214</p><p>- DD Form 256N</p><p>- US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card</p><p>- Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card</p><p>- Membership card from the American Legion</p><p>- Disabled American Veterans ID card</p><p>- CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation</p><p>To obtain an MST Courtesy Card with this proof, you can apply using the English application or Spanish application [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the application process details (location, hours, or cost), so contact MST directly for how to submit your application and proof.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "The Office of Military & Veterans Affairs provides these services and helps with the following benefits:\nComprehensive benefits counseling\nClaims preparation and submission\nClaims follow-up to ensure final decisions are fully adjudicated\nInitiate and develop appeals when appropriate\nNetworking and advocacy with federal, state, and local agencies\nhttps://www.countyofmonterey.gov/government/departments-i-z/military-veterans#mva"], "expected_facts": ["DD Form 214"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-008", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-009", "question": "Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nMST's published policy lists \"Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" as eligible for the discount fare [doc:mst-fares]. However, the passages do not specify what documentation a spouse or caregiver must show to prove that relationship or obtain an MST Courtesy Card for this rider class.\n\nThe passages state that veterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card with specific proof of service (DD Form 214, DD Form 256N, US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card, Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card, American Legion membership card, Disabled American Veterans ID card, or CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation) [doc:mst-fares], but they do not describe the application process or required proof for spouses or caregivers.\n\nTo learn what documentation is required and how to apply for a card as a veteran's spouse or caregiver, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does MST's veteran discount cover my wife too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>MST's published policy lists \"Veteran's spouse/caregiver\" as eligible for the discount fare [mst-fares]. However, the passages do not specify what documentation a spouse or caregiver must show to prove that relationship or obtain an MST Courtesy Card for this rider class.</p><p>The passages state that veterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card with specific proof of service (DD Form 214, DD Form 256N, US Department of Veterans Affairs ID card, Veterans of Foreign Wars ID card, American Legion membership card, Disabled American Veterans ID card, or CA driver license or ID card with \"veteran\" designation) [mst-fares], but they do not describe the application process or required proof for spouses or caregivers.</p><p>To learn what documentation is required and how to apply for a card as a veteran's spouse or caregiver, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "To end Veteran homelessness in Monterey County. Each Veteran is paired with a dedicated VTC Case Manager who designs and supervises their recovery program while addressing medical, psychological, occupational, financial, educational, and social issues. They offer several housing programs, including emergency, transitional, and permanent supportive housing.\nhttps://www.vtcofcalifornia.org/programsandservices\nVocational Rehabilitation Specialist Inc\nTo assist veterans in need of housing, job placement, job skill training, and more.\nhttps://vrspecialists.com/", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) offers a taxi voucher program to assist veterans in accessing important locations within their communities.", "The Office of Military & Veterans Affairs provides these services and helps with the following benefits:\nComprehensive benefits counseling\nClaims preparation and submission\nClaims follow-up to ensure final decisions are fully adjudicated\nInitiate and develop appeals when appropriate\nNetworking and advocacy with federal, state, and local agencies\nhttps://www.countyofmonterey.gov/government/departments-i-z/military-veterans#mva"], "expected_facts": ["spouse"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-009", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-010", "question": "My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 18 and under ride free [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My son is 17. What does he pay to ride Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Your son rides free on Yolobus. Youth ages 18 and under ride free [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus fixed route bus fares vary depending on which type of service you ride. All below fares are effective July 1, 2026 – June 30, 2027 ."], "expected_facts": ["free"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-010", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-011", "question": "Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nNo. MST and Yolobus have different policies for riders 17 years old.\n\n**Yolobus:** Youth ages 18 and under ride free [doc:yolobus-fares]. Since your 17-year-old is under 18, they ride free on Yolobus.\n\n**MST:** MST offers a discount fare for riders 18 years and under [doc:mst-fares], not a free fare. Proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [doc:mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the discount fare amount for this age group.\n\nTo confirm the exact MST discount fare for your 17-year-old, contact MST directly or visit their fares page.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is my 17-year-old free on MST like on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>No. MST and Yolobus have different policies for riders 17 years old.</p><p>**Yolobus:** Youth ages 18 and under ride free [yolobus-fares]. Since your 17-year-old is under 18, they ride free on Yolobus.</p><p>**MST:** MST offers a discount fare for riders 18 years and under [mst-fares], not a free fare. Proof of age or an MST Courtesy Card is required upon boarding [mst-fares]. The published policy does not specify the discount fare amount for this age group.</p><p>To confirm the exact MST discount fare for your 17-year-old, contact MST directly or visit their fares page.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below."], "expected_facts": ["18"]}
{"id": "edge-012", "question": "Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. Children under 45 inches tall ride free, with a limit of three children per adult [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, small children ride free on Santa Barbara MTD. Children under 45 inches tall ride free, with a limit of three children per adult [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "La tabla a continuación muestra la actualización de la estructura tarifaria con respecto a la actual. El objetivo de MTD con este ajuste tarifario es recuperar una parte de los aproximadamente $3.0 millones anuales en asistencia operativa del programa FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities, que se perdieron cuando el Censo de 2020 determinó que la población del área urbanizada de Santa Bárbara supera las 200,000 personas. Se estima que el ajuste tarifario generará aproximadamente $1.5 millones en ingresos adicionales anuales.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service."], "expected_facts": ["45 inches"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-012", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-013", "question": "Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass. The Senior/Disabled monthly pass costs $42.00 for Local Routes, Intercity Routes, or Express Routes [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nThe senior monthly pass works on express routes — in fact, it is valid on all routes, including local, intercity, and express routes [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nTo qualify for the senior reduced fare, you must be 62 or older and show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify which documents prove age; however, valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus includes a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nYou can purchase a monthly pass on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [doc:yolobus-fares]. If you need to obtain a reduced fare photo ID, you may visit the Yolo Transportation District office at 352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM) or the SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass, and does it work on express routes?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can get the senior price on a Yolobus monthly pass. The Senior/Disabled monthly pass costs $42.00 for Local Routes, Intercity Routes, or Express Routes [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The senior monthly pass works on express routes — in fact, it is valid on all routes, including local, intercity, and express routes [yolobus-fares].</p><p>To qualify for the senior reduced fare, you must be 62 or older and show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify which documents prove age; however, valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus includes a SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID, Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal), DMV Driver's License or government-issued ID Card, or any other discount ID issued by another transit agency [yolobus-fares].</p><p>You can purchase a monthly pass on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [yolobus-fares]. If you need to obtain a reduced fare photo ID, you may visit the Yolo Transportation District office at 352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM) or the SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center [yolobus-reduced-fare-id].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$42"]}
{"id": "edge-014", "question": "Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free. \"UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\" [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] This is part of an agreement where \"a portion of the students' registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\" [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nYour UCSB student ID is valid as your bus pass from the first day of the fall quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter, and the same pattern applies to winter, spring, and summer quarters. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that \"UCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\" [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, UCSB students ride SBMTD buses for free. \"UCSB &amp; SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\" [sbmtd-fares-passes] This is part of an agreement where \"a portion of the students' registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\" [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>Your UCSB student ID is valid as your bus pass from the first day of the fall quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter, and the same pattern applies to winter, spring, and summer quarters. [sbmtd-fares-passes] Note that \"UCSB &amp; SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\" [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["student ID"]}
{"id": "edge-015", "question": "Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the bus free for Hartnell College students?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters. [mst-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nMPC students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nCSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.", "ABOUT\nFresno State University, State Center Community College District, Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District, currently subsidize transit fares for students.\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nStudents, faculty and staff from Fresno State University, Fresno City College, and Clovis Community College will be able to swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride.\nHigh School students from Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District will be able to request a 31-Day Student Pass from their respective school administration and simply swipe the pass and show their valid student ID.\nAll other students will be able to purchase single fares for .75 cents or purchase a 31-Day monthly pass for $22 at any Ticket Vending Machine (TVM) along the BRT route 1 or at Manchester Transit Center. Students will need to show the driver a valid student Identification card to board the bus.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "Children under age 5\nUp to two children ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger. Student fares apply for additional children. Students in grades TK-12th may qualify for fare-free transit with the RydeFreeRT program. Visit rydefreert.com to learn more.\nGroup Passes\nLos Rios Community College District student access card, CSU Sacramento One Card, City of Elk Grove employee or dependent ID card or RT employee or dependent ID card may ride free provided that the card and sticker is current. All participating passes are subject to change at the discretion of SacRT.", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-016", "question": "I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, MST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit agencies. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nTo use your disabled rider ID from another agency for discounted fares on MST, you may be asked to show proof of eligibility when you board. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming with MST that your specific card will be accepted, since the published policy does not detail which other agencies' cards MST recognizes or any registration process for external IDs.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a disabled rider ID from another transit agency. Will MST honor it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, MST honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit agencies. [mst-fares]</p><p>To use your disabled rider ID from another agency for discounted fares on MST, you may be asked to show proof of eligibility when you board. [mst-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming with MST that your specific card will be accepted, since the published policy does not detail which other agencies' cards MST recognizes or any registration process for external IDs.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."]}
{"id": "edge-017", "question": "How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nTo obtain a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus, you must be a senior (62+) or disabled rider. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**Where to apply:**\n\nYou may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:\n\n1. **Yolo Transportation District**\n   - 352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776\n   - Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Sat 9:00 AM–3:00 PM\n   - (530) 666-BUSS (2877)\n\n2. **SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**\n   - 1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811\n   - Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM\n   - (916) 321-2877\n\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**What the published policy does not specify:** The passages do not state whether there is a cost to obtain the reduced-fare photo ID or what documents you must bring to apply.\n\n**Acceptable proof at boarding:** Once you have the reduced-fare ID, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. Qualifying forms of ID include a Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID, Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or another transit agency's Disabled ID. [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id] [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nFor details about the application process and any required documentation, contact Yolobus directly at one of the locations above.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How do I get a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus and what does it require?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>To obtain a reduced-fare photo ID for Yolobus, you must be a senior (62+) or disabled rider. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**Where to apply:**</p><p>You may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:</p><p>1. **Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>- 352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>- Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Sat 9:00 AM–3:00 PM</p><p>- (530) 666-BUSS (2877)</p><p>2. **SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**</p><p>- 1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811</p><p>- Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM</p><p>- (916) 321-2877</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**What the published policy does not specify:** The passages do not state whether there is a cost to obtain the reduced-fare photo ID or what documents you must bring to apply.</p><p>**Acceptable proof at boarding:** Once you have the reduced-fare ID, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding. Qualifying forms of ID include a Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID, Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or another transit agency's Disabled ID. [yolobus-reduced-fare-id] [yolobus-fares]</p><p>For details about the application process and any required documentation, contact Yolobus directly at one of the locations above.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["Woodland"]}
{"id": "edge-018", "question": "What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "If you lose your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nTo obtain a replacement card, the published policy does not specify the process. Contact SBMTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office) for instructions on how to apply for a replacement.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What happens if I lose my SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>If you lose your SBMTD reduced fare photo ID card, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>To obtain a replacement card, the published policy does not specify the process. Contact SBMTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office) for instructions on how to apply for a replacement.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["$5"]}
{"id": "edge-019", "question": "Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, many students in grades TK through 12 are eligible for free transit passes through SacRT's RydeFreeRT program. [doc:sacrt-fares] Your 8-year-old may qualify for fare-free rides, but you should check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT to confirm eligibility. [doc:sacrt-fares]\n\nThe published policy does not specify what documentation is required to enroll or how to apply for the RydeFreeRT program. To move forward, contact SacRT directly or visit their website at sacrt.com for enrollment details.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 8-year-old have to pay to ride SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, many students in grades TK through 12 are eligible for free transit passes through SacRT's RydeFreeRT program. [sacrt-fares] Your 8-year-old may qualify for fare-free rides, but you should check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT to confirm eligibility. [sacrt-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify what documentation is required to enroll or how to apply for the RydeFreeRT program. To move forward, contact SacRT directly or visit their website at sacrt.com for enrollment details.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days"], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-020", "question": "My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations ordering 25 monthly GoPasses.\nFor 21 or more GoPasses, the Monthly GoPass price is $45.00 per pass [doc:mst-fares].\nTo enroll in the Group Discount Program, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) [doc:mst-fares].\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming current pricing and enrollment details with MST directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My organization wants to buy 25 monthly GoPasses from MST. Is there a bulk discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, MST offers a bulk discount for organizations ordering 25 monthly GoPasses.</p><p>For 21 or more GoPasses, the Monthly GoPass price is $45.00 per pass [mst-fares].</p><p>To enroll in the Group Discount Program, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming current pricing and enrollment details with MST directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["$45"]}
{"id": "edge-021", "question": "Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. SBMTD transfers are not valid for return trips. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nTransfers are designed to complete your one-way trip only. If you need to return home later in the day, you would need to pay a new fare.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use an SBMTD transfer to come back home later in the day?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. SBMTD transfers are not valid for return trips. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Transfers are designed to complete your one-way trip only. If you need to return home later in the day, you would need to pay a new fare.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["one-way"]}
{"id": "edge-022", "question": "I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer policy works:\n\nWhen you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. If you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nSo you will receive two separate transfers — one to get you to the second bus, and a second transfer to get you to the third bus. Both transfers are free and can be used as your fare on those buses. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nNote that transfers are valid only to complete a one-way trip and are not valid for return trips. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I need three buses to get across Santa Barbara — does one transfer cover all of them?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, one transfer does not cover all three buses. Here's how MTD's transfer policy works:</p><p>When you pay your fare on the first bus, ask the driver for a transfer. That transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. If you need a third bus, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer, which will be valid for 30 minutes. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>So you will receive two separate transfers — one to get you to the second bus, and a second transfer to get you to the third bus. Both transfers are free and can be used as your fare on those buses. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Note that transfers are valid only to complete a one-way trip and are not valid for return trips. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["30 minutes"]}
{"id": "edge-023", "question": "I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a VCTC transfer slip. What do I pay on SBMTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00"]}
{"id": "edge-024", "question": "I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm taking the Pacific Surfliner to Santa Barbara — can I ride MTD free with my Amtrak ticket?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Programas de pases de autobús para estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC: todos los estudiantes de UCSB y SBCC pagan una tarifa de transporte y pueden usar su identificación de estudiante válida como pase de autobús al tocarla en la máquina de tarifas al abordar.\nProgramas de tarifas con descuento: las personas con discapacidades, las personas mayores y los jóvenes son elegibles para tarifas reducidas. También hay descuentos disponibles para pases prepagos, incluidos pases diarios, pases de 10 viajes y pases de 30 días.\nLímite de tarifas : el pago por uso (límite de tarifas) permite a los pasajeros viajar gratis después de haber pagado el equivalente a un pase diario o mensual al usar el pago sin contacto Tap2Ride. El límite de tarifas garantiza que no se le cobrará más de una cantidad específica por día o período de 30 días, sin importar cuántas veces viaje, siempre que pague con la misma tarjeta sin contacto o billetera móvil. Puede encontrar información adicional sobre el límite de tarifas de MTD y Tap2Ride aquí .\nTraslados gratuitos: si necesita usar más de un autobús para llegar a su destino, pídale al conductor un transbordo cuando pague su tarifa en el primer autobús. Los transbordos son gratuitos y se pueden usar como tarifa en hasta dos autobuses más. Los transbordos gratuitos funcionan de la siguiente manera: a pedido, los pasajeros pueden recibir un transbordo gratuito de 60 minutos para usar para abordar un segundo autobús, que se dirija en la misma dirección (es decir, una línea 20 de salida a una línea 6 de salida). Los pasajeros también pueden recibir una transferencia en ese segundo autobús que es válida por 30 minutos, también en la misma dirección. Puede encontrar más información sobre transferencias gratuitas aquí .\nOtras transferencias: ¡Con un comprobante de transferencia válido de VCTC, paga solo $1.00 para viajar en MTD!\nPrograma de transferencia de tránsito de Pacific Surfliner: el día que viaje en el tren Pacific Surfliner, muestre su boleto impreso o electrónico válido de Amtrak Pacific Surfliner al conductor cuando suba al autobús de conexión y viaje en MTD gratis. Visite Pacific Surfliner para obtener más información.\nFuentes de financiamiento alternativas: MTD está buscando activamente nuevas fuentes de financiamiento. MTD continúa solicitando subvenciones para operar y mejorar el servicio, mantener y reemplazar vehículos y mejorar las instalaciones de tránsito en todo el distrito.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-025", "question": "Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card if you meet the published criteria. The UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes, but only for Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study riders with a valid expiration date on the card [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how to obtain or activate the UC Davis Aggie Card for Yolobus use — that is a matter between you and UC Davis. When you board, you will need to present the card with a valid expiration date.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I ride Yolobus with my UC Davis Aggie Card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can ride Yolobus with your UC Davis Aggie Card if you meet the published criteria. The UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes, but only for Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study riders with a valid expiration date on the card [yolobus-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to obtain or activate the UC Davis Aggie Card for Yolobus use — that is a matter between you and UC Davis. When you board, you will need to present the card with a valid expiration date.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2026 – June 30, 2027.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["Undergrad"]}
{"id": "edge-026", "question": "What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nYolobus Special ADA paratransit fares are:\n\n- **Local trips: $4.00** [doc:yolobus-fares]\n- **Intercity trips: $4.50** [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nThere is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, but it applies only to medical facilities in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes; destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead [doc:yolobus-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does Yolobus Special paratransit cost for a local trip versus an intercity one?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>Yolobus Special ADA paratransit fares are:</p><p>- **Local trips: $4.00** [yolobus-fares]</p><p>- **Intercity trips: $4.50** [yolobus-fares]</p><p>There is also a Premium Fare of $6.00, but it applies only to medical facilities in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes; destinations within ¾ mile of a fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare instead [yolobus-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers ."], "expected_facts": ["$4.00", "$4.50"]}
{"id": "edge-027", "question": "I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. MST lists \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" as a discount fare category [doc:mst-fares]. \n\nThe published policy does not specify what proof of humanitarian parole status is required upon boarding, how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card for this category, or whether a card is needed. To learn how to apply and what documentation MST will accept, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or email [email protected].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm in the U.S. on humanitarian parole. Does MST have a discounted fare category for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. MST lists \"Special Humanitarian Parole\" as a discount fare category [mst-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify what proof of humanitarian parole status is required upon boarding, how to obtain an MST Courtesy Card for this category, or whether a card is needed. To learn how to apply and what documentation MST will accept, contact MST directly at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or email [email protected].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training"], "expected_facts": ["Humanitarian Parole"]}
{"id": "edge-028", "question": "Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. To obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. This is one of several documents SBMTD accepts for this purpose; you may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency or a valid Medicare ID card [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nThe published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or timeline for obtaining the card with a DMV Disabled Placard Receipt. You can contact SBMTD at 805.963.3366 for more information on how to apply [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the SBMTD reduced-fare ID with my DMV disabled placard receipt?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. To obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, you may present a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt [sbmtd-fares-passes]. This is one of several documents SBMTD accepts for this purpose; you may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency or a valid Medicare ID card [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or timeline for obtaining the card with a DMV Disabled Placard Receipt. You can contact SBMTD at 805.963.3366 for more information on how to apply [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["Placard"]}
{"id": "edge-029", "question": "I'm 62 — do I get the reduced fare on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the published criteria, you may qualify for a reduced fare on Humboldt Transit.\nThe published criteria state that seniors aged 62+ are eligible for reduced fares [doc:hta-fares].\nOn the Stored Value/Multi-Ride Passes ($10 or $20), which is the only pass eligible for reduced fares, riders entitled to a reduced fare pay $1 per ride instead of $2 [doc:hta-fares].\nThese passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems but not on Flex microtransit [doc:hta-fares].\nThe published policy does not specify how to document or verify your age or how to apply for reduced-fare status.\nContact Humboldt Transit Authority directly to learn the application process and what proof of age they require.\nYou can reach them at their office at 133 V Street in Eureka [doc:hta-fares], or visit hta.org.\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 62 — do I get the reduced fare on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the published criteria, you may qualify for a reduced fare on Humboldt Transit.</p><p>The published criteria state that seniors aged 62+ are eligible for reduced fares [hta-fares].</p><p>On the Stored Value/Multi-Ride Passes ($10 or $20), which is the only pass eligible for reduced fares, riders entitled to a reduced fare pay $1 per ride instead of $2 [hta-fares].</p><p>These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems but not on Flex microtransit [hta-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to document or verify your age or how to apply for reduced-fare status.</p><p>Contact Humboldt Transit Authority directly to learn the application process and what proof of age they require.</p><p>You can reach them at their office at 133 V Street in Eureka [hta-fares], or visit hta.org.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-030", "question": "Can I get the senior discount on a Humboldt Transit monthly pass?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No.\nThe published policy does not allow reduced fares with monthly passes.\nDay, Week, and Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides, but \"reduced fares are not eligible with these passes.\" [doc:hta-fares]\nHowever, Humboldt Transit Authority does offer reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20).\nIf you are a senior aged 62 or older, you can use a Stored Value pass and pay $1 per ride instead of $2.\n[doc:hta-fares] Stored Value passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.\n[doc:hta-fares]\nThe published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain a Stored Value pass or how to document senior status.\nPlease contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 133 V Street in Eureka or visit hta.org for details on the application process.\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get the senior discount on a Humboldt Transit monthly pass?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No.</p><p>The published policy does not allow reduced fares with monthly passes.</p><p>Day, Week, and Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides, but \"reduced fares are not eligible with these passes.\" [hta-fares]</p><p>However, Humboldt Transit Authority does offer reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20).</p><p>If you are a senior aged 62 or older, you can use a Stored Value pass and pay $1 per ride instead of $2.</p><p>[hta-fares] Stored Value passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.</p><p>[hta-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain a Stored Value pass or how to document senior status.</p><p>Please contact Humboldt Transit Authority at 133 V Street in Eureka or visit hta.org for details on the application process.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Free transfers are only provided between ETS routes for a single trip. No inter-agency transfers are available. Tell the driver you want a transfer when you board the bus. You can only get a transfer upon boarding. To use a transfer when you board a bus on another route simply insert it into the card reader. When you hear a beep the transfer is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus."], "expected_facts": ["Stored Value"]}
{"id": "edge-031", "question": "My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get a youth discount?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, based on the published policy. Youth aged 3–17 are eligible for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit [doc:hta-fares]. Your 16-year-old falls within that age range.\n\nThe reduced fare applies to **Stored Value passes only** ($10 or $20). On these passes, riders eligible for reduced fares pay $1 per ride instead of the regular $2 [doc:hta-fares]. These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Routes 299 and 101, Southern Humboldt, and Arcata & Mad River Transit System), and also on Dial-A-Ride, but not on Flex microtransit [doc:hta-fares].\n\nWhen boarding, your 16-year-old should let the driver know they are entitled to a reduced fare before inserting the pass [doc:hta-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify what proof of age or youth status is required at the point of use.\n\nYou can purchase Stored Value passes using the Token Transit app on a phone, at the HTA online store by mail, on the bus with exact cash, or at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check accepted) [doc:hta-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 16-year-old rides Humboldt Transit — do they get a youth discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, based on the published policy. Youth aged 3–17 are eligible for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit [hta-fares]. Your 16-year-old falls within that age range.</p><p>The reduced fare applies to **Stored Value passes only** ($10 or $20). On these passes, riders eligible for reduced fares pay $1 per ride instead of the regular $2 [hta-fares]. These passes work on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Routes 299 and 101, Southern Humboldt, and Arcata &amp; Mad River Transit System), and also on Dial-A-Ride, but not on Flex microtransit [hta-fares].</p><p>When boarding, your 16-year-old should let the driver know they are entitled to a reduced fare before inserting the pass [hta-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify what proof of age or youth status is required at the point of use.</p><p>You can purchase Stored Value passes using the Token Transit app on a phone, at the HTA online store by mail, on the bus with exact cash, or at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka (cash, Visa, Mastercard, or personal check accepted) [hta-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["3–17"]}
{"id": "edge-032", "question": "Do Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit's fixed-route systems. According to the published policy, students can \"show and swipe\" their current student ID when boarding, and \"all of your rides are free\" on the A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 routes. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride are not included in this benefit. [doc:hta-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Cal Poly Humboldt students ride free on Humboldt Transit's fixed-route systems. According to the published policy, students can \"show and swipe\" their current student ID when boarding, and \"all of your rides are free\" on the A&amp;MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 routes. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride are not included in this benefit. [hta-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["free"]}
{"id": "edge-033", "question": "Is the senior fare age the same on Humboldt Transit and MST?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the senior fare age thresholds differ between the two agencies.\n\n**Humboldt Transit Authority:** The published criteria for senior reduced fares are \"Aged 62+\" [doc:hta-fares].\n\n**Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST):** The published criteria for senior discount fares are \"65 years and older\" [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares", "mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the senior fare age the same on Humboldt Transit and MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the senior fare age thresholds differ between the two agencies.</p><p>**Humboldt Transit Authority:** The published criteria for senior reduced fares are \"Aged 62+\" [hta-fares].</p><p>**Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST):** The published criteria for senior discount fares are \"65 years and older\" [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit."], "expected_facts": ["62", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-034", "question": "I'm a senior on Yolobus. What's the reduced fare and where do I go to get the discount ID?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\n**Reduced fare for seniors:**\nSeniors (62+) on Yolobus pay $1.00 for a local single ride, $1.00 for an intercity single ride, and $1.50 for express routes [doc:yolobus-fares]. Monthly passes for seniors are $42.00 across all route types (local, intercity, and express) [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\n**Where to get your reduced fare ID:**\nYou can obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:\n\n1. **Yolo Transportation District**\n   352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776\n   (530) 666-BUSS (2877)\n   Hours: Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM; Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n2. **SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**\n   1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811\n   (916) 321-2877\n   Hours: Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**What to show when boarding:**\nWhen purchasing fares and boarding the bus, you must show proper identification. Qualifying forms include a Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or other transit agencies' Disabled ID [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a senior on Yolobus. What's the reduced fare and where do I go to get the discount ID?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>**Reduced fare for seniors:**</p><p>Seniors (62+) on Yolobus pay $1.00 for a local single ride, $1.00 for an intercity single ride, and $1.50 for express routes [yolobus-fares]. Monthly passes for seniors are $42.00 across all route types (local, intercity, and express) [yolobus-fares].</p><p>**Where to get your reduced fare ID:**</p><p>You can obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting either of these locations:</p><p>1. **Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>(530) 666-BUSS (2877)</p><p>Hours: Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM; Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>2. **SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**</p><p>1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811</p><p>(916) 321-2877</p><p>Hours: Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**What to show when boarding:**</p><p>When purchasing fares and boarding the bus, you must show proper identification. Qualifying forms include a Medicare Card, California Driver's License, California ID Card, or other transit agencies' Disabled ID [yolobus-reduced-fare-id].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00", "Woodland", "9:00"]}
{"id": "edge-035", "question": "How do I get the SBMTD Mobility Pass reduced-fare ID card, and what does a replacement cost?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\n**How to obtain the Mobility Pass reduced-fare ID card:**\n\nPassengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass. To obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, you must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application, get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nAlternatively, you may present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\n**Replacement cost:**\n\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nThe published policy does not specify an upfront cost for obtaining the initial Mobility Pass photo ID card. For details about the application process and any associated fees, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How do I get the SBMTD Mobility Pass reduced-fare ID card, and what does a replacement cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>**How to obtain the Mobility Pass reduced-fare ID card:**</p><p>Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass. To obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, you must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application, get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Alternatively, you may present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>**Replacement cost:**</p><p>If the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>The published policy does not specify an upfront cost for obtaining the initial Mobility Pass photo ID card. For details about the application process and any associated fees, contact MTD at 805.963.3366 or visit the MTD Transit Center.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers with disabilities may be eligible for the Mobility Pass, a reduced fare to ride MTD buses.\nTo obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card, passengers must have an application completed by a qualified physician or an MTD-certified social service agency. You may download an application here , or get one at the MTD Transit Center, or call 805.963.3366 for more information.\nPassengers may also present a disabled photo ID card from another transit agency, a valid Medicare ID card, or a valid California DMV Disabled Placard Receipt to obtain a reduced fare MTD photo ID card.\nIf the photo ID card is lost, a replacement fee of $5.00 will be charged.\nUCSB & SBCC STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nUCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["MTD Transit Center", "$5"]}
{"id": "edge-036", "question": "Where do I pick up an MST Courtesy Card application, and is there a charge for it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "You can obtain an MST Courtesy Card application in person at any of these locations [doc:mst-fares]:\n\n- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey\n- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina\n- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas\n\nYou can also access the application online (English or Spanish versions available) [doc:mst-fares].\n\nThere is no charge for the Courtesy Card itself. Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Where do I pick up an MST Courtesy Card application, and is there a charge for it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>You can obtain an MST Courtesy Card application in person at any of these locations [mst-fares]:</p><p>- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey</p><p>- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina</p><p>- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas</p><p>You can also access the application online (English or Spanish versions available) [mst-fares].</p><p>There is no charge for the Courtesy Card itself. Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician's written verification or a Medicare Card [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required."], "expected_facts": ["Bus Stop Shop", "free of charge"]}
{"id": "edge-037", "question": "If I go to the SacRT sales center to get my Yolobus reduced-fare ID, what are the hours?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center, where you can obtain your Yolobus reduced-fare ID, is located at 1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811. The hours are Monday through Friday, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. You can also reach them at (916) 321-2877.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I go to the SacRT sales center to get my Yolobus reduced-fare ID, what are the hours?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center, where you can obtain your Yolobus reduced-fare ID, is located at 1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811. The hours are Monday through Friday, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. You can also reach them at (916) 321-2877.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare."], "expected_facts": ["8:30", "R Street"]}
{"id": "edge-038", "question": "How do I apply for the disabled-person transit ID that gets me the reduced Humboldt Transit fare?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain the disabled-person Transit ID Card that qualifies for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit Authority.\n\nThe passages confirm that people with a disabled person Transit ID Card are eligible for reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride costs $1 instead of $2 [doc:hta-fares]. However, the passages do not state where to apply for this card, what documentation is required, whether there is an application fee, or any other details of the application process.\n\nTo learn how to obtain the disabled-person Transit ID Card, contact Humboldt Transit Authority directly.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How do I apply for the disabled-person transit ID that gets me the reduced Humboldt Transit fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify how to apply for or obtain the disabled-person Transit ID Card that qualifies for reduced fares on Humboldt Transit Authority.</p><p>The passages confirm that people with a disabled person Transit ID Card are eligible for reduced fares on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride costs $1 instead of $2 [hta-fares]. However, the passages do not state where to apply for this card, what documentation is required, whether there is an application fee, or any other details of the application process.</p><p>To learn how to obtain the disabled-person Transit ID Card, contact Humboldt Transit Authority directly.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["Humboldt Transit Authority"]}
{"id": "edge-039", "question": "I'm 52 and I have a Medicare card — can I get the SBMTD reduced fare, or do I have to be 65?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nSBMTD's published reduced-fare criteria do not require you to be 65. The passages state that persons with Medicare cards qualify for reduced fares [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. You may qualify for the reduced fare if you hold a Medicare card, regardless of age.\n\nHowever, I cannot determine your eligibility — SBMTD makes that decision. The published policy requires identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. To apply, click the application link on SBMTD's website or contact the agency directly to confirm what documentation they will accept and to complete the application process, as the published policy does not specify the application steps for Medicare card holders.\n\nThe reduced fares available are:\n- **One-way fare:** $1.25 [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n- **10-Ride Pass:** $8.50 [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n- **30-Day Pass:** $30.00 [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 52 and I have a Medicare card — can I get the SBMTD reduced fare, or do I have to be 65?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>SBMTD's published reduced-fare criteria do not require you to be 65. The passages state that persons with Medicare cards qualify for reduced fares [sbmtd-fares-passes]. You may qualify for the reduced fare if you hold a Medicare card, regardless of age.</p><p>However, I cannot determine your eligibility — SBMTD makes that decision. The published policy requires identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID [sbmtd-fares-passes]. To apply, click the application link on SBMTD's website or contact the agency directly to confirm what documentation they will accept and to complete the application process, as the published policy does not specify the application steps for Medicare card holders.</p><p>The reduced fares available are:</p><p>- **One-way fare:** $1.25 [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>- **10-Ride Pass:** $8.50 [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>- **30-Day Pass:** $30.00 [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-040", "question": "My kid is in 8th grade and rides SacRT — is there a student discount fare, or can they ride free?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers both options for your 8th grader:\n\n**Free fare:** Many students in grades TK through 12 are eligible for fare-free transit passes through the RydeFreeRT program. [doc:sacrt-fares] You should check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\n\n**Discount fare (if not eligible for free):** If your student does not qualify for RydeFreeRT, a student discount single ride ticket costs $1.25, a daily pass costs $3.50, and a monthly pass costs $20.00. [doc:sacrt-fares] Discount student monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid school-issued photo ID. [doc:sacrt-fares]\n\nThe published policy does not specify how to apply for the student discount pass or where to obtain it. For details on either program, contact SacRT directly or check their fare-categories page.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My kid is in 8th grade and rides SacRT — is there a student discount fare, or can they ride free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, SacRT offers both options for your 8th grader:</p><p>**Free fare:** Many students in grades TK through 12 are eligible for fare-free transit passes through the RydeFreeRT program. [sacrt-fares] You should check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.</p><p>**Discount fare (if not eligible for free):** If your student does not qualify for RydeFreeRT, a student discount single ride ticket costs $1.25, a daily pass costs $3.50, and a monthly pass costs $20.00. [sacrt-fares] Discount student monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid school-issued photo ID. [sacrt-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to apply for the student discount pass or where to obtain it. For details on either program, contact SacRT directly or check their fare-categories page.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["RydeFreeRT", "$1.25"]}
{"id": "edge-041", "question": "I'm a CSUMB student and I just turned 66 — do I ride MST free with my student ID, or use the senior discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYou ride MST free with your CSUMB student ID. The published policy states that \"CSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\" [doc:mst-fares]\n\nThe senior discount is a separate option available to riders 65 years and older, but as a CSUMB student, the free student fare takes precedence for you during the Fall and Spring semesters.\n\nNote: The free student fare applies only during Fall and Spring semesters. If you are traveling during Summer or Winter break, confirm with MST whether the student ID benefit still applies or whether you would use the senior discount instead.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a CSUMB student and I just turned 66 — do I ride MST free with my student ID, or use the senior discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>You ride MST free with your CSUMB student ID. The published policy states that \"CSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\" [mst-fares]</p><p>The senior discount is a separate option available to riders 65 years and older, but as a CSUMB student, the free student fare takes precedence for you during the Fall and Spring semesters.</p><p>Note: The free student fare applies only during Fall and Spring semesters. If you are traveling during Summer or Winter break, confirm with MST whether the student ID benefit still applies or whether you would use the senior discount instead.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nMPC students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nCSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["free", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-042", "question": "I go to UCSB and I just turned 65 — do I ride MTD free as a student, or pay the senior fare?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nYou ride as a UCSB student, not at the senior fare. The published policy states that \"All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\" [doc:sbmtd-farechange] Your student ID serves as your bus pass for the fare covered by your registration fee.\n\nThe senior fare applies to riders age 65+, but the student pass program takes precedence for UCSB students regardless of age. The passages do not state that seniors who are also students pay a different rate or must choose between the two — the student program is presented as the fare structure for UCSB students.\n\nTo confirm your student ID is set up correctly for boarding, contact UCSB or MTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office). [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I go to UCSB and I just turned 65 — do I ride MTD free as a student, or pay the senior fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>You ride as a UCSB student, not at the senior fare. The published policy states that \"All UCSB &amp; SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\" [sbmtd-farechange] Your student ID serves as your bus pass for the fare covered by your registration fee.</p><p>The senior fare applies to riders age 65+, but the student pass program takes precedence for UCSB students regardless of age. The passages do not state that seniors who are also students pay a different rate or must choose between the two — the student program is presented as the fare structure for UCSB students.</p><p>To confirm your student ID is set up correctly for boarding, contact UCSB or MTD directly at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office). [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["free", "$ 1.25"]}
{"id": "edge-043", "question": "I'm a Cal Poly Humboldt student and I'm 63 — do I ride Humboldt Transit free with my student ID, or pay the reduced senior fare?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10:\n\nAs a Cal Poly Humboldt student, you ride free with your current student ID. The published policy states: \"Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\" [doc:hta-fares]\n\nThis free student benefit applies to the fixed-route systems: A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride are not included. [doc:hta-fares]\n\nThe reduced senior fare ($1 per ride on Stored Value passes) is a separate program for riders aged 62+. [doc:hta-fares] However, the published policy does not specify whether a rider who qualifies for both the student free fare and the senior reduced fare must choose one or the other, or how the two interact. Since your student ID gives you free rides on the fixed routes, you would not need to pay the senior reduced fare on those same routes — but to confirm how the agency applies both eligibilities together, contact Humboldt Transit Authority or HSU Parking & Commuter Services for clarification.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a Cal Poly Humboldt student and I'm 63 — do I ride Humboldt Transit free with my student ID, or pay the reduced senior fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10:</p><p>As a Cal Poly Humboldt student, you ride free with your current student ID. The published policy states: \"Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free!\" [hta-fares]</p><p>This free student benefit applies to the fixed-route systems: A&amp;MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101. Flex microtransit and Dial-A-Ride are not included. [hta-fares]</p><p>The reduced senior fare ($1 per ride on Stored Value passes) is a separate program for riders aged 62+. [hta-fares] However, the published policy does not specify whether a rider who qualifies for both the student free fare and the senior reduced fare must choose one or the other, or how the two interact. Since your student ID gives you free rides on the fixed routes, you would not need to pay the senior reduced fare on those same routes — but to confirm how the agency applies both eligibilities together, contact Humboldt Transit Authority or HSU Parking &amp; Commuter Services for clarification.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit.", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["free", "Stored Value"]}
{"id": "edge-044", "question": "I'm a UC Davis undergrad with an Aggie Card and I'm 64 — do I ride Yolobus on the Aggie Card, or use the senior fare?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nYou have two independent paths to ride Yolobus at a reduced fare:\n\n**UC Davis Aggie Card path:** The UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus when you show it with a valid expiration date and you are an undergraduate or in the UCDE Global Study program [doc:yolobus-fares]. The published policy does not specify whether this pass itself costs anything or what the fare is.\n\n**Senior fare path:** Yolobus offers reduced fares for riders 62 and older. To qualify, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify the senior fare price.\n\nThe passages do not state which option takes priority, whether you must choose one, or whether using your Aggie Card prevents you from also using senior fares. To clarify which option applies to your situation and what each costs, contact Yolobus:\n\n**Yolo Transportation District**  \n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776  \n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)  \nMon–Fri 7:00 AM–7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM–3:00 PM [doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a UC Davis undergrad with an Aggie Card and I'm 64 — do I ride Yolobus on the Aggie Card, or use the senior fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>You have two independent paths to ride Yolobus at a reduced fare:</p><p>**UC Davis Aggie Card path:** The UC Davis Aggie Card is valid for unlimited rides on Yolobus when you show it with a valid expiration date and you are an undergraduate or in the UCDE Global Study program [yolobus-fares]. The published policy does not specify whether this pass itself costs anything or what the fare is.</p><p>**Senior fare path:** Yolobus offers reduced fares for riders 62 and older. To qualify, you must show proper identification when purchasing fares and when boarding [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]. The published policy does not specify the senior fare price.</p><p>The passages do not state which option takes priority, whether you must choose one, or whether using your Aggie Card prevents you from also using senior fares. To clarify which option applies to your situation and what each costs, contact Yolobus:</p><p>**Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>(530) 666-BUSS (2877)</p><p>Mon–Fri 7:00 AM–7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM–3:00 PM [yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2026 – June 30, 2027.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus."], "expected_facts": ["unlimited", "62"]}
{"id": "edge-045", "question": "I think I qualify for the MST senior discount — what do I do next to actually use it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nTo use the MST senior discount, you have two paths:\n\n**Path 1: Cal-ITP Benefits (online verification)**\nIf you meet the senior criterion (age 65+), you can verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link your discount to a contactless bank card. You'll need [doc:mst-fares-benefits]:\n- A Medicare or Login.gov account, or an active MST Courtesy Card\n- A contactless credit or debit card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express)\n- Your ID ready\n\nGo to the Cal-ITP Benefits website to start the process.\n\n**Path 2: MST Courtesy Card**\nYou can obtain an MST Courtesy Card by submitting an application. The published policy does not specify the application process, location, or any card cost for seniors. You'll need proof of age when you board [doc:mst-fares]. Contact MST directly to apply: call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location.\n\n**At the point of use:**\nWhen boarding, you can pay with a contactless card linked to your Cal-ITP discount, or present your MST Courtesy Card or proof of age [doc:mst-fares].\n\nThe agency makes the final eligibility determination. Confirm current details with MST before your first trip.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I think I qualify for the MST senior discount — what do I do next to actually use it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>To use the MST senior discount, you have two paths:</p><p>**Path 1: Cal-ITP Benefits (online verification)**</p><p>If you meet the senior criterion (age 65+), you can verify your eligibility online through Cal-ITP Benefits and link your discount to a contactless bank card. You'll need [mst-fares-benefits]:</p><p>- A Medicare or Login.gov account, or an active MST Courtesy Card</p><p>- A contactless credit or debit card (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, or American Express)</p><p>- Your ID ready</p><p>Go to the Cal-ITP Benefits website to start the process.</p><p>**Path 2: MST Courtesy Card**</p><p>You can obtain an MST Courtesy Card by submitting an application. The published policy does not specify the application process, location, or any card cost for seniors. You'll need proof of age when you board [mst-fares]. Contact MST directly to apply: call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit an MST customer service location.</p><p>**At the point of use:**</p><p>When boarding, you can pay with a contactless card linked to your Cal-ITP discount, or present your MST Courtesy Card or proof of age [mst-fares].</p><p>The agency makes the final eligibility determination. Confirm current details with MST before your first trip.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training"], "expected_facts": ["65"], "forbidden_terms": ["re:\\b(obtain|obtaining|apply for|applying for|get|request)\\b[^.\\n]{0,120}\\bcourtesy card\\b[^.\\n]{0,120}\\b(by|with|using|presenting|providing|showing|upon)\\b[^.\\n]{0,30}\\bproof of age\\b", "re:\\bproof of age\\b[^.\\n]{0,40}\\bto (obtain|apply for|get|request)\\b[^.\\n]{0,60}\\bcourtesy card\\b"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-045", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-046", "question": "I'm 70. How do I start getting the SBMTD senior fare?", "provenance": {"source": "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check the agency's website or customer service for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 70. How do I start getting the SBMTD senior fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check the agency's website or customer service for current information.</p></section>", "expected_facts": ["65"]}
{"id": "edge-047", "question": "Where and during what hours can I go in person to get the Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nYou can obtain a Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID at two locations:\n\n**Yolo Transportation District**\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776\nMonday–Friday, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Saturday, 9:00 AM–3:00 PM\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\n**SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center**\n1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811\nMonday–Friday, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM\n(916) 321-2877\n[doc:yolobus-reduced-fare-id]\n\nThe published policy does not specify any cost for obtaining the reduced-fare photo ID.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-reduced-fare-id"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Where and during what hours can I go in person to get the Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>You can obtain a Yolobus reduced-fare photo ID at two locations:</p><p>**Yolo Transportation District**</p><p>352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776</p><p>Monday–Friday, 7:00 AM–7:00 PM; Saturday, 9:00 AM–3:00 PM</p><p>(530) 666-BUSS (2877)</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>**SacRT Customer Service &amp; Sales Center**</p><p>1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811</p><p>Monday–Friday, 8:30 AM–5:00 PM</p><p>(916) 321-2877</p><p>[yolobus-reduced-fare-id]</p><p>The published policy does not specify any cost for obtaining the reduced-fare photo ID.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Reduced Fare ID</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required, and bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center.\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services.\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets, and/or Passes onto Connect Cards", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)"], "expected_facts": ["352 Industrial Way", "9:00"]}
{"id": "edge-048", "question": "Where do I go in person to pick up an MST Courtesy Card application?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "You can obtain an MST Courtesy Card application in person at any of these locations [doc:mst-fares]:\n\n- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey\n- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina\n- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas\n\nYou can also download an English or Spanish application online [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Where do I go in person to pick up an MST Courtesy Card application?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>You can obtain an MST Courtesy Card application in person at any of these locations [mst-fares]:</p><p>- MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey</p><p>- Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina</p><p>- Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas</p><p>You can also download an English or Spanish application online [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Courtesy Cards are available to all disabled individuals free of charge with a physician’s written verification or a Medicare Card. Use the card for discounted fares and passes as proof of eligibility. Obtain an application in person at the MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street in Monterey, Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road in Marina, Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street in Salinas, or here ( English application / Spanish application ).", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month."], "expected_facts": ["Pearl Street"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-048", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-049", "question": "I only ride Elk Grove routes E110 through E116. Is the cheaper Elk Grove monthly pass enough for me?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYes, the cheaper Elk Grove monthly pass is enough for you. The **Local Monthly Pass** is valid for 31 days from first use and covers only routes E110–E116, which are exactly the routes you ride [doc:etran-fares]. \n\nThe Local Monthly Pass costs $80.00 for the general public [doc:etran-fares]. The Unlimited Monthly Pass, which costs $100.00, is valid on all SacRT routes with an \"E\" designation, not just E110–E116 [doc:etran-fares], so you would be paying extra for coverage you do not need.\n\nIf you are eligible for a discounted fare (for example, as a senior age 62+, a person with disabilities, a student in grades TK–12, or military), the Local Monthly Pass costs $40.00 [doc:etran-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I only ride Elk Grove routes E110 through E116. Is the cheaper Elk Grove monthly pass enough for me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Yes, the cheaper Elk Grove monthly pass is enough for you. The **Local Monthly Pass** is valid for 31 days from first use and covers only routes E110–E116, which are exactly the routes you ride [etran-fares].</p><p>The Local Monthly Pass costs $80.00 for the general public [etran-fares]. The Unlimited Monthly Pass, which costs $100.00, is valid on all SacRT routes with an \"E\" designation, not just E110–E116 [etran-fares], so you would be paying extra for coverage you do not need.</p><p>If you are eligible for a discounted fare (for example, as a senior age 62+, a person with disabilities, a student in grades TK–12, or military), the Local Monthly Pass costs $40.00 [etran-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass.", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/"], "expected_facts": ["E110", "$80"]}
{"id": "edge-050", "question": "I am boarding an Elk Grove bus with three children who are all under 5. What do I pay for them?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nUp to two children under age 5 ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger [doc:etran-fares]. For the third child under 5, student fares apply [doc:etran-fares]. The student fare for ages 5–18 is $1.10 per single ride [doc:etran-fares].\n\nSo you pay nothing for the first two children, and $1.10 for the third child.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I am boarding an Elk Grove bus with three children who are all under 5. What do I pay for them?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Up to two children under age 5 ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger [etran-fares]. For the third child under 5, student fares apply [etran-fares]. The student fare for ages 5–18 is $1.10 per single ride [etran-fares].</p><p>So you pay nothing for the first two children, and $1.10 for the third child.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Children under age 5\nUp to two children ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger. Student fares apply for additional children. Students in grades TK-12th may qualify for fare-free transit with the RydeFreeRT program. Visit rydefreert.com to learn more.\nGroup Passes\nLos Rios Community College District student access card, CSU Sacramento One Card, City of Elk Grove employee or dependent ID card or RT employee or dependent ID card may ride free provided that the card and sticker is current. All participating passes are subject to change at the discretion of SacRT.", "about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "June 30, 2021\nGeneral\nIn July 2021, e- tran and e-van services annexed into SacRT . The current e- tran and e-van fares will remain the same at this time.\nFare Types | Basic | Military/Senior/Medicare/Disabled | Student (age 5 – 18)\nSingle Fare | $2.25 | $1.10 | $1.10\nDaily Pass | $6.00 | $3.00 | $3.00\n10-Ride Pass | $22.50 | $11.00 | $11.00\nMonthly Unrestricted Pass | $100.00 | $50.00 | $50.00\nMonthly Local Pass | $80.00 | $40.00 | $40.00\nAll Pass Sales are Final\nOnce a pass is sold, no refunds will be provided. Any unused, lost, destroyed, or stolen passes will not be replaced or refunded. Exchanges can only be made on defective passes at the SacRT’s discretion.\nBasic/General Public\nAll passengers (ages 5-61 years old) pay Basic fare unless eligible for Discount fare."], "expected_facts": ["two", "$1.10"]}
{"id": "edge-051", "question": "I am 65 and I live in Elk Grove. How do I start riding the E routes for free?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYou may qualify for the Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass program. The published criteria are: you must be 62 years of age or older and an Elk Grove resident [doc:etran-senior-pass]. Since you are 65 and live in Elk Grove, the agency will make the final decision on your eligibility.\n\n**To start riding for free, here is the concrete first step:**\n\nThe Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass will be issued at these locations starting in mid-May 2026:\n- Elk Grove City Hall, District 56 [doc:etran-senior-pass]\n- SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street (adjacent to the 13th Street Station) [doc:etran-senior-pass]\n\nTo get a pass, you will need to provide Elk Grove residency proof and proof of age [doc:etran-senior-pass].\n\n**How the program works:**\n\nOnce you have your pass or show a government-issued photo ID proving you are 62+, simply show it to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify your eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you on SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (the E-designated routes) [doc:etran-senior-pass]. This pilot program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first [doc:etran-senior-pass].\n\nFor assistance, you can call SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877) [doc:etran-senior-pass].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-senior-pass"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I am 65 and I live in Elk Grove. How do I start riding the E routes for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>You may qualify for the Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass program. The published criteria are: you must be 62 years of age or older and an Elk Grove resident [etran-senior-pass]. Since you are 65 and live in Elk Grove, the agency will make the final decision on your eligibility.</p><p>**To start riding for free, here is the concrete first step:**</p><p>The Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass will be issued at these locations starting in mid-May 2026:</p><p>- Elk Grove City Hall, District 56 [etran-senior-pass]</p><p>- SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street (adjacent to the 13th Street Station) [etran-senior-pass]</p><p>To get a pass, you will need to provide Elk Grove residency proof and proof of age [etran-senior-pass].</p><p>**How the program works:**</p><p>Once you have your pass or show a government-issued photo ID proving you are 62+, simply show it to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify your eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you on SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (the E-designated routes) [etran-senior-pass]. This pilot program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first [etran-senior-pass].</p><p>For assistance, you can call SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877) [etran-senior-pass].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Senior Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Children under age 5\nUp to two children ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger. Student fares apply for additional children. Students in grades TK-12th may qualify for fare-free transit with the RydeFreeRT program. Visit rydefreert.com to learn more.\nGroup Passes\nLos Rios Community College District student access card, CSU Sacramento One Card, City of Elk Grove employee or dependent ID card or RT employee or dependent ID card may ride free provided that the card and sticker is current. All participating passes are subject to change at the discretion of SacRT."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-052", "question": "I'm 68 and I have a Medicare card. Can I use it to apply for the Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nThe published criteria for applying for a Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card as an older adult are: you must be at least 65 years old and must submit one of the forms of identification listed in the policy (except Medicare ID) [doc:scmtd-accessibility].\n\nA Medicare ID Card alone is not listed as an acceptable form of identification for the Discount Photo ID Card application. However, the policy does state that older adults may present \"Identification that displays date of birth (e.g. passports & birth certificates)\" or a \"Current State Driver's License, or current State ID Card\" [doc:scmtd-accessibility].\n\nTo apply for the Discount Photo ID Card, visit Customer Service at METRO's Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz or Watsonville during business hours (Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM). There is a $2.00 processing fee for a new Photo ID Card [doc:scmtd-accessibility].\n\nThe agency makes the final decision on your application. I recommend contacting Santa Cruz METRO directly at 831-425-8600 to confirm which identification documents you should bring when you apply.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-accessibility"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 68 and I have a Medicare card. Can I use it to apply for the Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>The published criteria for applying for a Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card as an older adult are: you must be at least 65 years old and must submit one of the forms of identification listed in the policy (except Medicare ID) [scmtd-accessibility].</p><p>A Medicare ID Card alone is not listed as an acceptable form of identification for the Discount Photo ID Card application. However, the policy does state that older adults may present \"Identification that displays date of birth (e.g. passports &amp; birth certificates)\" or a \"Current State Driver's License, or current State ID Card\" [scmtd-accessibility].</p><p>To apply for the Discount Photo ID Card, visit Customer Service at METRO's Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz or Watsonville during business hours (Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM). There is a $2.00 processing fee for a new Photo ID Card [scmtd-accessibility].</p><p>The agency makes the final decision on your application. I recommend contacting Santa Cruz METRO directly at 831-425-8600 to confirm which identification documents you should bring when you apply.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Accessibility (discount fares and Discount Photo ID Card)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Visit Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville during business hours.\nProcessing and Replacement Fees\n$2.00 to process a new Photo ID Card\n$2.00 for the first replacement of a lost card\n$5.00 for the replacement of a lost card thereafter\nHow to Obtain the Application Forms\nRead the Discount Fare Program and Policy .\nPrint the application forms that are appended to the policy as Attachment A and Attachment B .\nIf your application requires confirmation of legal blindness, print and submit Attachment C .\nYou may also request the forms from Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville.\nAttendants\nPeople with disabilities who require an attendant are only required to pay one discount rate fare when they travel with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must indicate that you need an attendant. A “green dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.\nChildren\nChildren (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must include a completed Request for Reduced Children’s Fare with Discount Fare Card Form ( Attachment B ). A “gold dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.", "To make it easier for seniors and people with disabilities to ride public transit, we offer a METRO-issued Discount Photo ID Card. The Discount Photo ID provides proof of eligibility for METRO discount fares and passes. It also qualifies you to ride with attendants at no extra fare and/or children at the discount fare. People with METRO’s Discount Photo ID can use the card to qualify for the discount fares offered by other transit agencies.\nApplication Criteria for Older Adults\nMust be at least 65 years old\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above (except Medicare ID).\nApplication Criteria for People with Disabilities\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above.\nOR\nMust present a complete Professional Verification of Disability Status form. The form must be completed by a medical practitioner or other professional verifying your disability.", "What type of payment methods are accepted on METRO’s contactless payment system?\nMETRO accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. Accepted mobile wallets include Apple Pay & Google Pay, either on your phone or wearable.\nHow do I know if I have a contactless-enabled bank card?\nCards that are contactless-enabled have the symbol shown below (on the front or the back):\nMany banks and financial institutions have switched to contactless cards to make payments safer, faster, and more secure for their customers. Check with your card issuer if you are unsure if your card is contactless-enabled.\nIf you do not have a contactless card, you have options:\nLink an existing bank card to your smart device.\nOpen a safe, inclusive bank or checking account.\nOrder a contactless-enabled Cash App Card (no need to have a bank account).\nCan I still ride METRO if I do not have a contactless-enabled card or a payment-enabled device?\nYes. You can still pay for your fare using cash and Splash Pass. You will need to use the farebox to pay your fare, or visit either of our Customer Service Booths in Santa Cruz or Watsonville.\nHow do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on METRO?\nAs you board the bus to start your journey, look for the contactless symbol on the Tap2Pay readers:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat are Tap2Pay readers?\nTap2Pay payment readers are the face of METRO’s contactless fare payment system. The readers serve as point-of-sale terminals, allowing you to pay your fare and enter the system instantly. They are mounted at the front entrance of the bus and look like this:\nWhy doesn’t my contactless-enabled bank card work?\nIf your contactless-enabled bank card is not working, it may be due to one of the following reasons:\nYou have a new card. If you have a new contactless-enabled card, you might need to activate it first. If, after activating, your new contactless-enabled card it is still not working, please contact your card issuer at the phone number listed on the back of your card.\nYour card issuer has declined payment. This may be due to your bank account not having enough funds for the journey. Please check with your card issuer.\nYour card may be damaged or expired. If this happens, you’ll need to get your card reissued by contacting your card issuer.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Older adults (age 65 and over):\nA METRO Discount Photo ID Card (described below)\nA Discount Photo ID Card issued by another transit agency\nA Senior Citizen ID Card\nIdentification that displays date of birth (e.g. passports & birth certificates)\nCurrent State Driver’s License, or current State ID Card\nA Medicare ID Card\nPeople with disabilities:\nA valid ID Card for a California Disabled Person Parking Placard\nA Disabled Veteran’s ID Card"], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-053", "question": "If I pay with a contactless card on Santa Cruz METRO, what is the most I can be charged in one day?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nWhen paying with a contactless card on Santa Cruz METRO, the most you can be charged in one day is **$6** [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]. This is the daily fare-capping amount for regular fares. You can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for $2 per trip, and once your daily trips reach the $6 cap, additional rides that day are free [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz].\n\nIf you qualify for a discount fare (ages 65+, Veterans, or Medicare cardholders), the daily cap is lower—the published policy does not specify the exact daily cap amount for discount fares, but you can link your benefits eligibility to your Tap2Cruz payment card to receive the discount fare automatically [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-tap2cruz"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I pay with a contactless card on Santa Cruz METRO, what is the most I can be charged in one day?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>When paying with a contactless card on Santa Cruz METRO, the most you can be charged in one day is **$6** [scmtd-tap2cruz]. This is the daily fare-capping amount for regular fares. You can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for $2 per trip, and once your daily trips reach the $6 cap, additional rides that day are free [scmtd-tap2cruz].</p><p>If you qualify for a discount fare (ages 65+, Veterans, or Medicare cardholders), the daily cap is lower—the published policy does not specify the exact daily cap amount for discount fares, but you can link your benefits eligibility to your Tap2Cruz payment card to receive the discount fare automatically [scmtd-tap2cruz].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "What will I be charged?\nWhen paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a Tap2Cruz reader, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method—you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for only $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay.\nIs there a weekly or monthly payment option?\nYes, please see the table below for daily and monthly fare-capping amounts when paying with a contactless payment method.\nThe 31-day cap operates on a rolling basis. This means that a cap will apply for all trips taken within a 31-day period from the first time you tap to pay, and on a rolling basis, based on usage thereafter.\nThe 31-day period starts with the first tap.\nHow do charges appear on my card statement?\nThe fare is charged to your card’s account and will include the description: “Santa Cruz METRO”\nTo view your recent METRO rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal (or check your bank card statement).\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure?\nYes. Tap2Cruz transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private?\nPersonal data will never be stored, sold, or given away by METRO or any third-party agent. METRO’s Customer Service Representatives do not have access to full credit card account numbers. Customer Service Representatives will answer customer support queries in accordance with Payment Card Industry practices.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history?\nNo. Just like your card issuer would not see what you order from a coffee shop or other retailer, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on METRO?\nNo, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method?\nNo. Each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nTip: If you have both a physical card and a digital wallet version of that same card, they count as different payment methods.\nDo I always have to use the same payment method within the 2-hour transfer window?\nYes. You cannot switch payment methods during the 2-hour transfer window. The fare calculation software will only charge your card $2 throughout this window, no matter how many times it is tapped. If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\nImportant: You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments?\nNo. Fares paid via contactless payment will never cost more than the standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go up to the fare cap.\nPlease review the fare chart for Tap2Cruz fare capping information.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board within that 2-hour window?\nYes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 regardless how many times you tap. You will not be charged again until 2 hours have passed.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment?\nDiscounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts.\nUtilizing CalITP Benefits, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card, ensuring you always receive discount fare when tapping!\nRiders who are eligible for a discount fare can still pay the existing discount fare with cash or SmartCard when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What type of payment methods are accepted on METRO’s contactless payment system?\nMETRO accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. Accepted mobile wallets include Apple Pay & Google Pay, either on your phone or wearable.\nHow do I know if I have a contactless-enabled bank card?\nCards that are contactless-enabled have the symbol shown below (on the front or the back):\nMany banks and financial institutions have switched to contactless cards to make payments safer, faster, and more secure for their customers. Check with your card issuer if you are unsure if your card is contactless-enabled.\nIf you do not have a contactless card, you have options:\nLink an existing bank card to your smart device.\nOpen a safe, inclusive bank or checking account.\nOrder a contactless-enabled Cash App Card (no need to have a bank account).\nCan I still ride METRO if I do not have a contactless-enabled card or a payment-enabled device?\nYes. You can still pay for your fare using cash and Splash Pass. You will need to use the farebox to pay your fare, or visit either of our Customer Service Booths in Santa Cruz or Watsonville.\nHow do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on METRO?\nAs you board the bus to start your journey, look for the contactless symbol on the Tap2Pay readers:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat are Tap2Pay readers?\nTap2Pay payment readers are the face of METRO’s contactless fare payment system. The readers serve as point-of-sale terminals, allowing you to pay your fare and enter the system instantly. They are mounted at the front entrance of the bus and look like this:\nWhy doesn’t my contactless-enabled bank card work?\nIf your contactless-enabled bank card is not working, it may be due to one of the following reasons:\nYou have a new card. If you have a new contactless-enabled card, you might need to activate it first. If, after activating, your new contactless-enabled card it is still not working, please contact your card issuer at the phone number listed on the back of your card.\nYour card issuer has declined payment. This may be due to your bank account not having enough funds for the journey. Please check with your card issuer.\nYour card may be damaged or expired. If this happens, you’ll need to get your card reissued by contacting your card issuer.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted.\nCash and Splash Pass are still accepted, as well.\nTo use Tap2Cruz, use the same method of contactless payment on each trip and tap the payment reader as you board the bus. If traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately. Don’t use the same contactless payment for multiple riders.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "What can I do if my payment was not accepted at the payment reader?\nIf you tap your contactless card or payment-enabled device and the payment reader indicates that the payment was not accepted, you will need to use another payment method to pay for your ride. Your payment may not be accepted for a few reasons:\nIt is possible that your payment method has been blocked if we have not received payment for a prior fare. Until payment is received for that fare, that payment method will remain blocked, even if you use other payment methods in the meantime\nYour payment method has been blocked by your bank\nYour card or smart device is damaged\nYour card is not contactless-enabled\nYou may not have enough funds\nTo resolve an issue with your card, please contact your bank. In the meantime, you can use another contactless debit or credit card, including a card in your mobile wallet. If you are having trouble with your payment-enabled device, you may need to remove the card from your device and add it back in.\nHow can I get my blocked payment method accepted again?\nOnce you have resolved the issue with your payment method, it can take up to 15 minutes before you can continue to use the same payment method at METRO payment readers.\nWhat is “card clash?”\nCard clash happens when more than one payment method touches the payment reader at the same time. The payment reader may deduct the fare from the wrong bank card/device as it reads the first payment method it comes in contact with or it will not read either card. It is recommended that you present one payment method when paying your fare.\nNote : If you are paying for someone who is traveling with you, you will need to use a different card or device, or an alternative form of fare payment.\nHow do I apply for a refund?\nIf you suspect you have been charged more than once or have not been charged the correct fare, please contact METRO Customer Service at (831) 425-8600, daily, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.\nHow do I dispute a charge?\nIf you did not authorize a charge, or if you believe that you have been charged incorrectly, you can dispute a charge by speaking with a METRO Customer Service representative at (831) 425-8600, Monday through Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.\nWhat if my payment method is lost or stolen?\nIf your card is lost or stolen, contact your bank first to ensure that your card is canceled and to prevent unauthorized transactions.\nIf your card is found, and if your bank did not cancel it, you may continue using your card."], "expected_facts": ["cap", "31 -day"]}
{"id": "edge-054", "question": "My 16-year-old rides Santa Cruz METRO — do they get the discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYour 16-year-old does not qualify for the discount fare based on age alone. The published discount fare is for \"adults age 65 and over\" and \"people with disabilities\" [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]. At age 16, your child would pay the full adult fare of $2.00 per ride in cash, or $6 for a Day Pass, or $65 for a 31-Day Pass [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nHowever, your 16-year-old may ride free if they are a K-12 student through the Youth Cruz Free Program, which offers FREE fares on all routes within Santa Cruz County [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]. The published policy does not specify what documentation is required to qualify for this program. To confirm eligibility and learn how to apply, contact Santa Cruz METRO Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit the Customer Service Windows at the Temporary Riverfront Customer Experience Center (603 Front St., downtown Santa Cruz) or Watsonville Transit Center (475 Rodriguez St., Watsonville), open Monday–Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 16-year-old rides Santa Cruz METRO — do they get the discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Your 16-year-old does not qualify for the discount fare based on age alone. The published discount fare is for \"adults age 65 and over\" and \"people with disabilities\" [scmtd-fares-passes]. At age 16, your child would pay the full adult fare of $2.00 per ride in cash, or $6 for a Day Pass, or $65 for a 31-Day Pass [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>However, your 16-year-old may ride free if they are a K-12 student through the Youth Cruz Free Program, which offers FREE fares on all routes within Santa Cruz County [scmtd-fares-passes]. The published policy does not specify what documentation is required to qualify for this program. To confirm eligibility and learn how to apply, contact Santa Cruz METRO Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit the Customer Service Windows at the Temporary Riverfront Customer Experience Center (603 Front St., downtown Santa Cruz) or Watsonville Transit Center (475 Rodriguez St., Watsonville), open Monday–Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Visit Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville during business hours.\nProcessing and Replacement Fees\n$2.00 to process a new Photo ID Card\n$2.00 for the first replacement of a lost card\n$5.00 for the replacement of a lost card thereafter\nHow to Obtain the Application Forms\nRead the Discount Fare Program and Policy .\nPrint the application forms that are appended to the policy as Attachment A and Attachment B .\nIf your application requires confirmation of legal blindness, print and submit Attachment C .\nYou may also request the forms from Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville.\nAttendants\nPeople with disabilities who require an attendant are only required to pay one discount rate fare when they travel with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must indicate that you need an attendant. A “green dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.\nChildren\nChildren (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must include a completed Request for Reduced Children’s Fare with Discount Fare Card Form ( Attachment B ). A “gold dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.", "To make it easier for seniors and people with disabilities to ride public transit, we offer a METRO-issued Discount Photo ID Card. The Discount Photo ID provides proof of eligibility for METRO discount fares and passes. It also qualifies you to ride with attendants at no extra fare and/or children at the discount fare. People with METRO’s Discount Photo ID can use the card to qualify for the discount fares offered by other transit agencies.\nApplication Criteria for Older Adults\nMust be at least 65 years old\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above (except Medicare ID).\nApplication Criteria for People with Disabilities\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above.\nOR\nMust present a complete Professional Verification of Disability Status form. The form must be completed by a medical practitioner or other professional verifying your disability.", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "To show our appreciation to our customers and community, we offer several free fare days throughout the year. On these special days, every METRO route and service is completely free. It’s our way of saying thank you for your commitment to cleaner, greener transportation for all.\n2026 Free Fare Days\nTransit Equity Day: February 4, 2026\nEarth Day: April 22, 2026\nSanta Cruz County Fair: TBD (September 2026) (Only Applies to Routes 79 & 79F servicing the Fairgrounds)\nCA Clean Air Day: TBD (October 2026)"], "expected_facts": ["free", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-055", "question": "On Santa Cruz METRO I sometimes tap my phone and sometimes tap the plastic card from the same bank account. Does my fare still cap?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, your fare will not cap if you switch between payment methods.\n\nThe published policy states: \"You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\" [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz] Additionally, \"If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\" [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]\n\nSince your phone and plastic card are different payment methods—even though they draw from the same bank account—switching between them will break the fare cap. To qualify for fare capping, you must use the same contactless payment method (either your phone or your plastic card, but not both) for all your trips.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-tap2cruz"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>On Santa Cruz METRO I sometimes tap my phone and sometimes tap the plastic card from the same bank account. Does my fare still cap?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, your fare will not cap if you switch between payment methods.</p><p>The published policy states: \"You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\" [scmtd-tap2cruz] Additionally, \"If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\" [scmtd-tap2cruz]</p><p>Since your phone and plastic card are different payment methods—even though they draw from the same bank account—switching between them will break the fare cap. To qualify for fare capping, you must use the same contactless payment method (either your phone or your plastic card, but not both) for all your trips.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["What will I be charged?\nWhen paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a Tap2Cruz reader, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method—you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for only $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay.\nIs there a weekly or monthly payment option?\nYes, please see the table below for daily and monthly fare-capping amounts when paying with a contactless payment method.\nThe 31-day cap operates on a rolling basis. This means that a cap will apply for all trips taken within a 31-day period from the first time you tap to pay, and on a rolling basis, based on usage thereafter.\nThe 31-day period starts with the first tap.\nHow do charges appear on my card statement?\nThe fare is charged to your card’s account and will include the description: “Santa Cruz METRO”\nTo view your recent METRO rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal (or check your bank card statement).\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure?\nYes. Tap2Cruz transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private?\nPersonal data will never be stored, sold, or given away by METRO or any third-party agent. METRO’s Customer Service Representatives do not have access to full credit card account numbers. Customer Service Representatives will answer customer support queries in accordance with Payment Card Industry practices.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history?\nNo. Just like your card issuer would not see what you order from a coffee shop or other retailer, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on METRO?\nNo, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method?\nNo. Each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nTip: If you have both a physical card and a digital wallet version of that same card, they count as different payment methods.\nDo I always have to use the same payment method within the 2-hour transfer window?\nYes. You cannot switch payment methods during the 2-hour transfer window. The fare calculation software will only charge your card $2 throughout this window, no matter how many times it is tapped. If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\nImportant: You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments?\nNo. Fares paid via contactless payment will never cost more than the standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go up to the fare cap.\nPlease review the fare chart for Tap2Cruz fare capping information.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board within that 2-hour window?\nYes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 regardless how many times you tap. You will not be charged again until 2 hours have passed.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment?\nDiscounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts.\nUtilizing CalITP Benefits, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card, ensuring you always receive discount fare when tapping!\nRiders who are eligible for a discount fare can still pay the existing discount fare with cash or SmartCard when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What type of payment methods are accepted on METRO’s contactless payment system?\nMETRO accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. Accepted mobile wallets include Apple Pay & Google Pay, either on your phone or wearable.\nHow do I know if I have a contactless-enabled bank card?\nCards that are contactless-enabled have the symbol shown below (on the front or the back):\nMany banks and financial institutions have switched to contactless cards to make payments safer, faster, and more secure for their customers. Check with your card issuer if you are unsure if your card is contactless-enabled.\nIf you do not have a contactless card, you have options:\nLink an existing bank card to your smart device.\nOpen a safe, inclusive bank or checking account.\nOrder a contactless-enabled Cash App Card (no need to have a bank account).\nCan I still ride METRO if I do not have a contactless-enabled card or a payment-enabled device?\nYes. You can still pay for your fare using cash and Splash Pass. You will need to use the farebox to pay your fare, or visit either of our Customer Service Booths in Santa Cruz or Watsonville.\nHow do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on METRO?\nAs you board the bus to start your journey, look for the contactless symbol on the Tap2Pay readers:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat are Tap2Pay readers?\nTap2Pay payment readers are the face of METRO’s contactless fare payment system. The readers serve as point-of-sale terminals, allowing you to pay your fare and enter the system instantly. They are mounted at the front entrance of the bus and look like this:\nWhy doesn’t my contactless-enabled bank card work?\nIf your contactless-enabled bank card is not working, it may be due to one of the following reasons:\nYou have a new card. If you have a new contactless-enabled card, you might need to activate it first. If, after activating, your new contactless-enabled card it is still not working, please contact your card issuer at the phone number listed on the back of your card.\nYour card issuer has declined payment. This may be due to your bank account not having enough funds for the journey. Please check with your card issuer.\nYour card may be damaged or expired. If this happens, you’ll need to get your card reissued by contacting your card issuer.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted.\nCash and Splash Pass are still accepted, as well.\nTo use Tap2Cruz, use the same method of contactless payment on each trip and tap the payment reader as you board the bus. If traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately. Don’t use the same contactless payment for multiple riders.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route."], "expected_facts": ["same payment method"]}
{"id": "edge-056", "question": "I'm a veteran — can I get the discount fare on Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYes, Santa Cruz METRO offers a discount fare for veterans. The published policy states that \"Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts\" [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]. Using this program, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card to receive the discount fare when tapping.\n\nThe discount fare itself is $1.00 for a cash/1-ride payment, $3 for a day pass, or $32 for a 31-day pass [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify the application process, required documentation, or how to verify your veteran status for this discount. To apply and complete the verification, please contact Santa Cruz METRO's Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 or visit their website at scmetro.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-fares-passes", "scmtd-tap2cruz"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a veteran — can I get the discount fare on Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Yes, Santa Cruz METRO offers a discount fare for veterans. The published policy states that \"Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts\" [scmtd-tap2cruz]. Using this program, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card to receive the discount fare when tapping.</p><p>The discount fare itself is $1.00 for a cash/1-ride payment, $3 for a day pass, or $32 for a 31-day pass [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify the application process, required documentation, or how to verify your veteran status for this discount. To apply and complete the verification, please contact Santa Cruz METRO's Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 or visit their website at scmetro.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Monterey Salinas Transit (MST)\nMST will honor a free valid METRO transfer as payment for passengers boarding lines 28 and 29 at the Watsonville Transit Center from Lane 2.\nMETRO will honor free valid MST transfers for one-way travel within the METRO service area as far as the Temporary Riverfront Transit Center in downtown Santa Cruz.", "METRO passes and tickets can be purchased in-person at the Customer Service Windows at:\nTemporary Riverfront Customer Experience Center 603 Front St., downtown Santa Cruz (831) 425-8600 Served by all routes except 72-79\nWatsonville Transit Center 475 Rodriguez St., Watsonville (831) 425-8600 Served by routes 1, 2, 72-79, plus MST 28 & 29\nBoulder Creek Pharmacy 3081 Highway in Boulder Creek Served by route 35\nFree Fare Programs\nYouth Cruz Free\nCOLLEGE STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nFREE FARES FOR LEGALLY BLIND RIDERS", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026."], "expected_facts": ["veteran", "Cal-ITP"]}
{"id": "edge-057", "question": "If I have a Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card, does my 10-year-old ride free with me?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether your 10-year-old rides free with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card.\n\nThe passages do state that children (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card, but only if your application form includes a completed Request for Reduced Children's Fare with Discount Fare Card Form (Attachment B), which will result in a \"gold dot\" placed on your Discount Photo ID Card [doc:scmtd-accessibility]. This means your child would receive a discount fare, not a free fare.\n\nThe passages also describe a separate Youth Cruz Free program for K-12 students, but do not state whether your 10-year-old qualifies for that program or whether it applies when traveling with you [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nTo learn whether your child qualifies for the Youth Cruz Free program or to apply for the reduced children's fare with your Discount Photo ID Card, contact Santa Cruz METRO Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit the Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz (603 Front St.) or Watsonville (475 Rodriguez St.) during business hours [doc:scmtd-accessibility].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-accessibility", "scmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I have a Santa Cruz METRO Discount Photo ID Card, does my 10-year-old ride free with me?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify whether your 10-year-old rides free with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card.</p><p>The passages do state that children (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card, but only if your application form includes a completed Request for Reduced Children's Fare with Discount Fare Card Form (Attachment B), which will result in a \"gold dot\" placed on your Discount Photo ID Card [scmtd-accessibility]. This means your child would receive a discount fare, not a free fare.</p><p>The passages also describe a separate Youth Cruz Free program for K-12 students, but do not state whether your 10-year-old qualifies for that program or whether it applies when traveling with you [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>To learn whether your child qualifies for the Youth Cruz Free program or to apply for the reduced children's fare with your Discount Photo ID Card, contact Santa Cruz METRO Customer Service at (831) 425-8600 or visit the Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz (603 Front St.) or Watsonville (475 Rodriguez St.) during business hours [scmtd-accessibility].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Accessibility (discount fares and Discount Photo ID Card)</li><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "To make it easier for seniors and people with disabilities to ride public transit, we offer a METRO-issued Discount Photo ID Card. The Discount Photo ID provides proof of eligibility for METRO discount fares and passes. It also qualifies you to ride with attendants at no extra fare and/or children at the discount fare. People with METRO’s Discount Photo ID can use the card to qualify for the discount fares offered by other transit agencies.\nApplication Criteria for Older Adults\nMust be at least 65 years old\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above (except Medicare ID).\nApplication Criteria for People with Disabilities\nMust submit one of the forms of identification listed above.\nOR\nMust present a complete Professional Verification of Disability Status form. The form must be completed by a medical practitioner or other professional verifying your disability.", "Visit Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville during business hours.\nProcessing and Replacement Fees\n$2.00 to process a new Photo ID Card\n$2.00 for the first replacement of a lost card\n$5.00 for the replacement of a lost card thereafter\nHow to Obtain the Application Forms\nRead the Discount Fare Program and Policy .\nPrint the application forms that are appended to the policy as Attachment A and Attachment B .\nIf your application requires confirmation of legal blindness, print and submit Attachment C .\nYou may also request the forms from Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville.\nAttendants\nPeople with disabilities who require an attendant are only required to pay one discount rate fare when they travel with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must indicate that you need an attendant. A “green dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.\nChildren\nChildren (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must include a completed Request for Reduced Children’s Fare with Discount Fare Card Form ( Attachment B ). A “gold dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026."], "expected_facts": ["discount fare rate", "gold dot"]}
{"id": "edge-058", "question": "I'm 82 and I ride SolTrans — do I still pay the senior fare?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, you do not pay the senior fare. According to SolTrans policy, seniors 80+ ride **FREE** on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, you must present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator. [doc:soltrans-fare-table]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 82 and I ride SolTrans — do I still pay the senior fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, you do not pay the senior fare. According to SolTrans policy, seniors 80+ ride **FREE** on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, you must present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator. [soltrans-fare-table]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "Local Passes : Valid for travel within Vallejo and Benicia on SolTrans Local fixed routes * and local trips on SolanoExpress fixed routes. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travel outside Vallejo and Benicia.\nWithin Solano County Passes : Valid for travel within and between Solano County cities (Vallejo, Benicia, Fairfield, Vacaville, Dixon, and Davis) on SolanoExpress Lines. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travels outside of Solano County. ‍\nOutside Solano County Passes : Valid for travel both outside and within Solano County on SolanoExpress fixed routes and SolTrans Local fixed routes. Extra surcharge on SolanoExpress Route 82 to the San Francisco Ferry terminal. ‍ ‍", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers."], "expected_facts": ["FREE", "80"]}
{"id": "edge-059", "question": "What do I have to show the driver to get the reduced fare on SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nTo get the reduced fare on SolTrans, you must present one of the following to the operator [doc:soltrans-fare-table]:\n\n- Photo ID with birthdate indicating 65+\n- Valid Medicare Card with photo ID\n- Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\n- DMV Disabled License Plate Registration\n- DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\n\nAdditionally, if you are 80 or older, you ride free on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines by presenting a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator [doc:soltrans-fare-table].\n\nIf you qualify for the Clipper START Program (lower-income adults age 19-64), you can receive a 50 percent fare discount with a valid Clipper START Card [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. For information about the application process, visit Clipper START.\n\nThe published policy does not specify how to obtain a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or how to apply for the reduced-fare categories beyond the Clipper START program. For details on obtaining these credentials, contact SolTrans directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card", "soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What do I have to show the driver to get the reduced fare on SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>To get the reduced fare on SolTrans, you must present one of the following to the operator [soltrans-fare-table]:</p><p>- Photo ID with birthdate indicating 65+</p><p>- Valid Medicare Card with photo ID</p><p>- Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card</p><p>- DMV Disabled License Plate Registration</p><p>- DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout</p><p>Additionally, if you are 80 or older, you ride free on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines by presenting a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator [soltrans-fare-table].</p><p>If you qualify for the Clipper START Program (lower-income adults age 19-64), you can receive a 50 percent fare discount with a valid Clipper START Card [soltrans-clipper-card]. For information about the application process, visit Clipper START.</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to obtain a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or how to apply for the reduced-fare categories beyond the Clipper START program. For details on obtaining these credentials, contact SolTrans directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Please have the exact fare ready as drivers do not carry change (you can overpay if you don't have the exact amount). Put your money in the farebox located next to the driver; note that the farebox has a sticker that lists all fares. ‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "One ride and day passes can be purchased on the bus, all other passes can be purchased at one of our ticket retail locations ( Where to Buy SolTrans Tickets and Passes ). Please put the pass in the farebox located next to the driver with the arrow facing down. ‍", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card"], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "Regional Transit Connection"]}
{"id": "edge-060", "question": "I take one SolTrans bus and then transfer to another SolTrans bus. Do I pay twice?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you pay twice. There are no transfers within the SolTrans system. [doc:soltrans-clipper-card] The note states: \"There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.\" [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n\nIf you need to make multiple trips on the same day, you can purchase a Day Pass, which is valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. [doc:soltrans-fare-table] Day Passes are available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas and may be purchased onboard the bus with exact fare. [doc:soltrans-fare-table]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card", "soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I take one SolTrans bus and then transfer to another SolTrans bus. Do I pay twice?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you pay twice. There are no transfers within the SolTrans system. [soltrans-clipper-card] The note states: \"There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.\" [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>If you need to make multiple trips on the same day, you can purchase a Day Pass, which is valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. [soltrans-fare-table] Day Passes are available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas and may be purchased onboard the bus with exact fare. [soltrans-fare-table]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "One ride and day passes can be purchased on the bus, all other passes can be purchased at one of our ticket retail locations ( Where to Buy SolTrans Tickets and Passes ). Please put the pass in the farebox located next to the driver with the arrow facing down. ‍", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card"], "expected_facts": ["day pass"]}
{"id": "edge-061", "question": "I have a FAX reduced fare ID and I ride the bus for free. Is Handy Ride free for me too?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nNo. Handy Ride is not free for reduced-fare riders. The published policy distinguishes between FAX Fixed Route buses and Handy Ride:\n\nFor reduced-fare riders on Fixed Route buses, the policy states: \"Qualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider\" [doc:fax-fares]. However, this explicitly excludes Handy Ride: \"Handy Ride is not included\" [doc:fax-fares].\n\nFor Handy Ride, the fares are:\n- ADA Eligible Individual: $1.25 per ride\n- Companion to ADA certified individual: $1.25 per ride\n- Personal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual: Free [doc:fax-fares]\n\nYour reduced-fare ID covers Fixed Route buses, but Handy Ride operates under a separate fare structure. If you have questions about whether you may qualify for ADA-eligible Handy Ride fares, contact FAX at (559) 621-RIDE (7433).", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a FAX reduced fare ID and I ride the bus for free. Is Handy Ride free for me too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>No. Handy Ride is not free for reduced-fare riders. The published policy distinguishes between FAX Fixed Route buses and Handy Ride:</p><p>For reduced-fare riders on Fixed Route buses, the policy states: \"Qualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider\" [fax-fares]. However, this explicitly excludes Handy Ride: \"Handy Ride is not included\" [fax-fares].</p><p>For Handy Ride, the fares are:</p><p>- ADA Eligible Individual: $1.25 per ride</p><p>- Companion to ADA certified individual: $1.25 per ride</p><p>- Personal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual: Free [fax-fares]</p><p>Your reduced-fare ID covers Fixed Route buses, but Handy Ride operates under a separate fare structure. If you have questions about whether you may qualify for ADA-eligible Handy Ride fares, contact FAX at (559) 621-RIDE (7433).</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "Key | Pass Type\n* | Sells All Passes\n1 | 31 Day Full Fare\n*2 | 31 Day Reduced Fare – FREE at This Time\n3 | Handy Ride Pass\n4 | 31 Day Student Fare\nAll regular and discounted fares can be purchased at all ticket vending machines (TVMs) located at bus rapid transit stations along the Q (Route 1), as well as the TVM located outside the front doors of City Hall on the first floor.", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)"], "expected_facts": ["$1.25", "not included"]}
{"id": "edge-062", "question": "I'm 60 and I have a Medicare card. Can I get the FAX reduced fare, or do I have to be 65?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nThe published criteria for FAX reduced fares are: \"Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards.\" [doc:fax-fares]\n\nYou have a Medicare card, which is one of the three independent paths to qualify for reduced fares at FAX. The published policy does not require you to be 65 if you hold a Medicare card — that is a separate qualifying criterion from the age threshold. [doc:fax-fares]\n\nCurrently, all reduced fare riders ride free on FAX fixed-route buses. [doc:fax-fares] To board, you must show your Medicare card and proper identification at the time of boarding. [doc:fax-fares]\n\nThe agency makes the final decision on your eligibility. If you have questions, you can call FAX at (559) 621-RIDE (7433). [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares", "fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 60 and I have a Medicare card. Can I get the FAX reduced fare, or do I have to be 65?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>The published criteria for FAX reduced fares are: \"Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards.\" [fax-fares]</p><p>You have a Medicare card, which is one of the three independent paths to qualify for reduced fares at FAX. The published policy does not require you to be 65 if you hold a Medicare card — that is a separate qualifying criterion from the age threshold. [fax-fares]</p><p>Currently, all reduced fare riders ride free on FAX fixed-route buses. [fax-fares] To board, you must show your Medicare card and proper identification at the time of boarding. [fax-fares]</p><p>The agency makes the final decision on your eligibility. If you have questions, you can call FAX at (559) 621-RIDE (7433). [fax-reduced-fare-program]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order"], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-063", "question": "Where do I go in person to get the FAX reduced-fare ID card, and what hours?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "To get a FAX reduced-fare ID card in person, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC) located at **3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall**. ID cards are made **Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.** [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]\n\nYou must bring an official federal or state picture ID and one of the following documents to verify eligibility:\n- Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n- Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n- Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) Award Letter (dated within 12 months of your visit)\n\nYour FAX photo ID will be taken during your visit and provided the same day. [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]\n\nIf you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433). [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Where do I go in person to get the FAX reduced-fare ID card, and what hours?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>To get a FAX reduced-fare ID card in person, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC) located at **3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall**. ID cards are made **Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.** [fax-reduced-fare-program]</p><p>You must bring an official federal or state picture ID and one of the following documents to verify eligibility:</p><p>- Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)</p><p>- Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt</p><p>- Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) Award Letter (dated within 12 months of your visit)</p><p>Your FAX photo ID will be taken during your visit and provided the same day. [fax-reduced-fare-program]</p><p>If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433). [fax-reduced-fare-program]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "ABOUT\nFresno State University, State Center Community College District, Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District, currently subsidize transit fares for students.\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nStudents, faculty and staff from Fresno State University, Fresno City College, and Clovis Community College will be able to swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride.\nHigh School students from Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District will be able to request a 31-Day Student Pass from their respective school administration and simply swipe the pass and show their valid student ID.\nAll other students will be able to purchase single fares for .75 cents or purchase a 31-Day monthly pass for $22 at any Ticket Vending Machine (TVM) along the BRT route 1 or at Manchester Transit Center. Students will need to show the driver a valid student Identification card to board the bus.\nFOR ASSISTANCE"], "expected_facts": ["Manchester Transit Center", "8:30"]}
{"id": "edge-064", "question": "I have a disability but no Medicare card. What else can I bring to qualify for the FAX reduced fare?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nTo qualify for FAX's Reduced Fare Program with a disability but without a Medicare card, you can bring one of these documents instead [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]:\n\n- **Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt**, or\n- **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) Award Letter** (dated within 12 months of your visit)\n\nYou must also bring an official federal or state picture ID (such as a driver's license) [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].\n\n**To apply:**\nVisit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC) at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall. ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. Your FAX photo ID will be taken and your ID card will be provided during your visit [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]. If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433) [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].\n\nCurrently, Reduced Fare riders ride free on FAX Fixed Route buses [doc:fax-fares]. Your ID card will have an expiration date (maximum three years from issuance) and must be renewed upon expiration. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost or damaged ID cards [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares", "fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a disability but no Medicare card. What else can I bring to qualify for the FAX reduced fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>To qualify for FAX's Reduced Fare Program with a disability but without a Medicare card, you can bring one of these documents instead [fax-reduced-fare-program]:</p><p>- **Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt**, or</p><p>- **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) Award Letter** (dated within 12 months of your visit)</p><p>You must also bring an official federal or state picture ID (such as a driver's license) [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><p>**To apply:**</p><p>Visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC) at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall. ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. Your FAX photo ID will be taken and your ID card will be provided during your visit [fax-reduced-fare-program]. If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433) [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><p>Currently, Reduced Fare riders ride free on FAX Fixed Route buses [fax-fares]. Your ID card will have an expiration date (maximum three years from issuance) and must be renewed upon expiration. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost or damaged ID cards [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order"], "expected_facts": ["Social Security Disability", "Placard"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-064", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-065", "question": "My personal care attendant rides with me on FAX. Do they pay?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYour Personal Care Attendant (PCA) rides **free** [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]. \n\nTo establish this benefit, you must bring a note to the FAX Manchester Transit Center from your healthcare provider on the provider's letterhead stating the need for a PCA. Once verified by the MTC staff, your PCA can ride with you for free [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].\n\nThe Manchester Transit Center is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall. ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433) [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My personal care attendant rides with me on FAX. Do they pay?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Your Personal Care Attendant (PCA) rides **free** [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><p>To establish this benefit, you must bring a note to the FAX Manchester Transit Center from your healthcare provider on the provider's letterhead stating the need for a PCA. Once verified by the MTC staff, your PCA can ride with you for free [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><p>The Manchester Transit Center is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall. ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m. If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433) [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", " Carta de adjudicación del Seguro Social por discapacidad (SSI/SSDI) (fechada\ndentro de los 12 meses posteriores a su visita).\n3. El día de su visita:\n Debe traer una identificación oficial con foto federal o estatal (por ejemplo, licencia\nde conducir) y uno de los documentos mencionados anteriormente.\no Nota: si es menor de 16 años, por favor, traiga una identificación de la escuela o\nuna copia de su certificado de nacimiento con la identificación de sus padres\nemitida por el estado o el gobierno federal.\n Se tomará su foto para su identificación.\n Su tarjeta de identificación de FAX se proporcionará durante su visita.\n4. Su nueva tarjeta de identificación tendrá una fecha de vencimiento (máximo tres años\na partir de la emisión) y deberá renovarse al vencimiento.\n5. Hay una tarifa de reemplazo de $3 por tarjetas de identificación perdidas o dañadas.\nNOTA: Los pasajeros solo pueden tener una identificación a la vez; el personal de la\noficina destruirá las tarjetas antiguas.\n6. Si necesita que un asistente de cuidado personal (PCA) lo acompañe, traiga una nota\na MTC de su proveedor de atención médica, con el membrete del proveedor, que\nindique la necesidad. Una vez verificado por MTC, el PCA puede viajar contigo gratis.\n*Los pasajeros de tarifa reducida pueden viajar gratis en este momento mientras haya fondos de\nsubsidio disponibles.\nFresno Area Express no discrimina a las personas con discapacidades.", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order"], "expected_facts": ["free", "note"]}
{"id": "edge-066", "question": "My 10-year-old rides FAX with me. Does she pay?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nYour 10-year-old rides free on FAX. Children age 12 and under ride free, though children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult [doc:fax-fares]. Your child is within the free age range and does not need to pay a fare.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 10-year-old rides FAX with me. Does she pay?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Your 10-year-old rides free on FAX. Children age 12 and under ride free, though children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult [fax-fares]. Your child is within the free age range and does not need to pay a fare.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "autobuses con rutas fijas por FAX a un costo reducido*\n1. Para aplicar, visite el FAX Centro de Tránsito de Manchester (MTC)\n MTC está ubicado en 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., frente al centro comercial de\nManchester.\n Las tarjetas de identificación se realizan de lunes a viernes de 8:30 a.m. a 3:30 p.m.\n Si tiene preguntas antes de su visita, llame al (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Junto con su identificación federal o estatal con foto, se requerirá uno de los siguientes\ndocumentos para verificar su elegibilidad (vea los ejemplos en la parte posterior).\n Tarjeta de identificación de Medicare (tarjeta blanca con rayas rojas y azules)\n Recibo de placa para personas discapacitadas del Departamento de Vehículos"], "expected_facts": ["12", "free"]}
{"id": "edge-067", "question": "I take classes at Fresno City College. What do I pay on FAX?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nAs a Fresno City College student, you have two fare options on FAX:\n\n**Free rides:** Students from Fresno City College can swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride. [doc:fax-fares]\n\n**Paid alternative:** If you do not use the free program, you can purchase single fares for $0.75 or a 31-Day Student Pass for $22.00. To board with a paid student fare, you must show the driver a valid student identification card. [doc:fax-fares]\n\nThe free student program is subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District. [doc:fax-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I take classes at Fresno City College. What do I pay on FAX?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>As a Fresno City College student, you have two fare options on FAX:</p><p>**Free rides:** Students from Fresno City College can swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride. [fax-fares]</p><p>**Paid alternative:** If you do not use the free program, you can purchase single fares for $0.75 or a 31-Day Student Pass for $22.00. To board with a paid student fare, you must show the driver a valid student identification card. [fax-fares]</p><p>The free student program is subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District. [fax-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "ABOUT\nFresno State University, State Center Community College District, Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District, currently subsidize transit fares for students.\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nStudents, faculty and staff from Fresno State University, Fresno City College, and Clovis Community College will be able to swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride.\nHigh School students from Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District will be able to request a 31-Day Student Pass from their respective school administration and simply swipe the pass and show their valid student ID.\nAll other students will be able to purchase single fares for .75 cents or purchase a 31-Day monthly pass for $22 at any Ticket Vending Machine (TVM) along the BRT route 1 or at Manchester Transit Center. Students will need to show the driver a valid student Identification card to board the bus.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order", "Pass Outlets may charge an additional fee of up to $1.25 for passes and tokens.\nHours of outlet operations vary. As a result, it is recommended that you first call a pass outlet to confirm pass availability.\nPass Type | Location\n1, 3 | Fresno City College 1101 E. University (559) 489-2234\n1, 3 | Glen Agnes 603 W. Home (559) 445-0715\n* | Manchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Ave (559) 621-7433\n1 | Save Mart 5750 N. First St. (559) 435-9324\n3 | Valley Center for the Blind 3417 W. Shaw (559) 222-4447\n* | City Hall, Business Tax and Licensing Desk 2600 Fresno Street , Room 2162 (559) 621-6880\n*2 | Senior Citizen Village 1917 S. Chestnut (559) 251-8656", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]"], "expected_facts": ["free", ".75"]}
{"id": "edge-068", "question": "Do I need to buy a County Connection day pass before I ride?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, you do not need to buy a County Connection day pass before you ride. \n\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day [doc:cccta-clipper-card]. This means you pay per ride as you board, and once you reach the daily threshold, subsequent rides become free—you don't need to purchase a pass in advance.\n\nIf you use a Clipper card, the system tracks your spending automatically and applies the cap. If you prefer, you can also purchase an East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass on your Clipper card, which is valid for a rolling 31 days [doc:cccta-clipper-card], but that is optional.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do I need to buy a County Connection day pass before I ride?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, you do not need to buy a County Connection day pass before you ride.</p><p>The Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day [cccta-clipper-card]. This means you pay per ride as you board, and once you reach the daily threshold, subsequent rides become free—you don't need to purchase a pass in advance.</p><p>If you use a Clipper card, the system tracks your spending automatically and applies the cap. If you prefer, you can also purchase an East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass on your Clipper card, which is valid for a rolling 31 days [cccta-clipper-card], but that is optional.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card.", "County Connection accepts transfers as full fare payment at designated bus stops which are served by neighboring transit systems. Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper. ACE train passengers can ride Route 92X free with pre-purchased ACE train tickets. Amtrak train passengers can ride any County Connection bus for free using their digital transfer which has a date the transfers are good through or paper Amtrak ticket valid for the date printed and the following day.", "Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "You may wish to take advantage of County Connection LINK’s Advance Fare Payment System. The program was designed to simplify your travel by allowing you to prepay your trips.\nHow it works:\nMail a check to County Connection LINK, 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord, CA 94520.\nA minimum amount of $50.00 is required to establish an account. Make the check payable to County Connection LINK. Please state the name of the LINK client on the check.\nYour LINK trips will be automatically deducted from your account for the amount of your trip. If the balance in your account decreases to $25.00 or less, you will be notified by phone or mail. If your account reaches a “0” balance, you will be required to pay the cash/ticket fare for each scheduled pick-up until such time as your LINK account has sufficient funds.\nIf you have any questions regarding this information and/or the balance of your existing account, please contact LINK, Monday through Friday, from 8:00am to 5:00pm at (925) 938-7433.\nThe Advance Fare Payment System is offered as a convenience by County Connection. Individuals who fail to provide sufficient funds to cover their trips and fall into arrears will be required to pay for their trips as they board and reimburse any funds in arrears. Individuals whose checks bounce will be required to pay by money order or cash for future trips."], "expected_facts": ["$3.75", "automatic"]}
{"id": "edge-069", "question": "My 16-year-old doesn't have a Clipper card. How much is her cash fare on County Connection?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nYour 16-year-old's cash fare on County Connection is **$2.50** [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]. The published policy notes that exact change only is required, as drivers do not carry change [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices].\n\nThe youth fare discount (50% off Adult Clipper single-ride fares) is available only when using a Youth Clipper card and is not available when paying with cash [doc:cccta-clipper-card].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card", "cccta-fare-types-prices"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 16-year-old doesn't have a Clipper card. How much is her cash fare on County Connection?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Your 16-year-old's cash fare on County Connection is **$2.50** [cccta-fare-types-prices]. The published policy notes that exact change only is required, as drivers do not carry change [cccta-fare-types-prices].</p><p>The youth fare discount (50% off Adult Clipper single-ride fares) is available only when using a Youth Clipper card and is not available when paying with cash [cccta-clipper-card].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card.", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted)."], "expected_facts": ["$2.50", "Youth Clipper"]}
{"id": "edge-070", "question": "I have Medi-Cal. Can I use it to get the RTC discount card from County Connection?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. The published policy explicitly states that Medi-Cal is not accepted for RTC Discount Card eligibility. [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]\n\nTo apply for an RTC Discount Card from County Connection, you must bring original documents showing one of these instead:\n\n- A Medicare card (the federally-issued red, white, and blue card — only for those under 65 years of age)\n- A California DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt\n- Another transit agency card within the U.S. equivalent to the RTC card, or ADA Paratransit certification outside of a Bay Area transit agency\n- Proof of Veterans Disability (a copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher)\n- For ADA Paratransit, the name of the Bay Area transit agency where ADA-paratransit eligibility was established [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]\n\nApplications are accepted at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. New and renewal RTC cards are free; replacement cards cost $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card] [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-fare-types-prices", "cccta-rtc-discount-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have Medi-Cal. Can I use it to get the RTC discount card from County Connection?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. The published policy explicitly states that Medi-Cal is not accepted for RTC Discount Card eligibility. [cccta-rtc-discount-card]</p><p>To apply for an RTC Discount Card from County Connection, you must bring original documents showing one of these instead:</p><p>- A Medicare card (the federally-issued red, white, and blue card — only for those under 65 years of age)</p><p>- A California DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt</p><p>- Another transit agency card within the U.S. equivalent to the RTC card, or ADA Paratransit certification outside of a Bay Area transit agency</p><p>- Proof of Veterans Disability (a copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher)</p><p>- For ADA Paratransit, the name of the Bay Area transit agency where ADA-paratransit eligibility was established [cccta-rtc-discount-card]</p><p>Applications are accepted at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. New and renewal RTC cards are free; replacement cards cost $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). [cccta-rtc-discount-card] [cccta-fare-types-prices]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li><li>CCCTA: RTC Discount Card Program</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted).", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "Download the Attendant Card Application (PDF) ( En Español )\nAttendants qualify for a discount fare when traveling with RTC cardholders who have been certified as eligible for attendant assistance. A RTC cardholder may apply for an “Attendant Supplemental” RTC/Clipper card that can be used to pay the attendant’s fare when accompanying the RTC cardholder. The “Attendant Supplemental” card is issued to the RTC cardholder who is responsible for its care and proper use. The card may not be used by anyone other than the attendant and may be used only when traveling with the RTC cardholder. Any misuse of this card will result in the permanent loss of the attendant card privilege. Misuse of the attendant card may constitute fare evasion and result in a citation and fine.\nRules for Use of Supplemental Attendant Card\nRTC cardholder is solely responsible for care and appropriate use of attendant card.\nCard may only be used by the attendant to pay for trips when traveling together with the eligible RTC card.\nTravel history of both cards may be monitored to ensure appropriate use, that is, every trip taken using the attendant card corresponds exactly to a trip taken by the RTC cardholder. NO EXCEPTIONS!\nThe attendant card privilege will be revoked permanently if the card is misused.", "Download the Basic Eligibility Form (PDF) ( En Español )\nTo apply for Basic Eligibility, bring original documents of one of the following with your application:\nMedicare card, the federally-issued red, white, and blue card – only for those under 65 years of age. (NOTE: Medi-Cal is not accepted for eligibility.)\nCalifornia DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt. If you bring the blue or red placard, you must also bring the registration receipt. For applicants with disabled license plates (DP) instead of a placard, please submit a “Medical Eligibility” form.\nOther transit agency card within the U.S. that is equivalent to the RTC card or ADA Paratransit certification outside of a Bay Area transit agency.\nProof of Veterans Disability– A copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher.\nFor ADA Paratransit, please provide the name of the Bay Area transit agency ADA-paratransit eligibility was established.\nThe transit agency staff will take a photocopy of the supporting documentation and submit it with your application. Staff will also take your photo for printing on the RTC card."], "expected_facts": ["Medi-Cal is not accepted", "Medicare"]}
{"id": "edge-071", "question": "I'm 67 and disabled. Should I apply for an RTC card or a Senior Clipper card at County Connection?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nCounty Connection publishes two paths for you:\n\n**Senior Clipper Card:** People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card, which provides the same discounts and never expires [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]. A Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper [doc:cccta-clipper-card]. Applications for Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax, or you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices [doc:cccta-clipper-card].\n\n**RTC Discount Card:** The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]. To apply for the RTC card based on disability, bring original documents including a Medicare card (for those under 65), a California DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt, or other equivalent transit agency card [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]. Applications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations, and new applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card].\n\nThe published policy does not specify which option is preferable for someone who meets both criteria. The agency makes that decision. Contact County Connection to discuss which card best fits your situation.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card", "cccta-fare-types-prices", "cccta-rtc-discount-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 67 and disabled. Should I apply for an RTC card or a Senior Clipper card at County Connection?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>County Connection publishes two paths for you:</p><p>**Senior Clipper Card:** People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card, which provides the same discounts and never expires [cccta-rtc-discount-card]. A Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper [cccta-clipper-card]. Applications for Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax, or you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices [cccta-clipper-card].</p><p>**RTC Discount Card:** The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities [cccta-fare-types-prices]. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area [cccta-fare-types-prices]. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free [cccta-fare-types-prices]. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord [cccta-fare-types-prices]. To apply for the RTC card based on disability, bring original documents including a Medicare card (for those under 65), a California DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt, or other equivalent transit agency card [cccta-rtc-discount-card]. Applications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations, and new applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken [cccta-rtc-discount-card].</p><p>The published policy does not specify which option is preferable for someone who meets both criteria. The agency makes that decision. Contact County Connection to discuss which card best fits your situation.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li><li>CCCTA: RTC Discount Card Program</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted).", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "The following forms of ID are accepted with senior/disabled fares.\nRegional Transit Connection Discount Card (RTC)\nState issued photo ID as proof of age 65 or older\nPhoto ID and Medicare Card\nPhoto ID and DMV placard registration receipt\nVeterans Disability\nSenior Clipper Card", "If you applied or renewed your RTC card before January 2020 OR your disability is not permanent, your card has an expiration date. When it expires, you will need to renew and receive a new card. A renewal notice will be sent to you two months prior to the expiration date on your card. It is your responsibility to ensure that the transit agency has your current address.\nIf you have applied or renewed using the Medical Certification form after January 1, 2020 and your disability is permanent, your card does not have an expiration date. These cards do not need to be renewed. The RTC program will contact you to update your photo and preferred communication method every five years.\nIf you are a Senior who has been using an RTC card, you will be asked to apply for a Senior Clipper card when your RTC card expires.\nIf you do not receive a mail reminder,\nDownload the Renewal Application (PDF) Or, you may call your transit agency and request a renewal application.\nMail your completed application to the RTC Central Processor: RTC Central Processor P.O. Box 700 6 Stockton, CA 95267 Or you may bring your completed application to your transit agency.", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com ."], "expected_facts": ["Senior Clipper", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-072", "question": "I'm a veteran with a 30% VA disability rating. What does County Connection publish about getting the RTC card as a veteran?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, here is what County Connection publishes about the RTC card for veterans:\n\n**Eligibility criterion for veterans:**\nCounty Connection accepts \"Proof of Veterans Disability\" as one of the qualifying documents for RTC Discount Card Basic Eligibility. The published policy specifies that you must provide \"A copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher.\" [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]\n\nYour stated 30% VA disability rating does not meet the published threshold of 50% or higher for this pathway.\n\n**How to apply:**\nTo apply for the RTC Discount Card, bring your original documents (in your case, the Service Connected Disability ID card and VA Certification letter) with the Basic Eligibility Form to a County Connection office. The transit agency staff will photocopy your supporting documentation, take your photo for the card, and submit your application. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office for verification, and qualified applicants are mailed the new RTC card. [doc:cccta-rtc-discount-card]\n\n**Application location and cost:**\nRTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. New RTC cards are free. [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]\n\nHowever, the published policy does not specify whether a 30% disability rating qualifies under any other pathway. I recommend contacting County Connection directly to discuss your specific rating and any other eligibility options that may apply.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-fare-types-prices", "cccta-rtc-discount-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a veteran with a 30% VA disability rating. What does County Connection publish about getting the RTC card as a veteran?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, here is what County Connection publishes about the RTC card for veterans:</p><p>**Eligibility criterion for veterans:**</p><p>County Connection accepts \"Proof of Veterans Disability\" as one of the qualifying documents for RTC Discount Card Basic Eligibility. The published policy specifies that you must provide \"A copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher.\" [cccta-rtc-discount-card]</p><p>Your stated 30% VA disability rating does not meet the published threshold of 50% or higher for this pathway.</p><p>**How to apply:**</p><p>To apply for the RTC Discount Card, bring your original documents (in your case, the Service Connected Disability ID card and VA Certification letter) with the Basic Eligibility Form to a County Connection office. The transit agency staff will photocopy your supporting documentation, take your photo for the card, and submit your application. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office for verification, and qualified applicants are mailed the new RTC card. [cccta-rtc-discount-card]</p><p>**Application location and cost:**</p><p>RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. New RTC cards are free. [cccta-fare-types-prices]</p><p>However, the published policy does not specify whether a 30% disability rating qualifies under any other pathway. I recommend contacting County Connection directly to discuss your specific rating and any other eligibility options that may apply.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li><li>CCCTA: RTC Discount Card Program</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following forms of ID are accepted with senior/disabled fares.\nRegional Transit Connection Discount Card (RTC)\nState issued photo ID as proof of age 65 or older\nPhoto ID and Medicare Card\nPhoto ID and DMV placard registration receipt\nVeterans Disability\nSenior Clipper Card", "Download the Basic Eligibility Form (PDF) ( En Español )\nTo apply for Basic Eligibility, bring original documents of one of the following with your application:\nMedicare card, the federally-issued red, white, and blue card – only for those under 65 years of age. (NOTE: Medi-Cal is not accepted for eligibility.)\nCalifornia DMV Placard Registration parking placard receipt. If you bring the blue or red placard, you must also bring the registration receipt. For applicants with disabled license plates (DP) instead of a placard, please submit a “Medical Eligibility” form.\nOther transit agency card within the U.S. that is equivalent to the RTC card or ADA Paratransit certification outside of a Bay Area transit agency.\nProof of Veterans Disability– A copy of your Service Connected Disability ID card and your VA Certification letter demonstrating a disability rating for aid and attendance, or for a service-connected disability with a rating level of 50% or higher.\nFor ADA Paratransit, please provide the name of the Bay Area transit agency ADA-paratransit eligibility was established.\nThe transit agency staff will take a photocopy of the supporting documentation and submit it with your application. Staff will also take your photo for printing on the RTC card.", "County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted).", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "If you applied or renewed your RTC card before January 2020 OR your disability is not permanent, your card has an expiration date. When it expires, you will need to renew and receive a new card. A renewal notice will be sent to you two months prior to the expiration date on your card. It is your responsibility to ensure that the transit agency has your current address.\nIf you have applied or renewed using the Medical Certification form after January 1, 2020 and your disability is permanent, your card does not have an expiration date. These cards do not need to be renewed. The RTC program will contact you to update your photo and preferred communication method every five years.\nIf you are a Senior who has been using an RTC card, you will be asked to apply for a Senior Clipper card when your RTC card expires.\nIf you do not receive a mail reminder,\nDownload the Renewal Application (PDF) Or, you may call your transit agency and request a renewal application.\nMail your completed application to the RTC Central Processor: RTC Central Processor P.O. Box 700 6 Stockton, CA 95267 Or you may bring your completed application to your transit agency.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com ."], "expected_facts": ["50%", "aid and attendance"]}
{"id": "edge-073", "question": "Coming off BART, what do I pay to board a County Connection bus as an adult using Clipper cash value?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Coming off BART, you pay **$1.00** to board a County Connection bus as an adult using Clipper cash value. This is the BART-to-bus transfer fare for adult riders. [doc:cccta-transfers]\n\nThe fare is deducted from your card's stored cash value. If you hold a 31-Day Pass instead, there is no deduction for the BART transfer. [doc:cccta-transfers]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Coming off BART, what do I pay to board a County Connection bus as an adult using Clipper cash value?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Coming off BART, you pay **$1.00** to board a County Connection bus as an adult using Clipper cash value. This is the BART-to-bus transfer fare for adult riders. [cccta-transfers]</p><p>The fare is deducted from your card's stored cash value. If you hold a 31-Day Pass instead, there is no deduction for the BART transfer. [cccta-transfers]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "Clipper\nAdult (19-64 yrs)\nSingle Ride | $2.00\nBus-to-Bus Transfer | Free\nBART-to-Bus Transfer | $1.00\nClipper START/Youth (6-18)/ Senior (65+)/Disabled (RTC)\nSingle Ride | $1.00\nBART-to-Bus Transfer | $0.50\nLearn about Clipper START Get 50% off all Bay Area transit!\nCash\nAll Routes | $2.50\nYouth (6-18)\nSenior (65+)/Disabled\nAll Routes | $1.25\nSave by using Clipper! Click here to learn more\n* Exact cash fare only, drivers do not carry change\nClipper Passes Passes are only available on Clipper\nAdult (19-64)/Youth (6-18 yrs)/ Clipper START\nDay Pass* | $3.75\n31-Day Pass | $60\nSenior (65+)/Disabled (RTC)\nDay Pass* | $1.75\n* Daily fares are automatically capped at this amount\nChildren under 6 ride free when accompanied by an adult.\nTransfers\nBus-to-bus transfers are only available when using Clipper. One free transfer is automatically applied within 2 hours of initial boarding.\nClick here for more information on transfers.\nCash Fares\nFares may be paid with cash. Exact change only. Drivers do not carry change.\nYouth Fares\nYouth (ages 6-18) can get a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares when using a Youth Clipper card. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com .", "BART-to-bus transfer discounts are only available when using Clipper. BART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the card’s stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.", "Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00", "Clipper"]}
{"id": "edge-074", "question": "Does my 4-year-old ride free on County Connection?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, your 4-year-old rides free on County Connection. [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-fare-types-prices"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 4-year-old ride free on County Connection?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, your 4-year-old rides free on County Connection. [cccta-fare-types-prices]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "County Connection accepts transfers as full fare payment at designated bus stops which are served by neighboring transit systems. Free transfers from SolTrans, Tri Delta, WestCat and Wheels are only available using Clipper. ACE train passengers can ride Route 92X free with pre-purchased ACE train tickets. Amtrak train passengers can ride any County Connection bus for free using their digital transfer which has a date the transfers are good through or paper Amtrak ticket valid for the date printed and the following day.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card.", "The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses."], "expected_facts": ["free", "under 6"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-074", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-087", "question": "I'm 61 and live in Manteca. Am I old enough for San Joaquin RTD's senior discount fare?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo, you are not old enough for San Joaquin RTD's senior discount fare under the published criteria. San Joaquin RTD's senior discount applies to residents of Manteca who are **62 years or older** [doc:sjrtd-dfc]. Since you are 61, you do not meet that threshold.\n\nIf you believe you may qualify for a discount fare through another eligibility path — such as a disability, veteran status, or Medicare card — San Joaquin RTD's Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin) program may cover those conditions [doc:sjrtd-dfc]. To explore other options or to apply for a Discount Fare Card, contact Access San Joaquin directly through San Joaquin RTD's website at sanjoaquinrtd.com/access-sj/dfc/ or reach out to the agency.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-dfc"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 61 and live in Manteca. Am I old enough for San Joaquin RTD's senior discount fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No, you are not old enough for San Joaquin RTD's senior discount fare under the published criteria. San Joaquin RTD's senior discount applies to residents of Manteca who are **62 years or older** [sjrtd-dfc]. Since you are 61, you do not meet that threshold.</p><p>If you believe you may qualify for a discount fare through another eligibility path — such as a disability, veteran status, or Medicare card — San Joaquin RTD's Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin) program may cover those conditions [sjrtd-dfc]. To explore other options or to apply for a Discount Fare Card, contact Access San Joaquin directly through San Joaquin RTD's website at sanjoaquinrtd.com/access-sj/dfc/ or reach out to the agency.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Access San Joaquin staff will carefully review each application to ensure that only qualified persons are approved. Upon completion of review, a letter of certification or denial will be mailed. If your application for the DFC was denied, you have the right to appeal this decision.", "Fares | First 5 miles | Additional miles\nFull – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. | $4.00 | $0.50 per mile\nDiscount – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $3.00 | $0.50 per mile", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience."], "expected_facts": ["62"]}
{"id": "edge-088", "question": "What age does San Joaquin RTD's senior discount start? Does it matter where I live?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nSan Joaquin RTD's senior discount does depend on where you live in San Joaquin County. The published criteria are:\n\n- **Residing in Manteca or Lathrop**: 62 years or older [doc:sjrtd-dfc]\n- **Residing in Tracy or Escalon**: 65 years or older [doc:sjrtd-dfc]\n- **Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County**: 60 years or older [doc:sjrtd-dfc]\n\nOnce you meet the age threshold for your city, seniors with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC) receive the discount fare of $0.75 for a 1-ride ticket, $2.00 for a 1-day pass, or $30.00 for a 31-day pass [doc:sjrtd-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify the application process for seniors to obtain a DFC. To apply, contact San Joaquin RTD directly through their Access San Joaquin program at https://sanjoaquinrtd.com/access-sj/dfc/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-dfc", "sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What age does San Joaquin RTD's senior discount start? Does it matter where I live?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>San Joaquin RTD's senior discount does depend on where you live in San Joaquin County. The published criteria are:</p><p>- **Residing in Manteca or Lathrop**: 62 years or older [sjrtd-dfc]</p><p>- **Residing in Tracy or Escalon**: 65 years or older [sjrtd-dfc]</p><p>- **Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County**: 60 years or older [sjrtd-dfc]</p><p>Once you meet the age threshold for your city, seniors with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC) receive the discount fare of $0.75 for a 1-ride ticket, $2.00 for a 1-day pass, or $30.00 for a 31-day pass [sjrtd-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process for seniors to obtain a DFC. To apply, contact San Joaquin RTD directly through their Access San Joaquin program at https://sanjoaquinrtd.com/access-sj/dfc/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin)</li><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "Access San Joaquin staff will carefully review each application to ensure that only qualified persons are approved. Upon completion of review, a letter of certification or denial will be mailed. If your application for the DFC was denied, you have the right to appeal this decision."], "expected_facts": ["60", "62", "65"]}
{"id": "edge-089", "question": "I'm 45 but I have a Medicare card. Do I pay full fare on RTD buses?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo, you do not pay full fare. Even though you are 45, you may ride for a discount fare because you have a Medicare card.\n\nThe published policy states: \"Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).\" [doc:sjrtd-dfc]\n\nOn San Joaquin RTD's Express, Local, and Hopper buses, the discount 1-Ride fare is $0.75 (compared to the full fare of $1.50 for ages 18 to 59), the 1-Day discount is $2.00 (compared to full $4.00), and the 31-Day discount is $30.00 (compared to full $65.00). [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n\nYou will need to display your Medicare Card and a photo ID when you board to receive the discount fare. [doc:sjrtd-dfc]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-dfc", "sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 45 but I have a Medicare card. Do I pay full fare on RTD buses?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No, you do not pay full fare. Even though you are 45, you may ride for a discount fare because you have a Medicare card.</p><p>The published policy states: \"Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).\" [sjrtd-dfc]</p><p>On San Joaquin RTD's Express, Local, and Hopper buses, the discount 1-Ride fare is $0.75 (compared to the full fare of $1.50 for ages 18 to 59), the 1-Day discount is $2.00 (compared to full $4.00), and the 31-Day discount is $30.00 (compared to full $65.00). [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>You will need to display your Medicare Card and a photo ID when you board to receive the discount fare. [sjrtd-dfc]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin)</li><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "Fares | First 5 miles | Additional miles\nFull – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. | $4.00 | $0.50 per mile\nDiscount – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $3.00 | $0.50 per mile", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "Available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store, the Vamos Mobility App gives you the freedom to buy bus tickets right on your smartphone without having to wait in line or visit a Fare Vending Machine or Customer Service Center. This app only accepts credit/debit cards for payment.\nOn the Bus\nAll Express, Local, and Hopper buses has a farebox as you board through the front doors. Farebox accepts cash and coins. Please have exact amount ready. The farebox does not issue change.", "Fares | 1-Ride | Online Only\nFull – One-way fare. Reservations required in advance. For those individuals who are eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act ( ADA ) for paratransit services . *10 physical passes will be mail to your home address. | $3.00", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law)."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "$0.75"]}
{"id": "edge-090", "question": "I'm boarding an RTD bus in Stockton with my four kids, all age 3. What do I pay for them?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nFor your four children ages 3, San Joaquin RTD's published policy states: \"Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50.\" [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n\nThis means three of your children ride free, and the fourth child costs $1.50 for a single ride.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm boarding an RTD bus in Stockton with my four kids, all age 3. What do I pay for them?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>For your four children ages 3, San Joaquin RTD's published policy states: \"Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50.\" [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>This means three of your children ride free, and the fourth child costs $1.50 for a single ride.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "Fares | One-Way | Monthly\nRoute 150 – Stockton to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $191\nRoute 150 – Lathrop to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $175\nRoute 150 – Tracy to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $158\nRoute 163 – Stockton to Sacramento | $7.00 | $176\nRoute 163 – Lodi to Sacramento | $7.00 | $167", "Fares | First 5 miles | Additional miles\nFull – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. | $4.00 | $0.50 per mile\nDiscount – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $3.00 | $0.50 per mile", "Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified)."], "expected_facts": ["3", "$1.50"]}
{"id": "edge-091", "question": "I'm a veteran. How do I get the discount fare on San Joaquin RTD, and what proof do I need?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Proof of veteran status:**\nYou may qualify as a veteran if you have a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran's Discount Card. No medical certification is needed if you present one of these documents along with a valid photo ID [doc:sjrtd-dfc].\n\n**Discount fare:**\nWith a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC), veterans receive:\n- Express, Local, and Hopper buses: $0.75 per 1-ride, $2.00 per 1-day pass, or $30.00 per 31-day pass [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n- Van Go! service: $3.00 base fare for the first 5 miles, then $0.50 per additional mile [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n\n**How to apply:**\nThe published policy does not specify the application process or location for veterans applying for a Discount Fare Card. To start, contact San Joaquin RTD (Access San Joaquin) directly at the agency's published contact channels or through 511.org. Once you submit a completed application with your DD Form 214 or Veteran's Discount Card and photo ID, Access San Joaquin will respond within 21 working days and mail you a letter of certification or denial along with an identification card if certified [doc:sjrtd-dfc].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-dfc", "sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm a veteran. How do I get the discount fare on San Joaquin RTD, and what proof do I need?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Proof of veteran status:**</p><p>You may qualify as a veteran if you have a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran's Discount Card. No medical certification is needed if you present one of these documents along with a valid photo ID [sjrtd-dfc].</p><p>**Discount fare:**</p><p>With a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC), veterans receive:</p><p>- Express, Local, and Hopper buses: $0.75 per 1-ride, $2.00 per 1-day pass, or $30.00 per 31-day pass [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>- Van Go! service: $3.00 base fare for the first 5 miles, then $0.50 per additional mile [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>**How to apply:**</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process or location for veterans applying for a Discount Fare Card. To start, contact San Joaquin RTD (Access San Joaquin) directly at the agency's published contact channels or through 511.org. Once you submit a completed application with your DD Form 214 or Veteran's Discount Card and photo ID, Access San Joaquin will respond within 21 working days and mail you a letter of certification or denial along with an identification card if certified [sjrtd-dfc].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin)</li><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Access San Joaquin staff will carefully review each application to ensure that only qualified persons are approved. Upon completion of review, a letter of certification or denial will be mailed. If your application for the DFC was denied, you have the right to appeal this decision.", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "Fares | First 5 miles | Additional miles\nFull – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. | $4.00 | $0.50 per mile\nDiscount – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $3.00 | $0.50 per mile"], "expected_facts": ["DD Form 214"]}
