d Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "Fares | One-Way | Monthly\nRoute 150 – Stockton to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $191\nRoute 150 – Lathrop to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $175\nRoute 150 – Tracy to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $158\nRoute 163 – Stockton to Sacramento | $7.00 | $176\nRoute 163 – Lodi to Sacramento | $7.00 | $167", "Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "Fares | 1-Ride | Online Only\nFull – One-way fare. Reservations required in advance. For those individuals who are eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act ( ADA ) for paratransit services . *10 physical passes will be mail to your home address. | $3.00"], "expected_facts": ["free", "student ID"]}
{"id": "edge-093", "question": "I have an RTD Discount Fare Card. Does it make my paratransit rides cheaper too?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe published policy does not specify whether the Discount Fare Card (DFC) applies to paratransit fares. \n\nThe passages state that the DFC provides discounts on Express, Local, and Hopper buses, and on Van Go! service [doc:sjrtd-fares]. Paratransit has its own separate fare structure for individuals eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), with a one-way fare of $3.00 available online only [doc:sjrtd-fares]. However, the passages do not state whether the DFC discount applies to paratransit rides or whether paratransit riders can use their DFC there.\n\nTo confirm whether your Discount Fare Card works for paratransit, contact San Joaquin RTD directly through their website at sanjoaquinrtd.com or their Customer Service Center.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have an RTD Discount Fare Card. Does it make my paratransit rides cheaper too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The published policy does not specify whether the Discount Fare Card (DFC) applies to paratransit fares.</p><p>The passages state that the DFC provides discounts on Express, Local, and Hopper buses, and on Van Go! service [sjrtd-fares]. Paratransit has its own separate fare structure for individuals eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), with a one-way fare of $3.00 available online only [sjrtd-fares]. However, the passages do not state whether the DFC discount applies to paratransit rides or whether paratransit riders can use their DFC there.</p><p>To confirm whether your Discount Fare Card works for paratransit, contact San Joaquin RTD directly through their website at sanjoaquinrtd.com or their Customer Service Center.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "To be certified for the Discount Fare Card (DFC), passengers must complete the following steps. Once the steps have been completed and all information is submitted, you will be notified within 21 days whether or not you have been certified for a DFC.", "Available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store, the Vamos Mobility App gives you the freedom to buy bus tickets right on your smartphone without having to wait in line or visit a Fare Vending Machine or Customer Service Center. This app only accepts credit/debit cards for payment.\nOn the Bus\nAll Express, Local, and Hopper buses has a farebox as you board through the front doors. Farebox accepts cash and coins. Please have exact amount ready. The farebox does not issue change.", "Fares | 1-Ride | Online Only\nFull – One-way fare. Reservations required in advance. For those individuals who are eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act ( ADA ) for paratransit services . *10 physical passes will be mail to your home address. | $3.00", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult."], "expected_facts": ["$3.00"]}
{"id": "edge-actransit-001", "question": "I pay per ride with Clipper on AC Transit local buses. If I keep riding all day, what's the most I'll be charged?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nAC Transit applies fare maximums automatically when your spending reaches the cost of a pass within a calendar period. For local buses paid with Clipper, the Day Pass is the relevant maximum: once your per-ride fares reach the Day Pass cost during a single day, you will be upgraded to a Day Pass automatically, which gives you unlimited local rides from the time it is applied until 3:00 am the next day [doc:actransit-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify the exact dollar amount of the Day Pass price. For the current fare, contact AC Transit directly or visit their fares page.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I pay per ride with Clipper on AC Transit local buses. If I keep riding all day, what's the most I'll be charged?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>AC Transit applies fare maximums automatically when your spending reaches the cost of a pass within a calendar period. For local buses paid with Clipper, the Day Pass is the relevant maximum: once your per-ride fares reach the Day Pass cost during a single day, you will be upgraded to a Day Pass automatically, which gives you unlimited local rides from the time it is applied until 3:00 am the next day [actransit-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify the exact dollar amount of the Day Pass price. For the current fare, contact AC Transit directly or visit their fares page.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["AC Transit offers fare maximums, which means certain passes are automatically applied once spending reaches a certain amount during a calendar period. Fare maximums by week and month are only available with Next-Generation Clipper and contactless payments.\nLocal Day Pass\nA Day Pass is available as a prepaid or fare maximum option for unlimited local rides from the time applied or issued until 3:00 am the next day.\nLocal Weekly Pass\nUnlimited local rides for a week. Applied automatically for the balance of a Sunday–Saturday week.\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local and Transbay trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.", "The AC Transit EasyPass program is an annual group transit benefit for eligible organizations. This program is offered to employers, residential communities, and colleges or universities whose user group will consist of 100 or more people or residential units. Participation in this program provides access to unlimited bus rides all year long by paying an annual fee for everyone in your group. Sponsoring a pool of participants, keeps individual pass costs low, giving every participant access to unlimited bus service whenever they want. While EasyPass programs are invoiced annually, they cost as little as $5-$18 a month per participant depending on program, location and group size. Visit our EasyPass page to learn more.", "The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "Available on Clipper as a prepaid fare, this pass can only be purchased by Senior/Disabled riders and is valid at the beginning of the month. Riders who purchase this pass can take unlimited local rides. Riders can board Transbay routes by paying the Local to Transbay Upgrade fare. Eligible riders paying with AC Transit’s mobile payment platform don’t need to pre-pay for a Monthly Pass. Starting at the beginning of the month, once their fare purchases reach the cost of the pass, they will be upgraded to a Monthly Pass automatically.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements."], "expected_facts": ["$ 6.00", "fare maximum"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-actransit-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "edge-actransit-002", "question": "Can I get AC Transit's weekly pass if I always pay my fare in cash on the bus?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. AC Transit's Local Weekly Pass is not available when paying with cash. The published policy states that \"Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\" [doc:actransit-fares]\n\nIf you pay your fare in cash on the bus, you can use the Local Day Pass instead, which is available as a prepaid option for $6.50 and provides unlimited local rides from the time issued until 3:00 am the next day. [doc:actransit-fares]\n\nTo access the weekly pass, you would need to switch to paying with either a Next-Generation Clipper card or a contactless bank card. Contact AC Transit directly for details on how to obtain a Clipper card.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get AC Transit's weekly pass if I always pay my fare in cash on the bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. AC Transit's Local Weekly Pass is not available when paying with cash. The published policy states that \"Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\" [actransit-fares]</p><p>If you pay your fare in cash on the bus, you can use the Local Day Pass instead, which is available as a prepaid option for $6.50 and provides unlimited local rides from the time issued until 3:00 am the next day. [actransit-fares]</p><p>To access the weekly pass, you would need to switch to paying with either a Next-Generation Clipper card or a contactless bank card. Contact AC Transit directly for details on how to obtain a Clipper card.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Day, Weekly, and Monthly passes will be automatically applied when spending reaches the cost of these passes within the calendar period. Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\nFare Compliance\nTo maintain a fair and equitable bus system, fare payment is required to ride AC Transit. Riders who fail to pay their fare may receive a citation. Learn about fare compliance .\nRiders who need assistance paying their fare should see if they qualify for discounts. Learn about Discount Fares and programs .\nLocal Bus Transfers\nUnlimited free local transfers within two hours of your first ride when using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments.", "Fares Effective July 1, 2026\nAdult (Ages 19-64)\nDiscounted\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper or contactless bank card | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $2.75 | $3.00\nTransbay Single Ride | $6.50 | $6.50\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $3.75 | $3.50\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $6.00 | $6.50\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $27.50 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $99.00 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $234.00 | --\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper Card/Clipper Mobile | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $1.37 | $1.50\nTransbay Single Ride | $3.25 | $3.25\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $1.88 | $1.75\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $3.00 | $3.25\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $13.75 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $41.50 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | -- | --", "Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com .", "AC Transit offers many ways to pay for your ride so catching the bus is easier than ever. Choose to pay with cash, Clipper, or contactless payment. Learn about fares, how to get a Clipper card, paying with contactless payment, and more.", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements.", "When transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, riders receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3.\nNOTE: Riders must use the same card/account for all trips.\nLearn more at Transfers .\nLocal Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Local bus line .\nTransbay Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Transbay bus line over a major Bay Area bridge.\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade\nRiders with a local ticket, transfer, or pass pay the difference between local fare and Transbay fare when boarding a Transbay bus."], "expected_facts": ["Next-Generation Clipper", "$ 27.50"]}
{"id": "edge-actransit-003", "question": "If I ride AC Transit and then transfer to another transit agency using the same Clipper card, do I get a discount?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nWhen transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, you receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3 [doc:actransit-fares]. The published policy requires that you use the same card/account for all trips [doc:actransit-fares].\n\nThis is a credit toward your fare on the other agency, not a discount in the sense of a reduced rate. The amount of the credit depends on what you paid on AC Transit, capped at $3. Whether this credit fully covers, partially covers, or exceeds the fare on the other agency depends on that agency's own fares—the AC Transit policy does not specify.\n\nTo confirm how this credit applies to your specific transfer destination and whether it meets your needs, contact AC Transit or the receiving agency directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I ride AC Transit and then transfer to another transit agency using the same Clipper card, do I get a discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>When transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, you receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3 [actransit-fares]. The published policy requires that you use the same card/account for all trips [actransit-fares].</p><p>This is a credit toward your fare on the other agency, not a discount in the sense of a reduced rate. The amount of the credit depends on what you paid on AC Transit, capped at $3. Whether this credit fully covers, partially covers, or exceeds the fare on the other agency depends on that agency's own fares—the AC Transit policy does not specify.</p><p>To confirm how this credit applies to your specific transfer destination and whether it meets your needs, contact AC Transit or the receiving agency directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Day, Weekly, and Monthly passes will be automatically applied when spending reaches the cost of these passes within the calendar period. Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\nFare Compliance\nTo maintain a fair and equitable bus system, fare payment is required to ride AC Transit. Riders who fail to pay their fare may receive a citation. Learn about fare compliance .\nRiders who need assistance paying their fare should see if they qualify for discounts. Learn about Discount Fares and programs .\nLocal Bus Transfers\nUnlimited free local transfers within two hours of your first ride when using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments.", "When transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, riders receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3.\nNOTE: Riders must use the same card/account for all trips.\nLearn more at Transfers .\nLocal Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Local bus line .\nTransbay Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Transbay bus line over a major Bay Area bridge.\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade\nRiders with a local ticket, transfer, or pass pay the difference between local fare and Transbay fare when boarding a Transbay bus.", "Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com .", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "Youth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive discount fares and passes to ride AC Transit. Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit’s mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. Other discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements. Read on to find out if you are eligible, and how to purchase discount fares.\nYouth\nRiders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares. The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card . Children under 5 ride free.", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements."], "expected_facts": ["credit equal in value", "same card"], "forbidden_terms": ["$2.85"]}
{"id": "edge-actransit-004", "question": "I'm 70 years old. What senior discount do I get on AC Transit, and how do I pay for it?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Your senior discount:**\nRiders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares, which provide 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit. [doc:actransit-discounts]\n\n**How to pay:**\nThe best way to pay is by applying for a Senior Clipper card. [doc:actransit-discounts] When paying with cash, you should be prepared to show identification. [doc:actransit-discounts]\n\nYou can also purchase discount fares using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper, through AC Transit's mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. [doc:actransit-discounts]\n\n**To apply for a Senior Clipper card:**\nThe published policy does not specify the application process, location, or any card cost. Please contact AC Transit directly to apply for your Senior Clipper card and confirm current application details.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 70 years old. What senior discount do I get on AC Transit, and how do I pay for it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Your senior discount:**</p><p>Riders aged 65 and older are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares, which provide 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit. [actransit-discounts]</p><p>**How to pay:**</p><p>The best way to pay is by applying for a Senior Clipper card. [actransit-discounts] When paying with cash, you should be prepared to show identification. [actransit-discounts]</p><p>You can also purchase discount fares using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper, through AC Transit's mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. [actransit-discounts]</p><p>**To apply for a Senior Clipper card:**</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process, location, or any card cost. Please contact AC Transit directly to apply for your Senior Clipper card and confirm current application details.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Youth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive discount fares and passes to ride AC Transit. Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit’s mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. Other discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements. Read on to find out if you are eligible, and how to purchase discount fares.\nYouth\nRiders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares. The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card . Children under 5 ride free.", "The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "AC Transit offers many ways to pay for your ride so catching the bus is easier than ever. Choose to pay with cash, Clipper, or contactless payment. Learn about fares, how to get a Clipper card, paying with contactless payment, and more.", "Day, Weekly, and Monthly passes will be automatically applied when spending reaches the cost of these passes within the calendar period. Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\nFare Compliance\nTo maintain a fair and equitable bus system, fare payment is required to ride AC Transit. Riders who fail to pay their fare may receive a citation. Learn about fare compliance .\nRiders who need assistance paying their fare should see if they qualify for discounts. Learn about Discount Fares and programs .\nLocal Bus Transfers\nUnlimited free local transfers within two hours of your first ride when using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments.", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements.", "Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com ."], "expected_facts": ["65", "50%"]}
{"id": "edge-actransit-005", "question": "Do my kids ride free on AC Transit? One is 4 and one is 12.", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nYour 4-year-old rides free. [doc:actransit-discounts] states \"Children under 5 ride free.\"\n\nYour 12-year-old is eligible for Youth fares. [doc:actransit-discounts] states \"Riders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares.\" The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card. [doc:actransit-discounts] notes that \"Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit's mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center.\"\n\nThe published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or office hours for the Youth Clipper card. Contact AC Transit directly for details on how to apply.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do my kids ride free on AC Transit? One is 4 and one is 12.</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Your 4-year-old rides free. [actransit-discounts] states \"Children under 5 ride free.\"</p><p>Your 12-year-old is eligible for Youth fares. [actransit-discounts] states \"Riders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares.\" The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card. [actransit-discounts] notes that \"Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit's mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center.\"</p><p>The published policy does not specify the application process, cost, or office hours for the Youth Clipper card. Contact AC Transit directly for details on how to apply.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Youth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive discount fares and passes to ride AC Transit. Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit’s mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. Other discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements. Read on to find out if you are eligible, and how to purchase discount fares.\nYouth\nRiders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares. The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card . Children under 5 ride free.", "The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements.", "AC Transit offers fare maximums, which means certain passes are automatically applied once spending reaches a certain amount during a calendar period. Fare maximums by week and month are only available with Next-Generation Clipper and contactless payments.\nLocal Day Pass\nA Day Pass is available as a prepaid or fare maximum option for unlimited local rides from the time applied or issued until 3:00 am the next day.\nLocal Weekly Pass\nUnlimited local rides for a week. Applied automatically for the balance of a Sunday–Saturday week.\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local and Transbay trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "The AC Transit EasyPass program is an annual group transit benefit for eligible organizations. This program is offered to employers, residential communities, and colleges or universities whose user group will consist of 100 or more people or residential units. Participation in this program provides access to unlimited bus rides all year long by paying an annual fee for everyone in your group. Sponsoring a pool of participants, keeps individual pass costs low, giving every participant access to unlimited bus service whenever they want. While EasyPass programs are invoiced annually, they cost as little as $5-$18 a month per participant depending on program, location and group size. Visit our EasyPass page to learn more."], "expected_facts": ["under 5", "Youth"]}
{"id": "edge-actransit-006", "question": "I have a disability. What discount does AC Transit give me, and what card should I get?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nAC Transit offers two discount paths for riders with disabilities:\n\n**Clipper Access Card:** The Clipper Access Card is available to persons with qualifying disabilities. You may use the card as proof of eligibility to receive discount fares on fixed-route, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. [doc:actransit-discounts] To learn more and to apply for a Clipper Access card, visit AC Transit's Clipper Access Card web page. [doc:actransit-discounts]\n\n**Senior/Disabled fares with cash:** Riders who are disabled are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares. When paying with cash, you should be prepared to show identification. [doc:actransit-discounts]\n\nThe published policy does not specify what documentation proves a disability or the exact discount amount for either path. AC Transit makes the final eligibility determination. To apply for the Clipper Access Card or to learn which identification is accepted for cash fares, contact AC Transit through their Clipper Access Card web page or their general contact channels. [doc:actransit-discounts]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a disability. What discount does AC Transit give me, and what card should I get?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>AC Transit offers two discount paths for riders with disabilities:</p><p>**Clipper Access Card:** The Clipper Access Card is available to persons with qualifying disabilities. You may use the card as proof of eligibility to receive discount fares on fixed-route, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. [actransit-discounts] To learn more and to apply for a Clipper Access card, visit AC Transit's Clipper Access Card web page. [actransit-discounts]</p><p>**Senior/Disabled fares with cash:** Riders who are disabled are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares. When paying with cash, you should be prepared to show identification. [actransit-discounts]</p><p>The published policy does not specify what documentation proves a disability or the exact discount amount for either path. AC Transit makes the final eligibility determination. To apply for the Clipper Access Card or to learn which identification is accepted for cash fares, contact AC Transit through their Clipper Access Card web page or their general contact channels. [actransit-discounts]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Discounts (youth, senior, disabled, Clipper START, Student Transit Pass)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "Day, Weekly, and Monthly passes will be automatically applied when spending reaches the cost of these passes within the calendar period. Weekly and Monthly are only available through Next-Generation Clipper or a contactless bank card.\nFare Compliance\nTo maintain a fair and equitable bus system, fare payment is required to ride AC Transit. Riders who fail to pay their fare may receive a citation. Learn about fare compliance .\nRiders who need assistance paying their fare should see if they qualify for discounts. Learn about Discount Fares and programs .\nLocal Bus Transfers\nUnlimited free local transfers within two hours of your first ride when using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments.", "Riders aged 65 and older or riders who are disabled are eligible for Senior/Disabled fares. When paying with cash riders should be prepared to show identification. The best way to pay is by applying for a Senior Clipper card or a Clipper Access card for disabled riders . Disabled/Senior Monthly Pass and Paratransit Ticket Order Form (Approved Vendors Only)", "The Clipper Access Card (formerly RTC) is available to persons with qualifying disabilities. You may use the card as proof of eligibility to receive discount fares on fixed-route, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. The Clipper Access Program does not apply to Paratransit services. To learn more and to apply for a Clipper Access card, visit our Clipper Access Card web page.", "Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com ."], "expected_facts": ["50 %", "Clipper Access"]}
{"id": "edge-075", "question": "I paid cash on a local WestCAT bus. Can I transfer to the LYNX for free?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. The published policy does not state that transfers from WestCAT Local Fixed Route buses to WestCAT LYNX are free. \n\nWhen transferring from a WestCAT Local Fixed Route bus to WestCAT LYNX, the fare is $3.25 for adult riders paying cash [doc:westcat-fares-all]. \n\nThe passages do confirm that WestCAT transfers are valid for up to one hour to board another route [doc:westcat-transfers], but the transfer itself does not eliminate the LYNX fare for cash-paying riders in your age category.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all", "westcat-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I paid cash on a local WestCAT bus. Can I transfer to the LYNX for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. The published policy does not state that transfers from WestCAT Local Fixed Route buses to WestCAT LYNX are free.</p><p>When transferring from a WestCAT Local Fixed Route bus to WestCAT LYNX, the fare is $3.25 for adult riders paying cash [westcat-fares-all].</p><p>The passages do confirm that WestCAT transfers are valid for up to one hour to board another route [westcat-transfers], but the transfer itself does not eliminate the LYNX fare for cash-paying riders in your age category.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li><li>WestCAT: Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.)."], "expected_facts": ["$3.25"]}
{"id": "edge-076", "question": "Can I use a WestCAT paper transfer to ride back home on the same route?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. WestCAT transfers are \"not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc)\" and \"not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\" [doc:westcat-transfers] A paper transfer is valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete your one-way trip, but you cannot use it to ride back home on the same route you originally boarded. [doc:westcat-transfers]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use a WestCAT paper transfer to ride back home on the same route?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. WestCAT transfers are \"not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc)\" and \"not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\" [westcat-transfers] A paper transfer is valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete your one-way trip, but you cannot use it to ride back home on the same route you originally boarded. [westcat-transfers]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.)."], "expected_facts": ["one hour", "not valid"]}
{"id": "edge-077", "question": "Can WestCAT certify me for the Clipper Access (RTC) discount card?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. WestCAT does not certify Clipper Access cards. The published policy states: \"Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities.\" [doc:westcat-fares-all]\n\nWestCAT accepts Clipper Access cards for reduced fares on its buses, but the agency itself does not issue them. To apply for Clipper Access, you will need to contact Clipper directly. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is available at the Clipper Access link referenced in WestCAT's policy [doc:westcat-fares-all], or you can reach out to Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883 [doc:westcat-clipper] for guidance on the application process.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-clipper", "westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can WestCAT certify me for the Clipper Access (RTC) discount card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. WestCAT does not certify Clipper Access cards. The published policy states: \"Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities.\" [westcat-fares-all]</p><p>WestCAT accepts Clipper Access cards for reduced fares on its buses, but the agency itself does not issue them. To apply for Clipper Access, you will need to contact Clipper directly. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is available at the Clipper Access link referenced in WestCAT's policy [westcat-fares-all], or you can reach out to Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883 [westcat-clipper] for guidance on the application process.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "Clipper Card Costs Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load. Physical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online. Youth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free."], "expected_facts": ["honor", "certif"]}
{"id": "edge-078", "question": "If I pay with Clipper cash value all day on WestCAT, do I hit a day-pass cap? Does the LYNX count toward it?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, WestCAT publishes a day-pass cap for Clipper cash value, but LYNX does not count toward it.\n\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. After you reach that threshold, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. However, free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass. [doc:westcat-fares-all]\n\nThis means LYNX fares you pay do not count toward the $3.75 threshold, and LYNX rides you take after hitting the cap are not covered by the day pass.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I pay with Clipper cash value all day on WestCAT, do I hit a day-pass cap? Does the LYNX count toward it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, WestCAT publishes a day-pass cap for Clipper cash value, but LYNX does not count toward it.</p><p>If you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. After you reach that threshold, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. However, free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass. [westcat-fares-all]</p><p>This means LYNX fares you pay do not count toward the $3.75 threshold, and LYNX rides you take after hitting the cap are not covered by the day pass.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.)."], "expected_facts": ["$3.75", "LYNX Transbay service do not apply"]}
{"id": "edge-079", "question": "I have a Medi-Cal card and a photo ID. Is that enough for WestCAT's reduced fare?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. A Medi-Cal card alone is not sufficient for WestCAT's reduced fare. The published policy states that to receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: \"State issued ID (senior fare),\" \"Clipper Access (RTC),\" \"Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal),\" \"Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard,\" or \"Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\" [doc:westcat-fares-all].\n\nYour Medi-Cal card does not match any of these options. The policy specifically requires a **Medicare card** (not Medi-Cal) paired with a photo ID for one reduced-fare path. Since you have a Medi-Cal card instead, the published policy does not specify whether that qualifies you for a reduced fare.\n\nTo find out whether you may qualify for a reduced fare with your Medi-Cal card, contact WestCAT at (510) 724-7993 or visit the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday–Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., 601 Walter Avenue, Pinole, CA 94564) [doc:westcat-clipper].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-clipper", "westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medi-Cal card and a photo ID. Is that enough for WestCAT's reduced fare?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. A Medi-Cal card alone is not sufficient for WestCAT's reduced fare. The published policy states that to receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: \"State issued ID (senior fare),\" \"Clipper Access (RTC),\" \"Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal),\" \"Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard,\" or \"Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\" [westcat-fares-all].</p><p>Your Medi-Cal card does not match any of these options. The policy specifically requires a **Medicare card** (not Medi-Cal) paired with a photo ID for one reduced-fare path. Since you have a Medi-Cal card instead, the published policy does not specify whether that qualifies you for a reduced fare.</p><p>To find out whether you may qualify for a reduced fare with your Medi-Cal card, contact WestCAT at (510) 724-7993 or visit the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday–Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., 601 Walter Avenue, Pinole, CA 94564) [westcat-clipper].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "Medi-Cal"]}
{"id": "edge-080", "question": "How much is ADA paratransit on WestCAT? Is a regional trip the same price as a local one?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nWestCAT's ADA Paratransit fares differ between local and regional trips:\n\n- **ADA Paratransit Local**: $1.25 (one-way cash fare) [doc:westcat-fares-all]\n- **ADA Paratransit Regional**: $3.00 (one-way cash fare) [doc:westcat-fares-all]\n\nRegional trips cost more than local ones. The passages also show pass options: a 10-Ride Sheet for Local costs $10.00, and a 10-Ride Sheet for Regional costs $25.00 [doc:westcat-fares-all].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is ADA paratransit on WestCAT? Is a regional trip the same price as a local one?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>WestCAT's ADA Paratransit fares differ between local and regional trips:</p><p>- **ADA Paratransit Local**: $1.25 (one-way cash fare) [westcat-fares-all]</p><p>- **ADA Paratransit Regional**: $3.00 (one-way cash fare) [westcat-fares-all]</p><p>Regional trips cost more than local ones. The passages also show pass options: a 10-Ride Sheet for Local costs $10.00, and a 10-Ride Sheet for Regional costs $25.00 [westcat-fares-all].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.)."], "expected_facts": ["$1.25", "$3.00"]}
{"id": "edge-081", "question": "My 15-year-old has a Youth Clipper card. What balance does she need on it to board a WestCAT bus?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Your 15-year-old with a Youth Clipper card must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded, to board a WestCAT bus. [doc:westcat-clipper]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-clipper"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 15-year-old has a Youth Clipper card. What balance does she need on it to board a WestCAT bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Your 15-year-old with a Youth Clipper card must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded, to board a WestCAT bus. [westcat-clipper]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Checking Balances Balances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime: In real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards). Through Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards. Call Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883. In-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "In real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nIn-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares."], "expected_facts": ["$1.75"]}
{"id": "edge-094", "question": "I'm 81. Do I still pay the senior fare on RTA buses?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages, at 81 years old you ride for free on RTA buses.\n\nSLO RTA publishes two free-fare options for seniors in your age group:\n\n**VIP Seniors (80+):** \"VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes.\" [doc:slorta-discounts] You can also use the Tap2Ride contactless payment system: \"Fare Free for 80 years old and older\" when you register your contactless payment card at https://benefits.calitp.org/ [doc:slorta-contactless]\n\n**To obtain a VIP Pass (if you prefer an in-person ID card):** You may obtain a special photo ID card in person or fill out an application in advance with a photograph. VIP Passes must be obtained in person at:\n\nSLO Regional Rideshare  \n1114 Marsh Street  \nSan Luis Obispo, CA 93401\n\n[doc:slorta-passes]\n\nThe published policy does not specify whether there is a cost for the VIP Pass or application, or the office hours for obtaining it in person. Contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. to confirm.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-contactless", "slorta-discounts", "slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 81. Do I still pay the senior fare on RTA buses?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages, at 81 years old you ride for free on RTA buses.</p><p>SLO RTA publishes two free-fare options for seniors in your age group:</p><p>**VIP Seniors (80+):** \"VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes.\" [slorta-discounts] You can also use the Tap2Ride contactless payment system: \"Fare Free for 80 years old and older\" when you register your contactless payment card at https://benefits.calitp.org/ [slorta-contactless]</p><p>**To obtain a VIP Pass (if you prefer an in-person ID card):** You may obtain a special photo ID card in person or fill out an application in advance with a photograph. VIP Passes must be obtained in person at:</p><p>SLO Regional Rideshare</p><p>1114 Marsh Street</p><p>San Luis Obispo, CA 93401</p><p>[slorta-passes]</p><p>The published policy does not specify whether there is a cost for the VIP Pass or application, or the office hours for obtaining it in person. Contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. to confirm.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Can I still ride RTA if I do not have a contactless-enabled card or a payment-enabled device? Yes. You can still pay for your fare using cash, monthly/weekly/daily passes, or with passes purchased on the Token Transit app.\nCan I still use cash? Yes. You can still use cash when riding the bus. Just pay your fare using the farebox when you board the bus.", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares.", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "Riders can also link their contactless payment card with the California Integrated Travel Project (Cal ITP) to verify eligibility to pay discount fares. Once a payment card is registered , a rider can use that card to pay discount fares on the Tap2Ride payment system.\nTo start the card registration process, visit the Cal ITP benefits page here: https:// benefits.calitp.org / and select RTA for your agency."], "expected_facts": ["80", "free"]}
{"id": "edge-095", "question": "I'm 64. Do I get the senior discount on SLO RTA?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. The published criteria for SLO RTA's senior discounts are:\n\n- **Half-priced fares** for Seniors aged 65–79 [doc:slorta-discounts]\n- **Free fares** for VIP Seniors aged 80 or older [doc:slorta-discounts]\n\nAt age 64, you do not meet either threshold. To confirm whether SLO RTA offers any other discount programs you may qualify for, contact SLO Regional Rideshare at 1114 Marsh Street, San Luis Obispo, CA 93401, or visit slorta.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 64. Do I get the senior discount on SLO RTA?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. The published criteria for SLO RTA's senior discounts are:</p><p>- **Half-priced fares** for Seniors aged 65–79 [slorta-discounts]</p><p>- **Free fares** for VIP Seniors aged 80 or older [slorta-discounts]</p><p>At age 64, you do not meet either threshold. To confirm whether SLO RTA offers any other discount programs you may qualify for, contact SLO Regional Rideshare at 1114 Marsh Street, San Luis Obispo, CA 93401, or visit slorta.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "Discount fares are available through the Tap2Ride system for riders who have verified their discount eligibility. The discounts RTA offer through Tap2Ride are:\nFare Free for 80 years old and older, Half price fares for:\n65 years old and older\nMedicare Card Holders\nADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\nPlease see the DISCOUNTS fare page for eligibility requirements\nHow to qualify for fare discounts with your contactless card:\nRegister your contactless payment card with at https://benefits.calitp.org/", "Good for unlimited rides on all RTA, Paso Express, South County, Morro Bay Transit and SLO Transit routes for 31 consecutive days.\n$68.00 General Public\n$34.00 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A & B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated.\n$5.50 General Public\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "You can also go to the Token Transit website and select Send a Pass. Both you and your pass recipient need to have the app on your phones. Select the agency and pass you want and send a pass to any email.\nToken Transit Discount Fares*\nPass holders can qualify to use the Discounted fares if you have completed the Discount Eligibility Form with RTA administration. See the Discounts webpage for details."], "expected_facts": ["65"]}
{"id": "edge-096", "question": "My daughter is in 8th grade in Grover Beach. Does she get the K-12 student discount on the Route 21 bus?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. SLO RTA publishes a K–12 student discount, but it does not apply on Route 21. The published policy states: \"Half-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\" [doc:slorta-discounts].\n\nSince Route 21 is a South County route where the student discount does not apply, your daughter would pay the full general fare on that route. For details about fares on Route 21 or other options, contact SLO RTA directly or visit slorta.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My daughter is in 8th grade in Grover Beach. Does she get the K-12 student discount on the Route 21 bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. SLO RTA publishes a K–12 student discount, but it does not apply on Route 21. The published policy states: \"Half-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A &amp; B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\" [slorta-discounts].</p><p>Since Route 21 is a South County route where the student discount does not apply, your daughter would pay the full general fare on that route. For details about fares on Route 21 or other options, contact SLO RTA directly or visit slorta.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Unlimited rides on all four South County routes for the date purchased: Routes 21,24,27,28. This does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County as well.\n$3.00 General Public\n$1.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "Hartnell students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nMPC students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters.\nCSUMB students ride FREE on all routes with a valid photo student ID during the Fall and Spring semesters."], "expected_facts": ["South County"]}
{"id": "edge-097", "question": "I have an ADA paratransit ID card. Do I pay to ride the regular RTA bus, and does the person helping me ride free too?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**You (the ADA paratransit cardholder):** You ride for free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility for ADA Paratransit Service ID card or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card, and you may ride all RTA and South County routes fare-free. [doc:slorta-passes] [doc:slorta-discounts]\n\n**The person helping you:** Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. [doc:slorta-discounts] The published policy does not specify which full fare applies to them or how they pay.\n\nTo confirm the current details or ask about the companion fare, contact SLO RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-discounts", "slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have an ADA paratransit ID card. Do I pay to ride the regular RTA bus, and does the person helping me ride free too?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**You (the ADA paratransit cardholder):** You ride for free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility for ADA Paratransit Service ID card or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card, and you may ride all RTA and South County routes fare-free. [slorta-passes] [slorta-discounts]</p><p>**The person helping you:** Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. [slorta-discounts] The published policy does not specify which full fare applies to them or how they pay.</p><p>To confirm the current details or ask about the companion fare, contact SLO RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "ADA Paratransit customers that present a “Certificate of Eligibility for ADA Paratransit Service” ID card may ride all RTA and the South County routes for free. Learn more about the Runabout Paratransit service , eligibility requirements and applications.\nAll passes and fares are subject to change upon decision by the RTA Board of Directors.", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "RTA and South County routes offer cash fares as well as discount passes . Cash fares vary by route. Please see each route’s page for more information.\nCash Fares | Rt. 9,10,12,15 | South County/A&B/Morro Bay Transit\nRegular | $2.00 | $1.50\nSenior (65–79) | $1.00 | $0.75\nSenior (80+) | Free with VIP Card | Free with VIP Card\nADA Cardholders | Free with ADA Card | Free with ADA Card\nDisabled | $1.00 | $0.75\nMedicare Cardholders | $1.00 | $0.75\nStudents (K–12) | $1.00 | $1.50 SoCo & Morro Bay. A&B is $1\nChildren 44″ and under | Free with adult rider | Free with adult rider\nVisit the Runabout and Dial-A-Ride pages for more information about these services and fares"], "expected_facts": ["free", "full fare"]}
{"id": "edge-098", "question": "Does the money on an RTA Stored Value Pass expire?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. According to SLO RTA's published policy, stored value passes will deactivate one year from the date that they were created [doc:slorta-passes]. If your pass deactivates and you have not used its full value, you can contact RTA's administrative office to move any remaining value onto a new pass at (805) 781-4833 [doc:slorta-passes].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does the money on an RTA Stored Value Pass expire?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. According to SLO RTA's published policy, stored value passes will deactivate one year from the date that they were created [slorta-passes]. If your pass deactivates and you have not used its full value, you can contact RTA's administrative office to move any remaining value onto a new pass at (805) 781-4833 [slorta-passes].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Valid for travel on RTA & the South County fixed route service. The applicable fare is deducted from the card whenever you use it. Stored value passes will deactivate one year from the date that they were created. If your pass deactivates and you have not used its full value, please contact our administrative office to move any remaining value onto a new pass at (805) 781-4833.\n$15.00 All riders", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares."], "expected_facts": ["one year"]}
{"id": "edge-099", "question": "I tap my phone on some RTA trips and my watch on others. Will I still hit the fare cap?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, you will not hit the fare cap if you switch payment methods. \n\nThe published policy states: \"To take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap.\" The passage explains that if you use different devices — even if the same bank card is linked to both — \"you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\" [doc:slorta-contactless]\n\nSince you are tapping your phone on some trips and your watch on others, the system will treat these as separate payment methods and will not apply fare capping across those rides. Each device will be tracked independently.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming this with RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 (Monday through Friday, 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.) if you have questions about your specific account. [doc:slorta-contactless]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-contactless"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I tap my phone on some RTA trips and my watch on others. Will I still hit the fare cap?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, you will not hit the fare cap if you switch payment methods.</p><p>The published policy states: \"To take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap.\" The passage explains that if you use different devices — even if the same bank card is linked to both — \"you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\" [slorta-contactless]</p><p>Since you are tapping your phone on some trips and your watch on others, the system will treat these as separate payment methods and will not apply fare capping across those rides. Each device will be tracked independently.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming this with RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 (Monday through Friday, 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.) if you have questions about your specific account. [slorta-contactless]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares.", "You can also go to the Token Transit website and select Send a Pass. Both you and your pass recipient need to have the app on your phones. Select the agency and pass you want and send a pass to any email.\nToken Transit Discount Fares*\nPass holders can qualify to use the Discounted fares if you have completed the Discount Eligibility Form with RTA administration. See the Discounts webpage for details.", "Reach out to RTA Customer Service at tap2ride@slorta.org or cal; 805-541-2228 ext. 4833\nTo use Token Transit download the app to your smart phone from Google Play or the Apple App Store . When setting up the app, you will select RTA or South County Transit as your transit agency. You will need to enter in a payment card. Once set up you can purchase, store and then use bus passes on your phone. When its time to board, open the app and hold your phone up to the contactless fare equipment and it will beep.", "Discount fares are available through the Tap2Ride system for riders who have verified their discount eligibility. The discounts RTA offer through Tap2Ride are:\nFare Free for 80 years old and older, Half price fares for:\n65 years old and older\nMedicare Card Holders\nADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\nPlease see the DISCOUNTS fare page for eligibility requirements\nHow to qualify for fare discounts with your contactless card:\nRegister your contactless payment card with at https://benefits.calitp.org/"], "expected_facts": ["same"]}
{"id": "edge-100", "question": "My daughter is 40 inches tall. Does she ride free with me on the Route 21 bus?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nYour question asks about Route 21, which is a Napa Valley Vine Transit route. However, the passages provided do not cover Vine Transit's free-ride policy for children by height. \n\nThe Santa Barbara MTD passages do state that children under 45 inches tall ride free (three children per adult) [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes], but that applies only to Santa Barbara MTD, not to Vine Transit Route 21.\n\nFor Vine Transit's policy on children's fares and any free-ride eligibility based on height, please contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or visit vinetransit.com/fares/.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My daughter is 40 inches tall. Does she ride free with me on the Route 21 bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Your question asks about Route 21, which is a Napa Valley Vine Transit route. However, the passages provided do not cover Vine Transit's free-ride policy for children by height.</p><p>The Santa Barbara MTD passages do state that children under 45 inches tall ride free (three children per adult) [sbmtd-fares-passes], but that applies only to Santa Barbara MTD, not to Vine Transit Route 21.</p><p>For Vine Transit's policy on children's fares and any free-ride eligibility based on height, please contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or visit vinetransit.com/fares/.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited rides on all four South County routes for the date purchased: Routes 21,24,27,28. This does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County as well.\n$3.00 General Public\n$1.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes"], "expected_facts": ["44"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-001", "question": "I ride a VTA local bus and then transfer to an Express bus. What extra do I pay?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe surcharge depends on your rider class:\n\n**Adult passengers:** You must pay a surcharge of $2.50 when transferring from a local bus to an Express bus. If you're using a Clipper card, the surcharge must be paid in the form of Clipper cash (only), and cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. [doc:vta-fares]\n\n**Youth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders:** You will not have a surcharge when transferring from local to express service. [doc:vta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I ride a VTA local bus and then transfer to an Express bus. What extra do I pay?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The surcharge depends on your rider class:</p><p>**Adult passengers:** You must pay a surcharge of $2.50 when transferring from a local bus to an Express bus. If you're using a Clipper card, the surcharge must be paid in the form of Clipper cash (only), and cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. [vta-fares]</p><p>**Youth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders:** You will not have a surcharge when transferring from local to express service. [vta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nFor example: When a VTA Adult Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Rider tags and pays $2.50 for a VTA local fare, they will receive an automatic $2.50 discount from their BART fare. If their total BART fare was $6.00, then they would only pay $3.50. BART Website\nAC Transit\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. AC Transit Website", "VTA works closely with other area transit operators to provide convenient transfers for regional trips, including regional transfer locations and inter-operator transfer or fare agreements.\nHighway 17 Express\nVTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Hwy17 Express. HWY 17 Express Wesbite\nCaltrain\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. Caltrain Website", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nClipper VTA monthly pass holders and VTA SmartPass riders who board DB Express within 2.5 hours of tagging on VTA receive one local fare credit on DB Express. DB Express Website"], "expected_facts": ["$2.50", "Youth"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-002", "question": "I'm 66. What do I show to get the senior fare on VTA?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nTo get the senior fare on VTA, you must present one of the following [doc:vta-fares]:\n\n- Medicare Card\n- Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\n- DMV Disabled License Plate registration\n- DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\n- Proof of age (65 and older)\n\nA valid picture I.D. may be required [doc:vta-fares].\n\nSince you are 66, you meet VTA's age criterion for seniors (age 65 or older) [doc:vta-fares]. However, VTA no longer issues RTC cards to seniors; instead, the Senior Clipper card is available at VTA's Downtown Customer Service Center or River Oaks Administrative Offices and offers the same features and discounts with no application fee and does not expire [doc:vta-rtc-card]. The published policy does not specify whether you must obtain a Senior Clipper card in advance or whether you can simply present proof of age at the time you pay the fare. For details on obtaining a Senior Clipper card or clarification on your options, contact VTA Customer Service at (408) 321-2300 [doc:vta-rtc-card].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares", "vta-rtc-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 66. What do I show to get the senior fare on VTA?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>To get the senior fare on VTA, you must present one of the following [vta-fares]:</p><p>- Medicare Card</p><p>- Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider</p><p>- DMV Disabled License Plate registration</p><p>- DMV Disabled Parking Placard printout</p><p>- Proof of age (65 and older)</p><p>A valid picture I.D. may be required [vta-fares].</p><p>Since you are 66, you meet VTA's age criterion for seniors (age 65 or older) [vta-fares]. However, VTA no longer issues RTC cards to seniors; instead, the Senior Clipper card is available at VTA's Downtown Customer Service Center or River Oaks Administrative Offices and offers the same features and discounts with no application fee and does not expire [vta-rtc-card]. The published policy does not specify whether you must obtain a Senior Clipper card in advance or whether you can simply present proof of age at the time you pay the fare. For details on obtaining a Senior Clipper card or clarification on your options, contact VTA Customer Service at (408) 321-2300 [vta-rtc-card].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li><li>VTA: Regional Transit Connection Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "The Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities that entitles the bearer to ride for the appropriate Senior/Disabled fare.\nThe discount card makes it easy to demonstrate eligibility for the reduced fare on fixed route bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the bay area.\nWith the RTC card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card to pay reduced fares.\nRiders who have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper card can currently use the card to pay for fares on VTA, Caltrain, SamTrans, AC Transit, BART, Muni, Golden Gate Transit and Ferry. Riders who use transit agencies that do not accept Clipper can use the RTC card as proof of eligibility for reduced fares.\nComplete the application form for either \" Basic \" or \" Medical \" eligibility (described above), or for renewals or replacement/lost cards . Incomplete applications will not be accepted. The original application is required, copies will not be accepted.\nBring the application with you. Do not mail it in.\nPresent a valid photo identification - such as a current driver's license, California Identification Card, passport, Alien Registration Card, Military Identification Card or Military Dependent Card (originals only).\nUpdate: As of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards, and the replacement card fee is reduced to $3.\nRTC/Senior/Youth application fees: New – free Renewal ​​​​​​ – free Replacement/lost card - $3\nNew – free\nRenewal ​​​​​​ – free\nReplacement/lost card - $3\nYour photograph will be taken when you submit your application. Eligibility will be verified later, and your Discount ID Card will be mailed to you within 21 days. If you haven't received your card or a request for more information in the mail within 21 days, contact Customer Service at (408) 321-2300.\nUpon receiving your RTC Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the RTC Card at the time you pay the fare.\nVTA Headquarters\n3331 North First Street San Jose, CA, 95134 Monday - Friday 8:30 am to 4:00 pm", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "The RTC Program no longer issues cards to seniors (age 65 and over). The Senior Clipper card offers the same features and discounts, with no application fee, and does not expire. However, effective January 9, 2022, the exception is if the card holder requires an attendant. If an RTC applicant is over 65 and is eligible for an attendant card the RTC program is appropriate.\nThe Senior Clipper card is available at our Downtown Customer Service Center or River Oaks Administrative Offices.", "Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nClipper VTA monthly pass holders and VTA SmartPass riders who board DB Express within 2.5 hours of tagging on VTA receive one local fare credit on DB Express. DB Express Website"], "expected_facts": ["65", "Medicare"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-003", "question": "Is a VTA monthly pass good for 31 days from the day I buy it?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, VTA monthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased, not for 31 days from the day you buy it. [doc:vta-fares]\n\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15. [doc:vta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is a VTA monthly pass good for 31 days from the day I buy it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, VTA monthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased, not for 31 days from the day you buy it. [vta-fares]</p><p>Monthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15. [vta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "The Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities that entitles the bearer to ride for the appropriate Senior/Disabled fare.\nThe discount card makes it easy to demonstrate eligibility for the reduced fare on fixed route bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the bay area.\nWith the RTC card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card to pay reduced fares.\nRiders who have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper card can currently use the card to pay for fares on VTA, Caltrain, SamTrans, AC Transit, BART, Muni, Golden Gate Transit and Ferry. Riders who use transit agencies that do not accept Clipper can use the RTC card as proof of eligibility for reduced fares.\nComplete the application form for either \" Basic \" or \" Medical \" eligibility (described above), or for renewals or replacement/lost cards . Incomplete applications will not be accepted. The original application is required, copies will not be accepted.\nBring the application with you. Do not mail it in.\nPresent a valid photo identification - such as a current driver's license, California Identification Card, passport, Alien Registration Card, Military Identification Card or Military Dependent Card (originals only).\nUpdate: As of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards, and the replacement card fee is reduced to $3.\nRTC/Senior/Youth application fees: New – free Renewal ​​​​​​ – free Replacement/lost card - $3\nNew – free\nRenewal ​​​​​​ – free\nReplacement/lost card - $3\nYour photograph will be taken when you submit your application. Eligibility will be verified later, and your Discount ID Card will be mailed to you within 21 days. If you haven't received your card or a request for more information in the mail within 21 days, contact Customer Service at (408) 321-2300.\nUpon receiving your RTC Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the RTC Card at the time you pay the fare.\nVTA Headquarters\n3331 North First Street San Jose, CA, 95134 Monday - Friday 8:30 am to 4:00 pm", "2 North Market Street San Jose, CA 95113 Monday - Friday 9:00 am to 6:00 pm\nRTC Discount cards are valid for a maximum of five years. You must renew your card at each expiration even if your disability is permanent.\nYOUR CARD MAY BY VALID FOR LESS IF\nYour medical professional determines otherwise\nYour eligibility is tied to a qualifying document that is valid for a shorter period such as a DMV placard (2 years)\nYou used a card from another transit agency as proof of eligibility.\nRenew early so there is ample time to receive your card in the mail before it expires.\nYou may renew up to 60 days prior to expiration. Allow at least 21 days for mailing of the RTC Discount Card.", "VTA works closely with other area transit operators to provide convenient transfers for regional trips, including regional transfer locations and inter-operator transfer or fare agreements.\nHighway 17 Express\nVTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Hwy17 Express. HWY 17 Express Wesbite\nCaltrain\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. Caltrain Website"], "expected_facts": ["calendar month", "16th"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-004", "question": "With my VTA Paratransit ID card, do I also ride the Highway 17 Express for free?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, no. Passengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local and Express bus service and Light Rail, but the pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express [doc:vta-fares]. So while your Paratransit ID Card covers most VTA services for free, Highway 17 Express is explicitly excluded.\n\nFor Highway 17 Express, the published policy states that VTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Highway 17 Express [doc:vta-regional-transfers], but this benefit does not apply to Paratransit ID Card holders.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares", "vta-regional-transfers"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>With my VTA Paratransit ID card, do I also ride the Highway 17 Express for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, no. Passengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local and Express bus service and Light Rail, but the pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express [vta-fares]. So while your Paratransit ID Card covers most VTA services for free, Highway 17 Express is explicitly excluded.</p><p>For Highway 17 Express, the published policy states that VTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Highway 17 Express [vta-regional-transfers], but this benefit does not apply to Paratransit ID Card holders.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li><li>VTA: Regional Transfers</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "VTA works closely with other area transit operators to provide convenient transfers for regional trips, including regional transfer locations and inter-operator transfer or fare agreements.\nHighway 17 Express\nVTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Hwy17 Express. HWY 17 Express Wesbite\nCaltrain\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. Caltrain Website", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "Riders can use contactless bank cards—plastic or mobile Visa, Mastercard, American Express and Discover cards—to pay adult transit fares.\n2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\nPlease have exact change ready when boarding.", "The Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities that entitles the bearer to ride for the appropriate Senior/Disabled fare.\nThe discount card makes it easy to demonstrate eligibility for the reduced fare on fixed route bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the bay area.\nWith the RTC card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card to pay reduced fares.\nRiders who have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper card can currently use the card to pay for fares on VTA, Caltrain, SamTrans, AC Transit, BART, Muni, Golden Gate Transit and Ferry. Riders who use transit agencies that do not accept Clipper can use the RTC card as proof of eligibility for reduced fares.\nComplete the application form for either \" Basic \" or \" Medical \" eligibility (described above), or for renewals or replacement/lost cards . Incomplete applications will not be accepted. The original application is required, copies will not be accepted.\nBring the application with you. Do not mail it in.\nPresent a valid photo identification - such as a current driver's license, California Identification Card, passport, Alien Registration Card, Military Identification Card or Military Dependent Card (originals only).\nUpdate: As of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards, and the replacement card fee is reduced to $3.\nRTC/Senior/Youth application fees: New – free Renewal ​​​​​​ – free Replacement/lost card - $3\nNew – free\nRenewal ​​​​​​ – free\nReplacement/lost card - $3\nYour photograph will be taken when you submit your application. Eligibility will be verified later, and your Discount ID Card will be mailed to you within 21 days. If you haven't received your card or a request for more information in the mail within 21 days, contact Customer Service at (408) 321-2300.\nUpon receiving your RTC Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the RTC Card at the time you pay the fare.\nVTA Headquarters\n3331 North First Street San Jose, CA, 95134 Monday - Friday 8:30 am to 4:00 pm"], "expected_facts": ["free", "not honored"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-005", "question": "What are the income and age rules for Clipper START on VTA?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Age requirement:** To qualify for Clipper START, you must be 19-64 years old. [doc:vta-fares]\n\n**Income requirement:** You must have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less. [doc:vta-fares]\n\n**Additional eligibility criteria:** You must also be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area and not have a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Clipper Card for people with disabilities. [doc:vta-fares]\n\n**How to apply:** For more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page. [doc:vta-fares]\n\nThe published policy does not specify how VTA verifies income or residency, or what documents you must provide with your application.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What are the income and age rules for Clipper START on VTA?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Age requirement:** To qualify for Clipper START, you must be 19-64 years old. [vta-fares]</p><p>**Income requirement:** You must have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less. [vta-fares]</p><p>**Additional eligibility criteria:** You must also be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area and not have a Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Clipper Card for people with disabilities. [vta-fares]</p><p>**How to apply:** For more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page. [vta-fares]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how VTA verifies income or residency, or what documents you must provide with your application.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "The Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities that entitles the bearer to ride for the appropriate Senior/Disabled fare.\nThe discount card makes it easy to demonstrate eligibility for the reduced fare on fixed route bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the bay area.\nWith the RTC card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card to pay reduced fares.\nRiders who have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper card can currently use the card to pay for fares on VTA, Caltrain, SamTrans, AC Transit, BART, Muni, Golden Gate Transit and Ferry. Riders who use transit agencies that do not accept Clipper can use the RTC card as proof of eligibility for reduced fares.\nComplete the application form for either \" Basic \" or \" Medical \" eligibility (described above), or for renewals or replacement/lost cards . Incomplete applications will not be accepted. The original application is required, copies will not be accepted.\nBring the application with you. Do not mail it in.\nPresent a valid photo identification - such as a current driver's license, California Identification Card, passport, Alien Registration Card, Military Identification Card or Military Dependent Card (originals only).\nUpdate: As of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards, and the replacement card fee is reduced to $3.\nRTC/Senior/Youth application fees: New – free Renewal ​​​​​​ – free Replacement/lost card - $3\nNew – free\nRenewal ​​​​​​ – free\nReplacement/lost card - $3\nYour photograph will be taken when you submit your application. Eligibility will be verified later, and your Discount ID Card will be mailed to you within 21 days. If you haven't received your card or a request for more information in the mail within 21 days, contact Customer Service at (408) 321-2300.\nUpon receiving your RTC Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the RTC Card at the time you pay the fare.\nVTA Headquarters\n3331 North First Street San Jose, CA, 95134 Monday - Friday 8:30 am to 4:00 pm", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nFor example: When a VTA Adult Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Rider tags and pays $2.50 for a VTA local fare, they will receive an automatic $2.50 discount from their BART fare. If their total BART fare was $6.00, then they would only pay $3.50. BART Website\nAC Transit\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. AC Transit Website", "The RTC Program no longer issues cards to seniors (age 65 and over). The Senior Clipper card offers the same features and discounts, with no application fee, and does not expire. However, effective January 9, 2022, the exception is if the card holder requires an attendant. If an RTC applicant is over 65 and is eligible for an attendant card the RTC program is appropriate.\nThe Senior Clipper card is available at our Downtown Customer Service Center or River Oaks Administrative Offices."], "expected_facts": ["200%", "19-64"]}
{"id": "edge-vta-006", "question": "Does my 4-year-old pay to ride VTA? What about my 15-year-old?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Your 4-year-old:** Children under five ride free [doc:vta-fares].\n\n**Your 15-year-old:** Youth must be age 5-18 to qualify for discounted fares [doc:vta-fares]. The published policy does not specify the Youth fare price in the passages provided. To find the current Youth single-ride or pass price, contact VTA directly at (408) 321-2300 or visit vta.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 4-year-old pay to ride VTA? What about my 15-year-old?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Your 4-year-old:** Children under five ride free [vta-fares].</p><p>**Your 15-year-old:** Youth must be age 5-18 to qualify for discounted fares [vta-fares]. The published policy does not specify the Youth fare price in the passages provided. To find the current Youth single-ride or pass price, contact VTA directly at (408) 321-2300 or visit vta.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nFor example: When a VTA Adult Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Rider tags and pays $2.50 for a VTA local fare, they will receive an automatic $2.50 discount from their BART fare. If their total BART fare was $6.00, then they would only pay $3.50. BART Website\nAC Transit\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. AC Transit Website", "2 North Market Street San Jose, CA 95113 Monday - Friday 9:00 am to 6:00 pm\nRTC Discount cards are valid for a maximum of five years. You must renew your card at each expiration even if your disability is permanent.\nYOUR CARD MAY BY VALID FOR LESS IF\nYour medical professional determines otherwise\nYour eligibility is tied to a qualifying document that is valid for a shorter period such as a DMV placard (2 years)\nYou used a card from another transit agency as proof of eligibility.\nRenew early so there is ample time to receive your card in the mail before it expires.\nYou may renew up to 60 days prior to expiration. Allow at least 21 days for mailing of the RTC Discount Card.", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "The Regional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities that entitles the bearer to ride for the appropriate Senior/Disabled fare.\nThe discount card makes it easy to demonstrate eligibility for the reduced fare on fixed route bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the bay area.\nWith the RTC card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card to pay reduced fares.\nRiders who have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper card can currently use the card to pay for fares on VTA, Caltrain, SamTrans, AC Transit, BART, Muni, Golden Gate Transit and Ferry. Riders who use transit agencies that do not accept Clipper can use the RTC card as proof of eligibility for reduced fares.\nComplete the application form for either \" Basic \" or \" Medical \" eligibility (described above), or for renewals or replacement/lost cards . Incomplete applications will not be accepted. The original application is required, copies will not be accepted.\nBring the application with you. Do not mail it in.\nPresent a valid photo identification - such as a current driver's license, California Identification Card, passport, Alien Registration Card, Military Identification Card or Military Dependent Card (originals only).\nUpdate: As of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards, and the replacement card fee is reduced to $3.\nRTC/Senior/Youth application fees: New – free Renewal ​​​​​​ – free Replacement/lost card - $3\nNew – free\nRenewal ​​​​​​ – free\nReplacement/lost card - $3\nYour photograph will be taken when you submit your application. Eligibility will be verified later, and your Discount ID Card will be mailed to you within 21 days. If you haven't received your card or a request for more information in the mail within 21 days, contact Customer Service at (408) 321-2300.\nUpon receiving your RTC Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the RTC Card at the time you pay the fare.\nVTA Headquarters\n3331 North First Street San Jose, CA, 95134 Monday - Friday 8:30 am to 4:00 pm"], "expected_facts": ["under five", "Youth"]}
{"id": "edge-082", "question": "I'm 82. Do I ride the Vine for free?", "provenance": {"source": "VINE published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. [doc:vine-fares] Since you are 82, you meet the published age criterion.\n\nTo obtain your Lifetime Pass, you must complete an application. Download the Lifetime Pass application form, then send your completed application along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to:\n\nVine Mobility Manager\n625 Burnell St\nNapa, CA 94559\n\nVine will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass or make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. If you have questions, call (707) 259-8635. [doc:vine-fares]\n\nThe agency makes the final decision on your eligibility based on the documentation you provide.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vine-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm 82. Do I ride the Vine for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Riders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. [vine-fares] Since you are 82, you meet the published age criterion.</p><p>To obtain your Lifetime Pass, you must complete an application. Download the Lifetime Pass application form, then send your completed application along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to:</p><p>Vine Mobility Manager</p><p>625 Burnell St</p><p>Napa, CA 94559</p><p>Vine will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass or make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. If you have questions, call (707) 259-8635. [vine-fares]</p><p>The agency makes the final decision on your eligibility based on the documentation you provide.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VINE: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Transfers on the Vine are free. Please ask for transfers when boarding. Transfers are valid for 90 minutes after they are issued. No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11. There are no free transfers to the Route 29. Transfers cannot be used on the same route.", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes", "Calistoga | St. Helena | Yountville | Napa | American Canyon | Kaiser Vallejo\nCalistoga | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00 | $8.00\nSt. Helena | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nYountville | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nNapa | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00\nAmerican Canyon | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00\nKaiser Vallejo | $8.00 | $8.00 | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X\nX = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.\nVineGo Discount Pass Eligible members of VineGo may purchase discount passes at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center or online. The purchase price of a discount pass is $20 for a $24 value.\nReasonable accommodation\nPublic transit passengers with disabilities may request reasonable modifications be made to accommodate their use of public transit. Requests should be made in written form and submitted to the Manager of Public Transit at 625 Burnell St., Napa, CA 94559.\nComments and complaints\nComments and complaints from the public may be received electronically through our customer service portal.\nClick here to submit a comment or complaint to NVTA staff. Additionally, prepaid comment cards are available in English and Spanish on Vine vehicles.\nWhen complaints are received, NVTA staff and/or contractor will acknowledge receipt of the complaint within 24 hours from the time it is received. All complaints should be investigated and resolved within 5 business days. Complaining party shall be notified of the agency’s resolution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "VineGo is a shared ride service and provides transportation curb-to-curb, within 3/4 of a mile from the fixed route bus system. In order to use VineGo, you must be certified.\nPara Video en Español Haga Clic Aquí\nAre you in a VineGo service area ? Click the link to see if your address falls within out service area.\nWho qualifies?\nHow to apply?\nHow does the assessment take place?\nWhat should I bring?\nWhen do I know if I’m eligible? Is there an appeals process?\nHow does VineGo work?\nSchedule a ride\nTrack a ride (My Transit Manager)\nVineGo hours\nVineGo fares\nReasonable accommodation\nComments & complaints\nADA visitors to Napa Valley\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area\nDo I qualify for VineGo? Eligibility is on a case-by-case basis. According to ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) regulations, only people with physical and/or cognitive limitations that prevent them from riding the Vine bus can qualify for VineGo. Users who do not qualify for ADA paratransit are still eligible to receive travel training to learn how to navigate the Vine fixed-route system at no cost. If you are interested in receiving travel training, please call (707) 259-8635 or reach out to [email protected] .\nHow can I apply?\nTo apply to use paratransit, you must complete a two-part process:\nFirst, complete the VineGo Paratransit Application. Click below to access the VineGo Applications in your preferred language.\nEnglish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nSpanish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nTagalog VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nThen, e-mail the completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo as an attachment to your e-mail.\nAlternatively, if you prefer, you can print and mail your completed application to:\nParatransit Eligibility Office 625 Burnell Street Napa, CA 94559\nInstructions for completing a fillable PDF:\nClick one of the links above to access the paratransit application in your preferred language.\nClick on each individual field to enter your response.\nWhen finished, click the download button in the upper right hand corner of the document. (see image below)\nE-mail your completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo.\nNeed help with the application process? Call NVTA at (707) 259-8635 or the Paratransit Eligibility Desk at (707) 253-4649.\nAll assessments, including renewals, will be made by phone appointment. For more information or to schedule your appointment, please contact the VineGo Paratransit Eligibility desk at (707) 253-4649.\nADA eligibility interviews are conducted entirely over the phone by MV Transportation in Santa Rosa.\nIs there anything I should prepare for my assessment?\nYes. Be prepared with a list of:\nAny sort of mobility device you use\nA list of medications you are taking\nThe name of a personal care attendant (PCA) you travel with, if you have one.\nWhen will I know if I am eligible?\nThe Americans with Disabilities Act allows paratransit providers like VineGo up to 21 days to determine if someone is eligible. However, final decisions are typically made much sooner. If an application is not processed within 21 days, is presumptive eligibility granted on the 22nd day until and unless the application is denied in writing\nIs there an appeals process?\nIf a paratransit applicant is deemed ineligible and does not agree with the determination they have the right to appeal the decision. To formally appeal a decision a letter must be submitted to NVTA within 60 days of receiving an eligibility determination letter. The letter shall be addressed to NVTA 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559 to the attention of the Manger of Public Transit. Upon receiving the letter an appeals panel will be assembled to hear an applicant’s appeal. The applicant or someone they appoint to speak on their behalf shall be contacted and an in person meeting with the panel will be scheduled. The panel shall consist of a member of Napa County’s Paratransit Coordinating Council (PCC), a member of NVTA’s Evaluation Contractor’s evaluation staff, and the Manager of Public Transit or his/her designated staff member. The appeals panel will render a final written decision within thirty (30) days of hearing the appeal. Should the appeals panel not render a decision within the thirty (30) days after the completion of the appeals process, NVTA shall provide paratransit service to the applicant until a decision is rendered. Free transportation shall be provided to the appealing applicant and their personal care attendant (PCA) to the appeals hearing.\nHow VineGo works\nVineGo can pick up passengers from the doorsteps of their home to their destinations. If VineGo can’t pick up or drop off passengers due to difficulties or physical barriers, VineGo will pick up/drop off the passenger at the nearest safe location). Roundtrips are welcome.\nEnrollees can schedule trips up to 7 days in advance. Same-day reservations are also welcome; however, we recommend making trip reservations in advance, as this service is in high demand. All rides are scheduled over the phone.\nKey Notes:\nBE FLEXIBLE. VineGo dispatch can negotiate a pickup time up to 60 minutes before or after your requested pick-up time.\nRemember that this is a SHARED RIDE SERVICE (NOT A TAXI SERVICE). The driver may pick up or drop off other passengers along the way.\nScheduling a ride Call (707) 252-2600 to schedule a ride. (You must be enrolled in VineGo before scheduling a ride.)\nIf your mobility device has changed, please let your reservationist know when you call the schedule your ride. For example, if you previously used a walker but now use a wheelchair, please let the reservationist know. You and your loved ones or caretakers can track your ride and receive notifications using the My Transit Manager app.\nReservation hours: Monday – Friday: 8am – 6pm Saturday – Sunday: 8am – 5pm\nVineGo service hours Mondays – Fridays: 6 am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSaturdays: 6:30am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSundays: 8 am – 6 pm (6 pm-9 pm as available )\nFares\nFares range from $4 to $8 and are based on distance traveled. Passes are also available at a discounted price. Rides are charged based on the actual fare. ADA fares are no more than twice the adult fixed route fare charged by the Vine. Please note that VineGo is NOT an emergency transportation service. If you have an emergency of any nature, call 911.\nExact fare is required on all buses . All fares are non-refundable. Drivers cannot make change.\nVineGo One-Way Fares Calistoga St. Helena Yountville Napa American Canyon Kaiser Vallejo Calistoga X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 $8.00 St. Helena $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Yountville $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Napa $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 American Canyon $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 Kaiser Vallejo $8.00 $8.00 $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 X X = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.", "Riders can pay single-ride fares using cash directly on the bus. No change is given so please have the exact amount ready to pay as your board. Passes are sold at the following location:\nSoscol Gateway Transit Center 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559\nBulk Passes Need to buy passes in bulk? Fill out our bulk pass order form and send it to the emails listed in the form. Please allow a minimum of 24 hours upon receipt to process request for pick-up."], "expected_facts": ["80", "Lifetime Pass"], "pair_id": "pair-edge-082", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
