solution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "Riders can pay single-ride fares using cash directly on the bus. No change is given so please have the exact amount ready to pay as your board. Passes are sold at the following location:\nSoscol Gateway Transit Center 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559\nBulk Passes Need to buy passes in bulk? Fill out our bulk pass order form and send it to the emails listed in the form. Please allow a minimum of 24 hours upon receipt to process request for pick-up."], "expected_facts": ["three"]}
{"id": "edge-086", "question": "How much is a VineGo paratransit trip from Calistoga to American Canyon?", "provenance": {"source": "VINE published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A VineGo paratransit trip from Calistoga to American Canyon costs $8.00 [doc:vine-go].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vine-go"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a VineGo paratransit trip from Calistoga to American Canyon?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A VineGo paratransit trip from Calistoga to American Canyon costs $8.00 [vine-go].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VINE: VineGo Paratransit Service</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["VineGo is a shared ride service and provides transportation curb-to-curb, within 3/4 of a mile from the fixed route bus system. In order to use VineGo, you must be certified.\nPara Video en Español Haga Clic Aquí\nAre you in a VineGo service area ? Click the link to see if your address falls within out service area.\nWho qualifies?\nHow to apply?\nHow does the assessment take place?\nWhat should I bring?\nWhen do I know if I’m eligible? Is there an appeals process?\nHow does VineGo work?\nSchedule a ride\nTrack a ride (My Transit Manager)\nVineGo hours\nVineGo fares\nReasonable accommodation\nComments & complaints\nADA visitors to Napa Valley\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area\nDo I qualify for VineGo? Eligibility is on a case-by-case basis. According to ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) regulations, only people with physical and/or cognitive limitations that prevent them from riding the Vine bus can qualify for VineGo. Users who do not qualify for ADA paratransit are still eligible to receive travel training to learn how to navigate the Vine fixed-route system at no cost. If you are interested in receiving travel training, please call (707) 259-8635 or reach out to [email protected] .\nHow can I apply?\nTo apply to use paratransit, you must complete a two-part process:\nFirst, complete the VineGo Paratransit Application. Click below to access the VineGo Applications in your preferred language.\nEnglish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nSpanish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nTagalog VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nThen, e-mail the completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo as an attachment to your e-mail.\nAlternatively, if you prefer, you can print and mail your completed application to:\nParatransit Eligibility Office 625 Burnell Street Napa, CA 94559\nInstructions for completing a fillable PDF:\nClick one of the links above to access the paratransit application in your preferred language.\nClick on each individual field to enter your response.\nWhen finished, click the download button in the upper right hand corner of the document. (see image below)\nE-mail your completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo.\nNeed help with the application process? Call NVTA at (707) 259-8635 or the Paratransit Eligibility Desk at (707) 253-4649.\nAll assessments, including renewals, will be made by phone appointment. For more information or to schedule your appointment, please contact the VineGo Paratransit Eligibility desk at (707) 253-4649.\nADA eligibility interviews are conducted entirely over the phone by MV Transportation in Santa Rosa.\nIs there anything I should prepare for my assessment?\nYes. Be prepared with a list of:\nAny sort of mobility device you use\nA list of medications you are taking\nThe name of a personal care attendant (PCA) you travel with, if you have one.\nWhen will I know if I am eligible?\nThe Americans with Disabilities Act allows paratransit providers like VineGo up to 21 days to determine if someone is eligible. However, final decisions are typically made much sooner. If an application is not processed within 21 days, is presumptive eligibility granted on the 22nd day until and unless the application is denied in writing\nIs there an appeals process?\nIf a paratransit applicant is deemed ineligible and does not agree with the determination they have the right to appeal the decision. To formally appeal a decision a letter must be submitted to NVTA within 60 days of receiving an eligibility determination letter. The letter shall be addressed to NVTA 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559 to the attention of the Manger of Public Transit. Upon receiving the letter an appeals panel will be assembled to hear an applicant’s appeal. The applicant or someone they appoint to speak on their behalf shall be contacted and an in person meeting with the panel will be scheduled. The panel shall consist of a member of Napa County’s Paratransit Coordinating Council (PCC), a member of NVTA’s Evaluation Contractor’s evaluation staff, and the Manager of Public Transit or his/her designated staff member. The appeals panel will render a final written decision within thirty (30) days of hearing the appeal. Should the appeals panel not render a decision within the thirty (30) days after the completion of the appeals process, NVTA shall provide paratransit service to the applicant until a decision is rendered. Free transportation shall be provided to the appealing applicant and their personal care attendant (PCA) to the appeals hearing.\nHow VineGo works\nVineGo can pick up passengers from the doorsteps of their home to their destinations. If VineGo can’t pick up or drop off passengers due to difficulties or physical barriers, VineGo will pick up/drop off the passenger at the nearest safe location). Roundtrips are welcome.\nEnrollees can schedule trips up to 7 days in advance. Same-day reservations are also welcome; however, we recommend making trip reservations in advance, as this service is in high demand. All rides are scheduled over the phone.\nKey Notes:\nBE FLEXIBLE. VineGo dispatch can negotiate a pickup time up to 60 minutes before or after your requested pick-up time.\nRemember that this is a SHARED RIDE SERVICE (NOT A TAXI SERVICE). The driver may pick up or drop off other passengers along the way.\nScheduling a ride Call (707) 252-2600 to schedule a ride. (You must be enrolled in VineGo before scheduling a ride.)\nIf your mobility device has changed, please let your reservationist know when you call the schedule your ride. For example, if you previously used a walker but now use a wheelchair, please let the reservationist know. You and your loved ones or caretakers can track your ride and receive notifications using the My Transit Manager app.\nReservation hours: Monday – Friday: 8am – 6pm Saturday – Sunday: 8am – 5pm\nVineGo service hours Mondays – Fridays: 6 am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSaturdays: 6:30am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSundays: 8 am – 6 pm (6 pm-9 pm as available )\nFares\nFares range from $4 to $8 and are based on distance traveled. Passes are also available at a discounted price. Rides are charged based on the actual fare. ADA fares are no more than twice the adult fixed route fare charged by the Vine. Please note that VineGo is NOT an emergency transportation service. If you have an emergency of any nature, call 911.\nExact fare is required on all buses . All fares are non-refundable. Drivers cannot make change.\nVineGo One-Way Fares Calistoga St. Helena Yountville Napa American Canyon Kaiser Vallejo Calistoga X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 $8.00 St. Helena $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Yountville $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Napa $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 American Canyon $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 Kaiser Vallejo $8.00 $8.00 $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 X X = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.", "Calistoga | St. Helena | Yountville | Napa | American Canyon | Kaiser Vallejo\nCalistoga | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00 | $8.00\nSt. Helena | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nYountville | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nNapa | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00\nAmerican Canyon | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00\nKaiser Vallejo | $8.00 | $8.00 | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X\nX = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.\nVineGo Discount Pass Eligible members of VineGo may purchase discount passes at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center or online. The purchase price of a discount pass is $20 for a $24 value.\nReasonable accommodation\nPublic transit passengers with disabilities may request reasonable modifications be made to accommodate their use of public transit. Requests should be made in written form and submitted to the Manager of Public Transit at 625 Burnell St., Napa, CA 94559.\nComments and complaints\nComments and complaints from the public may be received electronically through our customer service portal.\nClick here to submit a comment or complaint to NVTA staff. Additionally, prepaid comment cards are available in English and Spanish on Vine vehicles.\nWhen complaints are received, NVTA staff and/or contractor will acknowledge receipt of the complaint within 24 hours from the time it is received. All complaints should be investigated and resolved within 5 business days. Complaining party shall be notified of the agency’s resolution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes", "Transfers on the Vine are free. Please ask for transfers when boarding. Transfers are valid for 90 minutes after they are issued. No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11. There are no free transfers to the Route 29. Transfers cannot be used on the same route.", "Riders can pay single-ride fares using cash directly on the bus. No change is given so please have the exact amount ready to pay as your board. Passes are sold at the following location:\nSoscol Gateway Transit Center 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559\nBulk Passes Need to buy passes in bulk? Fill out our bulk pass order form and send it to the emails listed in the form. Please allow a minimum of 24 hours upon receipt to process request for pick-up."], "expected_facts": ["$8.00"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-001", "question": "I'm riding SamTrans with my three kids, ages 2, 4, and 9. Who pays?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nFor your group of four:\n\n**Your 2-year-old:** travels free. [doc:samtrans-fares]\n\n**Your 4-year-old:** travels free. Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. [doc:samtrans-fares]\n\n**Your 9-year-old:** pays the Youth fare of $1.10 in cash or $1.00 on Clipper, or may use a Youth Day Pass for $2.00. [doc:samtrans-fares] [doc:samtrans-fare-types]\n\n**You (the adult):** pay the Adult fare of $2.25 in cash or $2.05 on Clipper, or may use an Adult Day Pass for $4.50. [doc:samtrans-fares] [doc:samtrans-fare-types]\n\nThe free rides for your two youngest children apply because you are paying an adult or eligible discount fare. [doc:samtrans-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-fare-types", "samtrans-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I'm riding SamTrans with my three kids, ages 2, 4, and 9. Who pays?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>For your group of four:</p><p>**Your 2-year-old:** travels free. [samtrans-fares]</p><p>**Your 4-year-old:** travels free. Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. [samtrans-fares]</p><p>**Your 9-year-old:** pays the Youth fare of $1.10 in cash or $1.00 on Clipper, or may use a Youth Day Pass for $2.00. [samtrans-fares] [samtrans-fare-types]</p><p>**You (the adult):** pay the Adult fare of $2.25 in cash or $2.05 on Clipper, or may use an Adult Day Pass for $4.50. [samtrans-fares] [samtrans-fare-types]</p><p>The free rides for your two youngest children apply because you are paying an adult or eligible discount fare. [samtrans-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li><li>SamTrans: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "The easiest way to use Clipper is with the Clipper mobile app, available for download via the App Store or Google Play.\nRiders can also purchase a physical Clipper card:\nAdults Adult (ages 19-64) Clipper cards can be purchased at the San Mateo County Transit District Administrative office in San Carlos as well as Clipper retail vendors . Discounts – Youth and Seniors To qualify for a discounted Clipper pass, Youth (ages 5-18) and Senior (ages 65+) customers must fill out an application to obtain a Clipper card.\nApply Now .\nSenior and Youth applications are available on the Clipper website or by calling 1-800-660-4287 (TTY 650-508-6448).\nCompleted applications and proof-of-age may be submitted by mail, e-mail, or fax as indicated on the application. To receive a card immediately, you can apply in person at the San Mateo County Transit District Administrative office in San Carlos or at other Clipper designated locations . The applying Senior/Youth does not have to be present.\nNote: A Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card once you turn 19. Please refer to FAQ's below for more information.\nCustomers with Disabilities/RTC Members Disabled fare customers must have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper-compatible card. For more information or to obtain one, please call 877.878.8883.\nAn Adult Clipper card is free for a limited time by downloading a virtual card via the Clipper mobile app via the App Store or Google Play . A minimum of $3 cash value is required to be loaded to the card when obtaining the Adult Clipper card on mobile for the first time.\nOtherwise, physical cards cost $3. The fee is waived if you sign-up for Autoload and obtain the card at the same time online. There is no fee for Youth and Senior Clipper cards.\nCash or passes can be loaded to your Clipper card immediately by going to:\nParticipating transit agencies\nclippercard.com/locations\nDowntown San Francisco Muni fare machines\nBART ticket machines\nAdd Value Machines at San Francisco or Palo Alto Caltrain stations\nSelect Caltrain ticket machines\nProducts/cash may also be loaded through the Clipper website or over the phone with the Clipper Customer Service Bureau, but the pass/cash may take up to five days to be loaded onto the card. Clipper also offers Autoload where your card is reloaded each month automatically. For more information, visit www.clippercard.com or call the Clipper Customer Service Bureau at 1-877-878-8883.\nA customer who qualifies for one of the discount categories (youth, senior, disabled) must apply for the appropriate Clipper card. When they travel using cash, the onboard validator recognizes the type of card and deducts the appropriate cash value.\nOn your 19 th birthday, your Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card. This means youth discounts will no longer be granted and adult fares will be required. Note: If you have a Youth Monthly Pass on your card, it will be cancelled. This will happen even if it is the beginning or middle of the month. If you have cash value on your card, the Clipper system will automatically start deducting adult fare. If your card is set up for autoload and your 19 th birthday is coming up, follow these steps: 1) Disable Autoload before the new pass is loaded. This will prevent a product from being loaded that you will not be able to fully use. 2) After your birthday has passed, set up Autoload to load an adult pass.\nYes. Regardless of whether you use cash value or a pass, you must tap your card to the onboard validator every time you board. Your tap is your proof-of-payment.\nWhen using Clipper, you must pay your entire fare with the pass and/or cash value on your Clipper card. You cannot combine cash, tickets, or tokens with a portion from your Clipper card.\nVisit our Interagency Transfers page .\nClipper cards are for individual use. Each rider 5 years of age or older must have his/her own card. The Clipper cards also have a feature that stops you from tapping more than once on the same bus within a 5-minute timeframe. This feature was built in to protect you from accidentally tapping and being charged twice.", "Next Generation Clipper will make riding SamTrans — and connecting to other Bay Area transit — even easier.\nContinue using your Clipper card like normal. Your card will automatically be transitioned to Next Generation Clipper over time; this transition period may take eight to twelve weeks.\nThe easiest way to check on the status of your Clipper card after Dec. 10 is to call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 and ask whether your card has been upgraded."], "expected_facts": ["Two children", "age 4"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-002", "question": "I have a Medicare card. What fare do I pay on SamTrans and what do I need to show?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Fare:** Medicare cardholders are eligible for the discounted fare. On SamTrans Local/Express routes, the discounted fare is $1.00 on Clipper or $1.10 with cash or mobile payment [doc:samtrans-fares]. The discounted monthly pass is $27.00 on Clipper [doc:samtrans-fares].\n\n**What to show:** Bus operators may ask to see your Medicare card [doc:samtrans-fare-types].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how to obtain a Clipper card as a Medicare cardholder or whether a separate application is required. For details on obtaining your card, contact SamTrans or Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-fare-types", "samtrans-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Medicare card. What fare do I pay on SamTrans and what do I need to show?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Fare:** Medicare cardholders are eligible for the discounted fare. On SamTrans Local/Express routes, the discounted fare is $1.00 on Clipper or $1.10 with cash or mobile payment [samtrans-fares]. The discounted monthly pass is $27.00 on Clipper [samtrans-fares].</p><p>**What to show:** Bus operators may ask to see your Medicare card [samtrans-fare-types].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how to obtain a Clipper card as a Medicare cardholder or whether a separate application is required. For details on obtaining your card, contact SamTrans or Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li><li>SamTrans: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["A faster, smarter Clipper has arrived. Next Generation Clipper has rolled out across Bay Area transit systems, bringing upgraded technology and new ways to pay your SamTrans bus fare.\nWith the new system, riders can:\nUse any contactless payment card or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, etc.) directly onboard — no need for a separate Clipper card.\nManage everything online or in the Clipper app, including balance, payment methods, and autoload settings.\nEnjoy faster updates when you reload or add passes.", "Next Generation Clipper will make riding SamTrans — and connecting to other Bay Area transit — even easier.\nContinue using your Clipper card like normal. Your card will automatically be transitioned to Next Generation Clipper over time; this transition period may take eight to twelve weeks.\nThe easiest way to check on the status of your Clipper card after Dec. 10 is to call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 and ask whether your card has been upgraded.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00", "Medicare"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-003", "question": "My 19th birthday is next week. What happens to the Youth Monthly Pass on my Clipper card for SamTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nWhen you turn 19, your Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card. Any active Youth Monthly Pass on your card will be cancelled on your 19th birthday, even if it is the beginning or middle of the month. [doc:samtrans-clipper]\n\nIf you have cash value remaining on your card, the Clipper system will automatically start deducting adult fares from that day on. [doc:samtrans-clipper]\n\nTo continue riding with discounted fares after you turn 19, you may qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64), which offers a 50% fare discount for eligible low-income riders. To learn more and apply, visit the Clipper START website. [doc:samtrans-fare-types]\n\nIf your card is set up for autoload and your birthday is coming up, disable Autoload before the new pass loads to prevent a product from being loaded that you will not be able to fully use. After your birthday, you can set up Autoload again to load an adult pass. [doc:samtrans-clipper]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-clipper", "samtrans-fare-types"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>My 19th birthday is next week. What happens to the Youth Monthly Pass on my Clipper card for SamTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>When you turn 19, your Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card. Any active Youth Monthly Pass on your card will be cancelled on your 19th birthday, even if it is the beginning or middle of the month. [samtrans-clipper]</p><p>If you have cash value remaining on your card, the Clipper system will automatically start deducting adult fares from that day on. [samtrans-clipper]</p><p>To continue riding with discounted fares after you turn 19, you may qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64), which offers a 50% fare discount for eligible low-income riders. To learn more and apply, visit the Clipper START website. [samtrans-fare-types]</p><p>If your card is set up for autoload and your birthday is coming up, disable Autoload before the new pass loads to prevent a product from being loaded that you will not be able to fully use. After your birthday, you can set up Autoload again to load an adult pass. [samtrans-clipper]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Clipper on SamTrans (Next Generation Clipper FAQ)</li><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Next Generation Clipper will make riding SamTrans — and connecting to other Bay Area transit — even easier.\nContinue using your Clipper card like normal. Your card will automatically be transitioned to Next Generation Clipper over time; this transition period may take eight to twelve weeks.\nThe easiest way to check on the status of your Clipper card after Dec. 10 is to call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 and ask whether your card has been upgraded.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "The easiest way to use Clipper is with the Clipper mobile app, available for download via the App Store or Google Play.\nRiders can also purchase a physical Clipper card:\nAdults Adult (ages 19-64) Clipper cards can be purchased at the San Mateo County Transit District Administrative office in San Carlos as well as Clipper retail vendors . Discounts – Youth and Seniors To qualify for a discounted Clipper pass, Youth (ages 5-18) and Senior (ages 65+) customers must fill out an application to obtain a Clipper card.\nApply Now .\nSenior and Youth applications are available on the Clipper website or by calling 1-800-660-4287 (TTY 650-508-6448).\nCompleted applications and proof-of-age may be submitted by mail, e-mail, or fax as indicated on the application. To receive a card immediately, you can apply in person at the San Mateo County Transit District Administrative office in San Carlos or at other Clipper designated locations . The applying Senior/Youth does not have to be present.\nNote: A Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card once you turn 19. Please refer to FAQ's below for more information.\nCustomers with Disabilities/RTC Members Disabled fare customers must have a Regional Transit Connection Clipper-compatible card. For more information or to obtain one, please call 877.878.8883.\nAn Adult Clipper card is free for a limited time by downloading a virtual card via the Clipper mobile app via the App Store or Google Play . A minimum of $3 cash value is required to be loaded to the card when obtaining the Adult Clipper card on mobile for the first time.\nOtherwise, physical cards cost $3. The fee is waived if you sign-up for Autoload and obtain the card at the same time online. There is no fee for Youth and Senior Clipper cards.\nCash or passes can be loaded to your Clipper card immediately by going to:\nParticipating transit agencies\nclippercard.com/locations\nDowntown San Francisco Muni fare machines\nBART ticket machines\nAdd Value Machines at San Francisco or Palo Alto Caltrain stations\nSelect Caltrain ticket machines\nProducts/cash may also be loaded through the Clipper website or over the phone with the Clipper Customer Service Bureau, but the pass/cash may take up to five days to be loaded onto the card. Clipper also offers Autoload where your card is reloaded each month automatically. For more information, visit www.clippercard.com or call the Clipper Customer Service Bureau at 1-877-878-8883.\nA customer who qualifies for one of the discount categories (youth, senior, disabled) must apply for the appropriate Clipper card. When they travel using cash, the onboard validator recognizes the type of card and deducts the appropriate cash value.\nOn your 19 th birthday, your Youth Clipper card will automatically change to an Adult Clipper card. This means youth discounts will no longer be granted and adult fares will be required. Note: If you have a Youth Monthly Pass on your card, it will be cancelled. This will happen even if it is the beginning or middle of the month. If you have cash value on your card, the Clipper system will automatically start deducting adult fare. If your card is set up for autoload and your 19 th birthday is coming up, follow these steps: 1) Disable Autoload before the new pass is loaded. This will prevent a product from being loaded that you will not be able to fully use. 2) After your birthday has passed, set up Autoload to load an adult pass.\nYes. Regardless of whether you use cash value or a pass, you must tap your card to the onboard validator every time you board. Your tap is your proof-of-payment.\nWhen using Clipper, you must pay your entire fare with the pass and/or cash value on your Clipper card. You cannot combine cash, tickets, or tokens with a portion from your Clipper card.\nVisit our Interagency Transfers page .\nClipper cards are for individual use. Each rider 5 years of age or older must have his/her own card. The Clipper cards also have a feature that stops you from tapping more than once on the same bus within a 5-minute timeframe. This feature was built in to protect you from accidentally tapping and being charged twice.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information."], "expected_facts": ["19", "cancel"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-004", "question": "Is there a way for low-income students to ride SamTrans free?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages, SamTrans does not offer free fares specifically for low-income students. However, there are two reduced-fare paths that may be relevant:\n\n**Youth Unlimited (for students 18 and younger):**\nSamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education, by swiping their pass in the farebox [doc:samtrans-fare-types]. The published policy does not specify how SED students obtain or apply for this pass.\n\n**Clipper START (for low-income adults ages 19–64):**\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus fares [doc:samtrans-clipper]. To learn more and apply, visit the Clipper START website [doc:samtrans-fare-types].\n\nIf a student is 18 or younger and qualifies as SED under California's definition, contact SamTrans directly to learn how to obtain the SED pass. If a student is 19 or older and meets the income criteria for Clipper START, you can apply through the Clipper START website.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-clipper", "samtrans-fare-types"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is there a way for low-income students to ride SamTrans free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages, SamTrans does not offer free fares specifically for low-income students. However, there are two reduced-fare paths that may be relevant:</p><p>**Youth Unlimited (for students 18 and younger):**</p><p>SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education, by swiping their pass in the farebox [samtrans-fare-types]. The published policy does not specify how SED students obtain or apply for this pass.</p><p>**Clipper START (for low-income adults ages 19–64):**</p><p>Income-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus fares [samtrans-clipper]. To learn more and apply, visit the Clipper START website [samtrans-fare-types].</p><p>If a student is 18 or younger and qualifies as SED under California's definition, contact SamTrans directly to learn how to obtain the SED pass. If a student is 19 or older and meets the income criteria for Clipper START, you can apply through the Clipper START website.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Clipper on SamTrans (Next Generation Clipper FAQ)</li><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "A faster, smarter Clipper has arrived. Next Generation Clipper has rolled out across Bay Area transit systems, bringing upgraded technology and new ways to pay your SamTrans bus fare.\nWith the new system, riders can:\nUse any contactless payment card or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, etc.) directly onboard — no need for a separate Clipper card.\nManage everything online or in the Clipper app, including balance, payment methods, and autoload settings.\nEnjoy faster updates when you reload or add passes.", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "The bus is an inexpensive way to travel throughout San Mateo County and into parts of San Francisco and Palo Alto."], "expected_facts": ["sed", "free"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-005", "question": "Do active-duty soldiers ride SamTrans for free?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, SamTrans publishes a waived-fare policy for active military personnel. [doc:samtrans-fare-types]\n\nAccording to the published policy, active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card ride SamTrans for free. [doc:samtrans-fare-types]\n\nThe published policy does not specify how active-duty soldiers obtain or register for this benefit — only what they must show at the point of use. For details on any application process or to confirm your eligibility, contact SamTrans directly.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-fare-types"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do active-duty soldiers ride SamTrans for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, SamTrans publishes a waived-fare policy for active military personnel. [samtrans-fare-types]</p><p>According to the published policy, active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card ride SamTrans for free. [samtrans-fare-types]</p><p>The published policy does not specify how active-duty soldiers obtain or register for this benefit — only what they must show at the point of use. For details on any application process or to confirm your eligibility, contact SamTrans directly.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "A faster, smarter Clipper has arrived. Next Generation Clipper has rolled out across Bay Area transit systems, bringing upgraded technology and new ways to pay your SamTrans bus fare.\nWith the new system, riders can:\nUse any contactless payment card or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, etc.) directly onboard — no need for a separate Clipper card.\nManage everything online or in the Clipper app, including balance, payment methods, and autoload settings.\nEnjoy faster updates when you reload or add passes."], "expected_facts": ["in uniform", "military identification"]}
{"id": "edge-samtrans-006", "question": "Can I use my Caltrain monthly pass to ride SamTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. According to SamTrans policy, \"The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\" [doc:samtrans-fares]\n\nThis means your Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass can be used on SamTrans Local and Express routes. You'll tap your Clipper card at the validator onboard the bus to use it. [doc:samtrans-clipper]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-clipper", "samtrans-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use my Caltrain monthly pass to ride SamTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. According to SamTrans policy, \"The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\" [samtrans-fares]</p><p>This means your Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass can be used on SamTrans Local and Express routes. You'll tap your Clipper card at the validator onboard the bus to use it. [samtrans-clipper]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Clipper on SamTrans (Next Generation Clipper FAQ)</li><li>SamTrans: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "A faster, smarter Clipper has arrived. Next Generation Clipper has rolled out across Bay Area transit systems, bringing upgraded technology and new ways to pay your SamTrans bus fare.\nWith the new system, riders can:\nUse any contactless payment card or mobile wallet (Apple Pay, Google Pay, etc.) directly onboard — no need for a separate Clipper card.\nManage everything online or in the Clipper app, including balance, payment methods, and autoload settings.\nEnjoy faster updates when you reload or add passes."], "expected_facts": ["2+ Zone", "Monthly"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-001", "question": "If I keep tapping my Clipper card on Marin Transit buses all day, what's the most I'll be charged?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nMarin Transit offers a daily maximum of $5 per day. The published policy states: \"Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride!\" [doc:marin-clipper] However, this frequent-rider maximum is only available on Clipper, and only trips on Marin Transit count towards the daily maximum. [doc:marin-clipper]\n\nSo if you keep tapping your Clipper card on Marin Transit buses all day, the most you'll be charged is $5 per day.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I keep tapping my Clipper card on Marin Transit buses all day, what's the most I'll be charged?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Marin Transit offers a daily maximum of $5 per day. The published policy states: \"Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride!\" [marin-clipper] However, this frequent-rider maximum is only available on Clipper, and only trips on Marin Transit count towards the daily maximum. [marin-clipper]</p><p>So if you keep tapping your Clipper card on Marin Transit buses all day, the most you'll be charged is $5 per day.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Advise the driver of your destination when boarding the bus and, if you need a transfer to continue your trip, be sure to ask for one when paying your cash fare. If you are using Clipper, you do not need to obtain a transfer, as transfers are automatically tracked on your card.\nTransfers to Marin Transit Buses: Riders are eligible for up to two free transfers within Marin County for up to two hours.\nTransfers to Golden Gate Transit Buses: Riders traveling within Marin County are eligible for up to two free transfers for up to two hours. Riders traveling regionally get a discount of the Marin Transit fare ($2.00 for cash fare, $1.80 for Clipper).\nTransfers to/from SMART: Riders transferring to or from SMART get a discount of $1.50. Riders must use Clipper or the SMART mobile app to use SMART.\nTransfers have the following restrictions:\nTravel must be made within two hours from time of issuance or otherwise indicated on the transfer\nTravel can be made on any local route and in any direction within the two hour window\nTransfers are issued by bus operator only at time fare is paid. Advise driver or agent of your destination and intention to transfer when boarding\nA transfer can be used only by the person to whom it is issued\nWith Next Generation Clipper, all riders who use Clipper will get a free local Marin Transit ride when transferring from any San Francisco Bay Area transit service and a discount when transferring to any other transit service in the Bay Area. Transfers are automatically tracked on Clipper cards (mobile and plastic) and contactless payment methods (card and mobile wallets).", "Clipper offers the following 50% discount cards for eligible riders. Discounts are good on Marin Transit and all other Bay Area transit agencies. There is no fee to apply. Your application information is secure—it is not shared with anyone else.\nClipper Youth Card: Riders ages 5-18 receive 50% standard Marin Transit adult fares. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Senior Card: Riders ages 65+ receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares and 50% off the Marin Transit 31-Day Pass. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Access Card (formerly RTC) : Riders with disabilities receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares. Caregivers may also receive the discount when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo. To apply, bring a completed application form to the Customer Service Center at the San Rafael Transit Center, Monday - Friday, 7 am to 6 pm, or call 415-455-2000. If you are unable to apply in-person for a personalized card due to disability, call 415-778-6622. Alternate format application materials are available. Learn more here .\nClipper START Card: Clipper START is an income-based program that provides 50% single-ride discounts to eligible riders. To qualify you must be a resident of the Bay Area, 19-64 years old and meet the income eligibility. CalFresh participants qualify automatically. Learn more here .", "Available on your phone or as a plastic card, Clipper is accepted on all Bay Area transit agencies . Add cash value or transit passes and pay fares with a tap!\nNote: Clipper is not currently accepted on Marin Access Paratransit or Marin Access Shuttles."], "expected_facts": ["$5", "clipper"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-002", "question": "Does Marin Transit's monthly fare maximum cover my Golden Gate Transit rides in Marin, like the 31-day pass does?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. Marin Transit's monthly fare maximum does not cover Golden Gate Transit rides. The published policy states that \"frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper\" and \"only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\" [doc:marin-clipper]\n\nBy contrast, the Marin Local 31-Day pass is \"good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County.\" [doc:marin-fares] So the 31-day pass covers both agencies within Marin County, but the daily ($5) and monthly ($40) fare maximums apply to Marin Transit trips only.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper", "marin-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does Marin Transit's monthly fare maximum cover my Golden Gate Transit rides in Marin, like the 31-day pass does?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. Marin Transit's monthly fare maximum does not cover Golden Gate Transit rides. The published policy states that \"frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper\" and \"only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\" [marin-clipper]</p><p>By contrast, the Marin Local 31-Day pass is \"good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County.\" [marin-fares] So the 31-day pass covers both agencies within Marin County, but the daily ($5) and monthly ($40) fare maximums apply to Marin Transit trips only.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>Marin Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are valid for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. In early 2026, the Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes will transition from a paper pass product to a pass used with Clipper. Paper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026.", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Local fares apply to travel within Marin County on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit services. Other fare information not shown in the table below include: Paratransit , Golden Gate Bus , Golden Gate Ferry , and SMART Train .\nFare Type | Cash | Clipper | 31 Day Pass\nAdult | $2.00 | $1.80 | $40.00\nSeniors (age 65+)* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nPersons with Disabilities* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nYouth (5-18) | $1.00 | $1.00 | $40.00\nChildren Under 5 (up to two per adult) | Free | Free | Free\nCollege of Marin students (With validated COM Card) | Free | Free | Free\n* To receive the cash discount, Seniors must have a valid Medicare Card, DMV ID card/license, or senior ID from another California transit service. Persons with disabilities must have a DMV Disabled Placard ID, RTC card, Medicare card, or ID card for persons with disabilities from another transit service. Personal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card.", "The Marin Access Fare Assistance (MAFA) program provides enrolled riders a variety of benefits to assist with fare payment including the ability to opt-in to receive a free pass valid for unlimited use on Marin Transit local bus service. The MAFA bus pass is not valid for use on Marin Access Paratransit, Muir Woods Shuttle, Golden Gate Transit, or SMART services. Learn More"], "expected_facts": ["only trips on Marin Transit", "31"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-003", "question": "How many free transfers do I get on Marin Transit, and does paying cash change that?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n**With Clipper:**\nYou get unlimited free transfers within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses are unlimited within that 2-hour window.\nAdditionally, free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\n[doc:marin-clipper]\n**With cash:**\nYou must advise the driver of your destination when boarding and ask for a transfer when paying your cash fare.\n[doc:marin-fares]\nSo yes, the payment method changes the transfer benefit: Clipper gives you unlimited free transfers within 2 hours, while cash fares allow up to two free transfers within 2 hours.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper", "marin-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How many free transfers do I get on Marin Transit, and does paying cash change that?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**With Clipper:**</p><p>You get unlimited free transfers within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.</p><p>Free transfers between Marin Transit buses are unlimited within that 2-hour window.</p><p>Additionally, free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.</p><p>[marin-clipper]</p><p>**With cash:**</p><p>You must advise the driver of your destination when boarding and ask for a transfer when paying your cash fare.</p><p>[marin-fares]</p><p>So yes, the payment method changes the transfer benefit: Clipper gives you unlimited free transfers within 2 hours, while cash fares allow up to two free transfers within 2 hours.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>Marin Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Advise the driver of your destination when boarding the bus and, if you need a transfer to continue your trip, be sure to ask for one when paying your cash fare. If you are using Clipper, you do not need to obtain a transfer, as transfers are automatically tracked on your card.\nTransfers to Marin Transit Buses: Riders are eligible for up to two free transfers within Marin County for up to two hours.\nTransfers to Golden Gate Transit Buses: Riders traveling within Marin County are eligible for up to two free transfers for up to two hours. Riders traveling regionally get a discount of the Marin Transit fare ($2.00 for cash fare, $1.80 for Clipper).\nTransfers to/from SMART: Riders transferring to or from SMART get a discount of $1.50. Riders must use Clipper or the SMART mobile app to use SMART.\nTransfers have the following restrictions:\nTravel must be made within two hours from time of issuance or otherwise indicated on the transfer\nTravel can be made on any local route and in any direction within the two hour window\nTransfers are issued by bus operator only at time fare is paid. Advise driver or agent of your destination and intention to transfer when boarding\nA transfer can be used only by the person to whom it is issued\nWith Next Generation Clipper, all riders who use Clipper will get a free local Marin Transit ride when transferring from any San Francisco Bay Area transit service and a discount when transferring to any other transit service in the Bay Area. Transfers are automatically tracked on Clipper cards (mobile and plastic) and contactless payment methods (card and mobile wallets).", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Clipper is the Bay Area’s electronic fare payment option that offers integrated transfers between all transit operators in the region. When paying with a Clipper Card onboard Marin Transit services, be sure you tag on when boarding the vehicle. You can setup a new card on your smartphone, buy one online, or buy a card in person. Learn More .", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Available on your phone or as a plastic card, Clipper is accepted on all Bay Area transit agencies . Add cash value or transit passes and pay fares with a tap!\nNote: Clipper is not currently accepted on Marin Access Paratransit or Marin Access Shuttles."], "expected_facts": ["up to two", "2 hours"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-004", "question": "How much is the youth fare for my 16-year-old on Marin Transit, and is there a free pass for students?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Youth fare (age 5–18):**\nFor your 16-year-old, the youth fare on Marin Transit is $1.00 with cash or Clipper, or $40.00 for a 31-Day Pass [doc:marin-fares].\n\n**Free pass for students:**\nMarin Transit offers two student options:\n\n1. **Annual Youth Pass (free for income-qualified students):** Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass [doc:marin-fares]. The published policy does not specify the income threshold or how to apply for this program.\n\n2. **College of Marin students (free fare):** College of Marin students with a validated COM Card receive free fares [doc:marin-fares]. This applies to COM students specifically, not all students.\n\nTo learn more about eligibility for the free annual Youth Pass or to apply, contact Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or call 415-455-2000 [doc:marin-clipper].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper", "marin-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is the youth fare for my 16-year-old on Marin Transit, and is there a free pass for students?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Youth fare (age 5–18):**</p><p>For your 16-year-old, the youth fare on Marin Transit is $1.00 with cash or Clipper, or $40.00 for a 31-Day Pass [marin-fares].</p><p>**Free pass for students:**</p><p>Marin Transit offers two student options:</p><p>1. **Annual Youth Pass (free for income-qualified students):** Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass [marin-fares]. The published policy does not specify the income threshold or how to apply for this program.</p><p>2. **College of Marin students (free fare):** College of Marin students with a validated COM Card receive free fares [marin-fares]. This applies to COM students specifically, not all students.</p><p>To learn more about eligibility for the free annual Youth Pass or to apply, contact Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or call 415-455-2000 [marin-clipper].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>Marin Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Clipper offers the following 50% discount cards for eligible riders. Discounts are good on Marin Transit and all other Bay Area transit agencies. There is no fee to apply. Your application information is secure—it is not shared with anyone else.\nClipper Youth Card: Riders ages 5-18 receive 50% standard Marin Transit adult fares. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Senior Card: Riders ages 65+ receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares and 50% off the Marin Transit 31-Day Pass. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Access Card (formerly RTC) : Riders with disabilities receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares. Caregivers may also receive the discount when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo. To apply, bring a completed application form to the Customer Service Center at the San Rafael Transit Center, Monday - Friday, 7 am to 6 pm, or call 415-455-2000. If you are unable to apply in-person for a personalized card due to disability, call 415-778-6622. Alternate format application materials are available. Learn more here .\nClipper START Card: Clipper START is an income-based program that provides 50% single-ride discounts to eligible riders. To qualify you must be a resident of the Bay Area, 19-64 years old and meet the income eligibility. CalFresh participants qualify automatically. Learn more here .", "Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Local fares apply to travel within Marin County on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit services. Other fare information not shown in the table below include: Paratransit , Golden Gate Bus , Golden Gate Ferry , and SMART Train .\nFare Type | Cash | Clipper | 31 Day Pass\nAdult | $2.00 | $1.80 | $40.00\nSeniors (age 65+)* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nPersons with Disabilities* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nYouth (5-18) | $1.00 | $1.00 | $40.00\nChildren Under 5 (up to two per adult) | Free | Free | Free\nCollege of Marin students (With validated COM Card) | Free | Free | Free\n* To receive the cash discount, Seniors must have a valid Medicare Card, DMV ID card/license, or senior ID from another California transit service. Persons with disabilities must have a DMV Disabled Placard ID, RTC card, Medicare card, or ID card for persons with disabilities from another transit service. Personal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card.", "Clipper provides a 10% discount off the adult cash fare for travel within Marin County. Learn more\nClipper START (income based) Clipper START provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders receive 50% discount off the cash fares on local riders within Marin County. To qualify, you must be a resident of San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old and have household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less. Learn more"], "expected_facts": ["$1.00", "income"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-005", "question": "Do children ride Marin Transit for free?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, children under 5 ride Marin Transit for free. The published policy states that \"Children Under 5 (up to two per adult)\" travel for free on both cash and Clipper fares, as well as on the 31-Day Pass [doc:marin-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do children ride Marin Transit for free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, children under 5 ride Marin Transit for free. The published policy states that \"Children Under 5 (up to two per adult)\" travel for free on both cash and Clipper fares, as well as on the 31-Day Pass [marin-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are valid for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. In early 2026, the Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes will transition from a paper pass product to a pass used with Clipper. Paper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026.", "Local fares apply to travel within Marin County on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit services. Other fare information not shown in the table below include: Paratransit , Golden Gate Bus , Golden Gate Ferry , and SMART Train .\nFare Type | Cash | Clipper | 31 Day Pass\nAdult | $2.00 | $1.80 | $40.00\nSeniors (age 65+)* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nPersons with Disabilities* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nYouth (5-18) | $1.00 | $1.00 | $40.00\nChildren Under 5 (up to two per adult) | Free | Free | Free\nCollege of Marin students (With validated COM Card) | Free | Free | Free\n* To receive the cash discount, Seniors must have a valid Medicare Card, DMV ID card/license, or senior ID from another California transit service. Persons with disabilities must have a DMV Disabled Placard ID, RTC card, Medicare card, or ID card for persons with disabilities from another transit service. Personal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card.", "The Marin Access Fare Assistance (MAFA) program provides enrolled riders a variety of benefits to assist with fare payment including the ability to opt-in to receive a free pass valid for unlimited use on Marin Transit local bus service. The MAFA bus pass is not valid for use on Marin Access Paratransit, Muir Woods Shuttle, Golden Gate Transit, or SMART services. Learn More", "Advise the driver of your destination when boarding the bus and, if you need a transfer to continue your trip, be sure to ask for one when paying your cash fare. If you are using Clipper, you do not need to obtain a transfer, as transfers are automatically tracked on your card.\nTransfers to Marin Transit Buses: Riders are eligible for up to two free transfers within Marin County for up to two hours.\nTransfers to Golden Gate Transit Buses: Riders traveling within Marin County are eligible for up to two free transfers for up to two hours. Riders traveling regionally get a discount of the Marin Transit fare ($2.00 for cash fare, $1.80 for Clipper).\nTransfers to/from SMART: Riders transferring to or from SMART get a discount of $1.50. Riders must use Clipper or the SMART mobile app to use SMART.\nTransfers have the following restrictions:\nTravel must be made within two hours from time of issuance or otherwise indicated on the transfer\nTravel can be made on any local route and in any direction within the two hour window\nTransfers are issued by bus operator only at time fare is paid. Advise driver or agent of your destination and intention to transfer when boarding\nA transfer can be used only by the person to whom it is issued\nWith Next Generation Clipper, all riders who use Clipper will get a free local Marin Transit ride when transferring from any San Francisco Bay Area transit service and a discount when transferring to any other transit service in the Bay Area. Transfers are automatically tracked on Clipper cards (mobile and plastic) and contactless payment methods (card and mobile wallets)."], "expected_facts": ["under 5", "up to two"]}
{"id": "edge-marin-006", "question": "I have a Clipper Access card with the attendant logo. Does my personal care assistant ride Marin Transit free?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, your personal care assistant does not ride free. Here is what the published policy states:\n\nPersonal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card [doc:marin-fares]. Additionally, caregivers may receive a 50% discount on Clipper fares when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo [doc:marin-clipper].\n\nSo your PCA receives a 50% discount on the adult fare—not a free ride. With cash, that would be $1.00 (50% off the $2.00 adult cash fare). With Clipper, the discount applies to the standard adult Clipper fare.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming these details with Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or 415-455-2000 if you have questions about your specific situation.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-clipper", "marin-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I have a Clipper Access card with the attendant logo. Does my personal care assistant ride Marin Transit free?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, your personal care assistant does not ride free. Here is what the published policy states:</p><p>Personal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card [marin-fares]. Additionally, caregivers may receive a 50% discount on Clipper fares when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo [marin-clipper].</p><p>So your PCA receives a 50% discount on the adult fare—not a free ride. With cash, that would be $1.00 (50% off the $2.00 adult cash fare). With Clipper, the discount applies to the standard adult Clipper fare.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming these details with Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or 415-455-2000 if you have questions about your specific situation.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li><li>Marin Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Local fares apply to travel within Marin County on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit services. Other fare information not shown in the table below include: Paratransit , Golden Gate Bus , Golden Gate Ferry , and SMART Train .\nFare Type | Cash | Clipper | 31 Day Pass\nAdult | $2.00 | $1.80 | $40.00\nSeniors (age 65+)* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nPersons with Disabilities* | $1.00 | $1.00 | $20.00\nYouth (5-18) | $1.00 | $1.00 | $40.00\nChildren Under 5 (up to two per adult) | Free | Free | Free\nCollege of Marin students (With validated COM Card) | Free | Free | Free\n* To receive the cash discount, Seniors must have a valid Medicare Card, DMV ID card/license, or senior ID from another California transit service. Persons with disabilities must have a DMV Disabled Placard ID, RTC card, Medicare card, or ID card for persons with disabilities from another transit service. Personal Care Assistants (PCAs) are eligible for 50% off the adult cash fare only when accompanying a person with disabilities who has an attendant logo on his/her RTC Discount Card.", "Clipper offers the following 50% discount cards for eligible riders. Discounts are good on Marin Transit and all other Bay Area transit agencies. There is no fee to apply. Your application information is secure—it is not shared with anyone else.\nClipper Youth Card: Riders ages 5-18 receive 50% standard Marin Transit adult fares. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Senior Card: Riders ages 65+ receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares and 50% off the Marin Transit 31-Day Pass. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Access Card (formerly RTC) : Riders with disabilities receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares. Caregivers may also receive the discount when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo. To apply, bring a completed application form to the Customer Service Center at the San Rafael Transit Center, Monday - Friday, 7 am to 6 pm, or call 415-455-2000. If you are unable to apply in-person for a personalized card due to disability, call 415-778-6622. Alternate format application materials are available. Learn more here .\nClipper START Card: Clipper START is an income-based program that provides 50% single-ride discounts to eligible riders. To qualify you must be a resident of the Bay Area, 19-64 years old and meet the income eligibility. CalFresh participants qualify automatically. Learn more here .", "Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "It’s easy to use Clipper to pay your fare on Marin Transit and other agencies:\nFind the Clipper reader on the bus or at fare gates at train stations or ferry terminals.\n“Tap on”—Hold your card or phone flat over the Clipper logo.\nWait for the beep or green light and continue boarding.\nNote: You do not need to tap off when you complete your ride on Marin Transit. For additional tutorials on using Clipper with other transit agencies, visit our YouTube channel .", "Available on your phone or as a plastic card, Clipper is accepted on all Bay Area transit agencies . Add cash value or transit passes and pay fares with a tap!\nNote: Clipper is not currently accepted on Marin Access Paratransit or Marin Access Shuttles."], "expected_facts": ["50%"]}
{"id": "fresh-001", "question": "How current is your MST fare information?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, the MST fare information in these passages was fetched on 2026-06-12 [doc:mst-fares], [doc:mst-fares-benefits], [doc:mst-veterans-resource]. \n\nFor the most current fares and policies, I recommend confirming directly with MST, as fare structures and programs can change. You can reach MST at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits", "mst-veterans-resource"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How current is your MST fare information?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, the MST fare information in these passages was fetched on 2026-06-12 [mst-fares], [mst-fares-benefits], [mst-veterans-resource].</p><p>For the most current fares and policies, I recommend confirming directly with MST, as fare structures and programs can change. You can reach MST at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or visit mst.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li><li>MST: Veterans Resource</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-002", "question": "Did SBMTD change its fares recently?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare increase in 16 years; MTD fares had remained unchanged since 2009 [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nThe fare change was implemented to address rising operating costs. Operating costs had increased by more than 10% since FY 2019, and MTD also lost approximately $3.0 million per year in Federal Transit Administration (FTA) Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding after being reclassified as a large urbanized area [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nNew passes became available on August 4, 2025, and MTD offered customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, toward the cost of a new pass. Old passes continued to work through August 31, 2025 [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Did SBMTD change its fares recently?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, SBMTD changed its fares recently. The base fare was adjusted from $1.75 to $2.50 as of August 18, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange]. This was the first fare increase in 16 years; MTD fares had remained unchanged since 2009 [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>The fare change was implemented to address rising operating costs. Operating costs had increased by more than 10% since FY 2019, and MTD also lost approximately $3.0 million per year in Federal Transit Administration (FTA) Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding after being reclassified as a large urbanized area [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>New passes became available on August 4, 2025, and MTD offered customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, toward the cost of a new pass. Old passes continued to work through August 31, 2025 [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here ."], "expected_facts": ["August 18, 2025"]}
{"id": "fresh-003", "question": "Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The published window had ended by the snapshot date.\n\nSacRT's ZipPass had a last day of use on April 30, 2026 [doc:sacrt-fares]. Since the corpus snapshot date is 2026-06-12, that deadline had already passed.\n\nFor current fare payment options on SacRT, the published policy describes the Transit Connect App and Tap2Ride as available methods [doc:sacrt-fares]. To confirm whether ZipPass has been replaced or whether any similar mobile fare option is now available, contact SacRT directly.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still use a ZipPass on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The published window had ended by the snapshot date.</p><p>SacRT's ZipPass had a last day of use on April 30, 2026 [sacrt-fares]. Since the corpus snapshot date is 2026-06-12, that deadline had already passed.</p><p>For current fare payment options on SacRT, the published policy describes the Transit Connect App and Tap2Ride as available methods [sacrt-fares]. To confirm whether ZipPass has been replaced or whether any similar mobile fare option is now available, contact SacRT directly.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Connect Transit Card is the region's electronic transit fare payment system. You can now purchase your transit fare online, never have to carry exact change again AND use the card on more than one agency!\n1. Go through the \"get a card\" process\n2. Create an account and buy your transit fare\n3. Receive an email confirming your order\n4. Your Connect Transit Card arrives in 7-10 Business Days"], "expected_facts": ["April 30, 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-004", "question": "How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The passages do not state an expiration date or effective period for the current Yolobus fares. The policy documents describe the fares and how to purchase them, but do not specify how long these rates remain in effect [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nFor information about whether fares may change or when the current rates expire, contact Yolobus directly:\n- **Yolobus Customer Service Center** (Woodland): Monday–Friday 7:00 am–7:00 pm, Saturday 9:00 am–3:00 pm\n- **SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center** (Sacramento): 1225 R St., Sacramento, (916) 321-2877\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long are the current Yolobus fares in effect?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The passages do not state an expiration date or effective period for the current Yolobus fares. The policy documents describe the fares and how to purchase them, but do not specify how long these rates remain in effect [yolobus-fares].</p><p>For information about whether fares may change or when the current rates expire, contact Yolobus directly:</p><p>- **Yolobus Customer Service Center** (Woodland): Monday–Friday 7:00 am–7:00 pm, Saturday 9:00 am–3:00 pm</p><p>- **SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center** (Sacramento): 1225 R St., Sacramento, (916) 321-2877</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus vehicles. To pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus.\nTransit Connect\nThe Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in 2026.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["June 30, 2027"]}
{"id": "fresh-005", "question": "Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No, the passages do not guarantee that fares will remain unchanged. Transit agencies adjust fares over time, and the published policies I have access to reflect only what was current as of their publication dates.\n\nThe passages show current fares for several agencies — for example, SLO RTA publishes $2 regular fare and $1 discount fare for Tap2Ride [doc:slorta-contactless], and Santa Cruz METRO shows $2 regular fare or $1 discount fare when tapping to pay [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]. However, none of these passages state that these prices are locked in or guaranteed for future dates. Fare changes are a normal part of transit operations and can happen at any time.\n\nTo confirm what you'll actually pay tomorrow, contact the specific agency you plan to ride and ask about their current fares. You can also check 511.org for real-time fare information.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-tap2cruz", "slorta-contactless"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Are the fare prices you quote guaranteed to be what I'll pay tomorrow?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No, the passages do not guarantee that fares will remain unchanged. Transit agencies adjust fares over time, and the published policies I have access to reflect only what was current as of their publication dates.</p><p>The passages show current fares for several agencies — for example, SLO RTA publishes $2 regular fare and $1 discount fare for Tap2Ride [slorta-contactless], and Santa Cruz METRO shows $2 regular fare or $1 discount fare when tapping to pay [scmtd-tap2cruz]. However, none of these passages state that these prices are locked in or guaranteed for future dates. Fare changes are a normal part of transit operations and can happen at any time.</p><p>To confirm what you'll actually pay tomorrow, contact the specific agency you plan to ride and ask about their current fares. You can also check 511.org for real-time fare information.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["In the event of a passenger lift failure, the bus operator will call for guaranteed back-up service, if another bus heading to the passenger’s destination will not arrive within 30 minutes.\nIf a METRO bus cannot be provided, our ParaCruz paratransit service will be used.\nEvery METRO bus is equipped with a Call Stop Announcement System that will announce every bus stop (except those within 600 feet of an earlier stop).\nUpon boarding, you may ask the bus operator for help in finding your destination.", "Discount fares are available through the Tap2Ride system for riders who have verified their discount eligibility. The discounts RTA offer through Tap2Ride are:\nFare Free for 80 years old and older, Half price fares for:\n65 years old and older\nMedicare Card Holders\nADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\nPlease see the DISCOUNTS fare page for eligibility requirements\nHow to qualify for fare discounts with your contactless card:\nRegister your contactless payment card with at https://benefits.calitp.org/", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "What will I be charged?\nWhen paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a Tap2Cruz reader, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method—you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for only $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay.\nIs there a weekly or monthly payment option?\nYes, please see the table below for daily and monthly fare-capping amounts when paying with a contactless payment method.\nThe 31-day cap operates on a rolling basis. This means that a cap will apply for all trips taken within a 31-day period from the first time you tap to pay, and on a rolling basis, based on usage thereafter.\nThe 31-day period starts with the first tap.\nHow do charges appear on my card statement?\nThe fare is charged to your card’s account and will include the description: “Santa Cruz METRO”\nTo view your recent METRO rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal (or check your bank card statement).\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure?\nYes. Tap2Cruz transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private?\nPersonal data will never be stored, sold, or given away by METRO or any third-party agent. METRO’s Customer Service Representatives do not have access to full credit card account numbers. Customer Service Representatives will answer customer support queries in accordance with Payment Card Industry practices.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history?\nNo. Just like your card issuer would not see what you order from a coffee shop or other retailer, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on METRO?\nNo, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method?\nNo. Each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nTip: If you have both a physical card and a digital wallet version of that same card, they count as different payment methods.\nDo I always have to use the same payment method within the 2-hour transfer window?\nYes. You cannot switch payment methods during the 2-hour transfer window. The fare calculation software will only charge your card $2 throughout this window, no matter how many times it is tapped. If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\nImportant: You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments?\nNo. Fares paid via contactless payment will never cost more than the standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go up to the fare cap.\nPlease review the fare chart for Tap2Cruz fare capping information.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board within that 2-hour window?\nYes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 regardless how many times you tap. You will not be charged again until 2 hours have passed.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment?\nDiscounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts.\nUtilizing CalITP Benefits, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card, ensuring you always receive discount fare when tapping!\nRiders who are eligible for a discount fare can still pay the existing discount fare with cash or SmartCard when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader."]}
{"id": "fresh-006", "question": "What will MST fares be next year?", "provenance": {"source": "Monterey-Salinas Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": true, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What will MST fares be next year?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-007", "question": "When does Transit Connect start working on Unitrans?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:\n\nTransit Connect will start working on Unitrans in summer 2026 [doc:yolobus-purchasing]. One passage also states \"with Unitrans joining in 2026\" [doc:yolobus-fares], which aligns with the summer 2026 timeframe.\n\nSince the corpus snapshot date is 2026-08-21, that summer 2026 window has already passed. For the current status of Transit Connect on Unitrans, contact Yolobus at their Customer Service Center (Woodland: Monday–Friday 7:00 am–7:00 pm, Saturday 9:00 am–3:00 pm) or Yolobus online at yolobus.com.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares", "yolobus-purchasing"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>When does Transit Connect start working on Unitrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21:</p><p>Transit Connect will start working on Unitrans in summer 2026 [yolobus-purchasing]. One passage also states \"with Unitrans joining in 2026\" [yolobus-fares], which aligns with the summer 2026 timeframe.</p><p>Since the corpus snapshot date is 2026-08-21, that summer 2026 window has already passed. For the current status of Transit Connect on Unitrans, contact Yolobus at their Customer Service Center (Woodland: Monday–Friday 7:00 am–7:00 pm, Saturday 9:00 am–3:00 pm) or Yolobus online at yolobus.com.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li><li>Yolobus: Purchasing a Ticket or Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus vehicles. To pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus.\nTransit Connect\nThe Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in 2026.", "1225 R Street, Sacramento, CA 95811 (Mon-Fri, 8:30 AM-5:00 PM)\n(916) 321-2877\nOther forms of IDs that qualify for a discount fare includes:\nMedicare Card\nCalifornia Driver’s License\nCalifornia ID Card\nOther transits agencies’ Disabled ID (such as Unitrans, SacRT, etc.)", "The Transit Connect mobile fare app allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone. Accepted on Yolobus and SacRT, with Unitrans joining in summer 2026. Download the free app from the App Store or Google Play.", "Connect Card is the Sacramento region’s electronic transit fare payment system and is accepted by most transit agencies in the Sacramento region. Connect Card readers are installed on all Yolobus and BeeLine vehicles.\nTo pay, tag the Connect Card reader with your card upon boarding the bus.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. Fares may be paid on board the bus with cash, but contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app.", "Riders have many choices when paying their fares. While fares can be paid onboard the bus with cash, contactless payment is more efficient and reduces bus boarding times—getting riders to their destinations faster. Contactless fare payment options include the Connect Card and the Transit Connect mobile app."], "expected_facts": ["summer 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-008", "question": "Is the SBMTD Line 16 shuttle still suspended?", "provenance": {"source": "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.", "refused": true, "language": "en", "citations": []}, "should_refuse": true, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the SBMTD Line 16 shuttle still suspended?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>I don't have a published policy document that answers that, and I won't guess about fares or eligibility. Please check your transit agency directly, or https://511.org (Bay Area) or the agency's own website for current information.</p></section>"}
{"id": "fresh-009", "question": "Can I still trade in my old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass after the fare change?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can still trade in your old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button on MTD's fare change page. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still trade in my old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass after the fare change?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can still trade in your old SBMTD magnetic-stripe pass. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused and partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button on MTD's fare change page. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district."], "expected_facts": ["September 30, 2025"]}
{"id": "fresh-010", "question": "When was the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change was January 1, 2009. [doc:sbmtd-farechange] The new fare structure took effect August 18, 2025, marking the first adjustment in 16 years. [doc:sbmtd-farechange]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>When was the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the last time SBMTD changed its fares before the 2025 change was January 1, 2009. [sbmtd-farechange] The new fare structure took effect August 18, 2025, marking the first adjustment in 16 years. [sbmtd-farechange]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["UCSB – First day of the quarter until 2 weeks into the next quarter. SBCC – 7 calendar days before the first day of classes until the last day of final exams, except Fall semester stickers are valid until 7 calendar days before Spring classes begin.\nUCSB & SBCC cards are not valid on the Downtown-Waterfront Shuttle.\n2025-2026 Valid Dates for Student Registration Stickers as Prepaid Bus Fare\nSBCC\nFall | Aug 18 – Jan 19\nSpring | Jan 19 – May 23\nSummer | May 25 – Aug 8\nUCSB\nFall | Sep 21 – Jan 15\nWinter | Jan 5 – Apr 10\nSpring | Mar 30 – July 3\nSummer | Jun 22 – Oct 2", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "New fare structure for both cash & passes went into effect August 18, 2025. New passes became available on August 4, 2025. MTD will offer customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass. Old passes will continue to work through August 31, 2025. To see the exchange matrix for old to new passes, please click the button below.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["2009"]}
{"id": "fresh-011", "question": "How much is the senior fare on Humboldt Transit and on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10:\n\n**Humboldt Transit Authority (HTA):** The published policy does not state a specific senior fare price. However, HTA does offer reduced fares for seniors aged 62 and older on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride is deducted at $1 for those eligible for a reduced fare [doc:hta-fares]. The policy states that \"the amount deducted will be either: $2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\" or \"$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\" [doc:hta-fares], but does not publish a single-ride senior fare amount.\n\n**Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST):** The published policy does not state a specific senior fare price. MST does offer discounts for seniors aged 65 and older [doc:mst-fares], and seniors can access discounted fares through the Cal-ITP Benefits program using a contactless card if they have a Medicare or Login.gov account or an active MST Courtesy Card [doc:mst-fares-benefits]. However, the exact discount amount is not published in the passages provided.\n\nFor current senior fare amounts at either agency, contact them directly or visit their websites.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares", "mst-fares", "mst-fares-benefits"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is the senior fare on Humboldt Transit and on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10:</p><p>**Humboldt Transit Authority (HTA):** The published policy does not state a specific senior fare price. However, HTA does offer reduced fares for seniors aged 62 and older on Stored Value passes ($10 or $20), where each ride is deducted at $1 for those eligible for a reduced fare [hta-fares]. The policy states that \"the amount deducted will be either: $2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\" or \"$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\" [hta-fares], but does not publish a single-ride senior fare amount.</p><p>**Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST):** The published policy does not state a specific senior fare price. MST does offer discounts for seniors aged 65 and older [mst-fares], and seniors can access discounted fares through the Cal-ITP Benefits program using a contactless card if they have a Medicare or Login.gov account or an active MST Courtesy Card [mst-fares-benefits]. However, the exact discount amount is not published in the passages provided.</p><p>For current senior fare amounts at either agency, contact them directly or visit their websites.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li><li>MST: Fares</li><li>MST: Benefits (Cal-ITP reduced fares)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations."], "expected_facts": ["62", "65"]}
{"id": "fresh-012", "question": "How long does the Elk Grove Senior Pass program run?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The Elk Grove Senior Pass program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first. [doc:etran-senior-pass]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the program is still active as of that snapshot date. For the most current status, contact SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877). [doc:etran-senior-pass]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-senior-pass"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long does the Elk Grove Senior Pass program run?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The Elk Grove Senior Pass program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first. [etran-senior-pass]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the program is still active as of that snapshot date. For the most current status, contact SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877). [etran-senior-pass]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Senior Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass.", "Children under age 5\nUp to two children ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger. Student fares apply for additional children. Students in grades TK-12th may qualify for fare-free transit with the RydeFreeRT program. Visit rydefreert.com to learn more.\nGroup Passes\nLos Rios Community College District student access card, CSU Sacramento One Card, City of Elk Grove employee or dependent ID card or RT employee or dependent ID card may ride free provided that the card and sticker is current. All participating passes are subject to change at the discretion of SacRT."], "expected_facts": ["June 30, 2027", "$100,000"]}
{"id": "fresh-013", "question": "Are the Elk Grove fares you have still the ones in effect?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the Elk Grove fares I have are dated **June 30, 2021** [doc:etran-fares]. That document states: \"In July 2021, e-tran and e-van services annexed into SacRT. The current e-tran and e-van fares will remain the same at this time.\" [doc:etran-fares]\n\nHowever, my information comes from documents fetched on **2026-08-13**, which is more than five years after that fare table was published. The passages do not state whether those 2021 fares remain in effect today or have changed since then.\n\nTo confirm the current fares, contact **SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877)** or visit their website.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Are the Elk Grove fares you have still the ones in effect?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the Elk Grove fares I have are dated **June 30, 2021** [etran-fares]. That document states: \"In July 2021, e-tran and e-van services annexed into SacRT. The current e-tran and e-van fares will remain the same at this time.\" [etran-fares]</p><p>However, my information comes from documents fetched on **2026-08-13**, which is more than five years after that fare table was published. The passages do not state whether those 2021 fares remain in effect today or have changed since then.</p><p>To confirm the current fares, contact **SacRT Customer Service at 916-321-BUSS (2877)** or visit their website.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "Children under age 5\nUp to two children ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger. Student fares apply for additional children. Students in grades TK-12th may qualify for fare-free transit with the RydeFreeRT program. Visit rydefreert.com to learn more.\nGroup Passes\nLos Rios Community College District student access card, CSU Sacramento One Card, City of Elk Grove employee or dependent ID card or RT employee or dependent ID card may ride free provided that the card and sticker is current. All participating passes are subject to change at the discretion of SacRT.", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass.", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877)."], "expected_facts": ["2021"]}
{"id": "fresh-020", "question": "Can I use a UC Davis Zip Pass on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The passages do not mention a UC Davis Zip Pass. The published Yolobus policy lists six pass types that are good for unlimited rides: UC Davis Aggie Card (Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only), UC Davis Extension International Program ID (with valid expiration date), Sacramento State Student ID (with valid commuter sleeve), Los Rios Student Access Card (with valid sticker), South Natomas TMA Pass (with valid expiration date), and Sacramento County DHA Pass (with valid sticker) [doc:yolobus-fares]. A UC Davis Zip Pass is not among them.\n\nFor current information about whether Yolobus accepts a UC Davis Zip Pass, contact Yolobus directly or visit 511.org.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I use a UC Davis Zip Pass on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The passages do not mention a UC Davis Zip Pass. The published Yolobus policy lists six pass types that are good for unlimited rides: UC Davis Aggie Card (Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only), UC Davis Extension International Program ID (with valid expiration date), Sacramento State Student ID (with valid commuter sleeve), Los Rios Student Access Card (with valid sticker), South Natomas TMA Pass (with valid expiration date), and Sacramento County DHA Pass (with valid sticker) [yolobus-fares]. A UC Davis Zip Pass is not among them.</p><p>For current information about whether Yolobus accepts a UC Davis Zip Pass, contact Yolobus directly or visit 511.org.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "The following passes are good for unlimited rides on Yolobus, including Express routes.\nAll fares are effective July 1, 2026 – June 30, 2027.\nUC Davis Aggie Card | UC Davis Extension International Program ID | Sacramento State Student ID | Los Rios Student Access Card | South Natomas TMA Pass | Sacramento County DHA Pass\nUndergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only | with valid expiration date | with valid commuter sleeve | with valid sticker | with valid expiration date | with valid sticker\nUC Davis Aggie Card: Undergraduate or UCDE Global Study Only\nUC Davis Extension International Program ID: with valid expiration date\nSacramento State Student ID: with valid commuter sleeve\nLos Rios Student Access Card: with valid sticker\nSouth Natomas TMA Pass: with valid expiration date\nSacramento County DHA Pass: with valid sticker", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["2026"], "forbidden_terms": ["is still valid", "you can still use", "will be accepted"]}
{"id": "fresh-014", "question": "Can I still buy a 7-Day Pass on Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the published window for purchasing 7-Day Passes on Santa Cruz METRO had ended by the snapshot date.\n\nThe policy states: \"Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.\" [doc:scmtd-fares-passes] However, 7-Day passes purchased on or before June 30, 2026 remain valid through December 31, 2026. [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nFor current pass options, Santa Cruz METRO offers 1-Day Passes ($6 for adults) and 31-Day Passes ($65 for adults), available through the METRO Splash Pass mobile app, the Transit app, or in person at Customer Service locations. [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nTo confirm whether Santa Cruz METRO has introduced any newer pass products since August 2026, contact them at (831) 425-8600 or visit scmetro.org.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still buy a 7-Day Pass on Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the published window for purchasing 7-Day Passes on Santa Cruz METRO had ended by the snapshot date.</p><p>The policy states: \"Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, &amp; 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.\" [scmtd-fares-passes] However, 7-Day passes purchased on or before June 30, 2026 remain valid through December 31, 2026. [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>For current pass options, Santa Cruz METRO offers 1-Day Passes ($6 for adults) and 31-Day Passes ($65 for adults), available through the METRO Splash Pass mobile app, the Transit app, or in person at Customer Service locations. [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>To confirm whether Santa Cruz METRO has introduced any newer pass products since August 2026, contact them at (831) 425-8600 or visit scmetro.org.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "To show our appreciation to our customers and community, we offer several free fare days throughout the year. On these special days, every METRO route and service is completely free. It’s our way of saying thank you for your commitment to cleaner, greener transportation for all.\n2026 Free Fare Days\nTransit Equity Day: February 4, 2026\nEarth Day: April 22, 2026\nSanta Cruz County Fair: TBD (September 2026) (Only Applies to Routes 79 & 79F servicing the Fairgrounds)\nCA Clean Air Day: TBD (October 2026)", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026.", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted.\nCash and Splash Pass are still accepted, as well.\nTo use Tap2Cruz, use the same method of contactless payment on each trip and tap the payment reader as you board the bus. If traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately. Don’t use the same contactless payment for multiple riders.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction."], "expected_facts": ["July 1, 2026", "December 31, 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-015", "question": "Can I tap my credit card to pay on a Santa Cruz METRO bus today?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can tap your credit card to pay on a Santa Cruz METRO bus. Santa Cruz METRO launched Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology, in Summer 2026. Contactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted. [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]\n\nWhen you tap to pay, your fare will be $2 for a Regular Fare or $1 for a Discount Fare, and you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide on that single tap. [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz] You must use the same payment method for each trip and tap the reader as you board; if traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately with their own card or device. [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]\n\nThere are no additional fees to use contactless payment. [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I'd recommend confirming current system status with METRO before your trip, as technology deployments can experience delays.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-tap2cruz"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I tap my credit card to pay on a Santa Cruz METRO bus today?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can tap your credit card to pay on a Santa Cruz METRO bus. Santa Cruz METRO launched Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology, in Summer 2026. Contactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted. [scmtd-tap2cruz]</p><p>When you tap to pay, your fare will be $2 for a Regular Fare or $1 for a Discount Fare, and you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide on that single tap. [scmtd-tap2cruz] You must use the same payment method for each trip and tap the reader as you board; if traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately with their own card or device. [scmtd-tap2cruz]</p><p>There are no additional fees to use contactless payment. [scmtd-tap2cruz]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I'd recommend confirming current system status with METRO before your trip, as technology deployments can experience delays.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, a contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express) and mobile wallets (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay, and Fitbit Pay) are accepted.\nCash and Splash Pass are still accepted, as well.\nTo use Tap2Cruz, use the same method of contactless payment on each trip and tap the payment reader as you board the bus. If traveling in a group, each rider must pay separately. Don’t use the same contactless payment for multiple riders.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "What will I be charged?\nWhen paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a Tap2Cruz reader, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method—you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for only $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay.\nIs there a weekly or monthly payment option?\nYes, please see the table below for daily and monthly fare-capping amounts when paying with a contactless payment method.\nThe 31-day cap operates on a rolling basis. This means that a cap will apply for all trips taken within a 31-day period from the first time you tap to pay, and on a rolling basis, based on usage thereafter.\nThe 31-day period starts with the first tap.\nHow do charges appear on my card statement?\nThe fare is charged to your card’s account and will include the description: “Santa Cruz METRO”\nTo view your recent METRO rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal (or check your bank card statement).\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure?\nYes. Tap2Cruz transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private?\nPersonal data will never be stored, sold, or given away by METRO or any third-party agent. METRO’s Customer Service Representatives do not have access to full credit card account numbers. Customer Service Representatives will answer customer support queries in accordance with Payment Card Industry practices.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history?\nNo. Just like your card issuer would not see what you order from a coffee shop or other retailer, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on METRO?\nNo, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method?\nNo. Each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nTip: If you have both a physical card and a digital wallet version of that same card, they count as different payment methods.\nDo I always have to use the same payment method within the 2-hour transfer window?\nYes. You cannot switch payment methods during the 2-hour transfer window. The fare calculation software will only charge your card $2 throughout this window, no matter how many times it is tapped. If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\nImportant: You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments?\nNo. Fares paid via contactless payment will never cost more than the standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go up to the fare cap.\nPlease review the fare chart for Tap2Cruz fare capping information.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board within that 2-hour window?\nYes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 regardless how many times you tap. You will not be charged again until 2 hours have passed.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment?\nDiscounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts.\nUtilizing CalITP Benefits, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card, ensuring you always receive discount fare when tapping!\nRiders who are eligible for a discount fare can still pay the existing discount fare with cash or SmartCard when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "What type of payment methods are accepted on METRO’s contactless payment system?\nMETRO accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. Accepted mobile wallets include Apple Pay & Google Pay, either on your phone or wearable.\nHow do I know if I have a contactless-enabled bank card?\nCards that are contactless-enabled have the symbol shown below (on the front or the back):\nMany banks and financial institutions have switched to contactless cards to make payments safer, faster, and more secure for their customers. Check with your card issuer if you are unsure if your card is contactless-enabled.\nIf you do not have a contactless card, you have options:\nLink an existing bank card to your smart device.\nOpen a safe, inclusive bank or checking account.\nOrder a contactless-enabled Cash App Card (no need to have a bank account).\nCan I still ride METRO if I do not have a contactless-enabled card or a payment-enabled device?\nYes. You can still pay for your fare using cash and Splash Pass. You will need to use the farebox to pay your fare, or visit either of our Customer Service Booths in Santa Cruz or Watsonville.\nHow do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on METRO?\nAs you board the bus to start your journey, look for the contactless symbol on the Tap2Pay readers:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat are Tap2Pay readers?\nTap2Pay payment readers are the face of METRO’s contactless fare payment system. The readers serve as point-of-sale terminals, allowing you to pay your fare and enter the system instantly. They are mounted at the front entrance of the bus and look like this:\nWhy doesn’t my contactless-enabled bank card work?\nIf your contactless-enabled bank card is not working, it may be due to one of the following reasons:\nYou have a new card. If you have a new contactless-enabled card, you might need to activate it first. If, after activating, your new contactless-enabled card it is still not working, please contact your card issuer at the phone number listed on the back of your card.\nYour card issuer has declined payment. This may be due to your bank account not having enough funds for the journey. Please check with your card issuer.\nYour card may be damaged or expired. If this happens, you’ll need to get your card reissued by contacting your card issuer."], "expected_facts": ["Summer 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-016", "question": "Can I buy a paper 31-day pass for SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, **no — paper 31-day passes are no longer available for SolTrans fixed routes**.\n\nEffective Monday, July 1, 2024, SolTrans eliminated all paper fare passes on fixed routes [doc:soltrans-paperless-fares]. The 31-Day Pass is still offered, but only through digital payment options: Clipper Card (for cash value and passes) or Token Transit [doc:soltrans-paperless-fares]. You may also purchase exact cash fares or day passes onboard the bus [doc:soltrans-paperless-fares].\n\n**Exception:** Paper passes remain available for SolTrans Paratransit service [doc:soltrans-paperless-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I recommend confirming current payment methods directly with SolTrans if you have questions about which digital option works best for your needs.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-paperless-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I buy a paper 31-day pass for SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, **no — paper 31-day passes are no longer available for SolTrans fixed routes**.</p><p>Effective Monday, July 1, 2024, SolTrans eliminated all paper fare passes on fixed routes [soltrans-paperless-fares]. The 31-Day Pass is still offered, but only through digital payment options: Clipper Card (for cash value and passes) or Token Transit [soltrans-paperless-fares]. You may also purchase exact cash fares or day passes onboard the bus [soltrans-paperless-fares].</p><p>**Exception:** Paper passes remain available for SolTrans Paratransit service [soltrans-paperless-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I recommend confirming current payment methods directly with SolTrans if you have questions about which digital option works best for your needs.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Service Alert: Paper Pass Elimination</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "One ride and day passes can be purchased on the bus, all other passes can be purchased at one of our ticket retail locations ( Where to Buy SolTrans Tickets and Passes ). Please put the pass in the farebox located next to the driver with the arrow facing down. ‍", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "All SolTrans paper passes expire and are not refundable or exchangeable. Passes worn or damaged to the point they are not readable by the farebox may not be accepted. Please do not laminate or punch holes in passes as this will invalidate the pass. ‍"], "expected_facts": ["July 1, 2024"], "forbidden_terms": ["you can buy a paper pass", "purchase a paper pass at"]}
{"id": "fresh-017", "question": "How old is the SolTrans fare information you're using?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the SolTrans fare information I'm using comes from documents fetched on 2026-08-13 [doc:soltrans-clipper-card, doc:soltrans-fare-table, doc:soltrans-ways-to-pay, doc:soltrans-paperless-fares]. That means the information reflects SolTrans's published policies as of that date.\n\nFor the most current fares and programs, I'd recommend confirming directly with SolTrans, as fare policies can change.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card", "soltrans-fare-table", "soltrans-paperless-fares", "soltrans-ways-to-pay"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How old is the SolTrans fare information you're using?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, the SolTrans fare information I'm using comes from documents fetched on 2026-08-13 [soltrans-clipper-card, doc:soltrans-fare-table, doc:soltrans-ways-to-pay, doc:soltrans-paperless-fares]. That means the information reflects SolTrans's published policies as of that date.</p><p>For the most current fares and programs, I'd recommend confirming directly with SolTrans, as fare policies can change.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li><li>SolTrans: Service Alert: Paper Pass Elimination</li><li>SolTrans: Ways to Pay</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "One ride and day passes can be purchased on the bus, all other passes can be purchased at one of our ticket retail locations ( Where to Buy SolTrans Tickets and Passes ). Please put the pass in the farebox located next to the driver with the arrow facing down. ‍", "All SolTrans paper passes expire and are not refundable or exchangeable. Passes worn or damaged to the point they are not readable by the farebox may not be accepted. Please do not laminate or punch holes in passes as this will invalidate the pass. ‍", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍"], "expected_facts": ["2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-018", "question": "Is the SolTrans Youth Ride Free program still running?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The passages provided do not mention a \"SolTrans Youth Ride Free program.\" \n\nThe passages do describe a Youth Fare Category available on SolTrans, which is available for the Local Service Area on 31-Day Passes and Day Passes [doc:soltrans-fare-table], but they do not state that youth riders receive free fares.\n\nThe only free-ride programs named in the passages are: children age 5 and under (maximum of two per fare-paying passenger) [doc:soltrans-fare-table], Solano Community College students with valid student ID [doc:soltrans-fare-table], and seniors 80+ [doc:soltrans-fare-table].\n\nTo confirm whether a Youth Ride Free program exists or has been introduced, please contact SolTrans directly or visit their website at soltrans.org.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the SolTrans Youth Ride Free program still running?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The passages provided do not mention a \"SolTrans Youth Ride Free program.\"</p><p>The passages do describe a Youth Fare Category available on SolTrans, which is available for the Local Service Area on 31-Day Passes and Day Passes [soltrans-fare-table], but they do not state that youth riders receive free fares.</p><p>The only free-ride programs named in the passages are: children age 5 and under (maximum of two per fare-paying passenger) [soltrans-fare-table], Solano Community College students with valid student ID [soltrans-fare-table], and seniors 80+ [soltrans-fare-table].</p><p>To confirm whether a Youth Ride Free program exists or has been introduced, please contact SolTrans directly or visit their website at soltrans.org.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍"]}
{"id": "fresh-019", "question": "Is the free reduced fare on FAX permanent?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the free reduced fare on FAX is **not stated to be permanent**. \n\nThe policy describes it as temporary: \"For a Limited Time, Reduced Fares are Free\" [doc:fax-fares] and \"*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available\" [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program]. The passages do not specify when this limited-time offer will end.\n\nTo confirm the current status and any changes to this program, contact FAX directly at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (open Monday–Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.).\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares", "fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the free reduced fare on FAX permanent?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the free reduced fare on FAX is **not stated to be permanent**.</p><p>The policy describes it as temporary: \"For a Limited Time, Reduced Fares are Free\" [fax-fares] and \"*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available\" [fax-reduced-fare-program]. The passages do not specify when this limited-time offer will end.</p><p>To confirm the current status and any changes to this program, contact FAX directly at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (open Monday–Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.).</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "Key | Pass Type\n* | Sells All Passes\n1 | 31 Day Full Fare\n*2 | 31 Day Reduced Fare – FREE at This Time\n3 | Handy Ride Pass\n4 | 31 Day Student Fare\nAll regular and discounted fares can be purchased at all ticket vending machines (TVMs) located at bus rapid transit stations along the Q (Route 1), as well as the TVM located outside the front doors of City Hall on the first floor."], "expected_facts": ["Limited Time"]}
{"id": "fresh-021", "question": "Is Clipper START still a pilot program, or is it permanent on County Connection now?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe passages describe Clipper START as \"a pilot program\" [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices] [doc:cccta-clipper-card], but they do not state whether it remains in pilot status or has become permanent. The published policy does not specify the program's current or intended status beyond calling it a pilot.\n\nTo confirm whether Clipper START is still operating and what its current status is, contact County Connection directly or visit their website at countyconnection.com.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card", "cccta-fare-types-prices"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is Clipper START still a pilot program, or is it permanent on County Connection now?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The passages describe Clipper START as \"a pilot program\" [cccta-fare-types-prices] [cccta-clipper-card], but they do not state whether it remains in pilot status or has become permanent. The published policy does not specify the program's current or intended status beyond calling it a pilot.</p><p>To confirm whether Clipper START is still operating and what its current status is, contact County Connection directly or visit their website at countyconnection.com.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card.", "The Clipper card will recognize one free bus-to-bus transfer within a 2-hour time period.\nThe Clipper card will recognize and honor free transfers between County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, WestCAT, SolTrans, and FAST at shared transit stops.\nBART transfer fares will be recognized and deducted accordingly ($1.00 for adult riders and $0.50 for Clipper START/youth/senior/RTC riders) from the cards stored cash value, or no deduction for 31-Day Pass users.\nMore About Clipper\nFor more information about Clipper or to set up an online account, please call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 or visit clippercard.com .", "You may wish to take advantage of County Connection LINK’s Advance Fare Payment System. The program was designed to simplify your travel by allowing you to prepay your trips.\nHow it works:\nMail a check to County Connection LINK, 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord, CA 94520.\nA minimum amount of $50.00 is required to establish an account. Make the check payable to County Connection LINK. Please state the name of the LINK client on the check.\nYour LINK trips will be automatically deducted from your account for the amount of your trip. If the balance in your account decreases to $25.00 or less, you will be notified by phone or mail. If your account reaches a “0” balance, you will be required to pay the cash/ticket fare for each scheduled pick-up until such time as your LINK account has sufficient funds.\nIf you have any questions regarding this information and/or the balance of your existing account, please contact LINK, Monday through Friday, from 8:00am to 5:00pm at (925) 938-7433.\nThe Advance Fare Payment System is offered as a convenience by County Connection. Individuals who fail to provide sufficient funds to cover their trips and fall into arrears will be required to pay for their trips as they board and reimburse any funds in arrears. Individuals whose checks bounce will be required to pay by money order or cash for future trips."], "expected_facts": ["pilot", "50%"]}
{"id": "fresh-024", "question": "How current is the San Joaquin RTD fare information you have?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, the San Joaquin RTD fare information I have was fetched on 2026-08-14 [doc:sjrtd-fares] [doc:sjrtd-dfc]. This is current as of that date. For the most up-to-date information or any changes made after that date, I recommend confirming directly with San Joaquin RTD at sanjoaquinrtd.com or by contacting their Customer Service Center.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-dfc", "sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How current is the San Joaquin RTD fare information you have?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, the San Joaquin RTD fare information I have was fetched on 2026-08-14 [sjrtd-fares] [sjrtd-dfc]. This is current as of that date. For the most up-to-date information or any changes made after that date, I recommend confirming directly with San Joaquin RTD at sanjoaquinrtd.com or by contacting their Customer Service Center.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Discount Fare Card (Access San Joaquin)</li><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Access San Joaquin staff will carefully review each application to ensure that only qualified persons are approved. Upon completion of review, a letter of certification or denial will be mailed. If your application for the DFC was denied, you have the right to appeal this decision.", "Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "To be certified for the Discount Fare Card (DFC), passengers must complete the following steps. Once the steps have been completed and all information is submitted, you will be notified within 21 days whether or not you have been certified for a DFC.", "Available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store, the Vamos Mobility App gives you the freedom to buy bus tickets right on your smartphone without having to wait in line or visit a Fare Vending Machine or Customer Service Center. This app only accepts credit/debit cards for payment.\nOn the Bus\nAll Express, Local, and Hopper buses has a farebox as you board through the front doors. Farebox accepts cash and coins. Please have exact amount ready. The farebox does not issue change."], "expected_facts": ["2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-actransit-001", "question": "Can I still pay my AC Transit fare with the Token Transit app?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes, you can still pay your AC Transit fare with the Token Transit app. [doc:actransit-fares] Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums.\n\nHowever, there is an important limitation: [doc:actransit-fares] Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. Additionally, [doc:actransit-fares] AC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future, and users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\n\nIf you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare through Token Transit, [doc:actransit-fares] please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still pay my AC Transit fare with the Token Transit app?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes, you can still pay your AC Transit fare with the Token Transit app. [actransit-fares] Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums.</p><p>However, there is an important limitation: [actransit-fares] Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. Additionally, [actransit-fares] AC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future, and users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.</p><p>If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare through Token Transit, [actransit-fares] please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "Unlike other AC Transit bus lines, Tempo riders pay at the station before boarding. Tempo Line 1T riders can pay with any valid AC Transit ticket or pass. Riders using Clipper can tag the reader at the station. Riders can also purchase paper tickets at the ticket vending machine with cash, debit or credit, or Apple Pay and Google Pay. Mobile payments are also accepted on Tempo. The cost to ride Tempo is the same as AC Transit local fares. Riders must carry proof of payment while onboard. Failure to show proof-of-payment may result in a citation. Learn more about Tempo Fares and Payment .\nDiscounted Fares\nYouth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive 50% off fares and passes to ride AC Transit.\nOther discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements.", "Youth, seniors, and riders with disabilities can receive discount fares and passes to ride AC Transit. Discount fares can be purchased using cash on board buses with valid ID, with Clipper by applying for a discount Clipper card, through AC Transit’s mobile payment platform, and at the AC Transit Customer Service Center. Other discount programs are provided through Clipper and are available to riders who meet certain eligibility requirements. Read on to find out if you are eligible, and how to purchase discount fares.\nYouth\nRiders aged 5-18 are eligible for Youth fares. The best way to pay is by applying for a Youth Clipper card . Children under 5 ride free.", "Los usuarios de Token Transit pueden seguir utilizando la aplicación para comprar viajes únicos y pases basados en el calendario mediante las tarifas máximas. Los usuarios de Token Transit no disfrutarán de las ventajas de transbordo disponibles a través de Clipper de última generación y las tarjetas bancarias sin contacto. Si tiene preguntas sobre reembolsos o tiene problemas técnicos durante la compra de tarifas móviles, comuníquese con Token Transit en help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit dejará de ofrecer soporte para Token Transit en el futuro. Se recomienda a los usuarios que cambien a Clipper de última generación o a las tarjetas bancarias sin contacto, ambas disponibles en una billetera móvil.\n¿Tiene preguntas sobre las tarifas? Sabemos que aún podría tener preguntas sobre qué tarifa es la mejor para usted. Si no encuentra la ayuda que busca, Comuníquese con nosotros .", "AC Transit offers many ways to pay for your ride so catching the bus is easier than ever. Choose to pay with cash, Clipper, or contactless payment. Learn about fares, how to get a Clipper card, paying with contactless payment, and more.", "The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express."], "expected_facts": ["discontinue", "transfer benefit"]}
{"id": "fresh-022", "question": "Does a virtual adult Clipper card cost anything on WestCAT?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nYes, a virtual adult Clipper card has a cost on WestCAT. Virtual Adult cards are free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load [doc:westcat-clipper]. This means while the card itself is free during this promotional period, you must load at least $3 in value onto it to obtain it.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-clipper"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does a virtual adult Clipper card cost anything on WestCAT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>Yes, a virtual adult Clipper card has a cost on WestCAT. Virtual Adult cards are free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load [westcat-clipper]. This means while the card itself is free during this promotional period, you must load at least $3 in value onto it to obtain it.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Clipper Card Costs Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load. Physical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online. Youth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "Riders using Clipper can load fare value and passes onto their cards at the WestCAT office in Pinole (some restrictions do apply), online at ClipperCard.com , and at participating retailers. Clipper users can also add fare value at terminals in BART stations."], "expected_facts": ["limited time", "$3"]}
{"id": "fresh-025", "question": "Did SLO RTA change its fares recently?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, I cannot tell you whether SLO RTA changed its fares recently. The passages show current fares and policies as of the snapshot date (2026-08-14) — for example, the Regional Day Pass at $5.50 and the Regional 31-Day Pass at $68.00 for general public — but they do not include historical fare information or announcements about recent changes [doc:slorta-passes].\n\nOne passage does note that \"All passes and fares are subject to change upon decision by the RTA Board of Directors\" [doc:slorta-passes], but it does not state when the most recent change occurred.\n\nTo learn about recent fare changes, contact SLO RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 ext. 4833 or tap2ride@slorta.org [doc:slorta-contactless].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-contactless", "slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Did SLO RTA change its fares recently?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, I cannot tell you whether SLO RTA changed its fares recently. The passages show current fares and policies as of the snapshot date (2026-08-14) — for example, the Regional Day Pass at $5.50 and the Regional 31-Day Pass at $68.00 for general public — but they do not include historical fare information or announcements about recent changes [slorta-passes].</p><p>One passage does note that \"All passes and fares are subject to change upon decision by the RTA Board of Directors\" [slorta-passes], but it does not state when the most recent change occurred.</p><p>To learn about recent fare changes, contact SLO RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 ext. 4833 or tap2ride@slorta.org [slorta-contactless].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "Unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A & B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated.\n$5.50 General Public\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "Good for unlimited rides on all RTA, Paso Express, South County, Morro Bay Transit and SLO Transit routes for 31 consecutive days.\n$68.00 General Public\n$34.00 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Valid for travel on RTA & the South County fixed route service. The applicable fare is deducted from the card whenever you use it. Stored value passes will deactivate one year from the date that they were created. If your pass deactivates and you have not used its full value, please contact our administrative office to move any remaining value onto a new pass at (805) 781-4833.\n$15.00 All riders", "Reach out to RTA Customer Service at tap2ride@slorta.org or cal; 805-541-2228 ext. 4833\nTo use Token Transit download the app to your smart phone from Google Play or the Apple App Store . When setting up the app, you will select RTA or South County Transit as your transit agency. You will need to enter in a payment card. Once set up you can purchase, store and then use bus passes on your phone. When its time to board, open the app and hold your phone up to the contactless fare equipment and it will beep.", "ADA Paratransit customers that present a “Certificate of Eligibility for ADA Paratransit Service” ID card may ride all RTA and the South County routes for free. Learn more about the Runabout Paratransit service , eligibility requirements and applications.\nAll passes and fares are subject to change upon decision by the RTA Board of Directors."], "expected_facts": ["April 6, 2026"]}
{"id": "fresh-026", "question": "Can I get my disabled discount automatically when I tap a contactless card on RTA?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo. The published policy does not state that disabled discounts are automatically applied when you tap a contactless card. Instead, you must register your card to verify your eligibility first.\n\nHere is how the discount works:\n\n**Eligibility:** ADA Disabled riders are eligible for discount fares on RTA [doc:slorta-contactless].\n\n**How to enable the discount on your contactless card:** You must register your contactless payment card at https://benefits.calitp.org/ to verify your discount eligibility [doc:slorta-discounts]. Once your card is registered, you can use it to pay discount fares on the Tap2Ride payment system [doc:slorta-discounts].\n\n**Important limitation:** The published policy states that \"ADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\" [doc:slorta-contactless]. This means that while the registration process exists, the discount may not yet be fully enabled through contactless payment for disabled riders. You may need to contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 (Monday–Friday, 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.) to confirm the current status of disabled-rider discounts on Tap2Ride, or you can pay the discount fare with cash when boarding [doc:slorta-contactless].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-contactless", "slorta-discounts"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I get my disabled discount automatically when I tap a contactless card on RTA?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No. The published policy does not state that disabled discounts are automatically applied when you tap a contactless card. Instead, you must register your card to verify your eligibility first.</p><p>Here is how the discount works:</p><p>**Eligibility:** ADA Disabled riders are eligible for discount fares on RTA [slorta-contactless].</p><p>**How to enable the discount on your contactless card:** You must register your contactless payment card at https://benefits.calitp.org/ to verify your discount eligibility [slorta-discounts]. Once your card is registered, you can use it to pay discount fares on the Tap2Ride payment system [slorta-discounts].</p><p>**Important limitation:** The published policy states that \"ADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\" [slorta-contactless]. This means that while the registration process exists, the discount may not yet be fully enabled through contactless payment for disabled riders. You may need to contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228 (Monday–Friday, 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.) to confirm the current status of disabled-rider discounts on Tap2Ride, or you can pay the discount fare with cash when boarding [slorta-contactless].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li><li>SLORTA: Discounts (eligibility and verification)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "Discount fares are available through the Tap2Ride system for riders who have verified their discount eligibility. The discounts RTA offer through Tap2Ride are:\nFare Free for 80 years old and older, Half price fares for:\n65 years old and older\nMedicare Card Holders\nADA Disabled and K-12 (on RTA trips) can pay the discount fare with cash as these discounts are not available on Tap2Ride yet\nPlease see the DISCOUNTS fare page for eligibility requirements\nHow to qualify for fare discounts with your contactless card:\nRegister your contactless payment card with at https://benefits.calitp.org/", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares.", "Riders can also link their contactless payment card with the California Integrated Travel Project (Cal ITP) to verify eligibility to pay discount fares. Once a payment card is registered , a rider can use that card to pay discount fares on the Tap2Ride payment system.\nTo start the card registration process, visit the Cal ITP benefits page here: https:// benefits.calitp.org / and select RTA for your agency.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "What can I do if my payment is not accepted by the payment reader? If you tap your contactless card or payment-enabled device and the payment reader indicates that the payment was not accepted, you will need to use another payment method to pay for your ride. Your payment may not be accepted for a few reasons:\nIt is possible that your payment method has been blocked if we have not received payment for a prior fare. Until payment is received for that fare, that payment method will remain blocked\nYour payment method has been blocked by your bank\nYour card or smart device is damaged\nYour card is not contactless-enabled\nYou may not have enough funds\nTo resolve an issue with your card, please contact your bank. In the meantime, you can use another contactless debit or credit card.\nHow can I get my blocked payment method accepted again? Once you have resolved the issue with your payment method, please contact RTA Customer Service by calling 888-678-2871, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Once the issue is resolved, it can take up to 15 minutes before you can continue to use the same payment method at RTA payment readers.\nHow do I apply for a refund or dispute a charge? If you suspect you have been charged more than once or have not been charged the correct fare, you can see all of your charges on the Traveler Portal and request a refund there as well.\nWhat if my payment method is lost or stolen?\nIf your card is lost or stolen, contact your bank first to ensure that your card is canceled and to prevent unauthorized transactions.\nIf your card is subsequently found, and if your bank did not cancel it, you may continue using your card."], "expected_facts": ["cash"]}
{"id": "fresh-vta-001", "question": "Can I still get a VTA Summer Youth Pass in mid-August?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe Summer Youth Pass is available to youth 18 and under from June through August [doc:vta-fares]. Since mid-August falls within that window, the published policy indicates the pass should still be available during that period.\n\nHowever, I recommend confirming directly with VTA before making your purchase, as the exact end date within August is not specified in the published policy. You can contact VTA at Fares@vta.org or call (408) 321-2300 (Monday–Friday, 8:30 am to 4:00 pm) [doc:vta-rtc-card].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares", "vta-rtc-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still get a VTA Summer Youth Pass in mid-August?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The Summer Youth Pass is available to youth 18 and under from June through August [vta-fares]. Since mid-August falls within that window, the published policy indicates the pass should still be available during that period.</p><p>However, I recommend confirming directly with VTA before making your purchase, as the exact end date within August is not specified in the published policy. You can contact VTA at Fares@vta.org or call (408) 321-2300 (Monday–Friday, 8:30 am to 4:00 pm) [vta-rtc-card].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li><li>VTA: Regional Transit Connection Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nClipper VTA monthly pass holders and VTA SmartPass riders who board DB Express within 2.5 hours of tagging on VTA receive one local fare credit on DB Express. DB Express Website", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nVTA Monthly Pass customers using Clipper receive a local fare credit if they board SamTrans within 2 hours of first boarding and tagging their Clipper card on VTA. SamTrans Website\nACE & Capitol Corridor\nVTA passengers do not receive credit on these systems. ACE Website - Capitol Corridor Website", "VTA works closely with other area transit operators to provide convenient transfers for regional trips, including regional transfer locations and inter-operator transfer or fare agreements.\nHighway 17 Express\nVTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Hwy17 Express. HWY 17 Express Wesbite\nCaltrain\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. Caltrain Website"], "expected_facts": ["June", "18"]}
{"id": "fresh-023", "question": "Can my teenager still get the $20 Summer Youth Pass on the Vine?", "provenance": {"source": "VINE published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The published passages do not mention a Summer Youth Pass for Vine Transit. The passages list Youth (6-18) fares and passes — including a Day Pass at $5.00, a 20-Ride Pass at $21.00, and a 31-Day Pass at $37.00 [doc:vine-fares] — but do not describe a Summer Youth Pass or a $20 pass product.\n\nTo ask whether Vine Transit currently offers a Summer Youth Pass, contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or visit vinetransit.com.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vine-fares"]}, "should_refuse": true, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can my teenager still get the $20 Summer Youth Pass on the Vine?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The published passages do not mention a Summer Youth Pass for Vine Transit. The passages list Youth (6-18) fares and passes — including a Day Pass at $5.00, a 20-Ride Pass at $21.00, and a 31-Day Pass at $37.00 [vine-fares] — but do not describe a Summer Youth Pass or a $20 pass product.</p><p>To ask whether Vine Transit currently offers a Summer Youth Pass, contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or visit vinetransit.com.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VINE: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Calistoga | St. Helena | Yountville | Napa | American Canyon | Kaiser Vallejo\nCalistoga | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00 | $8.00\nSt. Helena | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nYountville | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nNapa | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00\nAmerican Canyon | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00\nKaiser Vallejo | $8.00 | $8.00 | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X\nX = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.\nVineGo Discount Pass Eligible members of VineGo may purchase discount passes at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center or online. The purchase price of a discount pass is $20 for a $24 value.\nReasonable accommodation\nPublic transit passengers with disabilities may request reasonable modifications be made to accommodate their use of public transit. Requests should be made in written form and submitted to the Manager of Public Transit at 625 Burnell St., Napa, CA 94559.\nComments and complaints\nComments and complaints from the public may be received electronically through our customer service portal.\nClick here to submit a comment or complaint to NVTA staff. Additionally, prepaid comment cards are available in English and Spanish on Vine vehicles.\nWhen complaints are received, NVTA staff and/or contractor will acknowledge receipt of the complaint within 24 hours from the time it is received. All complaints should be investigated and resolved within 5 business days. Complaining party shall be notified of the agency’s resolution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes", "Transfers on the Vine are free. Please ask for transfers when boarding. Transfers are valid for 90 minutes after they are issued. No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11. There are no free transfers to the Route 29. Transfers cannot be used on the same route.", "VineGo is a shared ride service and provides transportation curb-to-curb, within 3/4 of a mile from the fixed route bus system. In order to use VineGo, you must be certified.\nPara Video en Español Haga Clic Aquí\nAre you in a VineGo service area ? Click the link to see if your address falls within out service area.\nWho qualifies?\nHow to apply?\nHow does the assessment take place?\nWhat should I bring?\nWhen do I know if I’m eligible? Is there an appeals process?\nHow does VineGo work?\nSchedule a ride\nTrack a ride (My Transit Manager)\nVineGo hours\nVineGo fares\nReasonable accommodation\nComments & complaints\nADA visitors to Napa Valley\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area\nDo I qualify for VineGo? Eligibility is on a case-by-case basis. According to ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) regulations, only people with physical and/or cognitive limitations that prevent them from riding the Vine bus can qualify for VineGo. Users who do not qualify for ADA paratransit are still eligible to receive travel training to learn how to navigate the Vine fixed-route system at no cost. If you are interested in receiving travel training, please call (707) 259-8635 or reach out to [email protected] .\nHow can I apply?\nTo apply to use paratransit, you must complete a two-part process:\nFirst, complete the VineGo Paratransit Application. Click below to access the VineGo Applications in your preferred language.\nEnglish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nSpanish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nTagalog VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nThen, e-mail the completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo as an attachment to your e-mail.\nAlternatively, if you prefer, you can print and mail your completed application to:\nParatransit Eligibility Office 625 Burnell Street Napa, CA 94559\nInstructions for completing a fillable PDF:\nClick one of the links above to access the paratransit application in your preferred language.\nClick on each individual field to enter your response.\nWhen finished, click the download button in the upper right hand corner of the document. (see image below)\nE-mail your completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo.\nNeed help with the application process? Call NVTA at (707) 259-8635 or the Paratransit Eligibility Desk at (707) 253-4649.\nAll assessments, including renewals, will be made by phone appointment. For more information or to schedule your appointment, please contact the VineGo Paratransit Eligibility desk at (707) 253-4649.\nADA eligibility interviews are conducted entirely over the phone by MV Transportation in Santa Rosa.\nIs there anything I should prepare for my assessment?\nYes. Be prepared with a list of:\nAny sort of mobility device you use\nA list of medications you are taking\nThe name of a personal care attendant (PCA) you travel with, if you have one.\nWhen will I know if I am eligible?\nThe Americans with Disabilities Act allows paratransit providers like VineGo up to 21 days to determine if someone is eligible. However, final decisions are typically made much sooner. If an application is not processed within 21 days, is presumptive eligibility granted on the 22nd day until and unless the application is denied in writing\nIs there an appeals process?\nIf a paratransit applicant is deemed ineligible and does not agree with the determination they have the right to appeal the decision. To formally appeal a decision a letter must be submitted to NVTA within 60 days of receiving an eligibility determination letter. The letter shall be addressed to NVTA 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559 to the attention of the Manger of Public Transit. Upon receiving the letter an appeals panel will be assembled to hear an applicant’s appeal. The applicant or someone they appoint to speak on their behalf shall be contacted and an in person meeting with the panel will be scheduled. The panel shall consist of a member of Napa County’s Paratransit Coordinating Council (PCC), a member of NVTA’s Evaluation Contractor’s evaluation staff, and the Manager of Public Transit or his/her designated staff member. The appeals panel will render a final written decision within thirty (30) days of hearing the appeal. Should the appeals panel not render a decision within the thirty (30) days after the completion of the appeals process, NVTA shall provide paratransit service to the applicant until a decision is rendered. Free transportation shall be provided to the appealing applicant and their personal care attendant (PCA) to the appeals hearing.\nHow VineGo works\nVineGo can pick up passengers from the doorsteps of their home to their destinations. If VineGo can’t pick up or drop off passengers due to difficulties or physical barriers, VineGo will pick up/drop off the passenger at the nearest safe location). Roundtrips are welcome.\nEnrollees can schedule trips up to 7 days in advance. Same-day reservations are also welcome; however, we recommend making trip reservations in advance, as this service is in high demand. All rides are scheduled over the phone.\nKey Notes:\nBE FLEXIBLE. VineGo dispatch can negotiate a pickup time up to 60 minutes before or after your requested pick-up time.\nRemember that this is a SHARED RIDE SERVICE (NOT A TAXI SERVICE). The driver may pick up or drop off other passengers along the way.\nScheduling a ride Call (707) 252-2600 to schedule a ride. (You must be enrolled in VineGo before scheduling a ride.)\nIf your mobility device has changed, please let your reservationist know when you call the schedule your ride. For example, if you previously used a walker but now use a wheelchair, please let the reservationist know. You and your loved ones or caretakers can track your ride and receive notifications using the My Transit Manager app.\nReservation hours: Monday – Friday: 8am – 6pm Saturday – Sunday: 8am – 5pm\nVineGo service hours Mondays – Fridays: 6 am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSaturdays: 6:30am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSundays: 8 am – 6 pm (6 pm-9 pm as available )\nFares\nFares range from $4 to $8 and are based on distance traveled. Passes are also available at a discounted price. Rides are charged based on the actual fare. ADA fares are no more than twice the adult fixed route fare charged by the Vine. Please note that VineGo is NOT an emergency transportation service. If you have an emergency of any nature, call 911.\nExact fare is required on all buses . All fares are non-refundable. Drivers cannot make change.\nVineGo One-Way Fares Calistoga St. Helena Yountville Napa American Canyon Kaiser Vallejo Calistoga X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 $8.00 St. Helena $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Yountville $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Napa $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 American Canyon $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 Kaiser Vallejo $8.00 $8.00 $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 X X = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.", "Riders can pay single-ride fares using cash directly on the bus. No change is given so please have the exact amount ready to pay as your board. Passes are sold at the following location:\nSoscol Gateway Transit Center 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559\nBulk Passes Need to buy passes in bulk? Fill out our bulk pass order form and send it to the emails listed in the form. Please allow a minimum of 24 hours upon receipt to process request for pick-up."]}
{"id": "fresh-samtrans-001", "question": "Is the SamTrans Summer Youth Pass still valid in August, and what does it cost?", "provenance": {"source": "SamTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The published window had ended by the snapshot date.\n\nThe Summer Youth Pass is valid from June 1 to August 31 [doc:samtrans-fare-types], but the corpus snapshot date is 2026-08-14. Since August 31, 2026 has not yet passed as of that date, the pass remains valid through the end of August. The cost is $40 [doc:samtrans-fare-types].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming current availability with SamTrans, as fare programs can change.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["samtrans-fare-types"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is the SamTrans Summer Youth Pass still valid in August, and what does it cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The published window had ended by the snapshot date.</p><p>The Summer Youth Pass is valid from June 1 to August 31 [samtrans-fare-types], but the corpus snapshot date is 2026-08-14. Since August 31, 2026 has not yet passed as of that date, the pass remains valid through the end of August. The cost is $40 [samtrans-fare-types].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming current availability with SamTrans, as fare programs can change.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SamTrans: Fare Types (passes, day passes, waived fares, Youth Unlimited)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["SamTrans offers a variety of fare products for youth, seniors, customers with disabilities and adult customers.\nThe Summer Youth Pass is a great deal for passengers 18 years old or younger with the fare offering unlimited rides from June 1 to August 31 for just $40.\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides, seven days a week during the month for which they are issued. Passes are valid for any route which has the same ride value or less as shown on the pass.\nLoad Monthly passes on Clipper cards via the Clipper mobile app ( available for download via the App Store or Google Play ), Clipper retail outlets , online or at the SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos.\nAll Senior and Youth riders are required to complete an application to receive their Clipper card.\nLocal/Express Eligible Discount Pass ($27) - For use by senior, disabled and Medicare cardholder customers on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age, disability or to see Medicare card.\nLocal/Express Youth Pass ($27) - For use by youth customers age 18 years and younger on any Local/Express route at any time. Bus operator may ask for proof of age.\nLocal/Express Pass ($65.60) - For use by adult customers on any Local/Express route at any time.\nDay passes allow SamTrans customers to ride all day using just one pass - an attractive choice for customers who ride more than one bus during the day. Day passes are available for Youth, Eligible Discount and Adult customers and are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus until the end of the service day (2 a.m.).\nDay passes are purchased via the SamTrans mobile app from the App Store or Google Play , onboard the bus or directly from the farebox. Customers should let the bus operator know they would like to purchase a Day Pass before inserting payment. Customers may use cash purchase. The farebox then will issue a magnetic Day Pass that customers will “swipe” at the farebox on future rides that day.", "Passenger Type | Local/Express Fare\nHow To Buy | Cash or Mobile# | Clipper# | Day Pass (Cash or Mobile) | Monthly Pass (Clipper)\nAdult (Age 19 through 64) | $2.25 | $2.05 | $4.50 | $65.60\nYouth* (Age 18 & younger) Eligible Discount** (Senior / Disabled / Medicare cardholder) | $1.10 | $1.00 | $2.00 | $27.00\n* Children: Two children age 4 or younger may travel free with each Adult or Eligible Discount fare-paying passenger. Additional children are subject to the Youth fare. ** Seniors (65 years or older) and passengers with disabilities , who present a Regional Transit Connection Discount Card or a current Disabled Placard Identification card issued by the Department of Motor Vehicles or a valid transit discount card issued by another California transit agency which is equivalent to the RTCDC, or those who are Medicare cardholders may ride for a discounted fare. Monthly Pass is sold from the 21st of the prior month through the 15 of the month when the pass is valid. For example, January Monthly Pass can be purchased from December 21 to January 15. Caltrain Riders: The Caltrain 2+ Zone Clipper Monthly Pass functions as a SamTrans monthly bus pass for all Local/Express routes.\nSpecial Fares for Youth, Seniors and More\nHow to Buy SamTrans Fares, Passes and Tickets\nHow to use Clipper on SamTrans\nSamTrans Fare Structure", "Riders can download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a free virtual card (for a limited time) with a $3 minimum load . Physical Clipper cards are available at participating retail locations, online at clippercard.com , and at select SamTrans transit centers.\nYouth (ages 5–18) and Senior (ages 65 and older) riders must submit an application to receive a discounted card. Applications can be completed online at Clippercard.com Riders with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card, formerly Regional Transit Connection Clipper Card (RTC).\nYou can add cash value or passes using the Clipper app, website, Caltrain ticket machines, or participating retailers.\nCash value never expires.\nPasses are time-limited. Autoload is available to automatically reload cash or passes when your balance is low. You can manage or cancel Autoload anytime through your Clipper account.\nBalances no longer display on validators but can be checked anytime:\nIn real-time via the Clipper app or website (for registered cards).\nThrough Apple Wallet or Google Wallet for mobile cards.\nCall Clipper Customer Service at 877.878.8883.\nAlways tap your card or device on the Clipper validator screen at the start of each trip.\nOn most SamTrans fixed routes, no tap-off is required.\nOn services such as RidePlus or other distance-based routes, you may need to tap off to ensure the correct fare.\nHold your card or device flat against the validator screen for at least several seconds until you hear a beep confirming a successful tap. If you tap too quickly, the validator can’t read your card.\nBay Area riders can now pay adult fares on SamTrans using a contactless bank card—credit or debit—Visa, Mastercard, American Express, or Discover, as well as through Apple Pay or Google Pay.\nImportant details:\nEach rider must pay with their own card or device.\nContactless bank card payments apply only to Adult fares. Discounted Youth, Senior, Clipper START, and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) fares still require a registered Clipper card.\nTo ensure the correct card is charged, remove your card from your wallet before tapping.\nAlways use the same card or device to tap on and off to avoid incorrect or multiple charges.\nCard Clash occurs when multiple contactless cards or devices are near the validator at the same time. This can cause a failed tap or charge the wrong fare. To avoid this:\nOnly hold the card or device you want to use near the validator.\nKeep other contactless cards or devices away while tapping.\nSamTrans Monthly Passes can be loaded onto Clipper cards for unlimited trips. They work in combination with stored cash value for transfers and special trips.\nFare capping for Open Payment is calculated separately from Monthly Pass usage. Riders should continue to tap every trip to receive transfer discounts and proper fare calculations.\nWhen a rider turns 19, their Youth Clipper card is no longer valid for discounted fares. They’ll need to apply for an Adult card and select appropriate fare products. Eligible riders can apply for Clipper START for low-income discounts or continue using standard Adult fares.\nOn your 19th birthday, Youth discounts end. If your card has cash value, Clipper charges adult fares from that day on. Any active Youth pass is canceled the day you become ineligible, even mid‑month. If you use Auto‑Reload for a Youth pass, turn it off before your birthday so a new Youth pass doesn’t load. To keep riding with discounts after 18, check if you qualify for Clipper START (ages 19–64). For mobile Youth cards, you can keep using remaining cash value, but you’ll need a new Adult card or Clipper START card to move your balance; then call Clipper Customer Service for a balance transfer.\nEach rider must use their own Clipper card or payment method. Clipper and Open Payment methods cannot be used to pay for multiple riders.\nEligible low-income riders (ages 19–64) can apply for Clipper START for discounted fares across SamTrans and other Bay Area agencies.\nYouth and Senior riders can apply online for discount cards, reducing paperwork and in-person visits. Note: Open Payment does not automatically qualify for discounted fares — a valid Clipper card must be used.\nRidePlus supports Open Payment for Adult fares. Discounted fares still require the appropriate Clipper card. Paratransit services such as RediWheels , RediCoast , and SamCoast will maintain their current fare collection systems.\nOpen Payment will not apply to these services during the initial rollout, and staff will continue following existing ADA and discount protocols.", "• Eligible Discount - $2.00 • Youth - $2.00 • Adult - $4.50\nEligible Discount, Youth and Adult ticket packages are available at SamTrans headquarters in San Carlos, at most Safeway stores in San Mateo County and at the Safeway stores at 4950 Mission St. and 3350 Mission St. in San Francisco, and other SamTrans sales outlets. The tickets may be used on all regular SamTrans bus routes. These are valid on all fixed-route buses and Ride Plus. The tickets may not be used to purchase Day passes and don't expire. Ticket Package Prices (10 tickets per package) Eligible Discount - $11.00 Youth - $11.00 Adult - $22.50\nThe Clipper ® START program offers a 50% fare discount for adult low-income SamTrans riders. To learn more and apply for the discount, visit the Clipper® START website .\nSamTrans offers a Monthly Parking Permit for its Colma Park and Ride customers.\nCertain Bay Area transit passes are accepted on SamTrans fixed-route service as a Local/Express-fare credit. Visit our Interagency Transfers page for participating agencies.\nTravel together for field trips or with friends, visit our Group Travel page.\nSan Mateo County public schools K-12 are eligible for free passes for field trips, please click here for more information.\nThe SamTrans Way2Go program allows companies, residential complexes and educational institutions to purchase annual unlimited-ride passes for all eligible employees, residents and students.\nLearn more about the Way2Go program .\nBus fares are waived for the following categories of passengers with proper identification or fare media.\nYouth Unlimited . In partnership with the San Mateo County Office of Education, SamTrans provides free bus fares for Socioeconomically Disadvantaged (SED) students, as defined by the California Department of Education by swiping their pass in the farebox.\nPeace Officers. Uniformed and non-uniformed, sworn peace officers showing proper identification that contains a color photo of the officer with printed description of his/her physical identifiers and a statement indicating the person is a peace officer – or has peace officer authority under a specific statute.\nMilitary Personnel. Active military personnel in uniform showing their non-expired military identification card.\nEmployees/Retirees. San Mateo County Transit District employees, qualified retirees, spouses, domestic partners and dependent children under the age of eighteen (18) by tapping their Clipper card to present the Way2Go Pass.\nBoard of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee Members. Board of Directors and Citizens Advisory Committee members showing their District identification.\nADA Paratransit-eligible Passengers. Passengers certified for Redi-Wheels and RediCoast (ADA) paratransit, and their personal care attendants by swiping their valid Redi-Wheels or RediCoast magnetic stripe identification cards.", "Clipper® is the all-in-one reloadable transit card for the Bay Area — and the easiest way to pay your SamTrans fare. Whether you ride every day or just occasionally, Clipper lets you tap and go without extra hassle.\nWhy Use Clipper on SamTrans?\nGo contactless. Tap your physical card or phone with the digital Clipper card (available in Apple or Google Wallet) onboard at the front of the bus at the Clipper machine reader.\nSimplify your commute. Load a Monthly Pass or add cash value to pay-as-you-go.\nHow to Get a Clipper Card\nOnline: clippercard.com\nIn person: At major retailers like Walgreens or at the SamTrans office in San Carlos.\nOn your phone: Download the Clipper mobile app and add your card to your digital wallet.\nDiscounts\nYouth and senior discount cards are free — simply complete a quick application online at clippercard.com.\nCustomers with disabilities can apply for a Clipper Access Card (Formerly RTC), available through participating transit offices. Call 877.878.8883 for more information on how to apply.\nIncome-qualifying adults can apply to the Clipper START program to receive 50% off single-ride bus, BART, rail, and ferry transit fares. Visit the Clipper Start site to apply today.\nClipper works on most Bay Area transit systems , so your card goes wherever you do.\nAs you board the bus, touch your card to the Clipper logo on the card reader located on your right (before the farebox). The device will read the Clipper card to see if you have a valid fare: current Monthly Pass; fare credit from another transit agency (such as a two-zone or greater Caltrain Monthly Pass); or cash value on the card.\nVisit the Clipper website for more information.", "Next Generation Clipper will make riding SamTrans — and connecting to other Bay Area transit — even easier.\nContinue using your Clipper card like normal. Your card will automatically be transitioned to Next Generation Clipper over time; this transition period may take eight to twelve weeks.\nThe easiest way to check on the status of your Clipper card after Dec. 10 is to call Clipper Customer Service at 877-878-8883 and ask whether your card has been upgraded."], "expected_facts": ["August 31", "$40"]}
{"id": "fresh-marin-001", "question": "Can I still buy a paper 31-day pass for Marin Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "Marin Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. The published window had ended by the snapshot date.\n\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026 [doc:marin-31day-transition]. As of the snapshot date (2026-08-14), that deadline has passed. Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are now only available on Clipper [doc:marin-clipper]. You can purchase a Marin Local 31-day pass online through the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card, or in person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center [doc:marin-31day-transition].\n\nPaper passes that were purchased before the deadline will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026 [doc:marin-31day-transition], but new paper passes are no longer available for purchase.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming the current status with Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or 415-455-2000 if you have questions about your options.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["marin-31day-transition", "marin-clipper"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Can I still buy a paper 31-day pass for Marin Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. The published window had ended by the snapshot date.</p><p>Paper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026 [marin-31day-transition]. As of the snapshot date (2026-08-14), that deadline has passed. Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are now only available on Clipper [marin-clipper]. You can purchase a Marin Local 31-day pass online through the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card, or in person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center [marin-31day-transition].</p><p>Paper passes that were purchased before the deadline will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026 [marin-31day-transition], but new paper passes are no longer available for purchase.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming the current status with Marin Transit at outreach@marintransit.gov or 415-455-2000 if you have questions about your options.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Marin Transit: Marin Transit Local 31-Day Passes on Clipper (paper-pass transition)</li><li>Marin Transit: Clipper (discounts, transfers, frequent-rider maximums)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in person at the San Rafael Transit Center or online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both! Do not dispose of your Clipper card ; keep it to use the next time you plan to purchase a 31-day pass or add cash value!\nPaper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026. Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026. Learn more here .\nAnnual Youth Pass\nAnnual passes are available for Marin County students ages 18 and under. Learn more . Students from income-qualified households may be eligible for a free annual Youth Pass .", "Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are valid for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. In early 2026, the Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes will transition from a paper pass product to a pass used with Clipper. Paper 31-day passes will no longer be sold after March 31, 2026.", "Clipper offers a variety of discounts to save you money on Marin Transit —making it easy to get around for less. ( Learn more about Clipper benefits on transit agencies all around the Bay Area.)\nReduced Adult Fares: Clipper riders receive a discount on standard adult cash fares. Local fixed-route service on Marin Transit costs $1.80 with Clipper—a 10% savings on the standard $2.00 cash fare.\nFree and Discounted Transfers: Clipper offers free and discounted transfers within the Marin Transit system and between Marin Transit and other agencies. Just tap your plastic or mobile Clipper card and the discounts are calculated automatically. Free transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus. Free transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus. Discounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFree transfers between Marin Transit buses: Unlimited free rides within 2 hours of first boarding a Marin Transit bus.\nFree transfers to a Marin Transit bus from any other transit agency will be free within 2 hours of the first tap on the Marin Transit bus.\nDiscounted transfers from Marin Transit to other agencies: Riders who transfer from a Marin Transit bus to another transit agency (e.g., Golden Gate Ferry, SMART Train) will get a discount of $2.85 off the total adult fare. Youth, Senior, Clipper Access and Clipper START cardholders receive half that discount.\nFrequent-Rider Maximums: Marin Transit offers daily and monthly maximums. Never pay more than $5 per day or $40 per month no matter how many times you ride! Frequent-rider maximums are only available on Clipper. Unlike the 31-day pass that riders purchase upfront and that is valid for local trips on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit, only trips on Marin Transit count towards the Marin Transit fare maximum.\n31-Day Passes: Paper 31-day passes are no longer available on Marin Transit ( learn more here ). Marin Transit Local 31-Day passes are only available on Clipper and are good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County. The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation.", "Clipper is the Bay Area’s electronic fare payment option that offers integrated transfers between all transit operators in the region. When paying with a Clipper Card onboard Marin Transit services, be sure you tag on when boarding the vehicle. You can setup a new card on your smartphone, buy one online, or buy a card in person. Learn More .", "Clipper offers the following 50% discount cards for eligible riders. Discounts are good on Marin Transit and all other Bay Area transit agencies. There is no fee to apply. Your application information is secure—it is not shared with anyone else.\nClipper Youth Card: Riders ages 5-18 receive 50% standard Marin Transit adult fares. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Senior Card: Riders ages 65+ receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares and 50% off the Marin Transit 31-Day Pass. Apply online or by mail, email or fax. You can also apply for a card in person at a Clipper Customer Service Center. You will need to verify your birth date with an accepted form of identification. Learn more here .\nClipper Access Card (formerly RTC) : Riders with disabilities receive 50% off standard Marin Transit adult fares. Caregivers may also receive the discount when accompanying a rider with a Clipper Access card that has an attendant logo. To apply, bring a completed application form to the Customer Service Center at the San Rafael Transit Center, Monday - Friday, 7 am to 6 pm, or call 415-455-2000. If you are unable to apply in-person for a personalized card due to disability, call 415-778-6622. Alternate format application materials are available. Learn more here .\nClipper START Card: Clipper START is an income-based program that provides 50% single-ride discounts to eligible riders. To qualify you must be a resident of the Bay Area, 19-64 years old and meet the income eligibility. CalFresh participants qualify automatically. Learn more here ."], "expected_facts": ["March 31, 2026", "Clipper"], "forbidden_terms": ["you can buy a paper pass", "purchase a paper pass at"]}
{"id": "ground-001", "question": "How much is a single ride on an MST bus if I pay cash?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A single ride on an MST bus costs **$2.00** if you pay cash. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nThe farebox accepts cash without requiring exact fare; if you do not have exact change, the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on an MST bus if I pay cash?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A single ride on an MST bus costs **$2.00** if you pay cash. [mst-fares]</p><p>The farebox accepts cash without requiring exact fare; if you do not have exact change, the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips. [mst-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "Single Ride 2 hours Cash or GoCard\nDaily GoPass valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly GoPass (7 Days)\nMonthly GoPass (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00\n— Contactless Payment\nSingle Ride 2 hours\nDaily valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly (7 Days)\nMonthly (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations."], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-002", "question": "What does a discounted monthly GoPass cost on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A discounted monthly GoPass on MST costs **$35.00** [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming this fare with MST for any time-sensitive plans.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a discounted monthly GoPass cost on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A discounted monthly GoPass on MST costs **$35.00** [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, I recommend confirming this fare with MST for any time-sensitive plans.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "Single Ride 2 hours Cash or GoCard\nDaily GoPass valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly GoPass (7 Days)\nMonthly GoPass (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00\n— Contactless Payment\nSingle Ride 2 hours\nDaily valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly (7 Days)\nMonthly (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window."], "expected_facts": ["$35"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-002", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-003", "question": "Does MST cap how much I can be charged per day with contactless payment?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, MST caps your daily charge at $6 per day when you pay with a contactless payment method. The cap also extends to $20 per week or $70 per month, whichever limit you reach first—as long as you use the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. [doc:mst-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does MST cap how much I can be charged per day with contactless payment?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, MST caps your daily charge at $6 per day when you pay with a contactless payment method. The cap also extends to $20 per week or $70 per month, whichever limit you reach first—as long as you use the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. [mst-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training"], "expected_facts": ["$6"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-003", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-004", "question": "How does the MST GoCard bonus work when I reload it?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "# MST GoCard Bonus\n\nWhen you reload your MST GoCard, you receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded [doc:mst-fares]. For example, if you load your card with $20, you receive $22 value on your GoCard [doc:mst-fares].\n\nA $10 minimum purchase is required [doc:mst-fares].\n\nYou can add value to your card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location, or you can purchase and reload online at the MST Store, by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871), or by mail to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940 [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How does the MST GoCard bonus work when I reload it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p># MST GoCard Bonus</p><p>When you reload your MST GoCard, you receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded [mst-fares]. For example, if you load your card with $20, you receive $22 value on your GoCard [mst-fares].</p><p>A $10 minimum purchase is required [mst-fares].</p><p>You can add value to your card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location, or you can purchase and reload online at the MST Store, by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871), or by mail to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940 [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "La GoCard es una tarjeta de valor almacenado. Los pasajeros pueden agregar valor a su tarjeta en cualquier momento visitando una ubicación de servicio al cliente de MST y usarla como una tarjeta de débito cuando viajan en autobús. La GoCard permite un embarque rápido. Simplemente toque su tarjeta en la caja de tarifas debajo de la pantalla digital y se deducirá la tarifa correspondiente. ¡Además, los pasajeros reciben un valor adicional del 10% cada vez que se recarga la tarjeta! Por ejemplo, carga tu tarjeta con $20 y recibe un valor de $22 en tu GoCard. Usa la tarjeta cuando la necesites y guarda el valor cuando no la necesites. Se requiere una compra mínima de $10."], "expected_facts": ["10%"]}
{"id": "ground-005", "question": "What is the regular one-way fare on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The regular one-way fare on Santa Barbara MTD is $2.50. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What is the regular one-way fare on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The regular one-way fare on Santa Barbara MTD is $2.50. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service."], "expected_facts": ["$2.50"]}
{"id": "ground-006", "question": "How much do seniors pay per ride on SBMTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nSeniors (age 65+) on SBMTD pay **$1.25 per one-way ride** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. \n\nAlternatively, seniors may purchase a 30-Day Pass for **$30.00** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes], or a 10-Ride Pass for **$8.50** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nSeniors can also take advantage of pay-as-you-go fare capping via Tap2Ride, which allows them to ride for free after paying the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass [doc:sbmtd-farechange].\n\nTo use the reduced senior fare, identification or proof of age is required [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-farechange", "sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much do seniors pay per ride on SBMTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>Seniors (age 65+) on SBMTD pay **$1.25 per one-way ride** [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Alternatively, seniors may purchase a 30-Day Pass for **$30.00** [sbmtd-fares-passes], or a 10-Ride Pass for **$8.50** [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Seniors can also take advantage of pay-as-you-go fare capping via Tap2Ride, which allows them to ride for free after paying the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass [sbmtd-farechange].</p><p>To use the reduced senior fare, identification or proof of age is required [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fare Change</li><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!"], "expected_facts": ["$1.25", "65"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-006", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-007", "question": "What does a 30-day adult pass cost on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A 30-day adult pass on Santa Barbara MTD costs $80.00 when purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a 30-day adult pass cost on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A 30-day adult pass on Santa Barbara MTD costs $80.00 when purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service."], "expected_facts": ["$80"]}
{"id": "ground-008", "question": "How long is a transfer valid on SBMTD if I need a second bus?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nOn SBMTD, a transfer is valid for **up to 60 minutes on the second bus** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. If you need a third bus to reach your destination, you can ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for **30 minutes** [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip only and are not valid for return trips [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. Also note that if you have a 30-day pass, transfers are not issued—you use your pass on each bus instead [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long is a transfer valid on SBMTD if I need a second bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>On SBMTD, a transfer is valid for **up to 60 minutes on the second bus** [sbmtd-fares-passes]. If you need a third bus to reach your destination, you can ask the driver on your second bus for another transfer, which will be valid for **30 minutes** [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Transfers are valid to complete your one-way trip only and are not valid for return trips [sbmtd-fares-passes]. Also note that if you have a 30-day pass, transfers are not issued—you use your pass on each bus instead [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus.\nTransfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. The transfer is valid for up to 60 minutes on the second bus. In the rare case that you need to transfer to a third bus to reach your destination, ask the driver on your second bus to give you another transfer. This transfer will be valid for 30 minutes.\nTransfers are valid to complete your one-way trip. Transfers are not valid for return trips. Passengers who use MTD’s 30 day passes will not be issued transfers. Use your pass on each bus in any direction.\nIt is MTD policy that drivers may wait up to 3 minutes beyond their scheduled departure times for transferring passengers. If you are concerned about catching your connecting bus, request that your driver call ahead to ask your next driver to wait for you.\nDuring peak-hour times when there is more frequent bus service, Lines 1, 2, 6 & 11 will not wait. There will be another bus in 10 minutes.\nMTD transfers are not valid on the Downtown and Waterfront Shuttles.\nOther Transfers\nWith a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!", "UCSB and SBCC Student Bus Pass Programs : All UCSB & SBCC students pay a transportation fee and may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding.\nDiscounted Fare Programs: Individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth are all eligible for reduced fares. Discounts are also available for pre-paid passes including Day passes, 10 – Ride passes, and 30 – Day passes.\nFare Capping: Pay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet. You can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nFree Transfers: If you need to use more than one bus to reach your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you pay your fare on the first bus. Transfers are free and can be used as your fare on up to two more buses. Free transfers work as follows: upon request, riders can receive a free 60 minute transfer to be used to board a second bus, heading in the same direction (i.e., an outbound Line 20 to an outbound Line 6). Riders may also receive a transfer on that second bus that is good for 30 minutes, also headed in the same direction. You can find more information on free transfers here .\nOther Transfers: With a valid transfer slip from VCTC, you pay only $1.00 to ride MTD!\nPacific Surfliner’s Transit Transfer Program: On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.\nAlternative Funding Sources: MTD is actively seeking new funding sources. MTD continues to apply to grant programs to operate and improve service, maintain and replace vehicles, and enhance transit facilities throughout the district.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "On the day you travel on the Pacific Surfliner train, show your valid Amtrak Pacific Surfliner paper ticket or e-ticket to the driver when you board the connecting bus or shuttle and ride MTD for free! Visit Pacific Surfliner for more info.", "All fares are one-way and may be paid with coins, bills, passes, or via Tap2Ride with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet. Exact change required when paying with cash; fareboxes do not give change and drivers cannot make change.\nYou can take advantage of pay-as-you-go discounted daily and monthly pass rates when you Tap2Ride.\nClick here to learn more, including how to get a contactless bank card or to add a bank card to the mobile wallet on your smart device.\nStandard\n$2.50 Regular one-way Youth (K-12th grade)\n$1.25 Seniors (age 65+) Persons with Disabilities* Persons with Medicare Cards* (Cash or Tap2Ride **) *Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\nFREE Children under 45 inches tall (three children per adult) Transfers (to complete a one-way trip) For more information on Transfers, click here .\n$6.00 Day Pass (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$2.50 Token for one-way fare", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap."], "expected_facts": ["60 minutes"]}
{"id": "ground-009", "question": "What's the local single-ride fare for adults on Yolobus?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The local single-ride fare for adults (ages 19–61) on Yolobus is $2.00 [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What's the local single-ride fare for adults on Yolobus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The local single-ride fare for adults (ages 19–61) on Yolobus is $2.00 [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-009", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-010", "question": "How much is an express bus ride on Yolobus for a regular adult?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "An express bus ride on Yolobus for a regular adult (ages 19–61) costs $3.25 for a single ride ticket. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is an express bus ride on Yolobus for a regular adult?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>An express bus ride on Yolobus for a regular adult (ages 19–61) costs $3.25 for a single ride ticket. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Our senior (62+) and disabled riders can take advantage of Yolobus’ reduced fares. To qualify for reduced fares, riders must show proper identification when purchasing fares and to bus operators when boarding.\nQualifying riders may obtain a reduced fare photo ID by visiting the Yolo Transportation District office or SacRT Customer Service & Sales Center:\nYolo Transportation District\n352 Industrial Way, Woodland, CA 95776 (Mon-Fri, 7:00 AM-7:00 PM, Sat 9:00 AM-3:00 PM)\n(530) 666-BUSS (2877)", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app."], "expected_facts": ["$3.25"]}
{"id": "ground-011", "question": "What does a monthly pass for local Yolobus routes cost?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided:\n\nA monthly pass for local Yolobus routes costs **$84.00 for regular adults (ages 19–61)** [doc:yolobus-fares] or **$42.00 for seniors and disabled riders (age 62+ or disabled)** [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nMonthly passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown and through the first business day of the following month [doc:yolobus-fares]. They can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a monthly pass for local Yolobus routes cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided:</p><p>A monthly pass for local Yolobus routes costs **$84.00 for regular adults (ages 19–61)** [yolobus-fares] or **$42.00 for seniors and disabled riders (age 62+ or disabled)** [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Monthly passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown and through the first business day of the following month [yolobus-fares]. They can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or on the Transit Connect app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "Yolobus-only and Sacramento Regional Transit Monthly Passes are valid for unlimited rides for the month shown, and through the first business day of the following month. Monthly passes can be purchased on the Connect Card website or the Transit Connect mobile app, but not onboard a Yolobus.", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers ."], "expected_facts": ["$84"]}
{"id": "ground-012", "question": "Is a Yolobus daily pass also good on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. A Yolobus Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on both Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Is a Yolobus daily pass also good on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. A Yolobus Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on both Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually on board a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect.", "A Daily Pass is valid for unlimited rides until 1:30am the day after purchase on Yolobus and Sacramento Regional Transportation (SacRT). A Daily Pass can be purchased individually onboard a Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare."]}
{"id": "ground-013", "question": "How much is a basic single ride on SacRT?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A basic single ride on SacRT costs $2.50 [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a basic single ride on SacRT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A basic single ride on SacRT costs $2.50 [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$2.50"]}
{"id": "ground-014", "question": "What does a SacRT daily pass cost for a regular adult?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A SacRT daily pass for a regular adult (age 19-61) costs $7.00 [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a SacRT daily pass cost for a regular adult?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A SacRT daily pass for a regular adult (age 19-61) costs $7.00 [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$7"]}
{"id": "ground-015", "question": "How long is a SacRT light rail single ride ticket valid?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A SacRT light rail single ride ticket is valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only [doc:sacrt-fares]. Alternatively, you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How long is a SacRT light rail single ride ticket valid?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A SacRT light rail single ride ticket is valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only [sacrt-fares]. Alternatively, you may pay a 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs."], "expected_facts": ["90 minutes"]}
{"id": "ground-016", "question": "Where can I buy MST GoPasses in Salinas?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, MST GoPasses are available in Salinas at two locations:\n\n1. **Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd.** (Regular GoPass only) [doc:mst-fares]\n2. **Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street** [doc:mst-fares]\n\nYou can also purchase GoPasses online at the MST Store, by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871), or by mail to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940. [doc:mst-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Where can I buy MST GoPasses in Salinas?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12, MST GoPasses are available in Salinas at two locations:</p><p>1. **Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd.** (Regular GoPass only) [mst-fares]</p><p>2. **Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street** [mst-fares]</p><p>You can also purchase GoPasses online at the MST Store, by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871), or by mail to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940. [mst-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations."], "expected_facts": ["Salinas Transit Center"]}
{"id": "ground-017", "question": "If I pay cash on MST and need a second bus, how do transfers work?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "If you pay cash on MST and need a second bus, you should request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus. [doc:mst-fares] You can then use that pass to transfer within the 2-hour window.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I pay cash on MST and need a second bus, how do transfers work?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>If you pay cash on MST and need a second bus, you should request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus. [mst-fares] You can then use that pass to transfer within the 2-hour window.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training", "MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required."], "expected_facts": ["2-hour"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-017", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-018", "question": "How much is a weekly GoPass on MST?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A weekly GoPass on MST costs $20.00 for a Regular Fixed Route or $10.00 for a Discount Fixed Route [doc:mst-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a weekly GoPass on MST?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A weekly GoPass on MST costs $20.00 for a Regular Fixed Route or $10.00 for a Discount Fixed Route [mst-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride 2 hours Cash or GoCard\nDaily GoPass valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly GoPass (7 Days)\nMonthly GoPass (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00\n— Contactless Payment\nSingle Ride 2 hours\nDaily valid until 2:00 AM\nWeekly (7 Days)\nMonthly (31 Days)\nRegular Fixed Route\n$ 2.00\n$ 6.00\n$ 20.00\n$ 70.00\nDiscount Fixed Route\n$ 1.00\n$ 3.00\n$ 10.00\n$ 35.00", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940.", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "MST accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets onboard all buses – and with fare capping, you will never be charged more than $6 per day, $20 per week, or $70 per month no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet each time you ride. (visit Benefits page for details about tapping and discounted fares)\nTransfers If you need to transfer and are paying with cash or a GoPass, request a 2-hour pass from your driver when boarding your first bus.\nIf you are paying with a contactless payment method, simply tap it on the contactless payment reader on each bus you board and you will only be charged $2 no matter how many times you tap and transfer within that 2-hour window.", "GoPasses are non-refundable and are available in monthly, weekly and daily options. The first time you use your GoPass, remember to insert it in the slot on the top left of the farebox with the front side of the pass facing you. This will activate your pass and print the dates the pass is valid so you will know when it is time to purchase a new one. After inserting the pass the first time you use it, the pass is swiped through the magnetic strip reader slot when boarding on subsequent trips.", "For your convenience, MST GoPasses are available at MST Customer Service locations and pass outlet locations throughout your community.\nCarmel Carmel Drug Store, San Carlos & Ocean\nGonzales City Hall, 147 Fourth St.\nGreenfield City Hall, 45 El Camino Real\nKing City City Hall, 212 S. Vanderhurst Ave.\nMarina Marina Transit Exchange, 280 Reservation Road\nMonterey MST Bus Stop Shop, 201 Pearl Street\nSalinas Costco, 1339 N. Davis Rd. (Regular GoPass only) Salinas Transit Center, 110 Salinas Street\nSand City Costco, 801 Tioga Ave. (Regular GoPass only)\nSeaside Mal’s Market, 1264 Noche Buena\nGopasses and Gocards may also be purchased anytime online at the MST Store , by phone at 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871) or by mail by sending your check, credit card information or money order to: GoPasses, 19 Upper Ragsdale Drive, Suite 200, Monterey, CA 93940."], "expected_facts": ["$20"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-018", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-019", "question": "I don't have exact change for the MST farebox — what happens?", "provenance": {"source": "MST published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "If you don't have exact change for the MST farebox, the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips. [doc:mst-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["mst-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>I don't have exact change for the MST farebox — what happens?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>If you don't have exact change for the MST farebox, the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips. [mst-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>MST: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["MST now accepts Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express contactless-enabled bank cards and mobile wallets. At this time, accepted mobile wallets include: Apple Pay , Google Pay, Samsung Pay , and Fitbit Pay . For more information, visit our Contactless Fare Payment Fast Facts and FAQs .\nCash\nCash is inserted into the farebox. Exact fare is not required. If you do not have exact fare the farebox will issue credit for use on future MST trips.", "The GoCard is a stored value card. Passengers can add value to their card at any time by visiting an MST customer service location and use it like a debit card when riding the bus. The GoCard allows for speedy boarding. Simply tap your card on the farebox below the digital screen and the appropriate fare will be deducted. Plus passengers receive an additional 10% value each time the card is reloaded! For example, load your card with $20 and receive $22 value on your GoCard. Use the card when you need it, save the value when you don’t. $10 minimum purchase required.", "Discount fare for:\n18 years and under\n65 years and older (see also: Benefits )\nIndividuals with disabilities\nMedicare Card holders (see also: Benefits )\nVeterans (see also: Benefits ), Veteran’s spouse/caregiver\nMST RIDES Paratransit Eligibility card holders\nSpecial Humanitarian Parole\nProof of age, an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) or Medicare Card is required upon boarding. MST also honors discount courtesy cards issued by other transit systems.\nVeterans may obtain an MST Courtesy Card ( English application / Spanish application ) with the following proof of service:\nDD Form 214\nDD Form 256N\nUS Department of Veterans Affairs ID card\nVeterans of Foreign Wars ID card\nMembership card from the American Legion\nDisabled American Veterans ID card\nCA driver license or ID card with “veteran” designation”\nContactless payment methods , cash, GoPasses, and GoCards can be used on all fixed route MST buses. Please have your payment ready when you board. The coach operator may ask to see proof of eligibility for a discounted fare (Individuals 18 years and under, 65 years and older, individuals with disabilities, and Medicare Card holders).\nGoPasses and GoCards can be purchased directly from MST, online, by phone or in person any MST customer service location. GoPasses may also be purchased at pass outlets throughout the county. See the pass outlets page for specific locations.", "MST has partnered with Cal-ITP —the California Integrated Travel Project, an initiative of the State of California—to offer a way to quickly verify your eligibility for a discount and then link that discount to your contactless-enabled bank card. (Visa, Mastercard, Discover, American Express). You’ll get your discount every time you pay with that same contactless card.\nRight now, the Cal-ITP Benefits website will work for anyone who meets the following criteria:\nIs eligible for the Medicare, Senior (age 65+), or Veteran Discount programs: Has a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card Has a contactless credit or debit card\nHas a Medicare or Login.gov account or has an active MST Courtesy card\nHas a contactless credit or debit card\n“Contactless” means a card you can tap instead of swipe to pay. Look for this symbol on the front or back of your card:\nSymbol description: four, curved lines facing sideways, similar to a wifi symbol.\nYou can get in touch with your bank card provider to request a contactless card—or you can order a contactless-enabled card from one of the providers in our Contactless Fare Payment guide.\nThe process to verify your eligibility for a discount and add it to your contactless card is completely online and secure! See how it works for MST’s Senior Discount, Veteran Discount, Medicare Discount and Courtesy Card holders.\nHave your ID and contactless bank card ready and get started now on the website below.\nTo continue, please click the below link to exit MST’s website and be redirected to the State of California’s eligibility verification website, Cal-ITP Benefits.\nGo to Cal-ITP Benefits", "The Monterey-Salinas Transit Group Discount Program provides 31 Day MST GoPasses at reduced costs. Based on the number of GoPasses your organization orders, participants can enjoy substantially reduced costs – making riding the bus an outstanding benefit. For more information, or to enroll, please complete the application or call 1-888-MST-BUS1 (1-888-678-2871).\n5-20 GoPasses – 25% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $52.00\n21+ GoPasses – 35% Discount\nMonthly GoPass – $45.00\nEnrolled Group Discount participants, please click here to place your order .\nGroup discount rates for residential complexes serving the elderly and disabled\nParticipating residential complexes, please click here to place your order .\nResidential housing complexes serving the elderly and disabled have the ability to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the complex pay upfront for a full year of passes for all of their residents. This would make the Monthly GoPass $210.00 per resident annually ($17.50 per month).\nNew! MST’s board of directors recently approved an expansion of the residential complex rate to include developments where 50% or more of the residents meet qualifying criteria of low-income, farm worker and/or other similar disadvantaged group. This program allows residential housing complexes to pre-purchase monthly bus passes in bulk at a 50% discount should the housing complex pay for a full year of passes for all of their units. This arrangement makes the GoPass $210.00 per unit annually or $17.50 per month.", "Monterey-Salinas Transit (MST) is partnering with veterans’ services organizations to select individuals needing transportation services. Selected veterans are provided free MST passes to honor their service to our country. For information email [email protected]\nVeterans Group Travel Training\nMonterey-Salinas Transit (MST) provides free fixed-route travel training to teach interested individuals how to safely and independently ride the MST bus system.\nTravel Training"], "expected_facts": ["credit"]}
{"id": "ground-020", "question": "What does the adult 10-ride pass cost on SBMTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The adult 10-ride pass on SBMTD costs $17.50. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the adult 10-ride pass cost on SBMTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The adult 10-ride pass on SBMTD costs $17.50. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Efficiency and Faster Boarding: Pre-paid fare systems allow passengers to pay before boarding, reducing the time buses need to spend at bus stops and instead getting you where you need to go.\nImproved Passenger Flow: Pre-paid systems contribute to smoother passenger flow on and off the bus, reducing crowding and making the overall transit experience more pleasant for everyone.\nCost Savings: Handling cash transactions can be resource intensive. Moving to a contactless payment system or pre-paid system can reduce the costs associated with cash handling services.\nFare Capping: Prepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually."], "expected_facts": ["$17.50"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-020", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-021", "question": "How much is a day pass on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A 1-Day Pass (Systemwide Day Pass) on Santa Barbara MTD costs **$6.00** and is good for unlimited trips on all routes in one calendar day. It can be purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride. [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a day pass on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A 1-Day Pass (Systemwide Day Pass) on Santa Barbara MTD costs **$6.00** and is good for unlimited trips on all routes in one calendar day. It can be purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride. [sbmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["$6"]}
{"id": "ground-022", "question": "What does the senior 30-day pass cost on Santa Barbara MTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SBMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The senior 30-day pass on Santa Barbara MTD costs $30.00 [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes]. This pass is for seniors aged 65+ and can be purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride [doc:sbmtd-fares-passes].\n\nTo obtain a senior 30-day pass, the published policy does not specify an application process. For details on how to purchase or register for this pass, contact Santa Barbara MTD at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office), or visit their fares page at sbmtd.gov/fares-passes/.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sbmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the senior 30-day pass cost on Santa Barbara MTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The senior 30-day pass on Santa Barbara MTD costs $30.00 [sbmtd-fares-passes]. This pass is for seniors aged 65+ and can be purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride [sbmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>To obtain a senior 30-day pass, the published policy does not specify an application process. For details on how to purchase or register for this pass, contact Santa Barbara MTD at 805.963.3366 (MTD Transit Center) or 805.963.3364 (MTD Business Office), or visit their fares page at sbmtd.gov/fares-passes/.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SBMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). In FY 2024, operating costs were nearly $3.0 million more than in FY 2019. Cost reductions would most certainly result in service reductions. Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses, and in fact the last time MTD adjusted fares was January 1, 2009. While our services are primarily government-funded, fares make up roughly 20% of our annual operating budget & with the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Higher fares will help us keep up with rising operational costs without cutting or reducing any of MTD’s current service. Scaling back our services could involve reducing bus operating hours, frequency, and, in some instances, eliminating routes altogether.\nMTD fares have remained unchanged since 2009.\nThe table above shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD adjusted the base fare as of August 18, 2025 from $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nMTD continues to provide reduced fares to eligible groups, including individuals with disabilities, seniors and youth. For a complete overview of the new discounted fare prices, please review the table above.\nTo see if you qualify for MTD’s reduced fare, click here .\nYes, UCSB & SBCC students may use their valid student ID as a bus pass by tapping it on the farebox upon boarding. UCSB staff & faculty ride Line 28 free and SBCC staff & faculty ride free by showing their employee ID.\nTo learn more about these programs, UCSB students, staff, & faculty click here and SBCC students, staff, & faculty click here .\nMTD has agreements with University of California, Santa Barbara and Santa Barbara City College whereby a portion of the students’ registration fees are provided to MTD. In exchange, the students can ride MTD buses for no additional charge.\nMTD is currently offering customers the opportunity to exchange unused & partially used magnetic-stripe passes through September 30, 2025, to go towards the cost of a new pass.\nYou have several options for paying your fares. To learn more about these forms of fare payments please click here .\nSpeed : Boarding the bus becomes quicker with pre-paid fares. This can reduce wait times at bus stops, prevent delays, and speed up your journey.\nConvenience : With pre-paid fares, you always have your fare ready to go. Simply board without worrying about having cash on hand.\nSavings : Pre-paid bus passes offer savings through fare capping and discounts compared to buying individual tickets.\nPay-As-You-Go (fare capping) allows riders to ride for free after they have paid the equivalent of a daily or monthly pass when using the Tap2Ride contactless payment. Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than a specified amount per day or 30 day period, no matter how many times you ride—as long as you pay with the same contactless card or mobile wallet.\nYou can find additional information on MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here .\nEligible Passes\nPrepaid fare capping is only available through Tap2Ride contactless payment. Learn more about MTD’s fare capping & Tap2Ride here\nRemaining Money\nWhen a passenger is $1 over the dollar value of pass activations needed to be fare capped, the passenger will be refunded the $1 back to the payment method used and the refund will show on the agency transfer reports as “fare capped.”\nTo learn more about MTD’s fare capping click here .\nWith fare capping, there is no upfront cost of a pass, just pay as you go and still enjoy the benefit of a multi-ride discount once you have reached the cap.", "Santa Barbara Metropolitan Transit District (MTD) is committed to providing reliable and affordable transit services to our community. While MTD’s fares have not changed in 16 years, cost pressures have come to the point of requiring a change in fare structure. We have carefully reviewed our fare system, received community feedback, and conducted a fare equity analysis. After careful consideration we adjusted our base fare from the previous fare of $1.75 to $2.50, consistent with the increased expense of operating services.\nImplementation of this new fare structure is a significant step toward assuring MTD’s services are dependable and economically sustainable in the long term.", "New fares are now effective as of August 18, 2025. For more information click here .\nUCSB CAMPUS STORE\n1 UCEN Road Santa Barbara, CA 93106 805.893.3819\nEASTSIDE LIBRARY\n1102 East Montecito Street Santa Barbara, CA 93103 Vending Machine\nMTD TRANSIT CENTER\n1020 Chapala Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3366\nMTD BUSINESS OFFICE\n550 Olive Street Santa Barbara, CA 93101 805. 963.3364 MTD accepts cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\nALBERTSON’S CARPINTERIA\n1018 Casitas Pass Road Carpinteria, CA 93013 Vending Machine\nGOLETA VALLEY COMMUNITY CENTER\n5679 Hollister Avenue Goleta, CA 93117 Vending Machine -Credit/Debit Card Only\nBY MAIL\nYou can order your passes through the mail by filling out & sending in an order form.", "The table below shows the update to fare structure from the current fare structure. MTD’s goal with this fare adjustment is to restore a portion of the approximately $3.0 million in annual FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities operating assistance that was lost when the 2020 Census found that the population of the Santa Barbara urbanized area exceeds 200,000 persons. The fare adjustment is estimated to generate approximately $1.5 million in increased revenue annually.", "The COVID pandemic caused unprecedented changes for the District from 2020 until 2024. Labor shortages forced reduction of services in 2022. Ridership remains below pre-pandemic levels, however it has continued to grow, rebounding to more than 72% of fiscal year (FY) 2019 passengers. One thing that has been a constant is the increase in operating expenses, which have grown year over year (with the exception of FY 2021, when the worst of the pandemic led to a substantial reduction in service and, thus, in cost). Fares have not kept pace with this growth in expenses.\nBudget Scenario Operating costs have increased by more than 10% since FY 2019. The average fare revenue collected per passenger has dropped from $1.11 to $1.07, still rebounding from the COVID pandemic. Fare revenue only accounts for 20% of MTD’s annual operating budget. The inflation rate for the fiscal year ending June 30, 2024 was 3.2%, indicating a trend for additional increases.\nThe major revenue sources for the District operations are fares, Federal Transit Administration (FTA) operating assistance, and sales tax. With the FTA classifying Santa Barbara as a large urbanized area (UZA) due to the population growth confirmed by the 2020 Census, MTD is no longer eligible for FTA Small Transit Intensive Cities (STIC) funding of approximately $3.0 million per year, thus eliminating that funding source. Federal operating assistance has grown a little; however, the apportionment of funds is fixed and there doesn’t appear to be much of a change looking forward. Sales tax revenues experienced growth with inflationary price increases during the COVID pandemic; however, recently these increases have been leveling out.\nRevenue shortfalls and increasing operating expenditures in the areas of wages and benefits, fuel, insurance, and utilities are expected, continuing to increase the deficit. Without a fare adjustment, the District would be forced to reduce service.", "10-RIDE PASS Discounted\n$17.50 Adult\n$12.50 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$8.50 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* Apply here for persons with disabilities.\n*Identification, proof of age, a Medicare card, or MTD photo ID is required. MTD can accept cash, a local imprinted check, or a money order (payable to “MTD”) as payment for passes.\n30-DAY PASS Discounted\n$80.00 Adult (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride )\n$65.00 Youth (K-12th grade)\n$30.00 Senior (65+)* Mobility (For Disabled Persons and Medicare Card Holders)* (purchased in advance or via Tap2Ride**) * Apply here for a physical pass for persons with disabilities. **Before you Tap2Ride, register your contactless bank card via Cal-ITP Benefits to receive a discounted fare.\n1-DAY PASS\nSystemwide Day Pass $6.00 (purchased in advance or pay-as-you-go via Tap2Ride) Good on all routes. Valid for unlimited trips in one calendar day.\n30-Day Passes are valid for 30 days from first use. 30-Day passes are not transferable and transfers are not available with this pass.\n10-Ride Passes do not expire.\nImproper use of passes may result in revocation or confiscation.\nAll pass sales are final. No refunds. Passes have no cash value."], "expected_facts": ["$30"]}
{"id": "ground-023", "question": "What's the regular adult fare for an intercity Yolobus trip?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The regular adult fare for an intercity Yolobus trip is $2.25. [doc:yolobus-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What's the regular adult fare for an intercity Yolobus trip?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The regular adult fare for an intercity Yolobus trip is $2.25. [yolobus-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Regular Adult (19-61) | Senior/Disabled Senior/Disabled (62+/Disabled*)\nSingle Ride Tickets\nLocal Fare | $2.00 | $1.00\nIntercity Fare | $2.25 | $1.00\nExpress | $3.25 | $1.50\nExpress Upgrade | $1.00 | N/A†\nMonthly Passes\nLocal Routes | $84.00 | $42.00+\nIntercity Routes | $93.50 | $42.00+\nExpress Routes | $121.00 | $42.00+\nTransfers\nBetween Yolobus (Non-Express) | $0.25 | $0.25\nBetween Yolobus (Express) | $1.00 | $0.50\nYolobus + SacRT Shared Passes\nDaily Pass SacRT + Yolobus | $7.00 | $3.50\nMonthly SacRT + Yolobus | $100.00 | $50.00\n* To meet senior/disabled criteria, must possess one of the following as valid proof of identification upon boarding Yolobus: SacRT Senior or Disabled Photo ID; Medicare Card (NOT Medi-Cal); DMV Driver’s License or government-issued ID Card; Any other discount ID issued by another transit agency † Senior/disabled monthly pass works on express routes already, does not require upgrade) ‡ Senior/Disabled monthly pass is valid on all routes, including local, intercity and express routes", "ADA-eligible riders can use ADA paratransit (“Yolobus Special”) service. The Yolobus Special fares vary by distance (local vs. intercity).\nADA Paratransit (\"Yolobus Special\") Products\nADA Paratransit Local Fare | $4.00\nADA Paratransit Intercity Fare | $4.50\nADA Paratransit Premium Fare* | $6.00\n* Premium service is available to medical facilities only in portions of Sacramento and Vacaville that are beyond ¾ miles of Yolobus fixed routes. Destinations within ¾ mile of a Yolobus fixed route are charged the Local or Intercity fare, whichever is applicable\nHow to purchase a ticket or a pass\nMore information on how to purchase a ticket or a pass is available below.", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers >", "If you are planning to connect with another Yolobus route to complete your trip, ask the driver for a transfer slip. You may also purchase a single fare + transfer in the Transit Connect app. Learn more about transfers .", "Tickets, passes, and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or the Transit Connect app.", "All Yolobus vehicles have fareboxes to accept cash payments. Exact change is required as our bus operators don’t carry change or provide refunds. Passes on Connect Card can be purchased with cash at the Yolobus Customer Service Center .\nIn-Person Ticket and Pass Sales\nThe Yolobus Customer Service Center in Woodland offers transit fare sales and Connect Card services:\nBusiness Hours\nMonday through Friday, excluding holidays 7:00 am – 7:00 pm\nSaturday 9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nConnect Card Services Offered\nIssue New or Replacement Connect Cards for all riders, including eligible Discount Riders (Seniors, Persons with Disabilities)\nLoad Cash Value, Tickets and/or Passes onto Connect Cards\nAlternative ticket and pass sales location in Sacramento\nTickets, passes and Connect Card services are also available in Sacramento at the Sacramento Regional Transit District (SacRT) Customer Service and Sales Center, 1225 R St., Sacramento (916) 321-2877\nSingle Ride\nTickets are for a one-way trip. A ticket can be purchased individually on Yolobus or by using Connect Card or Transit Connect."], "expected_facts": ["$2.25"]}
{"id": "ground-024", "question": "How much does a BeeLine on-demand ride in Woodland cost?", "provenance": {"source": "Yolobus published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A BeeLine on-demand ride in Woodland costs $3.00 for a regular fare [doc:yolobus-fares]. If you are a senior (62+), disabled, or youth (0-18), the fare is $1.50 [doc:yolobus-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-21.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["yolobus-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much does a BeeLine on-demand ride in Woodland cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A BeeLine on-demand ride in Woodland costs $3.00 for a regular fare [yolobus-fares]. If you are a senior (62+), disabled, or youth (0-18), the fare is $1.50 [yolobus-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-21.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>Yolobus: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["BeeLine on-demand microtransit fares are a one-way, flat-fee that is the same for all riders.\nRegular | Seniors (62+)/Disabled & Youth (0-18)\nKnights Landing | $2.00 | $1.50\nWinters | $2.00 | $1.50\nWoodland | $3.00 | $1.50", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "The AC Transit EasyPass program is an annual group transit benefit for eligible organizations. This program is offered to employers, residential communities, and colleges or universities whose user group will consist of 100 or more people or residential units. Participation in this program provides access to unlimited bus rides all year long by paying an annual fee for everyone in your group. Sponsoring a pool of participants, keeps individual pass costs low, giving every participant access to unlimited bus service whenever they want. While EasyPass programs are invoiced annually, they cost as little as $5-$18 a month per participant depending on program, location and group size. Visit our EasyPass page to learn more.", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "VineGo is a shared ride service and provides transportation curb-to-curb, within 3/4 of a mile from the fixed route bus system. In order to use VineGo, you must be certified.\nPara Video en Español Haga Clic Aquí\nAre you in a VineGo service area ? Click the link to see if your address falls within out service area.\nWho qualifies?\nHow to apply?\nHow does the assessment take place?\nWhat should I bring?\nWhen do I know if I’m eligible? Is there an appeals process?\nHow does VineGo work?\nSchedule a ride\nTrack a ride (My Transit Manager)\nVineGo hours\nVineGo fares\nReasonable accommodation\nComments & complaints\nADA visitors to Napa Valley\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area\nDo I qualify for VineGo? Eligibility is on a case-by-case basis. According to ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) regulations, only people with physical and/or cognitive limitations that prevent them from riding the Vine bus can qualify for VineGo. Users who do not qualify for ADA paratransit are still eligible to receive travel training to learn how to navigate the Vine fixed-route system at no cost. If you are interested in receiving travel training, please call (707) 259-8635 or reach out to [email protected] .\nHow can I apply?\nTo apply to use paratransit, you must complete a two-part process:\nFirst, complete the VineGo Paratransit Application. Click below to access the VineGo Applications in your preferred language.\nEnglish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nSpanish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nTagalog VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nThen, e-mail the completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo as an attachment to your e-mail.\nAlternatively, if you prefer, you can print and mail your completed application to:\nParatransit Eligibility Office 625 Burnell Street Napa, CA 94559\nInstructions for completing a fillable PDF:\nClick one of the links above to access the paratransit application in your preferred language.\nClick on each individual field to enter your response.\nWhen finished, click the download button in the upper right hand corner of the document. (see image below)\nE-mail your completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo.\nNeed help with the application process? Call NVTA at (707) 259-8635 or the Paratransit Eligibility Desk at (707) 253-4649.\nAll assessments, including renewals, will be made by phone appointment. For more information or to schedule your appointment, please contact the VineGo Paratransit Eligibility desk at (707) 253-4649.\nADA eligibility interviews are conducted entirely over the phone by MV Transportation in Santa Rosa.\nIs there anything I should prepare for my assessment?\nYes. Be prepared with a list of:\nAny sort of mobility device you use\nA list of medications you are taking\nThe name of a personal care attendant (PCA) you travel with, if you have one.\nWhen will I know if I am eligible?\nThe Americans with Disabilities Act allows paratransit providers like VineGo up to 21 days to determine if someone is eligible. However, final decisions are typically made much sooner. If an application is not processed within 21 days, is presumptive eligibility granted on the 22nd day until and unless the application is denied in writing\nIs there an appeals process?\nIf a paratransit applicant is deemed ineligible and does not agree with the determination they have the right to appeal the decision. To formally appeal a decision a letter must be submitted to NVTA within 60 days of receiving an eligibility determination letter. The letter shall be addressed to NVTA 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559 to the attention of the Manger of Public Transit. Upon receiving the letter an appeals panel will be assembled to hear an applicant’s appeal. The applicant or someone they appoint to speak on their behalf shall be contacted and an in person meeting with the panel will be scheduled. The panel shall consist of a member of Napa County’s Paratransit Coordinating Council (PCC), a member of NVTA’s Evaluation Contractor’s evaluation staff, and the Manager of Public Transit or his/her designated staff member. The appeals panel will render a final written decision within thirty (30) days of hearing the appeal. Should the appeals panel not render a decision within the thirty (30) days after the completion of the appeals process, NVTA shall provide paratransit service to the applicant until a decision is rendered. Free transportation shall be provided to the appealing applicant and their personal care attendant (PCA) to the appeals hearing.\nHow VineGo works\nVineGo can pick up passengers from the doorsteps of their home to their destinations. If VineGo can’t pick up or drop off passengers due to difficulties or physical barriers, VineGo will pick up/drop off the passenger at the nearest safe location). Roundtrips are welcome.\nEnrollees can schedule trips up to 7 days in advance. Same-day reservations are also welcome; however, we recommend making trip reservations in advance, as this service is in high demand. All rides are scheduled over the phone.\nKey Notes:\nBE FLEXIBLE. VineGo dispatch can negotiate a pickup time up to 60 minutes before or after your requested pick-up time.\nRemember that this is a SHARED RIDE SERVICE (NOT A TAXI SERVICE). The driver may pick up or drop off other passengers along the way.\nScheduling a ride Call (707) 252-2600 to schedule a ride. (You must be enrolled in VineGo before scheduling a ride.)\nIf your mobility device has changed, please let your reservationist know when you call the schedule your ride. For example, if you previously used a walker but now use a wheelchair, please let the reservationist know. You and your loved ones or caretakers can track your ride and receive notifications using the My Transit Manager app.\nReservation hours: Monday – Friday: 8am – 6pm Saturday – Sunday: 8am – 5pm\nVineGo service hours Mondays – Fridays: 6 am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSaturdays: 6:30am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSundays: 8 am – 6 pm (6 pm-9 pm as available )\nFares\nFares range from $4 to $8 and are based on distance traveled. Passes are also available at a discounted price. Rides are charged based on the actual fare. ADA fares are no more than twice the adult fixed route fare charged by the Vine. Please note that VineGo is NOT an emergency transportation service. If you have an emergency of any nature, call 911.\nExact fare is required on all buses . All fares are non-refundable. Drivers cannot make change.\nVineGo One-Way Fares Calistoga St. Helena Yountville Napa American Canyon Kaiser Vallejo Calistoga X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 $8.00 St. Helena $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Yountville $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Napa $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 American Canyon $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 Kaiser Vallejo $8.00 $8.00 $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 X X = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers."], "expected_facts": ["$3.00"]}
{"id": "ground-025", "question": "What does a SacRT daily pass cost with the senior discount?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:\n\nA SacRT daily pass with the senior discount costs **$3.50** [doc:sacrt-fares]. This price applies to riders age 62 and older [doc:sacrt-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a SacRT daily pass cost with the senior discount?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12:</p><p>A SacRT daily pass with the senior discount costs **$3.50** [sacrt-fares]. This price applies to riders age 62 and older [sacrt-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$3.50"]}
{"id": "ground-026", "question": "How much is a SacRT monthly pass for a student in grades TK-12?", "provenance": {"source": "SacRT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, SacRT offers **fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program** [doc:sacrt-fares]. The published policy does not specify a monthly pass price for this group because the program provides free fares rather than a discounted pass.\n\nFor details on how to enroll in RydeFreeRT, the passage directs you to check with your school district or visit RydeFreeRT [doc:sacrt-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-06-12.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sacrt-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a SacRT monthly pass for a student in grades TK-12?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, SacRT offers **fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade through the RydeFreeRT program** [sacrt-fares]. The published policy does not specify a monthly pass price for this group because the program provides free fares rather than a discounted pass.</p><p>For details on how to enroll in RydeFreeRT, the passage directs you to check with your school district or visit RydeFreeRT [sacrt-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-06-12.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SacRT: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride Ticket\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$10.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$20.00\n*Discount (senior, disabled or student) monthly or semi-monthly stickers must be affixed to a valid SacRT photo ID, with the exception of the student stickers, which may be attached to a school issued photo ID.\n**Many students in grades TK through 12, are eligible for free transit passes. Check with your school district for details or visit RydeFreeRT.\nBus\nPassengers are required to pay a basic or discount single fare for each trip or may purchase a daily pass valid for unlimited rides on that day.\nLight Rail\nSingle ride tickets are valid for 90 minutes from time of validation on light rail only, or pay the 25 cent transfer fee when boarding a bus.\nFare Accepted by Other Transit Agencies\nYour SacRT ticket or pass maybe eligible to transfer to and from other transit agencies. For more detailed fare info or a list of SacRT fares accepted by other transit agencies, click here .", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Our mobile fare app ZipPass allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus or light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nNeed a ticket for your next SacRT Light Rail trip?\nOur fare vending machines located at all SacRT light rail stations are simple to use!\n1. Choose your ticket or pass\n2. Pick your quantity\n3. Complete your payment (cash or card)\n4. Take your ticket\nTap2Ride\nTap your contactless card, mobile device or mobile wallet on Tap2Ride devices on bus and SacRT GO vehicles to pay fare.", "All fares are subject to change.\nExact change required only on bus; operators do not carry cash. Light rail station fare vending machines accept both cash/credit or debit. Pre-paid tickets and passes must be validated at the fare vending machines before boarding.\nMonthly passes cover the full month, and semi-monthly passes can be purchased for the 1st – 15th or 16th – end of the month.\nAll passengers must pay basic fare unless eligible for discount fare or free fare. View the fare categories page to see if you qualify for senior, disabled or student fare.\nAge 19-61 - Basic\nSingle Ride Ticket\n$2.50\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$7.00\nSemi-Monthly Pass\n$50.00\nMonthly Pass\n$100.00\nSenior (age 62+) - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00\nSuper Senior Monthly Pass/Sticker (age 75+)*\n$40.00\nPerson with Disabilities - Discount\nSingle\n$1.25\nTransfer Ticket\n$0.25\nDaily Pass\n$3.50\nSemi-Monthly Pass/Sticker*\n$25.00\nMonthly Pass/Sticker*\n$50.00", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect .", "SacRT offers a variety of fare options to meet your travel needs.\nImportant Notice for ZipPass Users\nLast day to use passes/tickets: April 30, 2026"], "expected_facts": ["$ 20"]}
{"id": "ground-027", "question": "How much is a single ride on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A single ride on Humboldt Transit costs $2.00 in cash [doc:hta-fares]. You can also pay $2.00 using Tap-to-Pay for a 2 Hour Unlimited Rides pass [doc:hta-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A single ride on Humboldt Transit costs $2.00 in cash [hta-fares]. You can also pay $2.00 using Tap-to-Pay for a 2 Hour Unlimited Rides pass [hta-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Pricing, payment types for fares and passes. Go Here to purchase tickets\nGo Here to purchase tickets\nCash, Passes and Tap to Pay\nType Cash Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride $2.00 -\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides - $2.00\n1 Day Pass $5.00 $5.00\n7 Day Pass $15.00 $15.00\n31 Day Pass $50.00 $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nType Regular Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00", "Insert the pass when you board and the value of each ride will be deducted. The amount deducted will be either:\n$2 (all rides unless eligible for a reduced fare)\n$1 (all rides if eligible for a reduced fare)\nThis is the only pass eligible for reduced fares. Let your driver know if you are entitled to a reduced fare before your insert your pass.\nReduced Fare Eligibility Requirements:\nSeniors Aged 62+\nYouth Aged 3-17\nPeople with a disabled person Transit ID Card\nThese passes work on all routes except Flex microtransit."], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-027", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-028", "question": "What does a 31-day pass cost on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A 31-day pass on Humboldt Transit costs $50.00 [doc:hta-fares]. This pass is valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back, and works on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Route 299, Southern Humboldt, North State Express Route 101, and Arcata & Mad River Transit System), but not on Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit [doc:hta-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a 31-day pass cost on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A 31-day pass on Humboldt Transit costs $50.00 [hta-fares]. This pass is valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back, and works on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Route 299, Southern Humboldt, North State Express Route 101, and Arcata &amp; Mad River Transit System), but not on Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit [hta-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Pricing, payment types for fares and passes. Go Here to purchase tickets\nGo Here to purchase tickets\nCash, Passes and Tap to Pay\nType Cash Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride $2.00 -\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides - $2.00\n1 Day Pass $5.00 $5.00\n7 Day Pass $15.00 $15.00\n31 Day Pass $50.00 $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nType Regular Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["$50"]}
{"id": "ground-029", "question": "How much is a day pass on Humboldt Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "HTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A 1 Day Pass on Humboldt Transit costs $5.00 if you pay cash, or $5.00 if you use Tap-to-Pay [doc:hta-fares]. The pass is valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back, and works on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Route 299, Southern Humboldt, North State Express Route 101, and Arcata & Mad River Transit System), but not on Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit [doc:hta-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-10.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["hta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a day pass on Humboldt Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A 1 Day Pass on Humboldt Transit costs $5.00 if you pay cash, or $5.00 if you use Tap-to-Pay [hta-fares]. The pass is valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back, and works on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems (Redwood Transit Service, Eureka Transit Service, North State Express Route 299, Southern Humboldt, North State Express Route 101, and Arcata &amp; Mad River Transit System), but not on Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit [hta-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-10.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>HTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Single Ride, Day, Week, and Month passes are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems. They are not accepted on any Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems.\nStored Value passes ($10 or $20) are accepted on all Ride Humboldt Fixed Route Systems, and on Dial-A-Ride. They are not accepted on Flex microtransit.", "Redwood Transit Service (RTS)\nEureka Transit Service (ETS)\nNorth State Express: Route 299: (NSE 299) [This system was formerly called Willow Creek (WC)\nSouthern Humboldt (SH)\nNorth State Express: Route 101 (NSE 101) [This system was formerly called Redwood Coast Express]\nArcata & Mad River Transit System (A&MRTS)\nThe Ride Humboldt Demand Response Systems are:\nDial-A-Ride\nFlex microtransit\nTicket Exclusions may apply\nLook for the Ride Humboldt logo on the bus:", "Cal Poly Humboldt students, faculty, and staff can ride A&MRTS, RTS, ETS, SH, NSE 299, and NSE 101 using their ID cards (Flex microtransit, and Dial-A-Ride routes are not included)! Students: Just show and swipe your current student ID when you board. All of your rides are free! Staff & Faculty: you can buy into the Jack Pass ID program for $60 per semester. Get more info from HSU Parking & Commuter Services .", "Purchase virtual passes anytime and anywhere on your phone using the Token Transit app! Pay with your credit, debit, or commuter benefits card and always have your pass with you. You can also purchase physical passes at the HTA online store with a credit card and have them mailed to you via the US Postal Service. Bus drivers sell passes on the bus, but only cash is accepted. You must have the exact amount as the drivers do not provide change. Important note: If you are not paying a cash fare do not put your money in the farebox until you tell the driver what you want to buy. You will not be refunded for money placed in farebox accidently. Passes can also be purchased at the Humboldt Transit Authority office at 133 V Street in Eureka using cash, Visa or Mastercard debit or credit card, or a personal check.", "Pricing, payment types for fares and passes. Go Here to purchase tickets\nGo Here to purchase tickets\nCash, Passes and Tap to Pay\nType Cash Tap-to-Pay\nSingle Ride $2.00 -\n2 Hour Unlimited Rides - $2.00\n1 Day Pass $5.00 $5.00\n7 Day Pass $15.00 $15.00\n31 Day Pass $50.00 $50.00\nStored Value Passes\nType Regular Reduced\n$10 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00\n$20 Stored Value Pass $2.00 $1.00", "Day, Week, & Month Passes are valid for unlimited rides until the date and time of expiration printed on the back of the pass. Each time you wish to use the pass after purchasing it, simply swipe it through the card reader. When you hear a beep the pass is recognized as valid and you may proceed to board the bus.\nThese passes work on all routes except Dial-A-Ride or Flex microtransit.\nReduced fares are not eligible with these passes."], "expected_facts": ["$5.00"]}
{"id": "ground-030", "question": "How much is a single ride on an Elk Grove e-tran bus?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A single ride on an Elk Grove e-tran bus costs $2.25 for basic/general public (ages 5–61 years old) [doc:etran-fares]. Discounted fares of $1.10 are available for military, seniors (age 62+), Medicare cardholders, and persons with disabilities [doc:etran-fares]. Students in grades TK–12 may ride free through the RydeFreeRT program [doc:etran-fares]. Children under age 5 ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger (up to two children per adult) [doc:etran-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on an Elk Grove e-tran bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A single ride on an Elk Grove e-tran bus costs $2.25 for basic/general public (ages 5–61 years old) [etran-fares]. Discounted fares of $1.10 are available for military, seniors (age 62+), Medicare cardholders, and persons with disabilities [etran-fares]. Students in grades TK–12 may ride free through the RydeFreeRT program [etran-fares]. Children under age 5 ride free when accompanied by an adult paying passenger (up to two children per adult) [etran-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "June 30, 2021\nGeneral\nIn July 2021, e- tran and e-van services annexed into SacRT . The current e- tran and e-van fares will remain the same at this time.\nFare Types | Basic | Military/Senior/Medicare/Disabled | Student (age 5 – 18)\nSingle Fare | $2.25 | $1.10 | $1.10\nDaily Pass | $6.00 | $3.00 | $3.00\n10-Ride Pass | $22.50 | $11.00 | $11.00\nMonthly Unrestricted Pass | $100.00 | $50.00 | $50.00\nMonthly Local Pass | $80.00 | $40.00 | $40.00\nAll Pass Sales are Final\nOnce a pass is sold, no refunds will be provided. Any unused, lost, destroyed, or stolen passes will not be replaced or refunded. Exchanges can only be made on defective passes at the SacRT’s discretion.\nBasic/General Public\nAll passengers (ages 5-61 years old) pay Basic fare unless eligible for Discount fare.", "about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass."], "expected_facts": ["$2.25"]}
{"id": "ground-031", "question": "What does a transfer cost on an Elk Grove bus and how long is it good for?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nAn Elk Grove transfer costs $0.50 and is valid for 120 minutes. [doc:etran-fares] The transfer may only be used once and must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or at the end of the time limit. [doc:etran-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a transfer cost on an Elk Grove bus and how long is it good for?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>An Elk Grove transfer costs $0.50 and is valid for 120 minutes. [etran-fares] The transfer may only be used once and must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or at the end of the time limit. [etran-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass.", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/"], "expected_facts": ["$0.50", "120"]}
{"id": "ground-032", "question": "How much does a senior pay per ride on the Elk Grove routes?", "provenance": {"source": "E-tran published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nA senior (age 62+) on Elk Grove routes pays **$1.10 per single ride** [doc:etran-fares]. Alternatively, seniors may ride for **no cost** if they board an Elk Grove route that begins or ends within the City of Elk Grove and show either an Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or a government-issued photo ID showing proof of age (62+) [doc:etran-senior-pass].\n\nThe free senior program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first [doc:etran-senior-pass].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["etran-fares", "etran-senior-pass"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much does a senior pay per ride on the Elk Grove routes?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>A senior (age 62+) on Elk Grove routes pays **$1.10 per single ride** [etran-fares]. Alternatively, seniors may ride for **no cost** if they board an Elk Grove route that begins or ends within the City of Elk Grove and show either an Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or a government-issued photo ID showing proof of age (62+) [etran-senior-pass].</p><p>The free senior program is available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended, whichever comes first [etran-senior-pass].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Transit Fares</li><li>E-tran: Elk Grove Senior Pass</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["about the program\nThe City of Elk Grove is funding fares for eligible seniors that ride SacRT fixed-route bus routes that begin or end within the City of Elk Grove (e.g., E10, E11, etc.). This special program is available to Elk Grove residents who are 62 years of age or older. This pilot program is designed to help improve mobility and expand access to essential services for seniors.\nStarting Monday, March 30, 2026, eligible riders can show one of the following when boarding:\nElk Grove Senior Transit Pass (available soon), or\nA governmentissued photo ID showing proof of age (62+)\nSimply show your Elk Grove Senior Transit Pass or an ID Card to the bus operator when boarding. The operator will verify eligibility, and your ride will be provided at no cost to you.\nWhere to get a pass in Elk Grove\nSacRT is working on producing the Elk Grove Senior Transit Passes and the City of Elk Grove will issue the passes at the following locations starting in mid-May 2026:\nElk Grove City Hall\nDistrict 56", "Unlimited Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Unlimited Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on SacRT routes with an “E” designation prior to the route number.\nLocal Monthly Passes\nValid for 31 days from the first use. Local Elk Grove 31 day passes are only valid on routes E110-E116.\nElk Grove only Daily Passes\nValid for unlimited rides routes designated with an “E” prefix until 1.a.m. the day after purchase or validation. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services\n10-Ride Passes\nValid for 10 trips on any route with an “E” prefix, with no expiration date. 10-Ride Passes are available for purchase any time. Please obtain a transfer, if transferring to other SacRT services,\nPaper Daily Passes\nMust be surrendered to bus operator. For prepaid daily pass users, passenger will receive a stamped SacRT daily pass valid for the day redeemed.\nSacRT Passes\nIn addition to the Elk Grove passes, routes designated with an “E” prefix will also accept all SacRT tickets and passes, including mobile tickets and light rail passes (transfer fee applies on light rail single ride ticket).\nPasses can be purchased at the following locations within Elk Grove and Sacramento.\nCity Hall (Connect Card passes only) – 8401 Laguna Palms Way, Elk Grove\nSacRT – 1225 R Street, adjacent from the 13th Street light rail Station, Sacramento\nThrough your employer – Please ask your transportation coordinator for details\nOrder by Mail – Call 916-321-2849 to request a mail order form", "Bus Route E10: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E10\nBus Route E11: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E11\nBus Route E12: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E12\nBus Route E13: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E13\nBus Route E14: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E14\nBus Route E15: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E15\nBus Route E16: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E16\nBus Route E17: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E17\nBus Route E18: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E18\nBus Route E19: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E19\nBus Route E110: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E110\nBus Route E111: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E111\nBus Route E112: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E112\nBus Route E113: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E113\nBus Route E114: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E114\nBus Route E115: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E115\nBus Route E116: https://www.sacrt.com/routes-schedules/#E116\nmore about the Elk Grove Transit Pass Program\nThe City of Elk Grove reimburses SacRT for each Senior Pass ride at the discounted single ride rate. This pilot program will be available through June 30, 2027, or until the $100,000 program fund is fully expended , whichever comes first. For assistance , call SacRT Customer Service at 916 – 321 – BUSS (2877).", "Requires certain forms of identification (ID) or age verification as follows:\nSeniors (age 62+)\nSeniors/Persons with e-van Eligibility Card, SacRT Senior photo ID card, Medicare card, State of California ID card, Senior ID card issued by another transit agency, or a passport showing date of birth.\nPersons with Disabilities (with physical and/or mental impairments)\nSenior/Persons with Disabilities Discount Card, SacRT Disabled photo ID card, Dept. of Motor Vehicles Disabled Placard ID card, Medicare Card or Disabled ID card issued by another transit agency. Attendants of persons with disabilities may ride for Discount fare with a passenger who has a SacRT Disabled card or any ID listed above.\nStudents (Grades TK-12)\nSacRT allows students/youth in grades TK-12 to ride free as part of the RydeFreeRT program for Elk Grove Transit fixed-route buses.\nRydeFreeRT is available all day, anywhere that SacRT operates. Learn more about the fare-free transit program for students/youth at rydefreert.com .\nMilitary\nPersons requesting the discounted fare will need to present an Armed Service’s picture identification.\nParatransit Fares\nFor ADA Paratransit Fares, visit https://www.sacrt.com/elk-grove-ada-paratransit-service-option/", "Elk Grove Transfers\nIssued only when boarding buses and are $0.50. Transfers are valid for 120 minutes and may only be used once. They must be surrendered to the bus operator upon boarding another route or the end of the time limit.\nFare Payment\nCash Fares\nExact change is required since bus operators do not carry or give change.", "The passes will also be available by mid-May 2026 at SacRT Customer Service and Sales Center at 1225 R Street ( adjacent to the 13 th Street Station). Elk Grove residency and proof of age will be required to get a pass."], "expected_facts": ["$1.10", "62"]}
{"id": "ground-033", "question": "How much is a single ride on Santa Cruz METRO local service if I pay cash?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A single ride on Santa Cruz METRO local service costs **$2.00 cash** for adults age 18 and over. [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nIf you are age 65 and over or have a disability, the discount fare is **$1.00 cash** per ride. [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on Santa Cruz METRO local service if I pay cash?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A single ride on Santa Cruz METRO local service costs **$2.00 cash** for adults age 18 and over. [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>If you are age 65 and over or have a disability, the discount fare is **$1.00 cash** per ride. [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Visit Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville during business hours.\nProcessing and Replacement Fees\n$2.00 to process a new Photo ID Card\n$2.00 for the first replacement of a lost card\n$5.00 for the replacement of a lost card thereafter\nHow to Obtain the Application Forms\nRead the Discount Fare Program and Policy .\nPrint the application forms that are appended to the policy as Attachment A and Attachment B .\nIf your application requires confirmation of legal blindness, print and submit Attachment C .\nYou may also request the forms from Customer Service at our Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz and Watsonville.\nAttendants\nPeople with disabilities who require an attendant are only required to pay one discount rate fare when they travel with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must indicate that you need an attendant. A “green dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.\nChildren\nChildren (age 17 and under) may ride at the discount fare rate when accompanying a parent who travels with a METRO Discount Photo ID Card. To qualify, your application form must include a completed Request for Reduced Children’s Fare with Discount Fare Card Form ( Attachment B ). A “gold dot” will then be placed on your Discount Photo ID Card, which allows you to receive this benefit.", "Monterey Salinas Transit (MST)\nMST will honor a free valid METRO transfer as payment for passengers boarding lines 28 and 29 at the Watsonville Transit Center from Lane 2.\nMETRO will honor free valid MST transfers for one-way travel within the METRO service area as far as the Temporary Riverfront Transit Center in downtown Santa Cruz."], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-033", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-034", "question": "What does the discount fare cost per ride on Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nThe discount fare on Santa Cruz METRO costs **$1.00 per ride in cash** [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]. This discount applies to adults age 65 and over and people with disabilities [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nWhen paying with contactless payment (Tap2Cruz), the discount fare is **$1 for up to 2 hours of systemwide travel** [doc:scmtd-tap2cruz].\n\nDiscount fare passes are also available: a Day Pass costs **$3** and a 31-Day Pass costs **$32** [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nTo apply for the discount fare program, you must visit Customer Service at the Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz or Watsonville during business hours [doc:scmtd-accessibility]. There is a **$2.00 processing fee for a new Photo ID Card** [doc:scmtd-accessibility]. The published policy does not specify the office hours for these locations.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-accessibility", "scmtd-fares-passes", "scmtd-tap2cruz"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the discount fare cost per ride on Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>The discount fare on Santa Cruz METRO costs **$1.00 per ride in cash** [scmtd-fares-passes]. This discount applies to adults age 65 and over and people with disabilities [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>When paying with contactless payment (Tap2Cruz), the discount fare is **$1 for up to 2 hours of systemwide travel** [scmtd-tap2cruz].</p><p>Discount fare passes are also available: a Day Pass costs **$3** and a 31-Day Pass costs **$32** [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>To apply for the discount fare program, you must visit Customer Service at the Transit Centers in downtown Santa Cruz or Watsonville during business hours [scmtd-accessibility]. There is a **$2.00 processing fee for a new Photo ID Card** [scmtd-accessibility]. The published policy does not specify the office hours for these locations.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Accessibility (discount fares and Discount Photo ID Card)</li><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>SCMTD: Tap2Cruz (contactless payment and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026.", "Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "What will I be charged?\nWhen paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a Tap2Cruz reader, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method—you can ride up to 2 hours systemwide for only $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay.\nIs there a weekly or monthly payment option?\nYes, please see the table below for daily and monthly fare-capping amounts when paying with a contactless payment method.\nThe 31-day cap operates on a rolling basis. This means that a cap will apply for all trips taken within a 31-day period from the first time you tap to pay, and on a rolling basis, based on usage thereafter.\nThe 31-day period starts with the first tap.\nHow do charges appear on my card statement?\nThe fare is charged to your card’s account and will include the description: “Santa Cruz METRO”\nTo view your recent METRO rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal (or check your bank card statement).\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure?\nYes. Tap2Cruz transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, Discover, and American Express transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private?\nPersonal data will never be stored, sold, or given away by METRO or any third-party agent. METRO’s Customer Service Representatives do not have access to full credit card account numbers. Customer Service Representatives will answer customer support queries in accordance with Payment Card Industry practices.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history?\nNo. Just like your card issuer would not see what you order from a coffee shop or other retailer, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on METRO?\nNo, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method?\nNo. Each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nTip: If you have both a physical card and a digital wallet version of that same card, they count as different payment methods.\nDo I always have to use the same payment method within the 2-hour transfer window?\nYes. You cannot switch payment methods during the 2-hour transfer window. The fare calculation software will only charge your card $2 throughout this window, no matter how many times it is tapped. If you switch between payment methods during the 2-hour window, it will be treated as a new trip, and you will be charged again.\nImportant: You must use the same payment method throughout all your trips to qualify for benefits like fare capping, and automatic discount fares (once verified and linked to your card).\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments?\nNo. Fares paid via contactless payment will never cost more than the standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go up to the fare cap.\nPlease review the fare chart for Tap2Cruz fare capping information.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board within that 2-hour window?\nYes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 regardless how many times you tap. You will not be charged again until 2 hours have passed.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment?\nDiscounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Veterans, and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts.\nUtilizing CalITP Benefits, you can link your benefits eligibility to your preferred Tap2Cruz payment card, ensuring you always receive discount fare when tapping!\nRiders who are eligible for a discount fare can still pay the existing discount fare with cash or SmartCard when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "Monterey Salinas Transit (MST)\nMST will honor a free valid METRO transfer as payment for passengers boarding lines 28 and 29 at the Watsonville Transit Center from Lane 2.\nMETRO will honor free valid MST transfers for one-way travel within the METRO service area as far as the Temporary Riverfront Transit Center in downtown Santa Cruz."], "expected_facts": ["$1.00", "65"]}
{"id": "ground-035", "question": "How much does the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express cost on Santa Cruz METRO?", "provenance": {"source": "SCMTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nSanta Cruz METRO's Amtrak/Highway 17 Express fares are:\n\n**Children and Adults (age 64 and under)**\n- $7.00 Cash/1 Ride [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n- $14 Day Pass [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n- $145 31-Day Pass [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\n**Discount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)**\n- $3.50 Cash/1 Ride [doc:scmtd-fares-passes]\n\nDay and 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes as well as local VTA service [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].\n\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz are also honored on the Highway 17 Express route [doc:scmtd-fares-passes].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["scmtd-fares-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much does the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express cost on Santa Cruz METRO?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Santa Cruz METRO's Amtrak/Highway 17 Express fares are:</p><p>**Children and Adults (age 64 and under)**</p><p>- $7.00 Cash/1 Ride [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>- $14 Day Pass [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>- $145 31-Day Pass [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>**Discount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)**</p><p>- $3.50 Cash/1 Ride [scmtd-fares-passes]</p><p>Day and 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes as well as local VTA service [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><p>Amtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz are also honored on the Highway 17 Express route [scmtd-fares-passes].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SCMTD: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Day & 31-Day Passes\nAllow unlimited rides on all METRO routes and local VTA service\n1-Ride Passes\nAre not honored on any VTA Service.\nAmtrak through-tickets to or from Santa Cruz\nAre honored on the Highway 17 Express route.\nVTA Light Rail Transfers\nAre issued by request only with Highway 17 Express Day Pass, Highway 17 Express Cruz Pass, or Cruz Cash cards.\nStudent ID Cards and Passes\nCabrillo College student ID cards, UCSC student ID cards, and other UCSC bus passes are not honored on the Amtrak/Highway 17 Express route.", "Local Service in Santa Cruz County\nAdult (age 18 and over)\n$2.00 Cash/1 Ride\nDay Pass\n$6\n31-Day Pass\n$65\nDiscount Fare (adults age 65 and over, people with disabilities)\n$1.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$3\n$32\nView Discount Fare Eligibility & Apply\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes (excluding Amtrak/Highway 17 Express).\nCarry exact change. Driver does not provide change for larger bills.\nAmtrak/Highway 17 Express\nChildren and Adults (age 64 and under)\n$7.00 Cash/1 Ride\n$14\n$145\n$3.50 Cash/1 Ride\nAdult & Discount Fare Day & 31-Day Passes allow unlimited rides on local Santa Cruz County routes, as well as local VTA service.\nCarry exact change. Driver’s do not provide change for larger bills.\n**Beginning July 1, 2026, the 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride Pass will no longer be sold.** 3-Day, 7-Day, & 15-Ride passes purchased on/before June 30, 2026 will be accepted through December 31, 2026.", "Request for Reasonable Accomodation\nSanta Cruz METRO does not discriminate against individuals with disabilities in the provision of transportation services and is committed to ensuring that no person, solely by reason of his or her disability, is excluded from participation in, is denied benefits of, or is subjected to discrimination under any Santa Cruz METRO’s programs or activities.\nSanta Cruz METRO considers all requests for reasonable modifications of its policies, practices or procedures when necessary to avoid discrimination on the basis of disability. Santa Cruz METRO is not required to grant requests for reasonable modifications that would fundamentally alter the nature of the Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; are not needed for access to Santa Cruz METRO services, programs or activities; or present a direct threat of injury to other persons or property. Due to the fact-specific nature of requests, individual decisions and one-time modifications are not precedential. If a modification is determined later to be unnecessary for the requesting individual to use the requested transit service, a fundamental alteration, or unsafe for other person(s) or property, Santa Cruz METRO may discontinue or change the modification.\nIf a customer believes they have been discriminated against by Santa Cruz METRO on the basis of a disability, they may submit a Request for Reasonable Modification using our online Cutsomer Service Report form and selecting “Request for Reasonable Accomodation” from the dropdown menu. They may also complete the request for Reasonable Modification form (Appendix D), which is available in English and Spanish .\nIf an individual is unable to write because of a disability and needs assistance in completing any form, District staff will assist by scribing the complaint by phone. Please contact our Customer Service Department at 831-425-8600 .\nRequests for reasonable modifications are handled by Santa Cruz METRO’s Chief Operating Officer (COO), who can be reached as follows:\nBy Phone: 831-426-6080 or Fax 831-426-9471\nBy email: mross@scmetro.org\nBy US mail: Santa Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO Attention: Margo Ross (COO) 110 Vernon Street Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nCOO will evaluate all requests for reasonable modifications, and provide a written response to the individual requestor within ten (10) business days.\nDownload our ADA Complaint Procedure in English or Spanish .\nAccessible Information Request\nYou may request accessible information online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Accessible Information Request” from the dropdown menu.\nTitle VI Discrimination Complaint\nYou may file a Title VI Discrimination Complaint online using our Customer Service Report form and selecting “Title VI Discrimination Complaint” from the dropdown menu.\nAccessibility Policies\nOur policies related to accessibility, ParaCruz, discounted fares, and other services and programs for people with disabilities can be found in our Resource Library .", "Transfers are free between METRO buses for riders on all local routes.\nIf you are paying with cash or a pass, you must request a transfer from the bus operators when boarding.\nTransfers will be available for two hours.\nMETRO Splash Pass fares are available for two hours in the mobile app.\nTransfers from a local to a Highway 17 bus will require payment of an “upgrade fare” equal to the difference between a local and Highway 17 fare.\nClipper Cards are not honored on METRO buses.", "Santa Cruz METRO will launch Tap2Cruz, an onboard, contactless fare payment technology for riders, in Summer 2026 .\nContactless-enabled bank cards & mobile wallets will be accepted.\nContactless payments are safe and secure by using a one-time code for every transaction.\nLocal Service in Santa Cruz County\nK-12 Students (Youth Cruz Free Program)\nFREE on all routes within Santa Cruz County\nCollege Students\nMust show student ID.\nThe fastest way to purchase passes and tickets is by using METRO’s mobile ticketing app, the METRO Splash Pass, valid for all fixed-route trips. Download Splash Pass from the Apple App Store or Google Play . For frequently asked questions, learn more here .\nStarting in March 2026, riders can purchase passes directly within the Transit app while planning trips and viewing real-time information, no need to use a separate application. Download Transit from the Apple App Store or Google Play .\nMETRO’s online store offers a selection of reloadable plastic CRUZ Cards and disposable paper METRO Passes for purchase on all fixed routes. Additionally, METRO offers ParaCruz Coupons for purchase for all paratransit riders. All passes and coupons are valid for purchase with a VISA or Mastercard.\nTERMS & SHIPPING\nThere is an additional charge of $1.50 for postage & handling\nPlease allow 5 business days from the date the order is received by Metro Staff\nAll sales are final. Fares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO assumes no responsibility for lost, misused or stolen passes.\nMETRO will not replace lost or stolen passes\nFor further information, please read METRO’s SMART CARD POLICY\nDownload and complete the Fixed-Route Mail Order Form to order tickets and passes by mail. Instructions are listed on the form.\nSend all mail order requests and payment by check to:\nSanta Cruz METRO\n110 Vernon Street ATTN: Customer Service Santa Cruz, CA 95060\nSanta Cruz METRO is proud to support our community partners and businesses by offering bulk rate discounts on select fare products. Whether you’re purchasing for employees, clients, or a large group, we make it easy and cost-effective to buy in bulk. Discounts are available for 1-Day and 31-Day Adult passes, with savings starting at quantities as low as 12.\nEligible Pass Types & Discount Rates\n1-Day Pass, Local Service, $6 adult\n100+ passes: 20%\n31-Day Pass, Local Service, $65 adult\n12-50 passes: 10%\n51-100 passes: 15%\n101+ passes: 20%\nAll other fare types may be purchased in bulk quantities, too! Please note: Customer Service Booths only sell quantities up to 50 passes at any time.\n*Discounts apply only to full-fare, adult passes. Other fare types do not currently qualify.*\nHow to Place a Bulk Order\nEmail your request to: bulkorders@scmetro.org Include the following: Your name, address, phone, email, and business/organization Pass type(s) and quantity Desired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.) Preferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nYour name, address, phone, email, and business/organization\nPass type(s) and quantity\nDesired pickup date (Please allow at least 5 business days for processing. Smaller orders may be processed quicker.)\nPreferred payment method (cash, check, or credit card)\nA METRO team member will respond with: Confirmation of your order Date of pickup An invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nConfirmation of your order\nDate of pickup\nAn invoice for any PO requirements you may have\nPickup & Payment Details\nPickup location:\nMETRO Customer Experience Center, 603 Front Street, Santa Cruz, CA\n*pickup from Watsonville Transit Center is available, if arranged at time of order.\nPayment: Due at time of pickup (check, card, or cash)\nPlease do not pay from the digital invoice.\nHours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 AM – 5:00 PM\nCertain bulk orders may be eligible for shipping, for an added fee. Please include this in your initial request for review, if desired.\nNeed fewer than 50 passes?\nYou may also purchase them in person at:\nSanta Cruz METRO Customer Service (Santa Cruz & Watsonville booths)\nDiscounts on 31-Day passes (starting at 12 passes) apply in person, too!\nImportant Terms\nAll sales are final.\nFares and passes/tickets are non-transferable, non-refundable, and non-exchangeable.\nSanta Cruz METRO is not responsible for lost, stolen, or misused passes.\nFor full policy details, visit our Terms & Conditions: https://scmtd.square.site/\nCheck Cashing Policy\nWe accept personal checks as payment for bus passes only.\nWe only accept personal checks at our Santa Cruz and Watsonville Transit Center Customer Service Windows.\nYour check must be local to the METRO service area, including counties that are contiguous to Santa Cruz County.\nYou must also present a valid California Driver’s license or legal photo ID when writing a check.\nThere is a $15.00 service charge on all returned checks.\nView METRO’s Check Cashing Policy here .\nCredit Card Policy\nVisa, Mastercard, and Discover are accepted for the purchase of bus passes only\nPlease have a legal photo ID available when using this method of payment", "METRO passes and tickets can be purchased in-person at the Customer Service Windows at:\nTemporary Riverfront Customer Experience Center 603 Front St., downtown Santa Cruz (831) 425-8600 Served by all routes except 72-79\nWatsonville Transit Center 475 Rodriguez St., Watsonville (831) 425-8600 Served by routes 1, 2, 72-79, plus MST 28 & 29\nBoulder Creek Pharmacy 3081 Highway in Boulder Creek Served by route 35\nFree Fare Programs\nYouth Cruz Free\nCOLLEGE STUDENTS RIDE FREE\nFREE FARES FOR LEGALLY BLIND RIDERS"], "expected_facts": ["$7.00"]}
{"id": "ground-036", "question": "Does a 17-year-old have to pay to ride SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify a fare for 17-year-olds on SolTrans.\n\nThe passages describe several rider categories — Adults, Seniors 65+, Seniors 80+, Youth, and Children age 5 and under — but do not state which category a 17-year-old falls into or what fare applies to that age. The passages mention that a \"Youth Fare Category\" exists and is available for the Local Service Area on certain passes [doc:soltrans-fare-table], but they do not define the age range for Youth or give a Youth fare price.\n\nTo find out whether your 17-year-old must pay and at what rate, contact SolTrans directly or visit their fares page at https://www.soltrans.org/fares/fare-table.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-fare-table"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does a 17-year-old have to pay to ride SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, the published policy does not specify a fare for 17-year-olds on SolTrans.</p><p>The passages describe several rider categories — Adults, Seniors 65+, Seniors 80+, Youth, and Children age 5 and under — but do not state which category a 17-year-old falls into or what fare applies to that age. The passages mention that a \"Youth Fare Category\" exists and is available for the Local Service Area on certain passes [soltrans-fare-table], but they do not define the age range for Youth or give a Youth fare price.</p><p>To find out whether your 17-year-old must pay and at what rate, contact SolTrans directly or visit their fares page at https://www.soltrans.org/fares/fare-table.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Fare Table</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card"], "expected_facts": ["ifree"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-036", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-037", "question": "If I ride a SolTrans local bus and then take the Vallejo ferry to San Francisco, do I get any credit?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. If you ride a SolTrans local bus and then transfer to the Vallejo-SF Ferry, you receive a local fare credit [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. The credit amount depends on your rider category:\n\n- **Adults**: $1.75 credit [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n- **Youth**: $1.50 credit [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n- **Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders**: $0.85 credit [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n\nThis means you pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry after transferring from a SolTrans local bus route [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]. Transfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry are valid on Clipper only for e-cash transfers; the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper [doc:soltrans-clipper-card].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I ride a SolTrans local bus and then take the Vallejo ferry to San Francisco, do I get any credit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. If you ride a SolTrans local bus and then transfer to the Vallejo-SF Ferry, you receive a local fare credit [soltrans-clipper-card]. The credit amount depends on your rider category:</p><p>- **Adults**: $1.75 credit [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>- **Youth**: $1.50 credit [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>- **Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders**: $0.85 credit [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>This means you pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry after transferring from a SolTrans local bus route [soltrans-clipper-card]. Transfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry are valid on Clipper only for e-cash transfers; the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper [soltrans-clipper-card].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "Local Passes : Valid for travel within Vallejo and Benicia on SolTrans Local fixed routes * and local trips on SolanoExpress fixed routes. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travel outside Vallejo and Benicia.\nWithin Solano County Passes : Valid for travel within and between Solano County cities (Vallejo, Benicia, Fairfield, Vacaville, Dixon, and Davis) on SolanoExpress Lines. Extra surcharge for SolanoExpress travels outside of Solano County. ‍\nOutside Solano County Passes : Valid for travel both outside and within Solano County on SolanoExpress fixed routes and SolTrans Local fixed routes. Extra surcharge on SolanoExpress Route 82 to the San Francisco Ferry terminal. ‍ ‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers.", "To get yourClipper Card:\nCall (877) 878-8883 or go to the Clipper website\nVisit the following retail locations: Vallejo Transit Center 311 Sacramento St. Vallejo, CA 94590 ‍ Walgreens 2647 Springs Rd Vallejo, CA 94591 ‍ Walgreens 1050 Redwood St Vallejo, CA 94590 ‍\nPlease visit our Frequently Asked Questions for information on discounted prices for Clipper Cards.", "Clipper is an all-in-one transit card that keeps track of any passes and cash value you load onto it while applying all applicable fares, discounts, and transfer rules. This lets you customize your card for your own transit needs. A Clipper card makes boarding faster, eliminates the need to carry exact change, and can be registered for added protection in case it’s lost.\nYou may use Clipper on many public transit services throughout the San Francisco Bay Area, including BART. ‍", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍"], "expected_facts": ["$1.75"]}
{"id": "ground-038", "question": "How much of a discount does Clipper START give on SolTrans?", "provenance": {"source": "SolTrans published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a **50 percent fare discount** on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I recommend confirming this discount with SolTrans for any time-sensitive details. For information about the application process and how to obtain a Clipper START Card, visit Clipper START as noted in the published policy. [doc:soltrans-clipper-card]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["soltrans-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much of a discount does Clipper START give on SolTrans?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a **50 percent fare discount** on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13, I recommend confirming this discount with SolTrans for any time-sensitive details. For information about the application process and how to obtain a Clipper START Card, visit Clipper START as noted in the published policy. [soltrans-clipper-card]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SolTrans: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Clipper START Program allows lower-income adults age 19-64 to receive a 50 percent fare discount on SolTrans with a valid Clipper START Card. For more information about the programs and the application process visit, Clipper START .\nPlease visit the Clipper on SolTrans site for instructions on how to use your Clipper Card", "Effective Monday, July 1, 2024\nSolTrans is going paperless and eliminating all paper fare passes effective Monday, July 1, 2024. SolTrans already has several digital fare payment options; like Token Transit , Clipper Card (cash value and passes), and Clipper START. Exact cash and coins will continue to be accepted to purchase one-way fares and day passes on all local and express routes.\nNote: Paper passes will still be available for purchase and accepted on SolTrans Paratransit service.", "Transfers to/from other Transit Agencies\nCurrently, Clipper supports transfers to/from SolTrans and connecting transit agencies including:\nGolden Gate Transit 40/42\nNAPA VINE Route 11\nSan Francisco Bay Ferry (Vallejo-SF Ferry only)*\nWestCat\nCounty Connection\nTransfers to and from other bus agencies are good for 60 minutes. Full fares are charged for transfers to/from all other transit agencies that use Clipper.\n* Transfers to/from Vallejo SF Bay Ferry\nTransfers between SolTrans and the Vallejo SF Bay Ferry receive a local fare credit of $1.75 for adults, $1.50 for youth, and $0.85 for Seniors/Persons with Disabilities/Medicare card holders.\nThis means SolTrans riders transferring from a local bus route can pay a reduced Clipper e-cash fare on the Vallejo-SF Ferry.\nFor Ferry riders transferring to the SolTrans system, transfers are free on local bus routes. Clipper will only detect e-cash transfers as the Ferry Monthly Pass is not currently available on Clipper.\nNote: There are no transfers within the SolTrans system, (i.e. from one SolTrans route to another). For same-day, multi-route trips, passengers should purchase day passes.", "$40.00\nLocal\nN/A\nSolanoExpress Within Solano County\nSolanoExpress Outside Solano County\nRoute 82\n* Available on Clipper\nNOTE: Clipper charges the Outside County fare on all SolTrans SolanoExpress lines. Please use cash or a paper pass for travel within Solano County or locally on SolanoExpress lines.\nChildren\nChildren age 5 and under ride free, maximum of two per fare-paying passenger. Additional children pay youth fare. ‍\nSolano Community College Students\nSolano Community College students ride free with their valid student ID. The FREE ride is valid on all SolTrans, FAST local, and SolanoExpress routes. ‍", "To qualify you must present one of the following:\nPhoto ID with birthdate indicating 65+\nValid Medicare Card with photo ID\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card\nDMV Disabled License Plate Registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout ‍\nSenior 80+\nSeniors 80+ ride FREE on SolTrans and FAST local routes and SolanoExpress lines. To qualify, present a photo ID with birth year indicating 80+ to the operator.\n‍", "31-Day Passes :Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes for 31 days from the first day of use. Available to Adult and Reduced Fare Categories for all Service Areas. Youth Fare Category is only available for the Local Service Area. ‍\nDay Passes : Valid for unlimited travel on fixed routes all day on the first day used. May be purchased onboard the bus with the exact fare. Available to all Fare Categories for all Service Areas. ‍\nLocal 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans fixed routes. Available to all Fare Categories for SolTrans Local Service Area only. ‍\nParatransit 10-Ride Pass : Valid for 10 one-way rides (single boarding) on SolTrans' ADA Paratransit demand response† service only within SolTrans’ local service area. ADA Paratransit Certification is required to ride. A driver may request to see a valid ADA Paratransit ID card.\n* Fixed route - Services provided on a repetitive, fixed schedule basis along a specific route with vehicles stopping to pick up and deliver passengers to specific locations; each fixed route trip serves the same origins and destinations.\n† Demand response - A transit mode operating in response to calls from passengers to the transit operator, who then dispatches a vehicle to pick up the passengers and transport them to their destinations. The vehicle may be dispatched to pick up several passengers at different pick-up points before taking them to their respective destinations and may be interrupted on route to these destinations to pick up other passengers."], "expected_facts": ["50"]}
{"id": "ground-039", "question": "How much is a regular one-way fare on FAX?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A regular one-way fare on FAX is $1.00. [doc:fax-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a regular one-way fare on FAX?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A regular one-way fare on FAX is $1.00. [fax-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)"], "expected_facts": ["$1.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-039", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-040", "question": "What does a 31-day pass cost on Fresno Area Express?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nFresno Area Express offers several 31-day pass options at different prices depending on rider class:\n\n- **31-Day Regular Unlimited Pass**: $36.00 [doc:fax-fares]\n- **31-Day Reduced Unlimited Pass**: Ride Free – no pass needed [doc:fax-fares]\n- **31-Day Student Unlimited Pass**: $22.00 [doc:fax-fares]\n\nThe 31-Day Reduced pass is currently free for riders who qualify for FAX's Reduced Fare Program and show accepted identification [doc:fax-fares].\n\nIf you have questions about which pass applies to you, contact Fresno Area Express at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (Monday–Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.).", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a 31-day pass cost on Fresno Area Express?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>Fresno Area Express offers several 31-day pass options at different prices depending on rider class:</p><p>- **31-Day Regular Unlimited Pass**: $36.00 [fax-fares]</p><p>- **31-Day Reduced Unlimited Pass**: Ride Free – no pass needed [fax-fares]</p><p>- **31-Day Student Unlimited Pass**: $22.00 [fax-fares]</p><p>The 31-Day Reduced pass is currently free for riders who qualify for FAX's Reduced Fare Program and show accepted identification [fax-fares].</p><p>If you have questions about which pass applies to you, contact Fresno Area Express at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (Monday–Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.).</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "ABOUT\nFresno State University, State Center Community College District, Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District, currently subsidize transit fares for students.\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nStudents, faculty and staff from Fresno State University, Fresno City College, and Clovis Community College will be able to swipe their student or staff ID for a free ride.\nHigh School students from Fresno Unified School District, and Central Unified School District will be able to request a 31-Day Student Pass from their respective school administration and simply swipe the pass and show their valid student ID.\nAll other students will be able to purchase single fares for .75 cents or purchase a 31-Day monthly pass for $22 at any Ticket Vending Machine (TVM) along the BRT route 1 or at Manchester Transit Center. Students will need to show the driver a valid student Identification card to board the bus.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]"], "expected_facts": ["$36"]}
{"id": "ground-041", "question": "If I pay cash on FAX, how long can I keep transferring?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:\n\nIf you pay cash on FAX, transfers are valid for **90 minutes on all routes in any direction** [doc:fax-fares]. The passage states: \"Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\"", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I pay cash on FAX, how long can I keep transferring?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13:</p><p>If you pay cash on FAX, transfers are valid for **90 minutes on all routes in any direction** [fax-fares]. The passage states: \"Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\"</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride fr