 If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "In addition to the FAX offices, passes may be purchased by mail and will be mailed directly to your home when purchased in advance.\nComplete one of the mail order forms: Fill Electronically and Print or Print and Fill by Hand\nSend a check or money order payable to CITY OF FRESNO along with the printed order form\nPlease allow approximately 10 days for processing and mailing delivery\nPay for up to 3 monthly passes per order", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "autobuses con rutas fijas por FAX a un costo reducido*\n1. Para aplicar, visite el FAX Centro de Tránsito de Manchester (MTC)\n MTC está ubicado en 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., frente al centro comercial de\nManchester.\n Las tarjetas de identificación se realizan de lunes a viernes de 8:30 a.m. a 3:30 p.m.\n Si tiene preguntas antes de su visita, llame al (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Junto con su identificación federal o estatal con foto, se requerirá uno de los siguientes\ndocumentos para verificar su elegibilidad (vea los ejemplos en la parte posterior).\n Tarjeta de identificación de Medicare (tarjeta blanca con rayas rojas y azules)\n Recibo de placa para personas discapacitadas del Departamento de Vehículos"], "expected_facts": ["90"]}
{"id": "ground-042", "question": "Do veterans ride free on FAX?", "provenance": {"source": "FAX published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, yes — Active Military/Veteran riders with valid ID ride free on FAX Fixed Route buses [doc:fax-fares]. The acceptable types of ID include Active Military I.D. or Veteran I.D. [doc:fax-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how veterans obtain a FAX ID card or whether a separate application process applies to this rider class. To confirm the process and required documents, contact FAX directly at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (open Monday–Friday 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.) [doc:fax-reduced-fare-program].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-13.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["fax-fares", "fax-reduced-fare-program"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do veterans ride free on FAX?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, yes — Active Military/Veteran riders with valid ID ride free on FAX Fixed Route buses [fax-fares]. The acceptable types of ID include Active Military I.D. or Veteran I.D. [fax-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how veterans obtain a FAX ID card or whether a separate application process applies to this rider class. To confirm the process and required documents, contact FAX directly at (559) 621-RIDE (7433) or visit the Manchester Transit Center at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., Fresno, CA 93726 (open Monday–Friday 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.) [fax-reduced-fare-program].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-13.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>FAX: Fares &amp; Passes</li><li>FAX: Reduced Fare Program Instructions</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fixed Route Buses\nFare | Cost\nRegular Fare | $1.00\nReduced Cash Fare: Seniors (65 years or older) with valid ID People with Disabilities with valid ID Medicare Card Holders | Free\nChildren* (age 12 and under) children under 6 must be accompanied by an adult | Free\nActive Military/Veteran (with valid ID) | Free\nStudents Fresno State University, State Center Community College District, and Fresno Unified School District currently ride for free. See “Attention Students” above for details”. | $0.75\nNote: Clovis Transit does not charge fares Children must be accompanied by a fare-paying family member. Student ID required for all student fare riders.\nMonthly Passes | Cost\n31-Day Regular Unlimited rides | $36.00\n31-Day Reduced* Unlimited rides | Ride Free – no pass needed\n31-Day Student* Unlimited rides | $22.00\nHandy Ride* Valid for up to 60 rides per month per individual | $36.00\nReplacement ID | $3.00\n*Correct identification required.\nReduced fares and passes require proper identification\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nValid school issued identification cards\nMedicare Card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nActive Military I.D. or Veteran I.D.\nTransfers Transfers are free and allow you to transfer for unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction.\nHandy Ride\nADA Eligible Individual Per Ride Fare | $1.25\nCompanion to ADA certified individual | $1.25\nPersonal Care Attendant to ADA certified individual | Free", "ABOUT\nCurrently, all Reduced Fare riders ride free. Reduced Fare riders include Seniors 65 or older with ID, Medicare card holders, and persons with disability placards. Correct ID required.\nReduced Fare Riders:\nSeniors (65 years or older) with valid ID\nPeople with Disabilities with valid ID\nMedicare Card Holders\nHOW TO USE THE PROGRAM\nQualifying Reduced Fare riders can board any FAX Fixed Route bus at no cost to the rider. *Riders must show identification at the time of boarding. Handy Ride is not included.\nAcceptable types of I.D. are:\nFAX issued identification cards\nMedicare card and proper identification\nDriver’s License or State issued I.D. card (DMV)\nDMV Parking Placard with registration form\nReduced fare identification cards issued from other transit agencies, will be accepted on FAX fixed route buses. Laminated passes will not be accepted.\nFOR ASSISTANCE", "Do you need to purchase FAX passes in bulk? You can do so at a discounted rate.\nItem | Cost\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $18.00\n20 Single Ride Prepaid Cards (Reduced Fare)* | $9.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* | $45.00\n50 Single Ride Prepaid Cards* (Reduced Fare)* | $22.50\n*Cash/Card fares are entitled to Unlimited transfers for 90 minutes on all routes in any direction and subject to FAX policies and procedures.\nFor a limited time, Reduced Fares are FREE ! Reduced fare passengers must show an accepted identification type to ride free.\nBulk purchases can be made at:\nManchester Transit Center 3590 N. Blackstone Fresno, CA 93726 Monday – Friday 8:30 AM to 4:00 PM\nor by email request to [email protected]", "A Program for Persons with Disabilities Riding FAX Fixed-Route Buses at a Reduced Cost*\n1. To apply, visit the FAX Manchester Transit Center (MTC)\n MTC is located at 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., in front of the Manchester Mall.\n ID cards are made Monday through Friday from 8:30 a.m. to 3:30 p.m.\n If you have questions before your visit, call (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Along with your federal or state picture ID, one of the following documents will be\nrequired to verify your eligibility (see samples on the back).\n Medicare Identification Card (white card with red and blue stripes)\n Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) Disabled Person Placard Receipt\n Social Security Disability Insurance (SSI/SSDI) A ward Letter (dated within 12\nmonths of your visit)\n3. On the day of your visit:\n You must bring an official federal or state picture ID (e.g., driver’s license)\nand one of the above-listed documents.\no Note: If you are under the age of 16, please bring a school ID or a copy of\nyour birth certificate with your parent’s state or federal-issued photo ID.\n Your FAX photo ID will be taken.\n Your FAX ID card will be provided during your visit.\n4. Your new ID card will have an expir ation date (maximum three years from\nissuance) and must be renewed upon expiration.\n5. There is a $3 replacement fee for lost/damaged ID cards. NOTE: Riders are only\nallowed one ID at a time; old cards will be destroyed by the office staff.\n6. If you require a Personal Care Attendant (PCA) to ride with you, please bring a\nnote to MTC from your healthcare provider, on the provider’s letterhead, stating\nthe need. Once verified by MTC, the PCA can ride with you for free.\n*Reduced Fare riders can ride free at this time while subsidy funding is available.\nFresno Area Express does not discriminate against persons with disabilities.\nREDUCED FARE PROGRAM\nYou can automatically qualify for the Reduced Fare Program if you have a\nState or Federal photo ID card and one of the following:", "FAX buses accept cash, passes, and transfers.\nReduced Fare Program Instructions – English\nPrograma de Tarifas Reducidas – Español\nCurrent Fare Structure – English\nPresente Estructura de Tarifas – Español\nFree Student Fares subsidized by State Center Community College District, Fresno State University, and Fresno Unified School District\nTarifas gratuitas para estudiantes subsidiadas por el Distrito de Colegios Comunitarios de State Center (State Center Community College District), la Universidad Estatal de Fresno (Fresno State University) y el Distrito Escolar Unificado de Fresno (Fresno Unified School District)", "autobuses con rutas fijas por FAX a un costo reducido*\n1. Para aplicar, visite el FAX Centro de Tránsito de Manchester (MTC)\n MTC está ubicado en 3590 N. Blackstone Ave., frente al centro comercial de\nManchester.\n Las tarjetas de identificación se realizan de lunes a viernes de 8:30 a.m. a 3:30 p.m.\n Si tiene preguntas antes de su visita, llame al (559) 621-RIDE (7433).\n2. Junto con su identificación federal o estatal con foto, se requerirá uno de los siguientes\ndocumentos para verificar su elegibilidad (vea los ejemplos en la parte posterior).\n Tarjeta de identificación de Medicare (tarjeta blanca con rayas rojas y azules)\n Recibo de placa para personas discapacitadas del Departamento de Vehículos"], "expected_facts": ["free", "valid ID"]}
{"id": "ground-043", "question": "How much is a single ride on County Connection if I pay with Clipper?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The published policy does not specify the price of a single ride on County Connection when paying with Clipper. The passages describe Clipper products available on County Connection — including the Day Pass (capped at $3.75 for adults, youth, and Clipper START riders; $1.75 for seniors and RTC customers) [doc:cccta-clipper-card] and the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass [doc:cccta-clipper-card] — but do not state the base single-ride fare for Clipper payment.\n\nFor the current single-ride fare, contact County Connection directly or visit their fares page at countyconnection.com.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on County Connection if I pay with Clipper?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The published policy does not specify the price of a single ride on County Connection when paying with Clipper. The passages describe Clipper products available on County Connection — including the Day Pass (capped at $3.75 for adults, youth, and Clipper START riders; $1.75 for seniors and RTC customers) [cccta-clipper-card] and the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass [cccta-clipper-card] — but do not state the base single-ride fare for Clipper payment.</p><p>For the current single-ride fare, contact County Connection directly or visit their fares page at countyconnection.com.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "You may wish to take advantage of County Connection LINK’s Advance Fare Payment System. The program was designed to simplify your travel by allowing you to prepay your trips.\nHow it works:\nMail a check to County Connection LINK, 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord, CA 94520.\nA minimum amount of $50.00 is required to establish an account. Make the check payable to County Connection LINK. Please state the name of the LINK client on the check.\nYour LINK trips will be automatically deducted from your account for the amount of your trip. If the balance in your account decreases to $25.00 or less, you will be notified by phone or mail. If your account reaches a “0” balance, you will be required to pay the cash/ticket fare for each scheduled pick-up until such time as your LINK account has sufficient funds.\nIf you have any questions regarding this information and/or the balance of your existing account, please contact LINK, Monday through Friday, from 8:00am to 5:00pm at (925) 938-7433.\nThe Advance Fare Payment System is offered as a convenience by County Connection. Individuals who fail to provide sufficient funds to cover their trips and fall into arrears will be required to pay for their trips as they board and reimburse any funds in arrears. Individuals whose checks bounce will be required to pay by money order or cash for future trips.", "Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "Where to buy Clipper Cards\nClipper, the Bay Area’s transit fare payment system, is accepted on almost all Bay Area transit systems, including County Connection, Tri Delta Transit , WestCAT and Wheels .\nProducts Available on Clipper\nClipper on County Connection means riders can stop carrying multiple forms of payment; instead store it on a Clipper card."], "expected_facts": ["$ 2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-043", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-044", "question": "What does the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass cost, and which buses accept it?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe published policy does not state a price for the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass. [doc:cccta-clipper-card]\n\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. [doc:cccta-clipper-card] When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month, with the 31-day period beginning when the card is first tagged. [doc:cccta-clipper-card]\n\nFor pricing and to purchase the pass, contact County Connection directly or check their Clipper card page.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-clipper-card"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass cost, and which buses accept it?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The published policy does not state a price for the East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass. [cccta-clipper-card]</p><p>The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. [cccta-clipper-card] When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month, with the 31-day period beginning when the card is first tagged. [cccta-clipper-card]</p><p>For pricing and to purchase the pass, contact County Connection directly or check their Clipper card page.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Clipper Card</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "Clipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.\nEast Bay Regional 31-Day Pass\nThe East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is available only on Clipper and is accepted on all County Connection, Tri Delta, Wheels and WestCAT (except LYNX) fixed-route buses. Click here to learn more.", "Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "What is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card? Clipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhat is Clipper and where can I get a Clipper card?\nClipper is the Bay Area's all-in-one transit card that allows riders to take advantage of a more convenient way to pay for transit. Add a Clipper card to your mobile wallet, order a card online, or buy one in person. Learn more here .\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Marin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways. - Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card. - In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nWhere can I purchase a Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nMarin Local 31-day passes can be purchased in a variety of ways.\n- Online through the Clipper website and/or app, by registering your Clipper card online and purchasing using a credit or debit card.\n- In person at the Customer Service Window at the San Rafael Transit Center.\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper? The Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nHow do I use the Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass with Clipper?\nThe Marin Local 31-Day pass is activated on the day it is first used and is valid for 31 days after activation. The pass is good for unlimited local rides on both Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County for the duration of the 31-day period. Simply tap your card on the Clipper reader when boarding the bus!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period? Do not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat should I do with my Clipper card after the end of the 31-day pass period?\nDo not dispose of your Clipper card! You can reload your Clipper card with cash value, passes or both!\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card? With Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I already have a Clipper card?\nWith Next Generation Clipper, cardholders can now add the Marin Transit Local 31-day pass to their Clipper card. Use the Clipper website and/or app by registering your Clipper card online and purchase the pass using a credit or debit card.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026? Paper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nWhat if I still have unused paper passes after March 31, 2026?\nPaper passes will still be valid for use on Marin Transit and Golden Gate Transit in Marin County through early summer 2026.\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass? Riders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nHow can I get a discounted Marin Transit Local 31-Day Pass?\nRiders eligible for a discounted paper 31-day pass (youth, senior, disabled) must first get the appropriate Clipper card type that offers the discounted pass you wish to purchase. Learn more here .\nWhy is this change being made? Marin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhy is this change being made?\nMarin Transit is shifting paper 31-day passes to Clipper as part of a fare transition plan that was adopted by the Marin Transit Board of Directors in 2024.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper? Riders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if I do not want to use Clipper?\nRiders that do not want to use Clipper will not have access to 31-day passes after March 31, 2026.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk? We encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if my agency or organization purchased paper 31-day passes in bulk?\nWe encourage you to distribute all paper passes prior to March 31, 2026. Marin Transit will work with agencies or organizations that have purchased 31-day passes in bulk to find the most appropriate method to exchange the passes and/or discuss options for bulk purchases of 31-day passes with Clipper. Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov for assistance.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance? Email us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nWhat if I have additional questions or need assistance?\nEmail us at outreach@marintransit.gov , we are happy to assist with any questions you may have.\nPage last updated 3/5/2026", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00"], "expected_facts": ["$ 60", "rolling"]}
{"id": "ground-045", "question": "What ID can I show for the senior or disabled cash fare on County Connection?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nFor **senior cash fares** (age 65+), you can show [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]:\n- State issued photo ID as proof of age 65 or older\n\nFor **disabled cash fares**, you can show [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices]:\n- Photo ID and Medicare Card\n- Photo ID and DMV placard registration receipt\n- Veterans Disability\n\nThe senior or disabled cash fare on County Connection is $1.25 [doc:cccta-fare-types-prices].\n\nNote: The published policy does not specify an application process for paying cash fares with these forms of ID at the point of boarding. If you need further details about acceptable documentation, contact County Connection directly.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-fare-types-prices"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What ID can I show for the senior or disabled cash fare on County Connection?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>For **senior cash fares** (age 65+), you can show [cccta-fare-types-prices]:</p><p>- State issued photo ID as proof of age 65 or older</p><p>For **disabled cash fares**, you can show [cccta-fare-types-prices]:</p><p>- Photo ID and Medicare Card</p><p>- Photo ID and DMV placard registration receipt</p><p>- Veterans Disability</p><p>The senior or disabled cash fare on County Connection is $1.25 [cccta-fare-types-prices].</p><p>Note: The published policy does not specify an application process for paying cash fares with these forms of ID at the point of boarding. If you need further details about acceptable documentation, contact County Connection directly.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: Fare Types &amp; Prices</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["The following forms of ID are accepted with senior/disabled fares.\nRegional Transit Connection Discount Card (RTC)\nState issued photo ID as proof of age 65 or older\nPhoto ID and Medicare Card\nPhoto ID and DMV placard registration receipt\nVeterans Disability\nSenior Clipper Card", "County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted).", "You can get an adult Clipper card and add value to any card at:\nWalgreens, Whole Foods, and other select retailers\nParticipating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices\nOnline at clippercard.com\nCall Clipper at 877-878-8883\nBART ticket machines\nView map of Clipper retail locations\nA Youth Clipper card is required to receive the youth fare discount of 50% off the Adult Clipper single-ride fares. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash.\nA Senior Clipper card is required to receive senior discounts on cash value fares and transfers when paying with Clipper.\nApplications for Youth and Senior Clipper Cards may be submitted by mail, email, or fax. To get a card immediately, you can apply in person at participating transit agency ticket offices, including at County Connection offices.\nYouth & Senior Clipper Card Application\nClipper START is a pilot program that provides single-ride discounts to eligible riders. Eligible riders can receive a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares. To qualify you must be a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area, 19-64 years old, and have a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nClick here for more information and to start an online application.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.", "The RTC Discount ID Card is available to qualified persons with disabilities. RTC Card holders are eligible for reduced fares on bus, rail, and ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. New RTC cards and renewal cards are free. Replacement cards cost $3. RTC cards are all Clipper-enabled. RTC applications are available at County Connection offices located at 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted). For more information, click here .", "Clipper\nAdult (19-64 yrs)\nSingle Ride | $2.00\nBus-to-Bus Transfer | Free\nBART-to-Bus Transfer | $1.00\nClipper START/Youth (6-18)/ Senior (65+)/Disabled (RTC)\nSingle Ride | $1.00\nBART-to-Bus Transfer | $0.50\nLearn about Clipper START Get 50% off all Bay Area transit!\nCash\nAll Routes | $2.50\nYouth (6-18)\nSenior (65+)/Disabled\nAll Routes | $1.25\nSave by using Clipper! Click here to learn more\n* Exact cash fare only, drivers do not carry change\nClipper Passes Passes are only available on Clipper\nAdult (19-64)/Youth (6-18 yrs)/ Clipper START\nDay Pass* | $3.75\n31-Day Pass | $60\nSenior (65+)/Disabled (RTC)\nDay Pass* | $1.75\n* Daily fares are automatically capped at this amount\nChildren under 6 ride free when accompanied by an adult.\nTransfers\nBus-to-bus transfers are only available when using Clipper. One free transfer is automatically applied within 2 hours of initial boarding.\nClick here for more information on transfers.\nCash Fares\nFares may be paid with cash. Exact change only. Drivers do not carry change.\nYouth Fares\nYouth (ages 6-18) can get a 50% discount off Adult Clipper single-ride fares when using a Youth Clipper card. The youth fare discount is not available when paying with cash."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "RTC", "Veteran"]}
{"id": "ground-046", "question": "How much is a County Connection LINK paratransit trip, and does my personal care attendant pay?", "provenance": {"source": "CCCTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**Your LINK paratransit fare:** The one-way fare for any LINK trip is $5.00 [doc:cccta-link-fares]. LINK accepts cash and ticket vouchers, and drivers do not carry change, so you are encouraged to carry exact fare [doc:cccta-link-fares].\n\n**Your personal care attendant's fare:** Eligible personal care attendants ride free [doc:cccta-link-fares]. However, a companion is required to pay the $5.00 one-way fare [doc:cccta-link-fares].\n\nThe published policy does not specify how your attendant is documented or verified as eligible for the free ride—only that eligible personal care attendants receive it. For details on how to establish your attendant's eligibility, contact County Connection LINK at (925) 938-7433, Monday through Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm [doc:cccta-link-fares].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["cccta-link-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a County Connection LINK paratransit trip, and does my personal care attendant pay?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**Your LINK paratransit fare:** The one-way fare for any LINK trip is $5.00 [cccta-link-fares]. LINK accepts cash and ticket vouchers, and drivers do not carry change, so you are encouraged to carry exact fare [cccta-link-fares].</p><p>**Your personal care attendant's fare:** Eligible personal care attendants ride free [cccta-link-fares]. However, a companion is required to pay the $5.00 one-way fare [cccta-link-fares].</p><p>The published policy does not specify how your attendant is documented or verified as eligible for the free ride—only that eligible personal care attendants receive it. For details on how to establish your attendant's eligibility, contact County Connection LINK at (925) 938-7433, Monday through Friday, 8:00am to 5:00pm [cccta-link-fares].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>CCCTA: LINK Fares (paratransit)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["LINK accepts cash and ticket vouchers.\nOne-way fare for any LINK trip is $5.00\nEligible personal care attendants ride free\nA companion is required to pay the $5.00 one-way fare\nPlease note that drivers do not carry change and cannot make unscheduled stops to make change. You are encouraged to carry exact fare.", "You may wish to take advantage of County Connection LINK’s Advance Fare Payment System. The program was designed to simplify your travel by allowing you to prepay your trips.\nHow it works:\nMail a check to County Connection LINK, 2477 Arnold Industrial Way, Concord, CA 94520.\nA minimum amount of $50.00 is required to establish an account. Make the check payable to County Connection LINK. Please state the name of the LINK client on the check.\nYour LINK trips will be automatically deducted from your account for the amount of your trip. If the balance in your account decreases to $25.00 or less, you will be notified by phone or mail. If your account reaches a “0” balance, you will be required to pay the cash/ticket fare for each scheduled pick-up until such time as your LINK account has sufficient funds.\nIf you have any questions regarding this information and/or the balance of your existing account, please contact LINK, Monday through Friday, from 8:00am to 5:00pm at (925) 938-7433.\nThe Advance Fare Payment System is offered as a convenience by County Connection. Individuals who fail to provide sufficient funds to cover their trips and fall into arrears will be required to pay for their trips as they board and reimburse any funds in arrears. Individuals whose checks bounce will be required to pay by money order or cash for future trips.", "Download the Attendant Card Application (PDF) ( En Español )\nAttendants qualify for a discount fare when traveling with RTC cardholders who have been certified as eligible for attendant assistance. A RTC cardholder may apply for an “Attendant Supplemental” RTC/Clipper card that can be used to pay the attendant’s fare when accompanying the RTC cardholder. The “Attendant Supplemental” card is issued to the RTC cardholder who is responsible for its care and proper use. The card may not be used by anyone other than the attendant and may be used only when traveling with the RTC cardholder. Any misuse of this card will result in the permanent loss of the attendant card privilege. Misuse of the attendant card may constitute fare evasion and result in a citation and fine.\nRules for Use of Supplemental Attendant Card\nRTC cardholder is solely responsible for care and appropriate use of attendant card.\nCard may only be used by the attendant to pay for trips when traveling together with the eligible RTC card.\nTravel history of both cards may be monitored to ensure appropriate use, that is, every trip taken using the attendant card corresponds exactly to a trip taken by the RTC cardholder. NO EXCEPTIONS!\nThe attendant card privilege will be revoked permanently if the card is misused.", "County Connection participates in the Regional Transit Discount Card Program, which allows people with disabilities, those traveling with an attendant, and certain veterans to ride at reduced fares on all Bay Area transportation systems. It is also a Clipper card , and you can add cash value or products. The RTC Program does not apply to paratransit services. However, if you receive paratransit services in the Bay Area, you are eligible for an RTC card. People over 65 should apply for the Senior Clipper card , which provides the same discounts and never expires.\nApplications for the RTC Discount ID Card are accepted at San Francisco Bay Area transit agency locations . New applicants must go to a transit agency to have their photo taken. Individuals with disabilities who are applying for the RTC Discount Card and do not have a valid Medicare card or DMV placard identification card must secure a physician’s certificate of disability. Applications are forwarded to a central processing office, where application information is verified. Qualified applicants are then mailed the new RTC card.\nUpon receiving your Discount ID Card, you are immediately eligible for reduced fares on all participating public transit systems. No additional proof of eligibility is required, but you must show the Discount ID Card at the time you pay the fare.\nAs of January 9, 2022 there are no fees for new and renewal cards. The replacement card fee is reduced to $3. Acceptable forms of payment are cash (no bills larger than $20) and checks (credit cards are not accepted).", "The East Bay Regional 31-Day Pass is accepted on County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, Wheels, and WestCAT (except LYNX) buses. When purchased on your Clipper card, the pass is valid for a rolling 31 days, rather than a set calendar month. The 31-day period begins when the card is first tagged.\nHow to Use Clipper\nInformation on how to get a card, add value, and use it on County Connection buses.", "The Day Pass gives you unlimited rides for a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, WestCAT and Wheels routes ($3.75 for adults, youth, & Clipper START/$1.75 for senior and RTC customers).\nThe Day Pass discount is applied automatically. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and RTC customers) on any combination of the participating transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Clipper Cards still need to be tagged to show the drivers the passenger have paid the fare.\nRegular fare charged\nFare capped at maximum\nSubsequent trips are free\nFree rides and fares paid on WestCAT Lynx Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass."], "expected_facts": ["$5.00", "free"]}
{"id": "ground-055", "question": "How much is a single ride on an RTD bus in Stockton if I pay cash?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nA single ride on an RTD bus in Stockton costs **$1.50** if you pay cash as a full fare. [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n\nThe farebox on Express, Local, and Hopper buses accepts cash and coins, and you should have exact change ready because the farebox does not issue change. [doc:sjrtd-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single ride on an RTD bus in Stockton if I pay cash?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>A single ride on an RTD bus in Stockton costs **$1.50** if you pay cash as a full fare. [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>The farebox on Express, Local, and Hopper buses accepts cash and coins, and you should have exact change ready because the farebox does not issue change. [sjrtd-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Fares | One-Way | Monthly\nRoute 150 – Stockton to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $191\nRoute 150 – Lathrop to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $175\nRoute 150 – Tracy to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $158\nRoute 163 – Stockton to Sacramento | $7.00 | $176\nRoute 163 – Lodi to Sacramento | $7.00 | $167", "Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "Available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store, the Vamos Mobility App gives you the freedom to buy bus tickets right on your smartphone without having to wait in line or visit a Fare Vending Machine or Customer Service Center. This app only accepts credit/debit cards for payment.\nOn the Bus\nAll Express, Local, and Hopper buses has a farebox as you board through the front doors. Farebox accepts cash and coins. Please have exact amount ready. The farebox does not issue change.", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law)."], "expected_facts": ["$1.50"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-055", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-056", "question": "What does the discounted 31-day pass cost on San Joaquin RTD?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe discounted 31-day pass on San Joaquin RTD costs **$30.00**. This fare is valid for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC). [doc:sjrtd-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the discounted 31-day pass cost on San Joaquin RTD?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The discounted 31-day pass on San Joaquin RTD costs **$30.00**. This fare is valid for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC). [sjrtd-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "The Federal Transportation Administration (FTA) stipulates that transportation agencies receiving funding under Section 5307 must allow the elderly, persons with disabilities, and Medicare cardholders to ride certain services for a fare that is no more than one-half the base fare charged other persons. Access San Joaquin, the consolidated transportation services agency of San Joaquin County, will issue a Discount Fare Card (DFC) to approved, qualified persons who meet the eligibility criteria. Any person, regardless of age or condition, may ride for a discount fare when displaying a Medicare Card or a placard registration form from DMV and a photo ID (under federal law).", "Access San Joaquin staff will carefully review each application to ensure that only qualified persons are approved. Upon completion of review, a letter of certification or denial will be mailed. If your application for the DFC was denied, you have the right to appeal this decision.", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience."], "expected_facts": ["$30"]}
{"id": "ground-057", "question": "How much does the RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento cost one way?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on the passages provided, I can answer part of your question.\n\n**Route 163 – Stockton to Sacramento** costs **$7.00 one way** [doc:sjrtd-fares].\n\nHowever, the passages do not specify which RTD Commuter route you are asking about. If you are asking about a different commuter route from Stockton to Sacramento, the published policy does not list it. The commuter fares shown are for Route 150 (Stockton to Dublin BART, Lathrop to Dublin BART, and Tracy to Dublin BART) and Route 163 (Stockton to Sacramento and Lodi to Sacramento).\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming the specific route and current fares directly with San Joaquin Regional Transit District at sanjoaquinrtd.com or by contacting them to ensure you have the most up-to-date information.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much does the RTD Commuter bus from Stockton to Sacramento cost one way?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on the passages provided, I can answer part of your question.</p><p>**Route 163 – Stockton to Sacramento** costs **$7.00 one way** [sjrtd-fares].</p><p>However, the passages do not specify which RTD Commuter route you are asking about. If you are asking about a different commuter route from Stockton to Sacramento, the published policy does not list it. The commuter fares shown are for Route 150 (Stockton to Dublin BART, Lathrop to Dublin BART, and Tracy to Dublin BART) and Route 163 (Stockton to Sacramento and Lodi to Sacramento).</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14, I recommend confirming the specific route and current fares directly with San Joaquin Regional Transit District at sanjoaquinrtd.com or by contacting them to ensure you have the most up-to-date information.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares | One-Way | Monthly\nRoute 150 – Stockton to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $191\nRoute 150 – Lathrop to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $175\nRoute 150 – Tracy to Dublin BART | $7.00 | $158\nRoute 163 – Stockton to Sacramento | $7.00 | $176\nRoute 163 – Lodi to Sacramento | $7.00 | $167", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "SacRT offers fare-free transit for students in Transitional Kindergarten through 12th grade with the RydeFreeRT program and has a partnership with Los Rios and Sacramento State for college students to ride SacRT.\nCalifornia State Workers\nSEIU members have access to free transit through SacRT! Other state employee unions are eligible for transit subsidies to cover most of your commute costs.", "Medical certification must be completed if you fall within the Discount Fare Card (DFC) eligible conditions. If you are one of the following, no medical certification is needed (a valid photo ID must be included):\nMedicare cardholder\nDMV disabled placard registration form\nA veteran with a valid United States Department of Defense DD Form 214 or a San Joaquin County Health Department Veteran’s Discount Card\nA visitor possessing a valid disabled ID issued by another transit agency\nSenior citizens* Residing in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older Residing in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older Residing in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nResiding in Manteca or Lathrop who are 62 years or older\nResiding in Tracy or Escalon who are 65 years or older\nResiding in Stockton, Lodi, Ripon, or other cities in San Joaquin County who are 60 years or older\nAccess San Joaquin will respond to your completed application within 21 working days of receipt. You will receive written notification of your certification and an identification card (if certified).", "SUSD and SJCOE Ride Free – San Joaquin County of Education ( SJCOE ) 7th-12th grade students, and Stockton Unified School District 9th-12th grade students ride for free with a valid student ID .\nBulk Pass Program – 50% discount available to all 501(c)(3) non-profit organizations who purchases 50 or more bus passes.", "Our mobile fare app Transit Connect allows you to pre-purchase, store and activate SacRT tickets and passes instantly on your smartphone for both bus and light rail. Simply download the app from either the App Store or Google Play to get started.\nRide buses and light rail trains for 90 minutes using a valid single ride ticket\nState of the art security to protect personal information\nPurchase single ride tickets, daily or monthly passes\nLearn more at sacrt.com/TransitConnect ."], "expected_facts": ["$7.00"]}
{"id": "ground-058", "question": "What does Van Go! cost for a short trip?", "provenance": {"source": "SJRTD published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Van Go! is San Joaquin RTD's service for longer trips. For a short trip (the first 5 miles), the cost depends on your rider class:\n\n- **Full fare**: $4.00 [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n- **Discount fare**: $3.00, valid for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC) [doc:sjrtd-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["sjrtd-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does Van Go! cost for a short trip?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Van Go! is San Joaquin RTD's service for longer trips. For a short trip (the first 5 miles), the cost depends on your rider class:</p><p>- **Full fare**: $4.00 [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>- **Discount fare**: $3.00, valid for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card (DFC) [sjrtd-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SJRTD: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares | First 5 miles | Additional miles\nFull – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. | $4.00 | $0.50 per mile\nDiscount – Based fare per passenger for a one-way trip. Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $3.00 | $0.50 per mile", "Fares | 1-Ride | 1-Day | 31-Day\nFull – Ages 18 to 59. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $65.00\nDiscount – Valid only for seniors (ages 60 and over), persons with disabilities, veterans, Medicare cardholders, and all other eligible passengers with a valid Discount Fare Card ( DFC ) . | $0.75 | $2.00 | $30.00\nStudent – Ages 5 to 17 and college students with valid student ID. | $1.50 | $4.00 | $40.00\nChildren – Up to three children ages 4 and under ride free of charge when accompanied by a fare-paying adult. The fare for each additional child costs $1.50. Riders under the age of 9 must be accompanied by an adult.", "Buy most tickets in-person with a live representative at the Customer Service Center. This location accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit cards. Due to safety reasons, we strongly encourage all customers to have exact change ready. Once a customer pays for a ticket and a ticket is issued, there is no refunds or exchanges.\nAvailable for purchase:\n1-Ride *\n1-Day *\n31-Day *\nCommuter One-Way\nParatransit\n*Validation required at the time of use.\nFare Vending Machine\nThis machine accepts cash, coins, and credit/debit card. Please have exact amount ready. The machine does not issue change.\nOnline\nEasily and securely pay your fare with the Vamos Mobility App , your all-in-one solution for a seamless and convenient travel experience.", "Available on Apple App Store and Google Play Store, the Vamos Mobility App gives you the freedom to buy bus tickets right on your smartphone without having to wait in line or visit a Fare Vending Machine or Customer Service Center. This app only accepts credit/debit cards for payment.\nOn the Bus\nAll Express, Local, and Hopper buses has a farebox as you board through the front doors. Farebox accepts cash and coins. Please have exact amount ready. The farebox does not issue change.", "Fares | 1-Ride | Online Only\nFull – One-way fare. Reservations required in advance. For those individuals who are eligible under the Americans with Disabilities Act ( ADA ) for paratransit services . *10 physical passes will be mail to your home address. | $3.00", "To be certified for the Discount Fare Card (DFC), passengers must complete the following steps. Once the steps have been completed and all information is submitted, you will be notified within 21 days whether or not you have been certified for a DFC."], "expected_facts": ["$4.00", "$ 0.50"]}
{"id": "ground-actransit-001", "question": "How much is a regular adult local single ride on AC Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A regular adult local single ride on AC Transit costs either $2.75 if you pay with Clipper or a contactless bank card, or $3.00 if you pay with cash [doc:actransit-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a regular adult local single ride on AC Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A regular adult local single ride on AC Transit costs either $2.75 if you pay with Clipper or a contactless bank card, or $3.00 if you pay with cash [actransit-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares Effective July 1, 2026\nAdult (Ages 19-64)\nDiscounted\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper or contactless bank card | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $2.75 | $3.00\nTransbay Single Ride | $6.50 | $6.50\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $3.75 | $3.50\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $6.00 | $6.50\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $27.50 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $99.00 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $234.00 | --\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper Card/Clipper Mobile | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $1.37 | $1.50\nTransbay Single Ride | $3.25 | $3.25\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $1.88 | $1.75\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $3.00 | $3.25\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $13.75 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $41.50 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | -- | --", "Clipper START offers eligible low-income riders in the Bay Area 50 percent off of AC Transit Adult single ride cash fares. Riders in the Clipper START program will receive their own personalized Clipper card in the mail to get their discounts at all the participating Clipper START transit agencies. Learn if you are eligible and apply at clipperstartcard.com .", "AC Transit offers fare maximums, which means certain passes are automatically applied once spending reaches a certain amount during a calendar period. Fare maximums by week and month are only available with Next-Generation Clipper and contactless payments.\nLocal Day Pass\nA Day Pass is available as a prepaid or fare maximum option for unlimited local rides from the time applied or issued until 3:00 am the next day.\nLocal Weekly Pass\nUnlimited local rides for a week. Applied automatically for the balance of a Sunday–Saturday week.\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local and Transbay trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.", "When transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, riders receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3.\nNOTE: Riders must use the same card/account for all trips.\nLearn more at Transfers .\nLocal Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Local bus line .\nTransbay Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Transbay bus line over a major Bay Area bridge.\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade\nRiders with a local ticket, transfer, or pass pay the difference between local fare and Transbay fare when boarding a Transbay bus.", "The Student Transit Pass gives eligible 6th–12th grade students in Alameda County free, unlimited rides on local AC Transit buses and discounts on regional transit. Students can use the pass year-round for any trip—school, work, activities, or just getting around—and it remains valid until they leave high school. Check eligibility and apply on the Student Transit Pass webpage .\nThis program does not apply to Dumbarton Express.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us ."], "expected_facts": ["$2.75", "$3.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-actransit-001", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-actransit-002", "question": "What does a Transbay single ride cost on AC Transit?", "provenance": {"source": "AC Transit published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A Transbay single ride on AC Transit costs $3.25 [doc:actransit-fares]. This price applies to both payment methods: $3.25 whether you pay with a Clipper Card/Clipper Mobile or with cash.\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["actransit-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does a Transbay single ride cost on AC Transit?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A Transbay single ride on AC Transit costs $3.25 [actransit-fares]. This price applies to both payment methods: $3.25 whether you pay with a Clipper Card/Clipper Mobile or with cash.</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>AC Transit: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Available on Clipper as a prepaid fare, this pass can only be purchased by Senior/Disabled riders and is valid at the beginning of the month. Riders who purchase this pass can take unlimited local rides. Riders can board Transbay routes by paying the Local to Transbay Upgrade fare. Eligible riders paying with AC Transit’s mobile payment platform don’t need to pre-pay for a Monthly Pass. Starting at the beginning of the month, once their fare purchases reach the cost of the pass, they will be upgraded to a Monthly Pass automatically.", "Fares Effective July 1, 2026\nAdult (Ages 19-64)\nDiscounted\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper or contactless bank card | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $2.75 | $3.00\nTransbay Single Ride | $6.50 | $6.50\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $3.75 | $3.50\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $6.00 | $6.50\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $27.50 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $99.00 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $234.00 | --\nFare Type | Pay with Clipper Card/Clipper Mobile | Pay with Cash\nLocal Single Ride | $1.37 | $1.50\nTransbay Single Ride | $3.25 | $3.25\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade | $1.88 | $1.75\nLocal Day Pass | Fare maximum at $3.00 | $3.25\nLocal Weekly Pass | Fare maximum at $13.75 | --\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass | Fare maximum at $41.50 | --\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass | -- | --", "When transferring to another regional system using Next-Generation Clipper or contactless payments, riders receive a credit equal in value to the last fare paid, up to $3.\nNOTE: Riders must use the same card/account for all trips.\nLearn more at Transfers .\nLocal Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Local bus line .\nTransbay Single Ride\nA one-way trip taken on a Transbay bus line over a major Bay Area bridge.\nLocal to Transbay Upgrade\nRiders with a local ticket, transfer, or pass pay the difference between local fare and Transbay fare when boarding a Transbay bus.", "The AC Transit EasyPass program is an annual group transit benefit for eligible organizations. This program is offered to employers, residential communities, and colleges or universities whose user group will consist of 100 or more people or residential units. Participation in this program provides access to unlimited bus rides all year long by paying an annual fee for everyone in your group. Sponsoring a pool of participants, keeps individual pass costs low, giving every participant access to unlimited bus service whenever they want. While EasyPass programs are invoiced annually, they cost as little as $5-$18 a month per participant depending on program, location and group size. Visit our EasyPass page to learn more.", "Token Transit users may continue to use the app to purchase single rides and calendar-based passes through fare maximums. Token Transit users will not receive transfer benefits available through Next-Generation Clipper and contactless bank cards. If you have questions about refunds or experience technical issues while purchasing mobile fare, please contact Token Transit at help@tokentransit.com .\nAC Transit will discontinue support for Token Transit in the future. Users are encouraged to switch to Next-Generation Clipper or contactless bank cards, both available in a mobile wallet.\nQuestions about fares? We know you might still have questions about what fare is best for you. If you don’t find the help you are looking for, Contact Us .", "AC Transit offers fare maximums, which means certain passes are automatically applied once spending reaches a certain amount during a calendar period. Fare maximums by week and month are only available with Next-Generation Clipper and contactless payments.\nLocal Day Pass\nA Day Pass is available as a prepaid or fare maximum option for unlimited local rides from the time applied or issued until 3:00 am the next day.\nLocal Weekly Pass\nUnlimited local rides for a week. Applied automatically for the balance of a Sunday–Saturday week.\nLocal Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days.\nTransbay Monthly/31-day Pass\nValid for unlimited local and Transbay trips within a calendar month when automatically applied. If prepaid, valid for 31 consecutive days."], "expected_facts": ["$ 6.50"]}
{"id": "ground-047", "question": "How much is the cash fare on a local WestCAT bus?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The cash fare for a local WestCAT bus is $1.75 for riders age 6–64 [doc:westcat-fares-all]. Riders age 65 and older or with a disability or Medicare card pay $0.75, and children under age 6 ride free [doc:westcat-fares-all]. Exact fare is required when paying with cash [doc:westcat-fares-all].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is the cash fare on a local WestCAT bus?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The cash fare for a local WestCAT bus is $1.75 for riders age 6–64 [westcat-fares-all]. Riders age 65 and older or with a disability or Medicare card pay $0.75, and children under age 6 ride free [westcat-fares-all]. Exact fare is required when paying with cash [westcat-fares-all].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes."], "expected_facts": ["$1.75"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-047", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-048", "question": "What does the LYNX bus to San Francisco cost, and is there a monthly pass?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\n**LYNX Transbay Bus fare to San Francisco:**\nThe one-way cash fare for LYNX Transbay Bus is $5.00 for ages 6–64, and $2.00 for ages 65+ or disabled/Medicare riders [doc:westcat-fares-all]. Children under age 6 ride free [doc:westcat-fares-all].\n\n**Monthly pass:**\nYes. WestCAT offers a LYNX 31-Day Pass, which allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses, and LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. The pass is good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use [doc:westcat-fares-all]. \n\nThe LYNX 31-Day Pass costs $140.00 for ages 6–64, and $70.00 for ages 65+ or disabled/Medicare riders [doc:westcat-fares-all]. Passes are also available on Clipper cards [doc:westcat-fares-all].", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the LYNX bus to San Francisco cost, and is there a monthly pass?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>**LYNX Transbay Bus fare to San Francisco:**</p><p>The one-way cash fare for LYNX Transbay Bus is $5.00 for ages 6–64, and $2.00 for ages 65+ or disabled/Medicare riders [westcat-fares-all]. Children under age 6 ride free [westcat-fares-all].</p><p>**Monthly pass:**</p><p>Yes. WestCAT offers a LYNX 31-Day Pass, which allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses, and LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. The pass is good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use [westcat-fares-all].</p><p>The LYNX 31-Day Pass costs $140.00 for ages 6–64, and $70.00 for ages 65+ or disabled/Medicare riders [westcat-fares-all]. Passes are also available on Clipper cards [westcat-fares-all].</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load.\nPhysical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online.\nYouth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free.\nAdding Value and Passes Add value through the Clipper app, Clipper website, BART Ticket Vending Machines, in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.) or at participating Walgreens locations. Products loaded online or by phone may take up to three to five days to appear. Autoload can automatically reload your card when your balance is low.\nAdding Value and Passes\nClipper START Riders ages 19–64 with qualifying incomes can apply for Clipper START to receive discounted fares. Riders must apply for and use the START card. Apply online at www.clipperstartcard.com", "Clipper Card Costs Virtual Adult cards: Free (limited time) with a $3 minimum load. Physical cards: $3 fee, waived with Autoload when ordered online. Youth, Senior, Clipper START and Clipper Access (formerly RTC) cards: Free."], "expected_facts": ["$5.00", "$140"]}
{"id": "ground-049", "question": "What ID can I show to get the reduced fare on WestCAT?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "To get a reduced fare on WestCAT, you may be asked to show one of the following [doc:westcat-fares-all]:\n\n- State issued ID (senior fare)\n- Clipper Access (RTC)\n- Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\n- Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\n- Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n\nThe published policy does not specify which ID document applies to which rider class beyond these categories. To confirm which document you need to show based on your situation, contact WestCAT at (510) 724-7993 [doc:westcat-clipper].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-clipper", "westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What ID can I show to get the reduced fare on WestCAT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>To get a reduced fare on WestCAT, you may be asked to show one of the following [westcat-fares-all]:</p><p>- State issued ID (senior fare)</p><p>- Clipper Access (RTC)</p><p>- Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)</p><p>- Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard</p><p>- Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)</p><p>The published policy does not specify which ID document applies to which rider class beyond these categories. To confirm which document you need to show based on your situation, contact WestCAT at (510) 724-7993 [westcat-clipper].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: Clipper Card</li><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares.", "Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nSenior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded.\nTagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep.\nFor more information on how to use Clipper, see the guide at ClipperCard.com/westcat or call WestCAT at (510) 724-7993.\nWhere to Get a Clipper Card Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card. Order a physical card at clippercard.com Purchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations. Obtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).", "Passengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding. Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Accepted only at established transfer points. Not valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc). Not valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip). Valid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip. Void if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable. May not be shared with another passenger. No transfers to the BART system. Other Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.\nPassengers may request a transfer at the time of boarding.\nValid for up to one hour to board another route to complete one-way trip.\nAccepted only at established transfer points.\nNot valid to reboard the same route after a stopover (to run an errand, get groceries, etc).\nNot valid to return to original boarding location (no round trip).\nVoid if improperly used. Transfers will not be accepted if rolled, mutilated, torn or information is not readable.\nMay not be shared with another passenger.\nNo transfers to the BART system.\nOther Operators (ex. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "Riders using Clipper can load fare value and passes onto their cards at the WestCAT office in Pinole (some restrictions do apply), online at ClipperCard.com , and at participating retailers. Clipper users can also add fare value at terminals in BART stations."], "expected_facts": ["Medicare", "placard", "Veteran"]}
{"id": "ground-050", "question": "Does my 5-year-old pay to ride WestCAT?", "provenance": {"source": "WestCAT published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nNo, your 5-year-old does not pay to ride WestCAT. Children under age 6 ride free on all WestCAT services, including Local Fixed Route and Express Bus, LYNX Transbay Bus, ADA Paratransit Local, ADA Paratransit Regional, Senior Dial-a-Ride Local, and Senior Dial-a-Ride Regional. [doc:westcat-fares-all]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["westcat-fares-all"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does my 5-year-old pay to ride WestCAT?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>No, your 5-year-old does not pay to ride WestCAT. Children under age 6 ride free on all WestCAT services, including Local Fixed Route and Express Bus, LYNX Transbay Bus, ADA Paratransit Local, ADA Paratransit Regional, Senior Dial-a-Ride Local, and Senior Dial-a-Ride Regional. [westcat-fares-all]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>WestCAT: All Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Full fare is required to ride all WestCAT buses. Failure to pay the required fare is considered Fare Evasion, which is against the Law (California Penal Code 640). Violators are subject to fines and may be banned from riding.\nWestCAT fares are among the lowest in the Bay Area. We offer a variety of options to pay your fare, including cash and an assortment of passes. We also accept Clipper, the all-inclusive transit pass for the Bay Area. The fare box accepts bills and coins. However, if you are paying with cash, exact fare is required. Transfers are issued and accepted on all WestCAT buses and we accept transfers from neighboring transit agencies.\nTo receive a reduced fare, a passenger may be asked to show one of the following: State issued ID (senior fare) Clipper Access (RTC)* Photo ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal) Photo ID and registration receipt / DMV placard Disabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\nState issued ID (senior fare)\nClipper Access (RTC)*\nPhoto ID and Medicare card (not Medi-Cal)\nPhoto ID and registration receipt / DMV placard\nDisabled Veterans ID card (disabled fare)\n*Currently, we honor but do not certify Clipper Access (formerly known as the Regional Transit Connection card- RTC). Clipper Access is available to qualified persons with disabilities. Clipper Access cardholders are eligible for reduced fares on Fixed-Route Transit Bus, Rail, and Ferry systems throughout the San Francisco Bay Area. Information on obtaining Clipper Access is at this link: Clipper Access\nAllows pass holder unlimited travel in any direction on local fixed-route and express buses only. Passes are valid only if stamped with correct date and are good for use through the last bus on the service day in which first used. May be pre-purchased through WestCAT or from the driver on the day of travel. Exact fare is required. Passes will be time stamped.\nIf you pay cash value with your Clipper card, you can automatically earn a day pass for unlimited rides in a single day on most County Connection, Tri Delta Transit, and WestCAT and Wheels routes. Once you pay $3.75 in fares in a day ($1.75 for senior and Clipper Access customers) on any combination of these transit services, your rides will be free of charge for the rest of that day. Free rides and fares paid on WestCAT LYNX Transbay service do not apply toward a Day Pass.\nDiscount Tickets provide 10 one-way rides per sheet. There is no expiration date. Tickets are not sold in any other denomination and cannot be purchased from the driver.\nPasses are non-transferable and expire 1-year from print date. Allows unlimited travel in any direction on Local Fixed Route and Express buses. LYNX passes also allow travel on Local Routes. Passes are good for 31 consecutive days beginning on the first day of use. Paper passes will be time stamped. Passes also available on Clipper cards.", "Download the Clipper mobile app from the App Store or Google Play to get a virtual card.\nOrder a physical card at clippercard.com\nPurchase Adult Clipper cards at BART Ticket Vending Machines (TVMs) or at participating Walgreens locations.\nObtain a card in-person at the WestCAT Administrative Office in Pinole (Monday - Thursday, 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.).\nYouth and Senior Discount Applications Youth (ages 5–18) and Seniors (65+) can now apply for their discount Clipper cards online at clippercard.com , making it easier than ever to access discounted fares.", "CASH FARES (one-way)** | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLocal Fixed Route and Express Bus | $1.75 | $0.75 | Free\nLYNX Transbay Bus | $5.00 | $2.00 | Free\nADA Paratransit Local | $1.25 | $1.25 | Free\nADA Paratransit Regional | $3.00 | $3.00 | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Local | N/A | $1.25 (senior only) | Free\nSenior Dial-a-Ride Regional†† | N/A | $3.00 (senior only) | Free\nTRANSFER FARES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nTo WestCAT fixed Route Buses From AC Transit | $1.00 | $0.50 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Fixed Route Buses to WestCAT LYNX | $3.25 | $1.25 | Free\nFrom WestCAT Local Bus to another WestCAT Local Bus | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free with transfer slip at shared bus stops only | Free\nPASS PRICES | Age 6-64 | Age 65+ / Disabled/Medicare* | Under age 6\nLYNX 10-Ride Passes | $50.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nLYNX 31-Day Pass | $140.00 | $70.00 | N/A\nFixed Route 31-Day Pass | $40.00 | $20.00 | N/A\nFixed Route Day Pass | $3.50 | $1.50 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Local | $10.00 | $10.00 | N/A\nADA Paratransit 10-Ride Sheet Regional | $25.00 | $25.00 | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Local | N/A | $10.00 (senior only) | N/A\nSenior Dial-a-Ride 10-Ride Sheet Regional†† | N/A | $25.00 (senior only) | N/A\nOther Operators' (i.e. AC Transit, BART, etc.) employee and dependent passes are not accepted on any of WestCAT’s routes.", "If you need to use more than one bus to get to your destination, ask the driver for a transfer when you first board the bus. WestCAT transfers will be honored only on the day of issue at established transfer points within one hour of their time of issue. They will not be accepted on any line that will return the passenger to the area in which the transfer was originally issued, nor to re-board the same route.\nAll Transfers from County Connection and Tri Delta Transit at shared stops in Martinez are free with a valid transfer. WestCAT will not issue another transfer upon receiving a transfer from one of these two agencies in Martinez.\nTransfers from Capital Corridor trains to WestCAT are free anywhere within WestCAT’s service area with validated transfer (exception LYNX to/from SF, and Paratransit/ Dial-a-Ride). See Capitol Corridor’s Transit Transfer Page for more information.\nIf you are using Clipper, Clipper automatically grants free or discounted transfers when transferring to another agency that accepts Clipper. Transfers are good for 120 minutes after boarding a bus. If you tag your second bus within the appropriate time frame, you will be granted your transfer. If you tag after that period, Clipper will deduct additional fare from your card.", "1) ONLINE (Credit Card Only) – Click Here to Purchase Online\n2) IN PERSON (Cash/Credit/Debit/Check/Money Order/Transportation Benefit) - WestCAT Administrative Office Open Monday - Thursday from 9:00 a.m. – 4:00 p.m. 601 Walter Avenue Pinole, CA 94564 (510) 724-3331 ADMINISTRATIVE OFFICE CLOSED FRIDAYS, WEEKENDS AND HOLIDAYS\n3) BY MAIL (Personal Check / Money Order / Commuter Check) - Please call WestCAT Administrative Office at 510-724-3331 to request a mail order envelope. It takes approximately 3-4 working days for all mail in orders. Please allow for weekends and holidays. Using a FedEx/UPS account? Please provide account information for billing purposes.", "WestCAT accepts Clipper cards on all WestCAT Local, Express, and LYNX routes (some restrictions do apply). Clipper cards allow you to store multiple passes and cash fare on one easy-to-use card. Clipper automatically figures out the cost of your ride, including all discounts and transfers. Just tag and go!\nBoarding Requirements Adult / Youth Cards: Must hold at least $1.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Senior / Access (formerly RTC) Cards: Must hold at least $0.75 in cash value, or have a valid pass loaded. Tagging: Always tap your card on the reader near the front door when boarding and wait for the green light and beep."], "expected_facts": ["free", "6"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-050", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-059", "question": "What's the cash fare on RTA Route 9 from Paso Robles to San Luis Obispo?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe cash fare on RTA Route 9 is **$2.00 for regular riders** [doc:slorta-fares]. This fare applies regardless of the origin and destination within the route.\n\nIf you qualify for a discount, the published fares are:\n- **$1.00** for Seniors (65–79), Disabled riders, or Medicare Cardholders [doc:slorta-fares]\n- **Free with VIP Card** for Seniors (80+) or ADA Cardholders [doc:slorta-fares]\n- **$1.00** for Students (K–12) [doc:slorta-fares]\n- **Free with adult rider** for Children 44″ and under [doc:slorta-fares]", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What's the cash fare on RTA Route 9 from Paso Robles to San Luis Obispo?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The cash fare on RTA Route 9 is **$2.00 for regular riders** [slorta-fares]. This fare applies regardless of the origin and destination within the route.</p><p>If you qualify for a discount, the published fares are:</p><p>- **$1.00** for Seniors (65–79), Disabled riders, or Medicare Cardholders [slorta-fares]</p><p>- **Free with VIP Card** for Seniors (80+) or ADA Cardholders [slorta-fares]</p><p>- **$1.00** for Students (K–12) [slorta-fares]</p><p>- **Free with adult rider** for Children 44″ and under [slorta-fares]</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "Unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A & B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated.\n$5.50 General Public\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on all RTA and Paso Robles routes.\n$47.00 General Public\n$23.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "RTA and South County routes offer cash fares as well as discount passes . Cash fares vary by route. Please see each route’s page for more information.\nCash Fares | Rt. 9,10,12,15 | South County/A&B/Morro Bay Transit\nRegular | $2.00 | $1.50\nSenior (65–79) | $1.00 | $0.75\nSenior (80+) | Free with VIP Card | Free with VIP Card\nADA Cardholders | Free with ADA Card | Free with ADA Card\nDisabled | $1.00 | $0.75\nMedicare Cardholders | $1.00 | $0.75\nStudents (K–12) | $1.00 | $1.50 SoCo & Morro Bay. A&B is $1\nChildren 44″ and under | Free with adult rider | Free with adult rider\nVisit the Runabout and Dial-A-Ride pages for more information about these services and fares"], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"]}
{"id": "ground-060", "question": "How much is the Regional Day Pass on SLO RTA?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "The Regional Day Pass on SLO RTA costs $5.50 for the General Public [doc:slorta-passes]. It provides unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A & B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated [doc:slorta-passes]. You can purchase it on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets [doc:slorta-passes].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is the Regional Day Pass on SLO RTA?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>The Regional Day Pass on SLO RTA costs $5.50 for the General Public [slorta-passes]. It provides unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A &amp; B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated [slorta-passes]. You can purchase it on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets [slorta-passes].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Good for unlimited rides on all RTA, Paso Express, South County, Morro Bay Transit and SLO Transit routes for 31 consecutive days.\n$68.00 General Public\n$34.00 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Unlimited rides on all RTA, South County routes, SLO Transit, Paso Robles Routes A & B, and Morro Bay Transit routes for the date indicated.\n$5.50 General Public\nAvailable on the Bus, Online, on Token Transit or Pass Outlets.", "Unlimited free rides for seniors 80 or older on all RTA, SLO Transit, South County routes, Paso Robles Routes A & B and Morro Bay Transit. You may obtain a special photo ID card in person from RTA or show your valid ID. You may fill out an application in advance with a photograph. Learn more about our discount policy . VIP Passes must be obtained in person at one of the following locations:\nSLO Regional Rideshare 1114 Marsh Street San Luis Obispo, CA 93401", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00"], "expected_facts": ["$5.50"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-060", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-061", "question": "What does the RTA 31-Day Pass cost, and is there a cheaper rate for seniors?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe RTA 31-Day Pass costs **$47.00 for the General Public** [doc:slorta-passes]. For seniors aged 65–79, the rate is **$23.50** [doc:slorta-passes], which is half the general price.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-passes"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the RTA 31-Day Pass cost, and is there a cheaper rate for seniors?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The RTA 31-Day Pass costs **$47.00 for the General Public** [slorta-passes]. For seniors aged 65–79, the rate is **$23.50** [slorta-passes], which is half the general price.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on all RTA and Paso Robles routes.\n$47.00 General Public\n$23.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Good for unlimited rides on all RTA, Paso Express, South County, Morro Bay Transit and SLO Transit routes for 31 consecutive days.\n$68.00 General Public\n$34.00 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled / Students (K–12)\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit", "Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "RTA offers multiple types of discounted fares for riders who qualify. These fall into the following categories:\nDiscounted Fares\nHalf-Priced | Half the regular fare for Seniors (65-79), Disabled, Medicare Card Holders, and Students (K-12). Student discount applies to RTA and Paso Robles routes A & B only. The Student Discount does not apply on the South County routes 21,24,27 and 28 or Morro Bay Transit\nFree Fare | VIP Seniors who are 80+ years of age ride for free on all SLO County fixed routes. Children 44″ and smaller ride free when traveling with a regular-fare adult. (Not available on the South County routes) ADA eligible passengers : ADA passengers ride fixed route transit fare-free. Present your Certificate of Eligibility or your ADA Paratransit Service ID card. Care attendants and companions must pay full fare. Please be prepared to show your ID or other information to the driver if requested."], "expected_facts": ["$47", "$23.50"]}
{"id": "ground-062", "question": "Does RTA cap what I'm charged in a day if I pay by tapping a contactless card?", "provenance": {"source": "SLORTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. When you pay with a contactless card on RTA, fare capping guarantees that you won't be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day [doc:slorta-contactless].\n\nThe daily cap varies by bus system:\n- RTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15: $5.50 daily cap\n- RTA Routes A & B: $5.50 daily cap\n- South County: $3.00 daily cap\n- Morro Bay: $4.00 daily cap\n\n[doc:slorta-contactless]\n\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must use the same payment method each time you tap. If you switch between devices (for example, tapping with your iPhone for one trip and your Apple Watch for the next), you will lose the benefit of fare capping, even if the same bank card is linked to both devices [doc:slorta-contactless].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["slorta-contactless"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Does RTA cap what I'm charged in a day if I pay by tapping a contactless card?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. When you pay with a contactless card on RTA, fare capping guarantees that you won't be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day [slorta-contactless].</p><p>The daily cap varies by bus system:</p><p>- RTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 &amp; 15: $5.50 daily cap</p><p>- RTA Routes A &amp; B: $5.50 daily cap</p><p>- South County: $3.00 daily cap</p><p>- Morro Bay: $4.00 daily cap</p><p>[slorta-contactless]</p><p>To take advantage of fare capping, you must use the same payment method each time you tap. If you switch between devices (for example, tapping with your iPhone for one trip and your Apple Watch for the next), you will lose the benefit of fare capping, even if the same bank card is linked to both devices [slorta-contactless].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>SLORTA: Contactless Fares (Tap2Ride and fare capping)</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Fares when paying with a contactless payment method\nRTA Routes 9, 10, 12, 14 & 15 | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $2 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $1 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nRTA Routes A & B | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Weekly Cap | RTA Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $5.50 | $16 | $47 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $5.50 | $16 | $23.50 | $34\nSouth County | Single Ride | Daily Cap | Local Monthly Cap | Regional Monthly Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $3.00 | $37 | $68\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $1.50 | $18.50 | $34\nMorro Bay | Single Ride | Daily Cap\nFixed Route Regular Fare | $1.50 | $4.00\nFixed Route Discount Fare | $0.75 | $2.00\nHow do charges appear on my card statement? Fare is $2 for each time you board the bus and is charged to your card’s account like a standard credit or debit card transaction with the description “RTA Tap2 Ride.” To view your recent RTA rides and see what fare you were charged, visit our Traveler Portal or check your bank card statement.\nIs paying via a contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device secure? Yes. Tap2-Ride transactions are processed through the same secure networks used for all other Visa, Mastercard, and Discover transactions. Contactless cards are built on secure EMV® Chip technology, which has proven effective at reducing counterfeit fraud. Just like inserting your card, each EMV® Contactless transaction delivers a dynamic, one-time code to the payment reader that protects payment information and authorizes the transaction with a single tap.\nWill my information be kept private? Personal data is never stored, sold, or given away by RTA or any third-party agent. RTA does not have access to full credit card account numbers.\nDoes my card issuer see my travel history? No. Just like your card issuer does not see what you order from a coffee shop, the issuer will only see the amount of the transaction.\nWill I be charged a service fee for using my contactless payment method on RTA? No, there are no additional fees to use contactless-enabled payment methods.\nCan I pay for more than one person using one contactless payment method? No. At this time, each rider must pay with their own contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device.\nWill it cost more to use contactless payments? No. Fares paid via contactless payment will cost the same as standard Fixed Route fares and will be capped at daily, 7-day, and 31-day amounts, allowing you to pay as you go to receive bulk-ride discounts.\nDo I need to tap on each bus I board? Yes, you must tap on a contactless payment reader on each bus you board. The fare calculation software will keep track of your taps and only charge you $2 or $1 discounted fare, no matter how many times you tap.\nAre discounted fares available with contactless payment? Discounted contactless fares are now available for older adults (ages 65+), Disabled and Medicare cardholders via a simple, digitized verification program that enables riders to apply for and receive fare discounts. See our DISCOUNTS page to learn how to enable these fares.\nAll riders who are eligible for the discount can still pay the discount fare with cash, when boarding a bus; you’ll pay at the farebox instead of using the contactless payment reader.", "How do I use my contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device on RTA? Always tap in at the start of your journey on a payment reader to pay the right fare.\nLook for the contactless symbol on the RTA readers as you board the bus:\nSimply tap your contactless-enabled bank card or payment-enabled device below the screen on the contactless symbol and wait for the green “GO!” screen.\nWhat will I be charged? When paying with a contactless bank card or mobile wallet at a contactless payment reader aboard an RTA bus, your fare will be the same as paying with any other method. $2 Regular Fare or $1 Discount Fare* when tapping to pay. *Discounted fares on Tap2Ried are available for older adults (ages 65+), Medicare cardholders on RTA trips. See our Discount page to learn how to enable these fares.", "What is fare capping? Fare capping guarantees that you won’t be charged more than the daily cap fare for any RTA bus pass, no matter how many times you ride in a day—as long as you pay with the same contactless debit/credit card or mobile wallet throughout the day.\nTo take advantage of fare capping, you must always use the same payment method each time you tap. For example: Don’t use your iPhone to tap on for one trip, and then tap for your next trip with your Apple Watch. Even though the same bank card may be linked via both devices, you will lose the benefit of fare capping.\nIf you would like more information on fare capping, please contact RTA Customer Service at 805-541-2228, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Offices are closed on weekends and observed holidays.\nHow does fare capping work? Here is an example: Let’s say a single ride costs $2, so a rider who commutes to and from work 40 times in a typical month would pay $80 for the month. But with fare capping that’s not what happens. When a rider uses the same payment card, the system will stop charging their card at the cost of an RTA 31-day pass which is $47 (if they are only riding RTA buses). Or if riding continues in that same 31-days and they were riding Regional buses, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 31-day pass which is $68. If the rider rides multiple times in a single day, it will stop charging the rider at the cost of a Regional 1-day pass which is $5.50.\nHow does the system keep track of my rides? The Contactless payment system can keep track of your rides only when you use the SAME payment card/device.\nWhy is this a good thing? Fare capping can make public transit more accessible and fair since riders don’t need to pay the full purchase of a pass upfront.", "What can I do if my payment is not accepted by the payment reader? If you tap your contactless card or payment-enabled device and the payment reader indicates that the payment was not accepted, you will need to use another payment method to pay for your ride. Your payment may not be accepted for a few reasons:\nIt is possible that your payment method has been blocked if we have not received payment for a prior fare. Until payment is received for that fare, that payment method will remain blocked\nYour payment method has been blocked by your bank\nYour card or smart device is damaged\nYour card is not contactless-enabled\nYou may not have enough funds\nTo resolve an issue with your card, please contact your bank. In the meantime, you can use another contactless debit or credit card.\nHow can I get my blocked payment method accepted again? Once you have resolved the issue with your payment method, please contact RTA Customer Service by calling 888-678-2871, Monday through Friday, from 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. Once the issue is resolved, it can take up to 15 minutes before you can continue to use the same payment method at RTA payment readers.\nHow do I apply for a refund or dispute a charge? If you suspect you have been charged more than once or have not been charged the correct fare, you can see all of your charges on the Traveler Portal and request a refund there as well.\nWhat if my payment method is lost or stolen?\nIf your card is lost or stolen, contact your bank first to ensure that your card is canceled and to prevent unauthorized transactions.\nIf your card is subsequently found, and if your bank did not cancel it, you may continue using your card.", "Riders can also link their contactless payment card with the California Integrated Travel Project (Cal ITP) to verify eligibility to pay discount fares. Once a payment card is registered , a rider can use that card to pay discount fares on the Tap2Ride payment system.\nTo start the card registration process, visit the Cal ITP benefits page here: https:// benefits.calitp.org / and select RTA for your agency.", "Unlimited rides for 31 days in a row on South County Routes: 21,24 27 and 28, and does not include Route 10 that operates in the South County.\n$37.00 General Public\n$18.50 Seniors (65–79) / Disabled\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nSouth County routes 20-Ride Pass\n20 one-way rides on the South County routes 21, 24, 27 and 28\n$24.00 General Public\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\n7-Day Pass\nGood on all RTA and South County routes for 7 consecutive days.\n$16.00\nAvailable at Pass Outlets, Online and on Token Transit\nMorro Bay Transit\nDay Pass: $4.00 Day Pass Discounted (Disabled and Seniors): $2.00"], "expected_facts": ["$5.50"]}
{"id": "ground-vta-001", "question": "If I pay per ride with Clipper cash on VTA, can I end up paying more than a day pass costs in one day?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "No. The Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures you will not pay more than a Day Pass in one day when using Clipper cash on VTA.\n\nHere's how it works: \"Just load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you've paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\" [doc:vta-fares]\n\nThe Day Pass costs $3.75 [doc:vta-fares], so once your individual rides add up to that amount, all additional local rides for that day are free.\n\nNote: This applies to local bus and light rail rides. Express bus service requires an additional surcharge. [doc:vta-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>If I pay per ride with Clipper cash on VTA, can I end up paying more than a day pass costs in one day?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>No. The Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures you will not pay more than a Day Pass in one day when using Clipper cash on VTA.</p><p>Here's how it works: \"Just load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you've paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\" [vta-fares]</p><p>The Day Pass costs $3.75 [vta-fares], so once your individual rides add up to that amount, all additional local rides for that day are free.</p><p>Note: This applies to local bus and light rail rides. Express bus service requires an additional surcharge. [vta-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nFor example: When a VTA Adult Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Rider tags and pays $2.50 for a VTA local fare, they will receive an automatic $2.50 discount from their BART fare. If their total BART fare was $6.00, then they would only pay $3.50. BART Website\nAC Transit\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. AC Transit Website", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$5\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\nN/A\nNO\nDay Pass\n$15\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$180\nAnnual Pass\n$1980\n*available through subscription only.", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$30\nAnnual Pass\n$330\n*available through subscription only.", "Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$2.50\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$5\nNO\nDay Pass\n$7.50\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$90\nAnnual Pass\n$990\n*available through subscription only."], "expected_facts": ["Day Pass Accumulator"]}
{"id": "ground-vta-002", "question": "Do I get free transfers on VTA if I pay my single ride with Clipper?", "provenance": {"source": "VTA published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Yes. When you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper, you receive 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail [doc:vta-fares]. Payment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail [doc:vta-fares].\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vta-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>Do I get free transfers on VTA if I pay my single ride with Clipper?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Yes. When you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper, you receive 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail [vta-fares]. Payment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail [vta-fares].</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VTA: Fares</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Cash\nClipper\nSingle Ride\n$1.25\nYES\nYES See afternote for more information\n*2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\n8-Hour Light Rail Pass\n$2.50\nNO\nDay Pass\n$3.75\n*available through the Clipper Day Pass Accumulator feature.\nMonthly Pass\n$35\nAnnual Pass\n$385\n*available through subscription only.\nThe Day Pass Accumulator feature ensures the Clipper® cash rider will never pay more than the price of a Day Pass.\nJust load your Clipper card with cash, tag it upon boarding, and once you’ve paid the equivalent of a Day Pass, any further local rides that day will be free.\nPayment for a single ride with Clipper will be valid for 2 hours of FREE transfers across VTA buses and light rail. Express bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nAdult passengers traveling from a local bus to an Express bus will have to pay a surcharge of $2.50:\nAdult Clipper card users must pay the surcharge in the form of Clipper cash (only). Cash value must be loaded prior to tagging on the express route. The same Clipper card must be used when tagging from local to express routes. See details for SmartPass holders.\nYouth and Senior/Disabled/Medicare riders who transfer from local to express service will not have a surcharge.\nMonthly passes are valid for the calendar month purchased. They are not 31-day passes.\nMonthly passes are available for purchase from the 16th of the previous month through the 15th of the pass month. For example, a monthly pass for July is available for purchase from June 16 through July 15.\nExpress bus fare is required for any trip that includes express service.\nExpress Fares are for Express Bus routes only. Youth and seniors pay their regular fare on Express Buses.\nTo qualify for the Senior/Disabled/Medicare fare, you must present one of the following:\nMedicare Card\nRegional Transit Connection (RTC) Discount Card or a valid card from another California transit provider\nDMV Disabled License Plate registration\nDMV Disabled Parking Placard printout\nProof of age (65 and older)\nA valid picture I.D. may be required.\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade.\nA valid picture I.D. may be required\nMay ride VTA Express Bus service without an upgrade\nChildren under five ride free\nYouth Clipper Card will automatically convert into an adult card on your 19th birthday\nClipper\nA Clipper card makes it easy and affordable to ride VTA with free two-hour transfers.\nYou can load a Monthly Pass on your card, as well as cash value for Single Rides or a Day Pass.\nSmartPass\nSmartPass is a deeply discounted annual calendar-year pass institutions can provide to employees, residents or students", "VTA works closely with other area transit operators to provide convenient transfers for regional trips, including regional transfer locations and inter-operator transfer or fare agreements.\nHighway 17 Express\nVTA Paper Day Pass holders (issued from farebox or TVM through Day Pass Token exchange) receive free Local fares on Hwy17 Express. HWY 17 Express Wesbite\nCaltrain\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. Caltrain Website", "Riders can use contactless bank cards—plastic or mobile Visa, Mastercard, American Express and Discover cards—to pay adult transit fares.\n2 Hours of FREE Transfers when you purchase a single-ride fare using Clipper.\nPlease have exact change ready when boarding.", "VTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag.\nFor example: When a VTA Adult Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Rider tags and pays $2.50 for a VTA local fare, they will receive an automatic $2.50 discount from their BART fare. If their total BART fare was $6.00, then they would only pay $3.50. BART Website\nAC Transit\nVTA Clipper Cash or Contactless Bank Card Riders will receive automatic discount up to $2.85 when transferring from one operator to another within two (2) hours of initial tag. AC Transit Website", "The Regional Transit Connection Discount Card is a version of the Clipper card issued to persons with disabilities.\nWith the card you can buy discounted VTA Senior/Disabled monthly passes using Clipper or load cash value onto the card.\nHow do I use a Ticket Vending Machine?\nBeginning 12/10/25, to add value on your Clipper card, please press the “ Add Value to my Clipper Card” button and place your Clipper card on the Clipper reader labeled “ Place Card Here.” You can...\nDoes VTA require and enforce proof of payment on light rail?\nVTA light rail operates on a proof-of-payment system. You must possess validated fare while on the light rail system. Fare inspectors regularly patrol the system and ask passengers at random to...\nWhere can I buy or load value on a Clipper card?\nFind out your options for loading value on a Clipper card on our Clipper page or on Clipper.com.\nWhat are VTA's regional transfer rules?\nVTA has agreements with the following regional transit operators to provide convenient transfers - find the details on our Regional Transfers page: Highway 17 Express Caltrain BART ACE AC Transit...\nWhat are the age limits for discounted fares?\nTo qualify for discounted fares, Seniors must be age 65 or older and Youth must be age 5-18. Visit VTA's Fares page for more information.\nHow do I use the farebox on VTA buses?\nBoarding with a Clipper Card Tag Clipper cards on the Clipper reader located next to the farebox. Coins You can use any U.S. coin up to $1.00 in value. Insert them one at a time. Foreign coins and...\nCan I get a refund for lost or stolen passes?\nNo refunds will be made for purchased passes, based on VTA Board approved Fare Policy. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes will not be replaced.", "Whether you’re going to festivals, movies, parades or other leisure destinations, the 8-Hour Excursion Pass is your ticket to fun on Light Rail!\nExcursion Passes are sold only at Light Rail station ticket vending machines and are valid any events throughout the VTA system — for example, at Levi's Stadium or the San Jose Convention Center.\nThey make getting there, and back, so much easier!\nOne pass is good for eight hours from the time of purchase\nPasses cost the same as two Single Rides\nAfter the fun, just hop onboard – you’ll already have your pass\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nFor Conventions/Hotels only, the Convention Pass is just the ticket for savings and unlimited rides on VTA Light Rail and bus during any convention, seminar, meeting and other large gatherings.\nPasses are 25% off of the regular Day Pass price from groups of 100 or more.​\nPasses come in multiple day options (1-Day, 3-Day & 5-Day) for varying schedules.​\nScratch-off design allows cardholder to select days of use.\nOrders must be submitted a minimum of 10 business days prior to the date needed.\nFor more information, please contact Fares@vta.org .\nPer VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: Convention Passes are available only to Conventions/Hotels and not valid on Highway 17 Express. No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nYouth 18 and under enjoy unlimited rides on VTA bus and light rail with the Summer Youth Pass from June through August. That’s three months of travel for the price of two Youth Monthly passes!\nLearn more about Summer Youth Pass.\nThis popular program allows teachers, grades K - 12, within Santa Clara County the opportunity to take their students on field trips traveling by VTA buses and light rail for free.\nEach teacher is eligible to receive up to two “class passes” every school year. This allows students, teachers and chaperones to ride VTA buses and light rail to museums, cultural events, libraries, and other popular destinations for free.\nThe Class Pass Program transports over 50,000 teachers and students during each school year.\nComplete our Class Pass Field Trip Application form at least three weeks prior to the scheduled trip for consideration and processing.\nOnce approved, your Class Pass and trip itinerary will be sent to you 2 weeks prior to your scheduled trip.\nLearn more about the Class Pass Program.\nPassengers with a valid Paratransit ID Card receive free rides on VTA Local, Express bus service and Light Rail. The pass is not honored on Highway 17 Express, Dumbarton Express, Caltrain and Sports Service.\nYou can pre-purchase monthly Adult, Youth, Senior/Disabled Passes for an entire year and have them sent to you each month. It's simple, saves you money and you can start anytime of the year.\nThe Annual Pass Subscription Program allows you to pre-purchase 12 monthly passes (for 12 consecutive months) for the price of 11 monthly passes.\nAnnual Pass Application with Terms and Conditions.\n​​Per VTA Board Approved Fare Policy: No refunds shall be made for purchased passes. Lost or stolen Day Passes or Monthly Passes shall not be replaced.\nClipper START is a region-wide pilot program providing single-ride discounts to eligible riders.\nStarting January 1 ,2024, patrons using a personalized START Clipper card are eligible to receive 50% discount on their Adult single-ride eCash fares upon tagging on VTA bus and light rail Clipper device.\nTo qualify you must be:\nBe a resident of the San Francisco Bay Area.\nBe 19-64 years old.\nNot have an RTC Clipper Card for people with disabilities.\nHave a household income of 200% of the federal poverty level or less.\nFor more information and to apply, visit Clipper's START page .\nRegional Transfers\nVTA has agreements with area transit operators to provide convenient transfers. These include:\nHighway 17 Express\nCaltrain\nBART\nACE\nAC Transit\nSamtrans\nand more"], "expected_facts": ["2 hours", "Eexpress"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-vta-002", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-051", "question": "How much is a single adult ride on the Vine?", "provenance": {"source": "VINE published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "A single adult ride (ages 19–64) on Vine Transit costs $2.00. [doc:vine-fares]\n\nBased on policies published as of 2026-08-14.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vine-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>How much is a single adult ride on the Vine?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>A single adult ride (ages 19–64) on Vine Transit costs $2.00. [vine-fares]</p><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14.</p><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li>VINE: Fares &amp; Passes</li></ul></section>", "sources": ["Eligible riders can apply for the Clipper START program and receive 50% off of your Vine Transit Rides. For more information, click the link HERE .\nRiders age 80 and above are eligible for a complementary Lifetime Pass, valid on all Vine Transit routes and services. Please click HERE to download Lifetime Pass application form. Please send your completed application, along with a copy of your valid ID to [email protected] or by mail to Vine Mobility Manager, 625 Burnell St, Napa, CA 94559. We will mail you your complementary Lifetime Pass, or can make it available for pickup at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center. Please call (707) 259-8635 with any questions.\nVisit our online store to buy paper passes mailed to your home!\nFares Adult (19-64) $2.00 Youth (6-18) $1.25 Half* $1.00 Express** $3.50 BART*** $6.00 *Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29 Passes Day Pass* 20-Ride Pass** 31-Day Pass* BART 31-Day Pass*** Adult (19-64) $7.00 $30.00 $55.00 $125.00 Youth (6-18) $5.00 $21.00 $37.00 $125.00 Half $3.50 $15.00 $27.50 $125.00 *Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes\nFares\nAdult (19-64) | $2.00\nYouth (6-18) | $1.25\nHalf* | $1.00\nExpress** | $3.50\nBART*** | $6.00\n*Seniors (65+), disabled individuals, or Medicare Card holders **Fare on Route 21 ***Fare for Route 29", "Day Pass* | 20-Ride Pass** | 31-Day Pass* | BART 31-Day Pass***\nAdult (19-64) | $7.00 | $30.00 | $55.00 | $125.00\nYouth (6-18) | $5.00 | $21.00 | $37.00 | $125.00\nHalf | $3.50 | $15.00 | $27.50 | $125.00\n*Not valid on Route 29 **Routes 10, 11 one \"ride,\" Route 21 two \"rides\", Route 29 three \"rides\" ***Valid on all Vine routes", "VineGo is a shared ride service and provides transportation curb-to-curb, within 3/4 of a mile from the fixed route bus system. In order to use VineGo, you must be certified.\nPara Video en Español Haga Clic Aquí\nAre you in a VineGo service area ? Click the link to see if your address falls within out service area.\nWho qualifies?\nHow to apply?\nHow does the assessment take place?\nWhat should I bring?\nWhen do I know if I’m eligible? Is there an appeals process?\nHow does VineGo work?\nSchedule a ride\nTrack a ride (My Transit Manager)\nVineGo hours\nVineGo fares\nReasonable accommodation\nComments & complaints\nADA visitors to Napa Valley\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area\nDo I qualify for VineGo? Eligibility is on a case-by-case basis. According to ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) regulations, only people with physical and/or cognitive limitations that prevent them from riding the Vine bus can qualify for VineGo. Users who do not qualify for ADA paratransit are still eligible to receive travel training to learn how to navigate the Vine fixed-route system at no cost. If you are interested in receiving travel training, please call (707) 259-8635 or reach out to [email protected] .\nHow can I apply?\nTo apply to use paratransit, you must complete a two-part process:\nFirst, complete the VineGo Paratransit Application. Click below to access the VineGo Applications in your preferred language.\nEnglish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nSpanish VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nTagalog VineGo Application (Click to download fillable PDF)\nThen, e-mail the completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo as an attachment to your e-mail.\nAlternatively, if you prefer, you can print and mail your completed application to:\nParatransit Eligibility Office 625 Burnell Street Napa, CA 94559\nInstructions for completing a fillable PDF:\nClick one of the links above to access the paratransit application in your preferred language.\nClick on each individual field to enter your response.\nWhen finished, click the download button in the upper right hand corner of the document. (see image below)\nE-mail your completed application to [email protected] . Include a headshot/face photo.\nNeed help with the application process? Call NVTA at (707) 259-8635 or the Paratransit Eligibility Desk at (707) 253-4649.\nAll assessments, including renewals, will be made by phone appointment. For more information or to schedule your appointment, please contact the VineGo Paratransit Eligibility desk at (707) 253-4649.\nADA eligibility interviews are conducted entirely over the phone by MV Transportation in Santa Rosa.\nIs there anything I should prepare for my assessment?\nYes. Be prepared with a list of:\nAny sort of mobility device you use\nA list of medications you are taking\nThe name of a personal care attendant (PCA) you travel with, if you have one.\nWhen will I know if I am eligible?\nThe Americans with Disabilities Act allows paratransit providers like VineGo up to 21 days to determine if someone is eligible. However, final decisions are typically made much sooner. If an application is not processed within 21 days, is presumptive eligibility granted on the 22nd day until and unless the application is denied in writing\nIs there an appeals process?\nIf a paratransit applicant is deemed ineligible and does not agree with the determination they have the right to appeal the decision. To formally appeal a decision a letter must be submitted to NVTA within 60 days of receiving an eligibility determination letter. The letter shall be addressed to NVTA 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559 to the attention of the Manger of Public Transit. Upon receiving the letter an appeals panel will be assembled to hear an applicant’s appeal. The applicant or someone they appoint to speak on their behalf shall be contacted and an in person meeting with the panel will be scheduled. The panel shall consist of a member of Napa County’s Paratransit Coordinating Council (PCC), a member of NVTA’s Evaluation Contractor’s evaluation staff, and the Manager of Public Transit or his/her designated staff member. The appeals panel will render a final written decision within thirty (30) days of hearing the appeal. Should the appeals panel not render a decision within the thirty (30) days after the completion of the appeals process, NVTA shall provide paratransit service to the applicant until a decision is rendered. Free transportation shall be provided to the appealing applicant and their personal care attendant (PCA) to the appeals hearing.\nHow VineGo works\nVineGo can pick up passengers from the doorsteps of their home to their destinations. If VineGo can’t pick up or drop off passengers due to difficulties or physical barriers, VineGo will pick up/drop off the passenger at the nearest safe location). Roundtrips are welcome.\nEnrollees can schedule trips up to 7 days in advance. Same-day reservations are also welcome; however, we recommend making trip reservations in advance, as this service is in high demand. All rides are scheduled over the phone.\nKey Notes:\nBE FLEXIBLE. VineGo dispatch can negotiate a pickup time up to 60 minutes before or after your requested pick-up time.\nRemember that this is a SHARED RIDE SERVICE (NOT A TAXI SERVICE). The driver may pick up or drop off other passengers along the way.\nScheduling a ride Call (707) 252-2600 to schedule a ride. (You must be enrolled in VineGo before scheduling a ride.)\nIf your mobility device has changed, please let your reservationist know when you call the schedule your ride. For example, if you previously used a walker but now use a wheelchair, please let the reservationist know. You and your loved ones or caretakers can track your ride and receive notifications using the My Transit Manager app.\nReservation hours: Monday – Friday: 8am – 6pm Saturday – Sunday: 8am – 5pm\nVineGo service hours Mondays – Fridays: 6 am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSaturdays: 6:30am – 6 pm (6 pm – 9 pm as available )\nSundays: 8 am – 6 pm (6 pm-9 pm as available )\nFares\nFares range from $4 to $8 and are based on distance traveled. Passes are also available at a discounted price. Rides are charged based on the actual fare. ADA fares are no more than twice the adult fixed route fare charged by the Vine. Please note that VineGo is NOT an emergency transportation service. If you have an emergency of any nature, call 911.\nExact fare is required on all buses . All fares are non-refundable. Drivers cannot make change.\nVineGo One-Way Fares Calistoga St. Helena Yountville Napa American Canyon Kaiser Vallejo Calistoga X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 $8.00 St. Helena $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Yountville $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 $4.00 $8.00 Napa $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 American Canyon $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 $4.00 X $4.00 Kaiser Vallejo $8.00 $8.00 $8.00 $4.00 $4.00 X X = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.", "Transfers on the Vine are free. Please ask for transfers when boarding. Transfers are valid for 90 minutes after they are issued. No free transfers between Routes 10 and 11. There are no free transfers to the Route 29. Transfers cannot be used on the same route.", "Calistoga | St. Helena | Yountville | Napa | American Canyon | Kaiser Vallejo\nCalistoga | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00 | $8.00\nSt. Helena | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nYountville | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00 | $4.00 | $8.00\nNapa | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00\nAmerican Canyon | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X | $4.00\nKaiser Vallejo | $8.00 | $8.00 | $8.00 | $4.00 | $4.00 | X\nX = If the city of your residence has public transportation that offers door-to-door services, Vine Go will only provide trips outside that city.\nVineGo Discount Pass Eligible members of VineGo may purchase discount passes at the Soscol Gateway Transit Center or online. The purchase price of a discount pass is $20 for a $24 value.\nReasonable accommodation\nPublic transit passengers with disabilities may request reasonable modifications be made to accommodate their use of public transit. Requests should be made in written form and submitted to the Manager of Public Transit at 625 Burnell St., Napa, CA 94559.\nComments and complaints\nComments and complaints from the public may be received electronically through our customer service portal.\nClick here to submit a comment or complaint to NVTA staff. Additionally, prepaid comment cards are available in English and Spanish on Vine vehicles.\nWhen complaints are received, NVTA staff and/or contractor will acknowledge receipt of the complaint within 24 hours from the time it is received. All complaints should be investigated and resolved within 5 business days. Complaining party shall be notified of the agency’s resolution of the complaint within 5 business days. NVTA’s contractor shall maintain documentation of all complaints received and their resolution and provide monthly written summaries to be included in the contractor’s monthly invoice to NVTA.\nADA visitors to Napa County\nADA-eligible visitors from outside of Napa County may also use the VineGo paratransit service. ADA visitors can contact their local paratransit service and ask to send their ADA service information to VineGo by e-mail at [email protected] or by fax at (707) 259-8638. Alternatively, VineGo staff can request this on your behalf, just send us an e-mail with your name & local paratransit ID number and we will let you know once you are in our system and ready to book rides. Once VineGo has your information in our database, visitors can use VineGo with a courtesy period of 30 days of service during any 365-day period beginning with the visitors’ first use of VineGo. If the ADA visitor needs more time than 30 days, they must be formally relocated into the VineGo service area and provide their permanent Napa address.\nWhat happens if I live outside the service area?\nPeople who live outside of the paratransit service area can still apply for ADA paratransit eligibility. Although VineGo paratransit does not serve areas more than 3/4 mile from the Vine fixed-route service, if a paratransit eligible person who lives outside the service area can, for example, catch a ride with a neighbor or otherwise navigate to a point inside the service area, the individual may use the VineGo paratransit service.", "Riders can pay single-ride fares using cash directly on the bus. No change is given so please have the exact amount ready to pay as your board. Passes are sold at the following location:\nSoscol Gateway Transit Center 625 Burnell St. Napa, CA 94559\nBulk Passes Need to buy passes in bulk? Fill out our bulk pass order form and send it to the emails listed in the form. Please allow a minimum of 24 hours upon receipt to process request for pick-up."], "expected_facts": ["$2.00"], "pair_id": "pair-ground-051", "is_reference": true, "language": "en"}
{"id": "ground-052", "question": "What does the Vine Route 29 to El Cerrito BART cost?", "provenance": {"source": "VINE published fare pages (see corpus/manifest.yaml)", "license": "no license granted by this project — source text remains the copyright of the respective transit agency, reproduced here as short excerpts for evaluation and analysis, not licensed for redistribution; see corpus/LICENSE-NOTE.md", "added": "2026-06-16", "note": "answer recorded from the deployed assistant pipeline"}, "target_response": {"text": "Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:\n\nThe passages state that Route 29 is a Vine Transit route, and the fare for Route 29 is $6.00 [doc:vine-fares]. However, the passages do not mention El Cerrito or any BART connection beyond Napa Valley Vine Transit's own service area. Route 29 appears to be a local Vine route within the Napa Valley system.\n\nIf you are asking about traveling from Vine Transit to BART in El Cerrito (which is outside Napa County), that would be outside the scope of Vine Transit's published fares. For questions about connections to BART or transit to El Cerrito, please contact Napa Valley Vine Transit directly at (707) 259-8635 or visit vinetransit.com, or contact 511.org for regional transit information.", "refused": false, "language": "en", "citations": ["vine-fares"]}, "should_refuse": false, "transcript_html": "<section lang=\"en\"><h2>Rider question</h2><p>What does the Vine Route 29 to El Cerrito BART cost?</p><h3>Answer</h3><p>Based on policies published as of 2026-08-14:</p><p>The passages state that Route 29 is a Vine Transit route, and the fare for Route 29 is $6.00 [vine-fares]. However, the passages do not mention El Cerrito or any BART connection beyond Napa Valley Vine Transit's own service area. Route 29 appears to be a local Vine route within the Napa Valley system.</p><p>If you are asking about traveling from Vine Transit to BART in El Cerrito (which is outside Napa County), that would be outside the s